const e={0:{zh:`两篇序文揭开了《品花宝鉴》的神秘面纱。

第一篇序者自称"幻中了幻居士"，盛赞此书兼得"绘形"与"绘声"之妙，将其与《聊斋》《红楼梦》并列，誉为说部中"另具一格"之作。他辗转从友人处借抄，历经七月校订，才将这部险些湮没的手稿刻版流传。第二篇序文则出自作者石函氏之手，娓娓道来创作缘起：他客居京都，科场失意，借问于歌楼舞榭间消遣苦闷，由此得识梨园声色之美、伶人性情之真伪。友人比部力劝他将亲历见闻写成小说，言词间自有褒贬，此即《品花宝鉴》诞生之契机。两篇序文相互辉映，一为传播者的感慨，一为创作者的夫子自道，共同构成这部奇书的入口。`,en:`Two prefaces open the doors of Pinhua Baojian. 

The first, by a writer styling himself "Master of Illusion within Illusion," lavishes praise on the novel's rare ability to evoke both image and sound — ranking it alongside Strange Tales from a Chinese Studio and Dream of the Red Chamber as a singular achievement in Chinese fiction. He describes his painstaking effort to borrow and copy the manuscript from friends, spending seven months in editorial revision to rescue it from near-oblivion before it could be printed. The second preface is the author's own voice: failing the imperial examinations, he found himself stranded in Beijing, drowning his frustrations nightly in the pleasure quarters of the theater world, where he learned to distinguish genuine feeling from performance, elegance from vulgarity. A friend urged him to transform these observations into a novel — and so Pinhua Baojian was born. Together, the two prefaces frame the work beautifully: one a reader's astonished discovery, the other a writer's candid confession, both insisting that even in this world of play and artifice, something true and human can be found.`},1:{zh:`开卷先立一副品评的尺度。京师歌台舞榭甲于天下，作者遂以游戏之笔摹写游戏之人：将缙绅子弟按"情"字分作正、上、高、逸、华、豪、狂、趣、和、乐十等，又将梨园名旦分作至、慧、韵、醇、淑、烈、直、酣、艳、媚十等；而淫、邪、黠、荡、贪、魔、祟、蠹八种下流，则不配以"情"字相加。六十卷《品花宝鉴》的褒贬，由此定下基调。

继而引出主人翁的家世：翰林院侍读学士梅士燮，金陵阀阅，三代单传，貌肃如秋而心和似春；夫人颜夫人于二十九岁上梦神人授玉，遂生梅子玉，今年十七，貌如良玉，质比精金，虽居罗绮丛中而绝无纨绔习气。一日先生放学，表兄颜仲清偕解元史南湘联袂来访——仲清三岁失怙，其母郑氏绝食殉节，由士燮抚养成人，后赘于王文辉家为婿。南湘忽以"世间何物最能娱耳悦目、动心荡魄"相诘，见子玉支吾其词，便从靴中取出新刻的《曲台花选》，历数袁宝珠、苏蕙芳、陆素兰、金漱芳、李玉林、王兰保、王桂保、林春喜八人的色艺，每人各系品题与赠诗。子玉看罢只是冷笑，力言以色事人者身既难洁、志亦太卑，天地灵秀只钟于女子。二人不服，援《毛诗》《赤壁赋》与陆闳、陶侃诸典相驳，又以梅花南枝先发、翎羽之美独在雄鸟为喻；子玉脸红语塞，心下将信将疑。

越两日，他往访表兄王恂，王恂径拉他上戏园——满园黑相公穿梭寻主，一个名字与袁宝珠仅差一字的蠢笨少年竟来摸他的手，子玉如坐涂炭，托词先归。归途车马拥塞，忽闻一缕非兰非麝的清香，掀帘望去，对面车中并坐两个妙童：一个艳若海棠，一个以玉为骨、以月为魂。四目相接，神摇目眩，车已过去。到家犹自恍惚，方悟南湘所言竟非虚妄。`,en:`The novel opens by laying down a scale of judgement. Beijing's theatres outshine all the empire's, and the author proposes to paint this world of play with a playful brush: gentry patrons are graded into ten degrees of "feeling" — upright, lofty, untrammelled, splendid, gallant, wild, playful, harmonious, joyous — and the celebrated dan performers into ten of their own; while eight baser types (lechery, corruption, cunning, dissipation, greed, obsession, malice, parasitism) are denied the word "feeling" altogether. This is the moral tuning-fork for all sixty chapters of Pinhua Baojian. Then the household: Mei Shixie, Hanlin Reader-in-Waiting, of an old Jinling line three generations sole-descended, austere as autumn in his bearing and mild as spring in his heart. 

At twenty-nine his wife Lady Yan dreamed that a spirit placed jade in her hands, and bore Mei Ziyu — now seventeen, fine as tempered gold, raised amid silk without a trace of the wastrel. One idle afternoon his cousin Yan Zhongqing arrives with the provincial laureate Shi Nanxiang. (Zhongqing lost his father at three; his mother Lady Zheng starved herself to follow her husband, and Mei Shixie raised the orphan, later marrying him into Wang Wenhui's household.) Nanxiang poses a challenge — what in this world most delights the eye and shakes the soul? — and when Ziyu equivocates, produces from his boot his freshly printed Qutai Huaxuan, cataloguing the beauty and artistry of eight performers: Yuan Baozhu, Su Huifang, Lu Sulan, Jin Shufang, Li Yulin, Wang Lanbao, Wang Guibao, and Lin Chunxi, each with an appraisal and a verse. Ziyu is unmoved. Those who trade on their looks, he argues, can keep neither their bodies clean nor their ambitions high; heaven's grace settles on women alone. His friends rebut him with the Mao Shi, the Rhapsody on the Red Cliff, and the histories of Lu Hong and Tao Kan, and with homelier proofs — the plum's south branch blooms first, and among birds it is always the male that wears the splendour. Ziyu flushes and falls silent, half-persuaded. 

Two days later he calls on his cousin Wang Xun, who hauls him off to a theatre, where the aisles teem with coarse boys hunting patrons and one dull-witted youth — whose name differs by a single character from Yuan Baozhu's — takes hold of his hand. Ziyu escapes as if from hot coals. Homeward, his carriage jams in the traffic; a scent that is neither orchid nor musk reaches him, and lifting the curtain he sees two boys seated in the opposite carriage — one lovely as a crab-apple blossom, the other with jade for bone and moonlight for soul. Their eyes meet; the carriage rolls on. He reaches home still dazed, and understands at last that Nanxiang was not exaggerating.`},2:{zh:`子玉一路想着车中所见的绝色，到家却撞见父亲正陪王文辉说话。二人先议开春同年团拜，已定下联锦班在姑苏会馆演戏，只是外放出差者众，怕凑不满四席；继而文辉托梅士燮为次女琼华物色佳婿，两人感叹择婿之难——尽有外貌甚好而内里平常者，也有小时聪明而大来变坏者。话未了，仆妇捧上两张红帖：一署"世愚侄魏聘才"，一署"门下晚学生李元茂"，都是自江南远道投奔而来。

花厅相见，两人判若云泥：魏聘才身材瘦小、面目伶俐，抢步上前满面堆笑便跪下叩头；李元茂却身形笨浊、浓眉近视，高高一揖后徐徐跪拜，如拜神像，掀唇咕噜一句竟无人听懂。士燮命许顺收拾书房后身两间屋子安置，又叫子玉引二人去见西席李性全。不过十日，那张千伶百俐的嘴已把满宅人哄得交口称好。

一夕元茂睡下，聘才与子玉闲话，先问相公之风，继而绘声绘色说起自己自扬州搭叶茂林的戏船北上，同舟十个苏州买来的孩子里，有琪官色如脂粉初调，更有一个姓杜的琴官，"世间的颜色比不上，画师也画不出这样神情"。子玉不动声色，逐一盘问衣色、同车何人、车围骡色、教师年纪，一一相符，这才拍手大笑——原来车中绝色正是此人。次早聘才携小厮四儿去投书，先会了颜仲清，继而王文辉归来，盘腿高坐，一面追述其父当年因刀笔损了阴骘而失掉解元，一面自夸三品京堂的俸银，说得聘才局促不安，心中暗比：这老头儿的架子，远不及梅老伯。是日王宅设宴，孙亮功携王桂保入席；席间行打擂令、飞花令，孙亮功装作小旦捧杯敬酒，学尽联锦班京丑的丑态，又被桂保吹了满脸梅花瓣，笑倒一堂，末了家人耳语一句，他便失色催饭而去。宴散，桂保得赏二十两，转到书房告诉王恂与仲清：班里新来了琴官、琪官两个，色艺俱佳，只怕史南湘的《花选》又要翻刻了。`,en:`Still turning over the face he glimpsed in the carriage, Ziyu comes home to find his father in conference with Wang Wenhui. The two are settling the New Year cohort banquet — the Lianjin troupe engaged, the Suzhou Guild Hall booked, though with so many colleagues posted away they may not fill four tables — after which Wenhui asks Mei Shixie to look out for a husband for his second daughter Qionghua, and the pair fall to lamenting how treacherous such choices are: handsome outsides that prove ordinary within, clever boys who curdle as they grow. A servant then brings in two red cards: one from "your unworthy nephew Wei Pincai," the other from "your humble student Li Yuanmao," both newly arrived from the south. 

In the reception hall the contrast is comic. Wei Pincai is slight and quick-eyed, springing forward all smiles to drop into a kowtow; Li Yuanmao is heavy and short-sighted, bowing high and then sinking slowly to his knees like a man saluting a temple idol, mumbling something no one can make out. Shixie has the steward Xu Shun prepare two rooms behind the study, and sends Ziyu to present them to the tutor Li Xingquan. Within ten days Pincai's nimble tongue has charmed the entire household. 

One evening, with Yuanmao asleep, he draws Ziyu into talk — first about the strange deference shown to actors in the capital, then about his own journey north, sharing a canal boat from Yangzhou with Ye Maolin and ten boys bought in Suzhou. Two were extraordinary: Qiguan, whose colour seemed mixed from powder and rouge, and a boy surnamed Du called Qinguan, whose beauty "no shade in the world can match, and no painter's brush record." Ziyu keeps his face still and cross-examines him — the colour of the robes, who shared the carriage, the blue cloth curtain, the white mule, the teacher's age — and when every detail answers, claps his hands and laughs aloud: these are the very boys. Next morning Pincai takes his page Si'er to deliver a letter, meets Yan Zhongqing, and then Wang Wenhui himself, who sits cross-legged in state, recalls how Pincai's father forfeited a provincial first place because a brush-and-knife omen appeared on his examination cover, and boasts of a third-rank stipend until his guest squirms — Pincai privately judging him far beneath the elder Mei. That evening Wang Wenhui feasts his cohort; Sun Lianggong arrives with Wang Guibao, and the drinking games turn riotous, Sun Lianggong mincing about in imitation of a dan actor to press wine on the guests, then blinded by plum petals Guibao blows into his face — until a whispered word from a servant sends him hurrying home to his formidable wife. Rewarded twenty taels, Guibao stops by the study to tell Wang Xun and Zhongqing of two newcomers, Qinguan and Qiguan, so gifted that Shi Nanxiang may have to reprint his Selection of Flowers.`},3:{zh:`魏聘才误了饭点，管事许顺留他在账房同吃，饭罢从紫竹书架上翻出一册刻本《曲台花选》，袖回房中细读，将八位名旦的色艺一一记熟，暗想京中相公果然名不虚传，却又疑心琴官的姿容未必输于此辈。正想着，叶茂林同联锦班大掌班金二来定堂会戏，为"分包"一事与许顺往复争执，末了议定正月初六在姑苏会馆开演。叶茂林又把园中行头脚色、堂会排场说得天花乱坠，并告知琴官正在师傅长庆处养病，琪官则定于腊月初十登台。

次日聘才带小厮四儿独往三乐园听联珠班的戏，但见楼上楼下相公穿梭，倚肩偎坐，应接不暇。邻座来了个胖子富三爷与七品小京官贵大爷，相公蓉官过来陪坐，说起青姨奶奶、白姨奶奶摔碗打架的家务丑事，语带尖酸，笑倒一座。忽有个颈生肉瘤的卖玉老者兜售琥珀鼻烟壶，索价十两，聘才随口还他二两，不料对方一口应承；聘才身边无银，正尴尬间，台上一声齐声喝彩，他身子一动，烟壶坠地摔碎，老者遂就势讹诈六两。亏得富三爷伸手喝止，赏了四百钱了事。聘才这才知二人一名富伦、一名贵芬，富伦之父曾任江宁藩司、广东巡抚，遂盛赞其父在南京已立名宦祠、水旱瘟疫屡屡显圣，哄得富伦十分受用。

楼上另有一个青黑脸的广东豪客，气焰逼人，那卖玉老者上楼兜搭，竟被家人乱推乱撵，砸了满盘料器，含泪而下。四人转往馆子小酌，隔壁恰是那豪客与相公春兰赌气：一个骂，一个砸，杯碗尽碎，末了掀翻桌子，四条大狗抢食满地，掌柜的赔笑打圆场，还教走堂的下回换细瓷来砸，好教大老爷听个清脆。原来此人是广东巨富奚十一，携十几万银子入京捐官，正是众人眼中的冤桶。散席时富伦抢着会账，约聘才次日到东城金牌楼相访。归家夜话，子玉又问琴官脾气，聘才只说他心里嵌着一块水晶，又硬又冷，任你怎样巴结也换不来一笑。子玉听了反而神往：这般清高自爱，才算十全人物，只怕落在庸耳俗目之中反要遭人白眼；一片怜惜，就此不知不觉系在琴官身上。`,en:`Wei Pincai, having missed the household meal, is invited to eat in the accounts room by the steward Xu Shun, and afterwards pulls a thin printed volume — the Qutai Huaxuan — from a bamboo shelf. Back in his room he reads it through, fixing in memory the eight performers and their arts, half convinced that the capital's famous boys deserve their fame, half suspecting that Qinguan's face would outshine them all. He is interrupted by Ye Maolin, who arrives with Jin Er, chief manager of the Lianjin troupe, to book the New Year performance; the two haggle with Xu Shun over whether the troupe may "split" its engagements, and settle at last on the sixth day at the Suzhou Guild Hall. Ye Maolin then paints the theatre in glowing colours — the costumes, the casts, the splendour of private banquets — and mentions that Qinguan is convalescing at his teacher Changqing's house, while Qiguan is to take the stage on the tenth. 

The next day Pincai takes his page Si'er to the Sanle Garden to hear the Lianzhu troupe, and finds the galleries thick with performers drifting from patron to patron, leaning on shoulders, settling at elbows. At the next table sit a stout man addressed as Third Master Fu and a seventh-rank clerk called Eldest Master Gui; the actor Rongguan joins them and retails, with acid sweetness, the domestic warfare between Concubine Qing and Concubine Bai — smashed porcelain, beaten maids — until the whole table is helpless with laughter. Then an old curio hawker with a swollen growth on his neck presses an amber snuff bottle on Pincai at ten taels; Pincai idly counters with two, and to his horror the old man accepts. He has no silver on him, and while he sits paralysed a roar of applause goes up, he starts, and the bottle shatters on the floor — whereupon the hawker demands six taels for the wreckage. Third Master Fu intervenes, silences the man, and settles it with four hundred cash. Only now does Pincai learn their names: Fu Lun and Gui Fen. Told that Fu Lun's late father served as provincial treasurer at Jiangning and governor of Guangdong, he launches into flattery — a shrine to the old man now stands in Nanjing, he says, and his spirit answers prayers in flood and plague — and Fu Lun glows with pleasure. 

Upstairs sits a dark-faced Cantonese grandee whose servants, when the hawker tries his luck there, shove the old man out bodily, smashing half his tray; he limps away in tears. The four adjourn to a restaurant, where the next room holds the same grandee quarrelling with the actor Chunlan: he curses, the boy smashes a cup, he smashes another, until the table itself goes over and four dogs fight over the spilled dishes. The proprietor soothes him with smiles and instructs the waiter to bring finer porcelain next time, so the breaking will ring more sweetly. This is Xi Shiyi, a Cantonese millionaire come to buy office with a hundred thousand taels — the fattest pigeon in Beijing. Fu Lun seizes the bill and invites Pincai to call on him at the Gold Tablet Tower. Home that night, Ziyu asks again about Qinguan's temper, and Pincai says the boy has a shard of crystal set in his heart, hard and cold, and that no amount of courting will buy a single smile. The answer only deepens Ziyu's fascination: such proud self-possession is the mark of a complete soul — and precisely what the vulgar world will punish. Without knowing it, his tenderness has fastened on Qinguan.`},4:{zh:`李元茂推门惊呼，原来腊雪骤至，一夜积了五寸，琼装玉琢，天地改观。西席李性全冒寒受了风，头晕咳嗽，自己抓了几味发散药蒙头睡去，子玉遂得半日清闲，独坐二十四琴斋——匾额还是其祖手笔——翻谢惠连《雪赋》消遣，读到"皓鹤夺鲜，白鹇失素"，叹古人摹绘之工。旋命厨下备馔，请魏聘才、李元茂同来赏雪。

席间又问琴官，聘才见他上心，索性改了口风，将琴官的身分性气大赞一场，说得子玉愈发倾慕，想此雪天若得见琼枝玉立，何异瑶岛看花。饭后煮雪煎茶。俄而俊儿送上颜仲清书信，附诗笺四纸：王恂的《雪山》《雪塔》《雪屏》《雪灯》，仲清的《雪狮》《雪猫》《雪罗汉》《雪美人》。子玉品评一过，觉仲清"奈他鼠辈只趋炎"一联寓意深长，才思犹在王恂之上。他不肯依韵唱和，另辟蹊径，从未下雪之前着想，以四个虚字连雪成题：《雪意》《雪影》《雪声》《雪色》，落笔便成。聘才凑了一首七绝，实是暗集《走雪》《独占》的曲文；元茂则一本正经献上五律，"白人双目近，长马四蹄轻""掘阅蜉游似，挖空狮子成"，不知所云。三卷同送过去，仲清一眼看破聘才用的是戏文，仍批"得天公玉戏之神"；至元茂诗则通篇密圈，批以"裁对工稳，用古入化"，王恂笑说挖苦得太狠。

次日雪霁，二人同车来访，路遇孙嗣徽满口之乎者也，说父母反目、请王大夫诊治，仲清叹这"虫蛀千字文"生可为名、死可为谥。到了梅宅，众人互评诗作，元茂把批语揣进怀里反复端详，又把"大作谅来多"听成皮褂虫蛀，闹了个满脸通红。仲清、王恂辞出后同往下洼子看雪，远眺怡园琪树参差、层楼耀目，路上遇见花榜最幼的林春喜，说起八人在怡园三日一聚的消寒会，今日以《冰床》命题，苏媚香夺魁，他自己与王蕊香因倒了平仄同居末座；又道班中新来琴官、琪官，琴官几与瑶卿不相上下，徐度香一见便重制衣饰相赠。正说着，对楼窗开，一位面如冠玉的美少年携蓉官、玉美、四喜、全福凭窗而坐，议论戏曲，谓与其戏雅而人俗，不如人雅而戏俗，又说胡琴之声如怨如慕，听之落泪，非世人所谓淫声。仲清暗惊此人清狂绝俗，决非庸碌之辈。少年索笔在粉墙上题《浪淘沙》一阕，落款"湘帆醉笔"，飘然而去；问店家，竟无人知其姓名。`,en:`Li Yuanmao bursts in with an alarm that proves to be snow — five inches fallen overnight, the world remade in white jade. The tutor Li Xingquan, chilled and coughing, doses himself with a dispersing draught and takes to his bed, and Ziyu inherits a free afternoon. He settles in the Twenty-Four Qin Chamber, whose plaque was brushed by his own grandfather, and reads Xie Huilian's Rhapsody on Snow, admiring the ancient art of description in the line about cranes and silver pheasants outshone. Then he orders dishes brought and invites Wei Pincai and Li Yuanmao to view the snow with him. 

Over the cups he returns to Qinguan, and Pincai — seeing where his host's heart lies — abandons his earlier scorn and praises the boy's breeding and spirit until Ziyu's longing sharpens: to meet such a person in such a landscape would be to walk among the immortals' flowering isles. Tea is brewed from melted snow. Then his page Junner brings a letter from Yan Zhongqing enclosing four sheets of verse — Wang Xun on the snow mountain, snow pagoda, snow screen, and snow lantern; Zhongqing on the snow lion, snow cat, snow arhat, and snow beauty. Ziyu judges Zhongqing the finer hand, especially the couplet on vermin that crowd toward warmth. Rather than answer in the same rhymes he opens new ground, taking his titles from the moment before snowfall: Snow Feeling, Snow Shadow, Snow Sound, and Snow Color. Pincai contributes a quatrain quietly assembled out of the libretti of Walking in Snow and Exclusive Possession, while Yuanmao gravely produces a regulated poem of magnificent nonsense — white men with close-set eyes, long horses of light hoof, mayflies boring out and lions hollowed. All three go across the city. Zhongqing spots the stolen libretti at once but writes an indulgent line of praise; Yuanmao's effort he circles throughout and commends for its faultless parallels and masterly use of the ancients, until Wang Xun protests that the joke is too cruel. 

Next morning, in clear frozen sunlight, the two friends drive over and meet Sun Sihui on the road, who explains in unbroken classical tags that his parents have come to blows and Doctor Wang has been called — leaving Zhongqing to observe that Worm-eaten Primer is a name fit to serve the man in life and as his posthumous title too. At the Mei house the poems are traded and admired; Yuanmao tucks his notice into his jacket, rereads it repeatedly, and then mishears a compliment about his prolific verse as a remark about moths in his fur coat, blushing scarlet. Afterwards Zhongqing and Wang Xun walk out to view the snowfields, the distant Yi Garden rising in tiers of gleaming towers, and meet Lin Chunxi, youngest of the Flower Register, returning from it. He tells them of the winter club that meets there every third day — today's set topic was the Ice Bed, with Su Meixiang taking first place while he and Wang Ruixiang tied for last after muddling their tones — and of two newcomers to his troupe, Qinguan, who nearly matches Yaoqing, and Qiguan; Xu Duxiang, on seeing them, had entire new wardrobes made for both. As they talk, a window opens across the way: a young man with a face like polished jade sits with Rongguan, Yumei, Sixi, and Quanfu, arguing that a vulgar opera with refined company beats a refined opera with vulgar company, and that the fiddle men call lewd sounds to him like grief itself and moves him to tears. Zhongqing marks him at once as no ordinary man. The stranger calls for a brush, writes a lyric to the tune "Waves Scouring Sand" on the whitewashed wall, signs it "Xiangfan, drunk," and departs — and the innkeeper cannot tell them his name.`},5:{zh:`本回细叙怡园的来历。主人徐子云，字度香，浙江山阴人，七世簪缨，祖孙宰相、父子尚书，其父现总督两广；子云年二十五，温文俊雅，博通经史，夫人袁氏花容绝代，夫妻相敬如宾。他买下宅前五六里的废园，托挚友萧次贤费三四年心血监造，成二十四处楼台、四百余间屋宇，大山连络，曲水湾环，遂为京都游览之冠。子云声气既广，四方名士星集，而其取友不在势力而在道义；史南湘《花选》中八名旦日夕来游，他视之如奇珍名花，只有爱惜而无亵狎之念，故众人待他如慈父恩母。继叙琴官身世：父杜琴师以制琴为业，为豪贵殴辱，碎琴而卒；母逾年悲痛而亡；叔父收养三年又死，婶母改嫁，遂将他卖入梨园。

他六岁识字，过目成诵，性既爱洁，志复自高，堕落时曾数度投缳不死，后为叶茂林赎出携京。入京前夕梦见梅花如海，自己陷入深坑，一美少年提他而出，转身没入林中，树上结一玉梅——次日车中所见，正是那救他的人。袁宝珠引他往怡园道谢，途中与梅子玉两车相值，一掀帘、一含笑，转瞬风驰而过；子玉叹夜光难掩，琴官则暗认此人必是有情有义的正人。

到园中，子云、萧次贤围炉相待，问其出身，他撇去苏音，说到父母双亡、婶母再蘸，声如微风振箫。次贤叹这般灵秀不钟于香闺而钟于歌楼，实是恨事；子云遂立下规矩：不许称"老爷"，不必请安，直呼度香、静宜，一如瑶卿。又因嫌"琴官"之名不雅，命次贤替他改名琴言，取号玉侬。席间宝珠盛赞他腹中书卷更胜媚香，子云听了愈喜。归后子玉将重逢之事告知聘才，聘才顺口说出旧事：当日船到济宁，见琴言独自垂泪，他递帕子去揩，反被夺去掷入河中——本欲中伤，不料子玉听了更生敬慕。数日后聘才进城访富伦，路遇一队人马：顶马引马、绣蟒貂裘的美少年、二三十骑跟班、十几车粉妆孩子，赶车的说这是锦春园的华公子。富伦细道其详：此人名华光宿，号星北，公爵之子，年二十一，文武皆能，娶靖边侯之女，妆奁百万，府中有十珠婢；只是脾气难伺候，一席酒要三四十样菜，赏则元宝，怒则鞭挞——富伦曾荐小顺儿唱《十不闲》一曲触其厌，当场被逐，破费一千多吊反被讥为俗恶；其亲随林珊枝，是花八千两银子买来的。席间内院果然吵闹起来，青姨奶奶、白姨奶奶争执不休，富伦两头弹压不住，聘才只得草草辞出，方信蓉官所言非虚。转眼除夕，梅宅灯彩辉煌，李性全携李元茂辞岁，梅士燮亲为斟酒，席间尽是修身立行的训诲；子玉则先已约定团拜那日随王恂赴宴，聘才心中暗喜——那一天，或许就能见着琴言的戏了。`,en:`This chapter tells how the Yi Garden came to be. Its master is Xu Ziyun, styled Duxiang, of Shanyin in Zhejiang — seven generations of office, grandfathers and grandsons ministers, his own father now governor-general of the two Guang provinces. At twenty-five he is refined, widely read, and married to a wife of the Yuan family whose beauty is matched by her learning; the two live in courteous devotion. He bought the derelict park before his house, five or six li around, and set his sworn friend Xiao Cixian to build on it — three or four years of labour producing twenty-four terraces and four hundred rooms among linked hills and winding water, now the finest prospect in the capital. Scholars gather to him from everywhere, but he chooses friends by principle rather than influence; the eight performers of Shi Nanxiang's Selection of Flowers come and go daily, and he cherishes them as one cherishes rare birds and famous flowers, with care and never appetite, so that they treat him as they would a kind parent. Then Qinguan's history: his father Du Qinshi made and taught the qin until a powerful man beat and humiliated him, whereupon he smashed his instrument and died; his mother followed within the year; an uncle took the boy in and died three years later, and the widow, remarrying, sold him into the theatre. 

He had read since six and could recite at a glance; proud and fastidious by nature, he tried more than once to hang himself before Ye Maolin bought him out and brought him north. On the last night of that journey he dreamed of a sea of plum blossom in which he fell into a pit, and a youth of godlike beauty lifted him out and vanished into the trees, leaving a plum of carved jade on a branch — and the next day, in the crush of carriages, he saw that same face. Now Yuan Baozhu escorts him to the garden to give thanks, and on the way their carriage meets Mei Ziyu's: a curtain lifts, a smile is exchanged, and both are swept apart. Ziyu reflects that true radiance cannot be hidden; Qinguan concludes that this is a man of feeling and honour. 

At the garden Ziyun and Xiao Cixian receive him by the brazier and ask his story, and he tells it in careful northern speech, his voice like wind across a flute when he reaches his mother's death and his aunt's remarriage. Cixian laments that such grace should be born to the stage rather than the inner chambers; Ziyun lays down his house rule — no one is to be called "master," no one need kneel, he is Duxiang and his friend is Jingyi, exactly as with Yaoqing. Judging "Qinguan" too common a name, he has Cixian rename the boy Qinyan, with the style Yunong. Baozhu declares that the newcomer's reading outstrips even Meixiang's, and Ziyun is delighted. Home again, Ziyu tells Wei Pincai of the meeting, and Pincai lets slip an old grievance: on the canal at Jining he had found Qinyan weeping alone and offered a handkerchief, only to have it snatched and flung into the river. He means to injure; instead Ziyu admires the boy more. Days later Pincai rides into the city to call on Fu Lun and is passed by a cavalcade — outriders, a youth in embroidered sable, thirty mounted attendants, a dozen carriages of powdered boys — which the driver says belongs to the great young master of Jinchun Garden. Fu Lun supplies the particulars: he is Hua Guangsu, styled Xingbei, a duke's son of twenty-one, accomplished in arms and letters, married to a marquis's daughter with a dowry of a million and served by ten pearl-named maids; but he is impossible to please, requiring forty dishes at a sitting, rewarding with silver ingots and punishing with the whip. Fu Lun once engaged Xiaoshun'er to sing Ten Non-Idle Acts and so offended him that the singer was thrown out, the host spending a thousand strings only to be called vulgar; the young master's own attendant, Lin Shanzhi, cost eight thousand taels. As they talk, a quarrel erupts in the inner rooms between Concubine Qing and Concubine Bai, and Fu Lun cannot quiet it; Pincai takes his leave, satisfied that Rongguan's gossip was true. New Year's Eve arrives, the Mei house blazing with lanterns; Li Xingquan brings Li Yuanmao to pay the season's respects, Mei Shixie pours the wine himself, and the talk runs to self-cultivation and right conduct. Ziyu has already arranged to attend the cohort banquet with Wang Xun — and Pincai privately exults, for on that day he may at last see Qinyan on the stage.`},6:{zh:`新正应酬络绎。初三日王文辉送来知单，同年团拜只十二位，比去年更形冷落；初五日颜夫人亦设内宴，请了王、孙两家女眷。

两位陆夫人是嫡堂姊妹，皆已四十上下而风韵不减。作者顺笔写尽孙家后院的参差：孙嗣徽之妻沈芸姑出身书香，嫁得这红鼻子丈夫，几欲自尽，回娘家又被父亲教训送回，末了只得把陪房丫头松儿与他收房，自己背地垂泪；孙嗣元之妻巴来风是总兵之女，十七岁过门，掀帐一见那期期艾艾的嘴脸便哭了半日，夜里丈夫上床动手，被她一掌打得半边脸肿，又一拳打翻在地，此后五六年犹未谙人伦之乐。席间孙亮功的陆夫人快人快语，一句"你的外甥好，我的外甥女也不错"，竟把子玉与琼华的婚事说成了；两位亲家当场对拜，陆夫人拔下颜夫人头上一枝玉燕钗替琼华戴上，羞得那位小姐几乎落泪。众少奶奶带她逛到子玉书房，见镜屏上的小照人人赞叹，独琼华走到书桌边翻出一纸《车中人》七绝，见第三、四首末句竟押着"琼""华"二字，大惊，忙用指甲挖去，红着脸走开。

次日姑苏会馆团拜，李元茂嫌衣裳不好，先借子玉的穿不进，又换梅士燮的，长得扫地，束起来成了前鸡胸后驼背，见王文辉行礼时还把人家帽子碰歪。东楼上刘文泽做主，座中有史南湘、张仲雨、高品、沈伯才、巴霖，后来冯子佩才从锦春园华星北处赶到。众人怕孙家兄弟扫兴，偏被高品、沈伯才、巴霖轮流以"红而光"三字取笑嗣徽的酒糟鼻，又替嗣元编出一部结巴的《三字经》，笑倒满座。席间王恂说破前情：那日戏园中缠住子玉的黑相公，名字与袁宝珠只差一字，子玉误认作花榜第一，众人大笑。及至开戏，《瑶台》一出，子玉惊为天人，问是何人，方知即《曲台花选》第二的苏蕙芳，自叹夏虫不可语冰。接着《惊梦》登场，琴官莲步香风而至，一句"良辰美景奈何天"字字打入心坎，子玉几乎垂泪；琴官第二次出场，眼波只往他这边流转，看得刘文泽、史南湘打趣不休，子玉红着脸有口难辩。此后陆素兰的《舞盘》、金漱芳的《题曲》、李玉林的《偷诗》接连登场，无不香艳绝伦，子玉只有向南湘一再认错。散时刘文泽约定初九日小酌清谈，子玉欣然应允。`,en:`The New Year visits come thick and fast. On the third day Wang Wenhui sends round the roster for the cohort banquet — only twelve names this year, thinner than the last — and on the fifth Lady Yan holds her own gathering for the women of the Wang and Sun houses. The two Madam Lu are first cousins, both around forty and both still handsome. 

The author uses the occasion to lay bare the Sun family's inner courtyard. Sun Sihui's wife Shen Yungu, bred to books, nearly took her own life on seeing the red-nosed creature she had married; sent home, she was lectured by her father and returned, and finally gave her own maid Song'er to her husband as a concubine so as to be left in peace, weeping in private thereafter. Sun Siyuan's wife Ba Laifeng, daughter of a brigade general, came at seventeen, wept half a day at the first sight of her stammering bridegroom, and when he reached for her that night slapped one side of his face swollen and knocked him to the floor with a punch; five or six years on she is a wife in name only. Over the wine Sun Lianggong's Madam Lu, blunt and cheerful, remarks that if her hostess admires her own nephew so, there is a niece to match him — and so the betrothal of Ziyu and Qionghua is settled on the spot. The two mothers bow to each other, and Madam Lu pulls a jade swallow pin from Lady Yan's hair and fixes it in the girl's, leaving her scarlet and close to tears. The young wives take her off to view Ziyu's study, where all admire his portrait on the mirror-screen; only Qionghua drifts to the desk, turns up a sheet of quatrains titled The Person in the Carriage, and finds the third and fourth closing on the very characters of her own name. 

Appalled, she scratches them out with a fingernail and slips away. The next day brings the banquet at the Suzhou Guild Hall. Li Yuanmao, ashamed of his clothes, cannot fit into Ziyu's, and in Mei Shixie's sweeps the floor until, belted up, he is all pigeon chest and hump; making his bow to Wang Wenhui he knocks the man's hat askew. Upstairs Liu Wenze presides, with Shi Nanxiang, Zhang Zhongyu, Gao Pin, Shen Bocai, and Ba Lin, and at last Feng Zipei, delayed at Hua Xingbei's Jinchun Garden. The company had hoped to avoid the Sun brothers, but instead Gao Pin, Shen Bocai, and Ba Lin take turns capping a rhyme on "red and shining" at the expense of Sihui's nose, and compose for Siyuan a stammerer's Three-Character Classic that leaves the table helpless. Wang Xun then clears up an old confusion: the coarse boy who pawed at Ziyu in the theatre bears a name differing by a single character from Yuan Baozhu's, and Ziyu had taken him for the first of the Flower Register. Then the performance begins. At The Jade Terrace Ziyu is thunderstruck and asks who this is — it is Su Huifang, second in the Qutai Huaxuan — and confesses himself a summer insect who cannot be told of ice. Next comes Waking from a Dream: Qinguan enters on a breath of perfume, and the line about fair season and lovely scene goes straight into Ziyu's heart, so that he can barely hold back tears. On the boy's second entrance his glance travels again and again to Ziyu's seat, until Liu Wenze and Shi Nanxiang tease him openly and he blushes with nothing to say. Lu Sulan's Dancing on the Tray, Jin Shufang's Inscribing the Song, and Li Yulin's Stealing the Poem follow, each more exquisite than the last, and Ziyu can only keep apologising to Nanxiang. As they part, Liu Wenze invites him to a quiet gathering on the ninth, and he gladly accepts.`},7:{zh:`会馆散后，子玉把琴官的戏想了整整两日，将苏蕙芳、陆素兰、金漱芳、李玉林逐一品评，觉得蕙芳神色最胜、素兰可以匹敌，然与琴官相较，终觉毫厘之逊。他又替造化设想：这般美貌若不堕入梨园，天下人反无从瞻仰，正如生了花王，还须生许多花相来陪衬；只是好花供赏不过一季，而人之颜色可及十年，所以爱人之心自当胜于爱花。想取旧作《车中人》再续两首，却见第三、四首各被指甲挖去一字。

小丫鬟道破原委：那日太太请客，王家二姑娘曾翻他的书，还听雪儿说孙家做媒，已把二姑娘许了少爷，姑娘还戴着太太的簪子回去。子玉初时将信将疑，细想这位表妹的眉眼竟与琴官有几分相似；再一转念，才恍然自己无意间犯了"琼华"二字的讳，对方必疑他有心相思，这桩不白之冤竟无从分辩——既已订亲，被怪也就罢了。初九日刘文泽再邀，子玉更衣赴约。园中甬道曲折，太湖石叠成假山，绿竹红阑掩映，卷棚上悬着屈本立所书"倚剑眠琴之室"匾额，室内水仙半开、绿萼白梅初绽。众人叙齿入座：史南湘与高品同年二十五，颜仲清二十四，王恂二十三，子玉最幼十八，主人刘文泽二十四。

因名旦都在怡园作消寒会，席上无人相陪，众人便以对诗行令：刘文泽出"子玉人如玉"，高品应声对"卯金面是金"，说中秋摊上兔儿爷的脸原是描金的，笑倒一堂。此后拆字联对，一句"双凤云中扶辇下"，颜仲清对"孤鸿天外寄书来"，子玉对"九驾天上听琴来"，公推第一；高品却对出"八鸡露后靠舟前"，一字不连，倒转来念更成谐谑，众人笑得伏案流泪，罚酒五杯。其后"舞台收影费鸾肠""白发如丝日日新""香销南国美人尽"数令迭出，颜仲清评点甲乙，谓子玉句稳而大方，不落纤巧，众皆心服；高品则一路以《传灯录》《西游记》强作典故，屡遭叉抹，而满座笑声几乎透出街衢。饭罢已亥正二刻，众人告辞。归途中子玉赞园子幽雅，刘文泽却说这尚不及徐度香的怡园十分之一，约他同去。高品又向颜仲清透露：京中新来了一位"精品"，也是南京人，现寓宏济寺，风雅而阔绰，只是尚未往来——一段新的缘分，就此埋下伏笔。`,en:`For two days after the guild hall Ziyu can think of nothing but Qinguan's performance. He weighs Su Huifang, Lu Sulan, Jin Shufang, and Li Yulin in turn, granting Huifang the finest presence and Sulan an equal claim, yet finding both a hair's breadth short. He reasons on heaven's behalf: had such beauty not fallen into the theatre, no one would ever have been permitted to see it — as the king of flowers requires its attendant blossoms. But a flower can be admired for one season only, while a face lasts ten years; love of a person must therefore outrank love of a flower. 

Meaning to add to his old sequence The Person in the Carriage, he finds a character gouged from each of the third and fourth poems. A maid explains: during the ladies' visit the second Wang daughter looked through his books, and by Xue'er's account the Sun family have made a match and the girl went home wearing his mother's hairpin. Ziyu is half incredulous — then notices that this cousin's features somewhat resemble Qinguan's, and finally realises with a start that he has unwittingly rhymed on the two characters of Qionghua's name, so that she must believe the poem was written for her. It is a charge he can never answer; and since they are betrothed, he decides he can bear being blamed. On the ninth Liu Wenze's invitation takes him to a garden of winding paths, piled Taihu rock, green bamboo against red railings, and a hall whose plaque — The Chamber for Leaning on a Sword and Sleeping by the Qin — was brushed by Qu Benli, where narcissus stands half open and a white plum is just breaking bud. The company seat themselves by age: Shi Nanxiang and Gao Pin both twenty-five, Yan Zhongqing twenty-four, Wang Xun twenty-three, Ziyu youngest at eighteen, and Liu Wenze, twenty-four, as host. With every famous performer away at the Yi Garden's winter club, they entertain themselves with matching couplets. 

Wenze offers "Ziyu, a man like jade"; Gao Pin caps it instantly with a line on gilded faces, observing that the rabbit-god figures sold at Mid-Autumn are painted gold, and the table dissolves. In the character-splitting rounds, against "Twin phoenixes in cloud draw the carriage down," Zhongqing answers with a lone wild goose bearing a letter from beyond the sky, and Ziyu with nine chariots descending to hear the qin — judged the finest. Gao Pin's effort connects to nothing at all, and read backwards proves so obscene that the others weep with laughter and fine him five cups. Further rounds follow, and Zhongqing, ranking them, praises Ziyu's line as steady and ample where others are merely ingenious; all concede the point. Gao Pin meanwhile defends his nonsense with straight-faced citations from the Transmission of the Lamp Record and Journey to the West, is struck through each time, and keeps the room laughing loudly enough to be heard in the street. They eat at last near midnight and part. Walking out, Ziyu admires the garden, and Liu Wenze replies that it is not a tenth of Xu Duxiang's Yi Garden, and promises to take him. Gao Pin, meanwhile, mentions to Zhongqing that a new man of quality has come to the capital — a fellow southerner from Nanjing, lodging at the Hongji Temple, elegant and evidently rich, whom he has not yet met.`},8:{zh:`子玉赴刘文泽之约归来已过二更，魏聘才、李元茂却不知去向。原来二人趁他出门，商量着要去听戏作乐。元茂手中无钱，聘才便替他细算：带相公上馆子不过七八吊京钱，折银三两有零，在南边还摆不成一台花酒；又撺掇他往父亲箱里"暂借"。元茂开箱摸出一大包碎银，拣了五六块，聘才嫌少，又添两块，共八九两掖在搭链里，雇车直奔太和园。将进园门，元茂只顾看那五花云彩、老虎花架，不防绊在一张矮板凳上栽了个倒栽葱，旁边一人抢上来双手扶起，替他拍去身上尘土，说些"幸亏拉得快"的好话——这一拍，那搭链便悄没声息地去了。

楼上遇见张仲雨，说起清早才到锦春园华府、又往怡园商议事、同起盛银号的潘三吃了饭，还约了宏济寺的唐和尚在此相候。他递过玉烟壶，元茂当作痧药猛嗅，连打七八个嚏喷，涕泪交流。不多时来了二喜和另一个黑相公，一个装烟一个倒茶，一个挨紧膀子一个挤紧腿，把元茂夹在中间，喜得他心花大放。台上琪官唱《女弹词》一出，装束华美，两楼喝彩，可他卸妆后只对聘才微微一笑，转身却上了对楼陪两个华服贵客，教聘才好不扫兴。众人转到春阳馆，二喜出了个"乐中乐、苦中苦"的酒令：一杯输了唱曲，二杯输了说笑话，三杯输了敬皮杯。元茂不会唱，求人代唱；轮到吃皮杯，二喜含酒口对口灌下，他一笑竟从鼻孔喷出，满座笑倒，那一个笑得跌落凳下，仲雨笑翻了一身酒。

唐和尚寻来催仲雨去办事，席间与二喜互相戏谑，说人间有四光：和尚脑袋、媳妇腿、老斗银包、相公嘴，说罢大笑而去。及至散席开发，元茂遍身摸索，那袋银子早已不知所终，这才想起进门跌交时被人扶起的蹊跷。两个相公不肯罢休，说回去要交账，非要跟着上门见师傅不可。正僵持间，恰遇孙嗣徽也在馆中，聘才忙请他进来。嗣徽摸着红鼻子，把一部千字文拆得七零八落，从"金生丽水"数到"骸垢想浴"，满口成章而不知所云，把聘才、元茂笑倒，两个相公也如听串戏。末了他勉强担了这笔人情，打发二人明日再来。走堂的报账，连内外共五十六吊七百八十文；掌柜的盘问了姓名住处，才勉强准其挂账，聘才只得写下鸣珂坊梅宅魏字，又添两吊酒钱。两人提着灯笼，一步步低头踱回——乘兴而来，扫兴而返。`,en:`Ziyu comes home from Liu Wenze's past the second watch to find Wei Pincai and Li Yuanmao gone. The two had seized his absence to go to the theatre. Yuanmao has no money, so Pincai does the arithmetic for him — taking performers to a restaurant costs only seven or eight strings of cash, barely three taels, not enough for a single flower-banquet in the south — and then suggests he "borrow" from his father's strongbox. Yuanmao digs out a heavy packet of broken silver, picks five or six pieces, adds two more at Pincai's urging until he has eight or nine taels in a waist-pouch, and they hire a carriage to Taihe Garden. At the gate, gaping at the painted clouds and tiger-frames, Yuanmao trips over a low bench and goes down head first; a bystander springs forward, lifts him with both hands, dusts him off, and remarks how lucky it was he caught him — and with that brushing the pouch is gone, unnoticed. 

Upstairs they meet Zhang Zhongyu, who has been to the Hua mansion at Jinchun Garden that morning, then to the Yi Garden on business, then to lunch with Pan Third of the Qisheng bank, and is now waiting on Monk Tang of the Hongji Temple. He offers his jade snuff bottle; Yuanmao, mistaking it for headache powder, inhales deeply and sneezes seven or eight times, streaming. Presently two dark-skinned boys attach themselves — Er Xi and another — one filling his pipe, one pouring his tea, one pressed to his shoulder and one to his knee, until Yuanmao is glowing with delight. On stage Qiguan sings Female Ballad-Singing in gorgeous costume to applause from both galleries, but after changing he gives Pincai only the faintest smile and goes to sit in the opposite gallery attending two richly dressed strangers, to Pincai's considerable chagrin. The party moves to the Chunyang Restaurant, where Er Xi proposes a drinking game: lose the first cup and sing, the second and tell a joke, the third and give a "skin cup" — wine passed mouth to mouth. Yuanmao cannot sing and begs a substitute; when the skin cup falls to him, Er Xi takes wine in his mouth and pours it into his, whereupon Yuanmao laughs and sends it jetting out of both nostrils. 

The room collapses — one boy slides off the bench to the floor, Zhongyu overturns his cup down his own front. Monk Tang arrives to hurry Zhongyu away to business, trading insults with Er Xi and declaring that the world has four shining things: a monk's skull, a wife's thighs, a patron's purse, and an actor's mouth. When the reckoning comes, Yuanmao searches himself in mounting panic; the silver is long gone, and only now does the helpful stranger at the gate make sense. The two boys refuse to let it drop — they must account to their master, and insist on coming home with him. At the impasse Pincai spots Sun Sihui in the restaurant and calls him in; rubbing his red nose, Sihui delivers an oration assembled entirely from the Thousand Character Classic, marching from gold born in water to washing filth from the body, magnificently sonorous and entirely meaningless, which leaves Pincai and Yuanmao weak with laughter while the boys listen as though to an opera. In the end he grudgingly stands surety and sends the pair off to return tomorrow. The waiter totals the bill at fifty-six strings and seven hundred eighty cash; the proprietor questions their names and lodgings before allowing credit, and Pincai has to sign it to the Mei house at Mingke Lane, adding two strings for the staff. Lantern in hand, the two walk home in silence — having set out in high spirits and returned in none.`},9:{zh:`聘才、元茂三更方归，次日只得央许顺借了十吊钱的票子，分作两张，托四儿送与叶茂林转给那两个相公，算是了结；子玉盘问时，二人推说张仲雨请去听戏下馆子，将实情瞒过。转眼元宵，李性全带李元茂赴会馆吃年酒，聘才出门逛灯未回，子玉独坐无聊，恰好梅进来报：刘文泽、颜仲清、王恂三位在门口相邀。四人弃车步行，走入灯市。玻璃灯、画纱灯、灯屏灯楼次第辉映，泥筒花炮如流星赶月，锣鼓丝竹，一派太平气象。

忽有一队香车拥来，子玉被车轴拦住，车中少妇目不转睛地看他，还把身子一松，伸出一只三寸莲钩，盘好了才嫣然微笑；文泽扯着王恂低声说是"要显显他的莲瓣"，仲清偏说"焉知不是为着你"。车沿下挂的小灯上写着起盛银号潘其观的字号——这一笔闲墨，日后自有下文。继而一群妇女结队横冲直撞，一个踏了王恂的新靴，一个高个的踉跄跌来，一把揪住子玉胸前才站稳，唬得他心头乱跳；那些妇人反怪爷们睁着大眼挡路，惹得众人大笑。子玉叹道，比起方才车中人，真有天壤之别。文泽提议往怡园看灯猜谜，仲清说徐度香、萧次贤都不拘形迹，子玉这才同去。园门口彩坊高结，亭上悬着灯虎字条，四人一路猜将下去：《礼记》《易经》各中一条，"落花人独立，微雨燕双飞"打一"俩"字，又打出古人名与古乐府数题，报进去竟皆中的。萧次贤闻声出迎，与子玉一见如故。彩礼分送笔墨端砚，独子玉那一条的彩最重，是古锦囊中的瑶琴一张——原来这谜是园主人为杜玉侬而设，暗寓作合之意，琴上还要镌铭，众人皆不解其故。

次贤复引众人登石台观灯：先是数百泥筒流星，继而九连灯、万年欢、火烧红莲寺、赤壁烧兵诸般烟火，再挂出数千树灯，舞出金、青、白、乌、火五条巨龙，最后象、虎、犀、狮、黑熊、赤豹数百兽灯衔尾疾驰，鼻里生烟口中吐火，如雷轰电掣，看得子玉等神惊肤栗；次贤说这一场不过用了不到二百人，众名士叹服。中途史南湘醉得踉跄上台，当众大吐，宝珠捧上度香自制的仙桃益寿丸，一丸下去宿酒尽消，醒来却问"这是什么地方"，逗得满座大笑。众人转入宜春阁，见屈本立题额、陆素兰小楷、袁宝珠墨兰，皆精绝。子玉悄问袁宝珠可曾见着琴官，宝珠反问他二人可相好，问得他窘住，只说闻名思慕；宝珠这才告知：他如今已改名琴言，今日恰被度香带去赴席了。子玉怅然，只觉缘悭一面。临散前次贤取出两封以药名、花名藏尾的情书底稿，说昨日被一个不知姓名的少年一举猜破——众人叹此人聪明绝世，却无从访问。`,en:`Pincai and Yuanmao get home at the third watch, and next morning must beg ten strings' worth of notes from the steward Xu Shun, split into two, for Si'er to carry to Ye Maolin for the two boys. When Ziyu questions them they claim Zhang Zhongyu took them out, and the truth stays hidden. The Lantern Festival comes. With Li Xingquan and Li Yuanmao away at a guild feast and Pincai out among the lanterns, Ziyu sits alone until Mei Jin announces Liu Wenze, Yan Zhongqing, and Wang Xun at the gate. The four leave their carriages and walk into the lantern market, past glass lanterns and painted gauze lanterns, lantern screens and lantern towers, while firework tubes send up shooting stars and drums and strings sound on every side. 

A file of perfumed carriages presses through; Ziyu is pinned against an axle, and the young wife inside watches him steadily, then eases her body, extends a three-inch bound foot, tucks it back, and smiles. Wenze whispers to Wang Xun that she means to show off her lotus petals, while Zhongqing insists she may be looking at Wenze instead. The small lamp beneath the carriage bears the mark of Pan Qiguan's Qisheng bank — an idle stroke that will matter later. Then a pack of women comes shoving through; one treads on Wang Xun's new boot, another, tall and unsteady, lurches into Ziyu and seizes his chest to keep her feet, leaving his heart hammering — and the women complain that these gentlemen block the way and stare. Ziyu observes that between such creatures and the lady in the carriage lies the distance of heaven from earth. Wenze proposes the Yi Garden for lanterns and riddles; Zhongqing assures him that Xu Duxiang and Xiao Cixian stand on no ceremony, and Ziyu consents. Riddle slips hang from a pavilion lamp, and the four work down them — one from the Liji, one from the Book of Changes, a couplet on a solitary figure among falling petals yielding a single character, then names from antiquity and titles of old ballads — and every answer reported is right. Xiao Cixian comes out to greet them and takes to Ziyu at once. The prizes are brushes, ink, and inkstones, but Ziyu's is far the richest: a qin in an old brocade bag. It emerges that the riddle was set by the garden's master for Du Yunong, with a quiet matchmaking intent, and that the instrument is still to be inscribed — none of them understands why. 

Cixian then leads them up to a stone terrace for the display: hundreds of firework tubes, then set pieces, then several thousand lanterns blossoming in the trees, then five great dragons — gold, green, white, black, and fire — writhing together, and finally hundreds of beasts on wheels, elephants and tigers, rhinoceros and lions, black bears and red leopards, smoking at the nostrils and breathing flame, thundering past until the watchers' skin creeps. Fewer than two hundred men, Cixian remarks, and the company marvels. Mid-display Shi Nanxiang staggers up the steps hopelessly drunk and is violently sick; Yuan Baozhu brings him one of Duxiang's own Immortal Peach Longevity Pills, which dissolves the wine at a stroke, though on waking he asks where he is, to general laughter. They move to the Yichun Pavilion, hung with Qu Benli's plaque, Lu Sulan's small-character calligraphy, and Yuan Baozhu's ink orchids. Ziyu quietly asks Baozhu whether he has seen Qinguan; Baozhu asks in return whether they are close, which leaves Ziyu flustered, admitting only that he admires a reputation. Baozhu tells him the boy is now called Qinyan, and that Duxiang has taken him out to a banquet that very evening. Ziyu is left with the sense of a fate always just missing. Before they part, Cixian produces two love letters whose lines conceal the names of medicines and of flowers, solved the day before by a nameless youth whom nobody thought to detain — a mind, the company agrees, of rare brilliance.`},10:{zh:`琴言自谒见徐子云之后，日日随众名旦出入怡园，子云爱之不亚于袁宝珠；然他生性高傲，任凭黄金满斗也买不动一笑，纵知感恩，也只算得半个知己——因为他心上真正挂着的，是进京路上认出的那个梦中救他出坑的人。姑苏会馆看戏那日，他从叶茂林口中得知那位公子姓梅，正应了梅花树下之兆，自此幽情如沾泥柳絮，夜夜入梦：或相对而笑，或执手同哭，或送行、或重逢，醒来又记不真切；甚至梦见袁宝珠变作那人模样，与他同唱一出《惊梦》。萧次贤将灯谜之夜梅子玉猜中瑶琴的事说与子云，又盛赞其才貌，子云随口道要替琴言"觅个配对"，竟把他吓得泪流满面，激切辩白：只求不被糟蹋，何来配对之说。子云自知失言，托词避开，留宝珠劝慰。宝珠试探着说起那位十七八岁的梅公子曾单单问起他，琴言便一句句盘问相貌，说着说着自己泄了底，把梦中情事和盘托出，二人在隔壁窃听的子云、次贤推门而出，羞得他背转身去。子云次日便去拜望子玉，约定十九日小酌，心中却另有主意：琴言脾气难测，子玉是否真情也未可知，不如用个"移花接木"之计试他一试。

是日子玉至梅崦，万树梅花围屋，榻上横陈古锦囊中的瑶琴。子云取出琴来，琴背镌着"琴心沉沉，琴德愔愔；其人如玉，相与赏音"，下署"山阴徐子云为玉侬杜琴言移赠庾香名士清赏"，又有次贤撰句、静宜手镌两方图章。子玉这才恍然玉侬即琴官，大喜称谢。子云再三试探，问他与琴言相与深浅、比之宝珠如何、若以琴言相赠当如何处之，子玉一一从容应对。

少顷宝珠引了一个"琴言"进来，眉目虽似而神采迥异；此人百般作态，倚肩贴脸，问他可知相思、可解人意，子玉初则疑惑，继而动怒，正色道人之相知贵在知心，声色之奉本非正人所尚，若务求下品，连乡党自好者都不为——说罢愤然欲去，镜屏却推之不开。子云、次贤、宝珠这才笑盈盈现身，称他是坐怀不乱的柳下惠。转入套间，另有一人背身垂泪，回过脸来才是真正的琴言，神采奕奕，丽若天仙。子玉如醉方醒，一腔心事竟说不出一字，连敬酒的坐次都错了。席间以"琴言开口"行令，他每说一句，对话者便饮一杯；宝珠改唐诗"寻常一样琴前月，才有梅花便不同"来打趣他的名字，子玉便称赞宝珠"胸有智珠"替他解围，琴言深感其情。他一笑之间美目流波，连相处将近一月的子云也是初见，不免爱极生妒，转看子玉与琴言并坐宛如一对玉人，又羡而忘妒。子玉细看真假两人，眉目手指皆不同，正自疑惑，子云唤出那假琴言——原来是华公子家八龄班中的玉龄，本是子云家人，因面貌相似而借来演这一出。疑团尽释，子玉不免赧然。饮罢，琴言恐他醉了不适，亲取仙桃益寿丸泡好吹凉喂他服下；临别悄问后会之期，子玉只勉强答以"稍有空闲，即当相聚"，两人心许目成，尽在不言之中。`,en:`Since first calling on Xu Ziyun, Qinyan has come daily to the Yi Garden with the other celebrated performers, cherished there scarcely less than Yuan Baozhu. Yet he is proud by nature, and no weight of gold buys a smile from him; grateful as he is, Ziyun remains only half a confidant, because the person who truly occupies him is the youth from his dream, recognised on the road into the capital. At the guild hall performance he learned from Ye Maolin that the young gentleman's surname was Mei — fulfilling the omen of the plum trees — and since then the attachment has clung to him like willow-down in mud. He dreams of the man nightly: laughing together, weeping together, seeing him off, meeting again; once he even dreamed Yuan Baozhu had taken the man's face and sang Waking from a Dream with him. When Xiao Cixian reports that Mei Ziyu solved the qin riddle and praises his talent and looks, Ziyun jokes that he will find Qinyan a "match" — and the boy dissolves in tears, protesting that he asks only not to be defiled. Ziyun withdraws, abashed, leaving Baozhu to comfort him. Baozhu mentions that a Master Mei of seventeen or eighteen asked after him by name, and Qinyan interrogates him about the man's features so eagerly that he betrays himself and pours out the whole story of the dream — whereupon Ziyun and Cixian, listening through the wall, push open the door and leave him crimson and turned away. Next day Ziyun calls on Ziyu and fixes a small banquet for the nineteenth, having privately resolved on a test: Qinyan's temper is unpredictable and Ziyu's feeling unproven, so he will graft one branch onto another and see. 

On the day, Ziyu comes to the Plum Slope, where thousands of trees enclose the rooms, and finds the brocade-bagged qin laid on a couch. Ziyun draws it out; the back is inscribed — the qin's heart is deep, its virtue still; the man is like jade, and shall share its music — and signed by Xu Ziyun of Shanyin, presenting it on behalf of Yunong, Du Qinyan, to the distinguished Mei Yuxiang, with seals recording that Cixian composed the lines and Jingyi cut them. Only now does Ziyu grasp that Yunong is Qinguan, and he bows in delighted thanks. Ziyun probes him — how well does he know Qinyan, how does he rank him against Baozhu, what would he do if Qinyan were given to him — and Ziyu answers each with composure. Then Baozhu ushers in a "Qinyan" whose features resemble the boy's but whose spirit does not. 

This creature leans on him, presses cheek to cheek, asks whether he knows what longing is; Ziyu, puzzled and then angry, declares that between friends what matters is knowledge of the heart, that the pleasures of the senses are no pursuit for an upright man, and that to grovel after the basest of them is beneath even a decent villager. He turns to leave — and the mirror-screen will not open. Ziyun, Cixian, and Baozhu appear laughing, saluting him as a paragon of continence. In the inner room another figure sits weeping with its back turned, and when it turns, it is the true Qinyan, radiant as a spirit. Ziyu is like a man waking from wine; he cannot utter a word, and takes the wrong seat when the toasts begin. A game is proposed in which every remark Qinyan makes costs his interlocutor a cup; Baozhu alters a line of Tang verse to tease him on his name, and Ziyu deflects it by praising Baozhu's quickness, for which Qinyan is quietly grateful. When the boy laughs, his eyes shining, even Ziyun — a month in his company — has never seen it, and feels a pang of jealousy that dissolves as he watches the two young men seated side by side like a matched pair of jade figures. Comparing the two faces and even the two hands, Ziyu is still perplexed, until Ziyun summons the impostor: Yuling of Young Master Hua's boys' troupe, once a servant of Ziyun's own house, borrowed back for the resemblance. The mystery clears, and Ziyu is left blushing. As the party ends, Qinyan, fearing the wine has been too much for him, fetches an Immortal Peach Longevity Pill, prepares it and blows it cool before giving it to him; and at parting he asks softly when they will meet again, to which Ziyu can only manage that he will come whenever he is free. Nothing more is said, and everything is understood.`}},a={11:{zh:`徐子云送客回宅，与妻袁绮香谈起梅子玉与琴言的缱绻。袁绮香却问：这些相公究竟比自家丫鬟好在何处？子云答得冠冕：他们"面有女容，身无女体"，可以娱目又可以制心，使人有欢乐而无欲念；且外间酒席不便携女眷，正是两全其美。妻笑他说得漂亮，他自称生平不说违心话，又举白日里梅子玉被玉龄戏谑几句便怒得连自己都骂，以为佐证。

次日是华夫人苏浣香二十岁正寿，袁绮香先遣人送去玉连环、翠羽扇、珊瑚搔头、青瑶琴、沉水香瑟柱、奇楠香串等十样重宝，随后带六个"红"字丫鬟乘香车赴宴。作者于此细写华府：华光宿年方二十一，气焰虽豪而性情纯粹，骑射既精，词赋更妙；夫人苏浣香乃兵部尚书之女，与其妹苏浣兰并称"侯门大小苏"，外间有"不愿得龙宫十斛珠，只愿一见侯门大小苏"之谣；府中十个美婢名字皆带"珠"字，年皆十五六岁，针黹夺天工，词令成妙品；又有八龄班小戏子，名字皆带"龄"字。是日苏浣兰先至，袁绮香继来，华公子拜过家庙便回避出去。三位女眷卧房中设席，袁绮香提出一个新酒令：掷六骰成色样，以骨牌名起句，次用五言唐诗，次用《西厢》曲文，次用曲牌名，末用《毛诗》，五句须一韵到底。于是"群鸦噪风""铁索缆孤舟""将军挂印""落红满地""二士入桃源""梅梢月上"次第掷出，三人各逞才思，或旖旎，或雄放，或温秀，彼此贺酒相赞，又互相打趣：华夫人笑袁绮香是怨丈夫日日携相公醉归，袁绮香便反笑她想起新婚燕尔的光景。

袁绮香又极称通政司之女王琼华，说她十七岁便顷刻成《灯月词》七八百字，光怪陆离，恰似太白复生。后房里十珠与六红也掷骰行令，年貌相当而各怀好胜，代人捉刀之际句句藏刺，笑闹推挤，掀翻酒杯，直闹到两位夫人遣人查问。两位夫人本欲正色责备，听婢女们把六个骂人的令一一背出，反笑得珠钿乱颤；末了评定轻重，重者罚酒兼打手掌，轻者单罚酒，苏浣兰又替她们讨情。日暮席散，华夫人以"金菊对芙蓉"一令收煞，送客上轿而别。`,en:`Having seen his guest out, Xu Ziyun goes home and tells his wife Yuan Qixiang of the tenderness between Mei Ziyu and Qinyan. She asks what these performers have that her own maids lack, and he answers handsomely: they have a woman's face without a woman's body, so they delight the eye while governing the heart, giving pleasure without appetite — and besides, one cannot bring women to a banquet. She laughs at how well it sounds; he protests that he has never in his life said a thing he did not mean, and offers as proof that Mei Ziyu, teased that afternoon by Yuling, grew angry enough to abuse even his host. 

The next day is Lady Hua's twentieth birthday. Yuan Qixiang first sends ahead ten treasures — a linked jade ring, a kingfisher-feather fan, a coral hairpin, a jade-green qin, sandalwood zither pegs, a string of agarwood beads — and then follows in a perfumed carriage with six maids whose names all carry the character for red. Here the Hua household is set out in full: Hua Guangsu, twenty-one, commanding in style yet unspoiled in temper, a fine archer and a finer poet; his wife Su Huanxiang, daughter of the Minister of War, who with her sister Su Huanlan is known as the greater and lesser Su of the marquis's gate, so that the city says it would rather see them once than own ten pecks of dragon-palace pearls; ten beautiful maids of fifteen or sixteen whose names all carry the character for pearl, whose needlework outdoes heaven and whose talk is a series of small masterpieces; and a boys' troupe whose names all carry the character for years. Su Huanlan arrives first, Yuan Qixiang after, and Hua Guangsu, having paid respects at the family shrine, withdraws. The three women dine in the inner chamber, where Yuan Qixiang proposes a new game: throw six dice for a named pattern, then compose five rhyming lines — opening with a domino name, then a line of five-character Tang verse, then a phrase from the Western Chamber, then a tune title, then a line from the Mao Shi. Pattern after pattern comes up — crows clamouring in wind, an iron chain mooring a lone boat, the general hanging his seal, fallen petals filling the ground, two scholars entering Peach Blossom Spring, moonrise on a plum branch — and each woman answers in her own register, one voluptuous, one martial, one delicately chaste, toasting and praising and teasing by turns: Lady Hua suggests that Yuan Qixiang's verses complain of a husband who comes home drunk from the garden with actors, and Yuan Qixiang retorts that Lady Hua is plainly remembering her wedding nights. 

She also praises Wang Qionghua, daughter of the Commissioner of Transmission, who at seventeen dashed off Lantern and Moon Lyrics running to seven or eight hundred characters, strange and dazzling, as though Li Bai had come again. In the back room the ten pearls and the six reds play the same game, evenly matched in age and looks and equally determined to win, so that every verse composed on another's behalf carries a barb; they shriek, shove, and overturn a cup, until the ladies send a servant to investigate. Meaning to be severe, the two mistresses instead hear the six most insulting compositions recited back to them and laugh until their hairpins tremble; in the end the worst offenders are fined wine and struck on the palm, the milder ones fined only, and Su Huanlan begs them off. At dusk Lady Hua closes the game with a graceful verse on golden chrysanthemum matched with lotus, and the guests are handed into their sedan chairs.`},12:{zh:`袁绮香归家将华府所行酒令一一说与徐子云，子云竟瞒着夫人把十六个令刻版印行，分作上下二等，因首令是"群鸦噪凤"，遂题为群鸦噪凤令，外间传为美谈；及夫人知道，已流布无从追回。仲春时节，书中正式引出一位要角：田春航，号湘帆，金陵人而寄居扬州，年二十三。其父田浩中进士即殁于京师，母田太夫人张氏苦节抚孤，学问多得母教，十三岁进学，十八岁中副举，貌朗如玉山，情性蕴藉，只是三试不第。去年奉母命游学京师，携老仆田安北上，先在杭州、苏州盘桓半年，诗文传抄殆遍，人谓可与侯太史、屈本立争名，因而囊橐充盈；又绕道东瞻泰岱、西谒华山，十一月底方抵京，寓城南宏济寺，恰与高品前后隔院。他嫌京中妓女粗俗，专心听戏，偏又不爱昆腔而爱乱弹，被几个下作相公迷住，做衣买器、随分子，数月间千金散尽，衣服典当将完，那些相公便渐渐疏远，他却毫不介意，只抱定"财尽而交绝"，能乐一日算一日。高品见他青蔬白饭而待人极厚，讥他"待己如此之薄，待人如此之厚"，春航便索性引《孟子》"知好色则慕少艾"一路痛驳：好女而不好男终是好淫，既重色自不敢淫；又说造物费尽气力生出这些人物，正要缙绅名士品题赏识，若吝己之财而爱人之色，是以烂臭粪土换奇香宝花。

高品无从驳起，只说他床头金尽，恐为郑元和所笑。恰值颜仲清来访，高品讲起怡园两封灯谜情书被同庙的田湘帆猜破，仲清一听"湘帆"二字，便记起去年酒楼粉壁题词的少年，即请引见；相见之下果是其人，细问家世，才知春航之妻竟是他的本家叔父之女，于是亲上加亲，谈得十分投契。仲清劝他所赏识的玉美、翠宝、蓉官不过狐媚善妒，视钱财为性命，不如史南湘《曲台花选》中袁宝珠、苏蕙芳一流可作良友；春航听了默然，却仍说赏识须自具眼力，又发下宏愿：他日得志，当起金屋数重，将这班人尽配作玉瑟瑶琴，还普天下八万三千大心愿。仲清怜其才，先赠二百金试其心，不上半月又化为乌有。一日春航在联珠班看戏，衣衫敝旧坐于暗处，蓉官竟迟疑半晌不敢招呼，末了才过来请安；富伦、魏聘才在旁窃议"这种人有什么巴结处"，蓉官红着脸回护，说他从不欠人开发、不要人替担账。散场时细雨路滑，一辆骡车擦身而过，春航一滑跌了满身烂泥；车中人急令勒住牲口、扶起路人，探身连连道歉——那是个如冰雪抟成、明月梨花般的绝色相公，春航一腔怒气顿消，反以两只泥手扶着车沿连说不妨。回庙后他向高品描摹其容貌衣色，高品戏作一章"其雨其雨，梨园之东"的拟《诗经》体，猜是苏蕙芳，又戏言见了第一名只怕要跌进沟里。

次日春航私取田安的茧绸皮袍去当了六吊钱，一连看了五天联锦班的戏，终于见着那人唱一出《独占》，访得果然是苏蕙芳；蕙芳认出这位泥中人，向他微微一笑，他便喜得说不出话，自此日思夜梦，几成相思。及至煤米全无、房钱催逼，他才想起写信向仲清求援。仲清却只遣人送来唐寅所画鹑衣乞食的郑元和小像，上题两首七绝相讥。春航大怒，当即挥笔和作，说"欲使蛾眉成义侠，忍教骏骨暂支离""五陵年少休相薄，后日功名若个如"。仲清与王恂看了，知其志不可夺，又闻他已断炊，便再备百金交高品相机点化。`,en:`Yuan Qixiang comes home and recites every forfeit from the Hua banquet to Xu Ziyun, who — without telling her — has all sixteen cut on blocks and printed, ranked in two classes, and titled after the first pattern, Crows Clamouring Round the Phoenix. The set is admired all over town, and by the time his wife learns of it there is no recalling them. Mid-spring brings forward one of the novel's central figures: Tian Chunhang, styled Xiangfan, of Jinling but settled in Yangzhou, twenty-three years old. His father Tian Hao died in the capital in the very year he took his jinshi; his mother, Lady Tian, kept her widowhood and taught the boy herself, so that he entered the district school at thirteen and took a supplementary degree at eighteen. He is bright as a jade mountain and gracious in temper, but has failed three provincial examinations. Sent north last year to study, with the old servant Tian An in attendance, he lingered half a year in Hangzhou and Suzhou, where his writings were copied everywhere and men said he could rival Hou Taishi and Qu Benli, and where his purse filled accordingly; then he came by way of Mount Tai and Mount Hua, reaching Beijing only at the end of the eleventh month and lodging at the Hongji Temple across the courtyard from Gao Pin. Finding the capital's courtesans coarse, he gave himself to the theatre — and, preferring the rough regional style to kunqu, fell in with several worthless performers, buying them clothes and trinkets until a thousand taels were gone and his own wardrobe pawned. As his money failed, the boys drifted off; he minded not at all, holding that when the money ends the friendship ends, and that a day's pleasure is a day gained. 

Gao Pin, finding him at a meal of plain rice and greens while lavishing everything on others, mocks him for treating himself so meanly and others so well — and Chunhang answers with a whole philosophy, citing Mencius on how a man who knows beauty longs for the young and fair: to love women and not men is mere lechery, whereas one who truly reveres beauty will not defile it; heaven took great trouble to make such creatures precisely so that gentlemen might appraise and cherish them; and to hoard one's own money while coveting another's beauty is to trade rotting dung for a fragrant jewel. Gao Pin cannot answer, and falls back on warning that an empty purse will make him a laughing-stock like Zheng Yuanhe. Just then Yan Zhongqing calls, and Gao Pin mentions that the two riddle love-letters at the Yi Garden were solved by a fellow lodger named Tian Xiangfan — at which Zhongqing remembers the youth who inscribed the tavern wall the previous winter and asks to be introduced. It proves to be the same man, and on questioning it emerges that Chunhang's wife is the daughter of Zhongqing's own uncle, so that friendship is doubled by kinship. Zhongqing argues that the performers Chunhang favours — Yumei, Cuibao, Rongguan — are merely coquettes who treat money as life and clothing as affection, and that the names in Shi Nanxiang's Qutai Huaxuan, Yuan Baozhu and Su Huifang above all, are fit to be friends. Chunhang falls silent, but maintains that discernment must be one's own, and declares that if fortune ever favours him he will raise golden houses and settle the whole company in them, fulfilling eighty-three thousand wishes at a stroke. Pitying his gifts, Zhongqing sends two hundred taels to test him; within a fortnight they have evaporated. One day at the Lianzhu theatre, seated in shadow in worn clothes, Chunhang finds that Rongguan hesitates a long while before daring to greet him, while Fu Lun and Wei Pincai remark audibly that such a man can hardly be worth cultivating — and Rongguan, colouring, defends him as one who never left a bill unpaid or asked another to stand surety. Leaving in the drizzle, he is clipped by a mule carriage and goes down full length in the mud. 

The passenger cries out to hold the beast and lift the man, leaning from the window to apologise again and again: a performer of surpassing beauty, moulded of snow and jade, like pear blossom under moonlight. Chunhang's anger vanishes; he steadies himself on the carriage rail with two muddy hands and insists it was his own carelessness. Back at the temple he describes the face and clothes to Gao Pin, who composes a mock ode in the manner of The Classic of Poetry on rain east of the theatre, guesses Su Huifang, and jokes that the sight of the register's first name would land him in a ditch. Next morning Chunhang quietly pawns Tian An's silk-lined fur robe for six strings and watches the Lianjin troupe five days running until at last the same performer appears in Exclusive Possession, and enquiry confirms it is Su Huifang. Huifang, recognising the man from the mud, gives him the faintest smile, and he is left speechless with joy, then sleepless with longing. Only when the coal and rice run out and the monks press for rent does he write to Zhongqing for help — and receives in reply Tang Yin's painting of Zheng Yuanhe begging in rags, inscribed with two cutting quatrains. Furious, Chunhang answers on the spot in matching verse: to make a beauty into a woman of honour, one must let a fine horse go lean awhile; let the young lords of the capital not despise him, for whose future will match his? Reading it, Zhongqing and Wang Xun recognise a will that cannot be turned, and hearing that his fire is now cold, they put together another hundred taels and leave it with Gao Pin to use as occasion serves.`},13:{zh:`颜仲清将王恂所备百金送到高品处充作薪水，春航告贷无门，只得暂时寄食。他一心记着苏蕙芳，无钱听戏，便天天站在联锦班戏园门口守候，看他上车下车，如醉如痴，一连十余日。蕙芳早认出这是那日跌在泥中的人，见他面带憔悴而珊珊玉骨、情韵盎然，心生怜念，屡以秋波相赠；一日索性命跟班的请他进门。春航羞涩不敢入，经再三相请才整衣而进，但见满院嫣红姹紫，小小客厅钟鼎琴书，十分精雅。蕙芳出来见礼，二人并立几乎一般高；春航见壁上悬着度香、静宜、竹君、剑潭、前舟、庸庵六人和韵的七律，方知他竟能作诗，更加起敬。问起出身，蕙芳眼圈一红，细述前情：本是官宦人家，随父宦游云南，八岁丧母，十二岁上父亲被参劾气病而亡，两袖清风，仅存的箱笼又被亲戚长随瓜分，只剩一个老家人相依，辗转一年零两月回到苏州，亲邻冷眼，一饭难求；再搭粮船北上投亲，那亲戚却已发配口外。

走到前门，幸遇蒙师顾先生——此人也曾把银钱听戏听尽，索性烧了文章入了联锦班唱小生——收留他读书写字、讲究诗词；不料老家人进京两月即病故，先生也于去年夏天染疫而亡，他这才孤身立于梨园。春航听得也伤心，遂发一番议论：人生有四等好友，一曰好天，二曰好地，三曰好书，四曰好诗，然天不能常好、地不能尽游、书多灾梨祸枣、诗每再吟如嚼蜡，故皆可宝而非至宝；惟有相公一流，色、体、容、态、情、妆、艺俱全，才是四友之外的"宝友"。蕙芳听得又是感动又觉太过，心中已把那些富贵王孙尽压下去。二人对酌吐露肺腑，蕙芳临别执其手立誓："今日订交，此生勿负"，又嘱他从此不必再来戏园守候，须自重努力前程，自己每日必去看他一次。

次日晨起，张仲雨竟同着开起盛银号的潘其观登门。这潘其观山西籍而两代居京，坐拥百万，开着三个银号两个当铺，为人鄙吝龌龊而好色，偏又惧内到极处，续娶之妻一见他夜不归宿便领人搜遍窑子，因此他动了买娈童的念头，看中蕙芳，重金托张仲雨说合。席间他絮絮肉麻，竟拉住蕙芳的手要他做干儿子，说"我又没有儿子，要这分家财作什么"。蕙芳几乎气哭，转念心生一计，假意殷勤劝酒，又借口欠人银两，向他张口借二百两；潘其观凑了二百吊钱票递上，蕙芳收进靴掖，便含酒作势敬了个"皮杯"，随即假称鲠住喉咙，只说潘三多饮几杯他便不疼——两人被灌到十二分，先后翻倒在地。蕙芳吩咐把二人扶到客厅炕上同盖一被，笑说"想吃天鹅肉，自作自受"，随即收拾五套衣服连那二百吊钱票，带着铺盖乘车去寻春航。到胡同口，月下正见春航痴立——他候了一日不见人来，已在门外徘徊多时。到了庙中，蕙芳见他行李萧条，将衣服钱票尽数留下，说"你不受，便看轻我了"，二人直谈到鸡鸣，成了道义之交。那边潘其观四更酒醒，摸黑撒了一鞋的尿，又把张仲雨错认成蕙芳一把搂住乱扯；仲雨被摇醒，一口秽物尽吐在他脸上，两人互骂"兔子""你爹"，扭打滚倒，惊动满家掌灯来看，只见一个光着脚坐在地下满面狼藉，一个踏着棉被叉手而立，笑倒众人。等到天明认清了彼此，才拍手大笑。问起蕙芳，家人只说华公子昨夜遣人来接，已进城去了，二人无可奈何，白丢了二百吊钱，郁郁而去。`,en:`Yan Zhongqing leaves Wang Xun's hundred taels with Gao Pin as a maintenance fund, and Chunhang, with nowhere else to turn, accepts board from his neighbour. His mind is wholly on Su Huifang; lacking the price of a seat, he simply stands at the door of the Lianjin theatre day after day to watch her come and go, rapt and unblinking, for more than ten days together. Huifang has recognised the man from the mud, and seeing him worn but finely made, she begins to answer his gaze; at last she has a servant call him in. He is too abashed to enter until asked three times, then straightens his clothes and follows into a courtyard blazing with late-spring flowers and a small hall of bronzes, books, and a qin. When she comes out to greet him they stand almost of a height. On her wall hang six regulated poems written in matching rhymes by Duxiang, Jingyi, Zhujun, Jiantan, Qianzhou, and Yong'an, and Chunhang, realising she composes verse herself, grows more respectful still. Asked about her origins, her eyes redden and the story comes out: she was born to an official family and travelled with her father to Yunnan; her mother died when she was eight; at twelve her father was impeached, sickened with rage, and died within the month, leaving nothing, and what little remained was carved up by relatives and retainers, so that only one old servant stayed with her. It took them a year and two months to reach Suzhou, where neighbours looked coldly on and not a meal could be had; they took a grain boat north to a kinsman in Zhili, only to learn he had been banished beyond the frontier. 

At the city gate she happened on her old tutor, Master Gu — a man who had likewise spent his money on the theatre and ended by burning his essays and joining the Lianjin troupe as a young-male lead — who took them in and taught her reading and verse. But the old servant died of the journey within two months, and the tutor of an epidemic last summer, leaving her alone in the theatre. Moved, Chunhang offers a discourse of his own: a man may have four excellent friends — fine weather, fine country, fine books, fine poems — yet the weather turns, the country cannot all be walked, most books are waste paper, and a poem admired at night tastes of wax by morning; all are precious but none supreme. Only performers, complete in colour, form, face, bearing, feeling, adornment, and art, are the treasure-friend beyond those four. Huifang finds it moving and excessive at once, but from that hour every rich young lord she knows has been eclipsed. 

They drink together and speak from the heart, and at parting she takes his hand and swears that the friendship sealed this day will not be betrayed, forbidding him to wait outside the theatre again, urging him to look to his own future, and promising to visit him daily. The next morning Zhang Zhongyu arrives with Pan Qiguan of the Qisheng bank — a Shanxi man of the second generation in the capital, worth a million, master of three banks and two pawnshops, mean, filthy, and lecherous, yet so terrified of his wife that a single night away brings her out in a carriage to search the brothels for him. Hence his scheme to buy a boy; he has settled on Huifang and has paid Zhongyu to negotiate. Over the wine he grows unbearable, seizing Huifang's hand and proposing that the boy become his adopted son, since he has no heir and asks what all his money is for. Huifang, nearly weeping with rage, changes tack: she plays the willing host, then pleads a debt and asks to borrow two hundred taels. Pan Qiguan produces two hundred strings in notes, which she tucks into her boot before offering him a mouth-to-mouth cup, then feigns a bone caught in her throat that only eases, she says, when Pan drinks another. Both men are poured to the brim and go over backwards, and Huifang has them laid on the hall couch under a single quilt, remarking that those who covet swan's flesh may enjoy what they get. She then packs five suits of her own clothes and the notes, takes bedding, and drives to find Chunhang — meeting him at the lane's mouth in the moonlight, where he has been loitering since evening, having waited all day for her. Seeing how bare his lodging is, she leaves everything, telling him that refusing would be to think little of her, and they talk until cockcrow, their bond now one of principle rather than desire. Back at her house Pan Qiguan wakes at the fourth watch, urinates into his own shoe in the dark, then gropes his way onto the couch, mistakes Zhang Zhongyu for Huifang, and embraces him; Zhongyu, shaken awake, is sick over his face. The two abuse each other as rabbit and father, grapple, and go down, rousing the household, who arrive with lamps to find one man seated on the floor, barefoot and befouled, pointing at the other, who stands on the trampled quilt. By daylight they recognise each other and burst out laughing. Told that Young Master Hua sent for Huifang in the night and she has gone into the city, they can do nothing — two hundred strings lost — and depart in silence.`},14:{zh:`蕙芳亲手替春航解衣换上新制的小毛衣裳、新靴新帽，又拿镜子倚着他一照，映出两个玉人。春航心上一荡，忽念此人是患难知己，岂可稍涉邪念，便敛神自持。蕙芳翻检书案，得他的诗稿《燕台旅稿》，随手一揭正是一首《恼公》七古，遂低声吟诵，句句品评：说这一路是学温、李三十六体，纤秾之极；赞"狸奴"句胜似"燕子"，"飘烟抱月一尺腰，星眸欲妒春云娇"更妙不可言。

及至"日近云鬟身不动"，便笑问既已如愿，何必天天上戏园；又猜诗中人非袁瑶卿即金瘦香，春航只摇头说是"寓言空空"之人。念到"井底水如妾心意，路旁尘惹君身衣"，蕙芳凄然作色，叹一句一往情深。高品进来，偏说这一句该改作"车前泥染君身衣"才真切有味，又故意占了一卦，念出"田获一兔，往遇雨，需于泥"的爻辞来打趣，说自己详不出来。庙里的唐和尚也钻进来凑趣，合掌说佛殿上龙女菩萨不见了，原来在此；高品揪下他的帽子出对"若锥处囊中，颖脱而出"，蕙芳应声对以"如飘浮水面，顶圆而光"，敏捷工巧，满座称好。饭后蕙芳要听琴，春航和了弦，先弹《结客少年场》三段，抑扬顿挫，索铃短锁诸般指法迭出；蕙芳又要他弹《胡笳十八拍》《洞天春晓》，说这两套曾听萧次贤弹得最好，静宜还教过他吹箫，春航却择了一套十二段的《水仙操》，弹到中段如湘娥啼竹、列子御风，如怨如慕，高品与蕙芳都正襟危坐。忽然弦音自乱，春航停手说：必有会弹琴的人在外听着——院中果然爆出一阵狂笑，原来是史南湘挟着王兰保、携着李玉林，带着几分酒意闯来，手中还拈着怡园折来的杏花。南湘久闻田湘帆之名，当即请见，一见如故，谈琴谱文艺，彼此敬服。

高品戏称"史竹君是梨园的狄梁公，田湘帆是戏班的李药师"，又把春航跌进车辙的旧事一五一十抖了出来，羞得他无地自容；南湘则笑赠一联"孙寿堕妆，梁冀下马；苏郎唱戏，田子跟车"。蕙芳反唇，把刘文泽替高品编的一长串"曲部尚书、世袭一等史国公"的滑稽官衔一口气背出，笑倒一堂。席间行"奚"字令，从《四书》中依次找出第一至第十一个奚字，抢说、错说、说两句的层出不穷，罚酒动辄五杯九杯十八杯，兰保、玉林、蕙芳纷纷代饮。中途蕙芳家中来人回报：昨夜那两个醉汉已各自回去，此刻另有一位广东来的奚十一老爷慕名候着，带着四个跟班，光景很阔。蕙芳只回说今日不归——这位新客的名字，就此第一次落到他门上。二更后席散，蕙芳先归，南湘送兰保、玉林回家，路过王恂门口口渴，便进去寻王恂、颜仲清说话。`,en:`Huifang unfastens Chunhang's coat with her own hands, dresses him in newly made furs, new boots and cap, then leans beside him with a mirror so that two jade faces show in it together. Something turns over in him; he reminds himself sharply that she is the friend of his adversity and no object of appetite, and steadies himself. Turning over his desk she finds his manuscript, Travel Drafts from Yantai, opens it at a long poem in the old style titled Annoying the Lord, and reads it aloud with running commentary: this manner descends from Wen Tingyun and Li Shangyin at their most sumptuous; the line on the cat outdoes the line on the swallows; the couplet on a waist that holds the moon and eyes that shame the spring clouds is finer still. Coming to a line about being near the beloved's hair and not stirring, she asks with a smile why, being so satisfied, he still haunted the theatre daily, and guesses the subject must be Yuan Yaoqing or Jin Shouxiang; Chunhang only shakes his head and calls the figure an invention. 

At the couplet comparing her heart to water at the bottom of a well and his robe to dust from the roadside, she turns grave and murmurs that here is a man of deep feeling. Gao Pin comes in to say the line would gain by reading "mud before the carriage stains your robe," and then pretends to cast a hexagram, reciting an oracle about catching a hare, meeting rain, and waiting in the mud, which he claims he cannot interpret. Monk Tang from the temple squeezes in too, palms together, announcing that the dragon-maiden bodhisattva has vanished from the hall and is evidently here; Gao Pin snatches his cap and offers a couplet about an awl in a bag whose point works through, to which Huifang instantly answers with one about a gourd afloat on water, round-topped and shining — quick and exact, and much admired. After the meal she asks for music. Chunhang tunes and plays three sections of Young Gallants Gathering Clients, full of ringing bell-strokes and short locks, then, when she asks for Eighteen Songs of a Nomad Flute or Spring Dawn in the Grotto Heaven — pieces she has heard Xiao Cixian play best, for Jingyi once taught her the flute — chooses instead the twelve sections of the Narcissus Lyric. By the middle sections it is like the goddess weeping on the bamboo, like Liezi riding the wind, plaintive and reproachful, and both listeners sit upright and still. Then the tuning slips of itself, and Chunhang stops: someone outside must know the qin. A burst of laughter from the courtyard proves him right — Shi Nanxiang has arrived half drunk with Wang Lanbao on one arm and Li Yulin on the other, carrying apricot blossom picked at the Yi Garden. 

Having heard of Tian Xiangfan, he asks to be introduced, and the two fall at once into talk of scores and letters, each impressed. Gao Pin dubs Shi Zhujun the theatre's great minister of talent and Tian Xiangfan its great strategist, then retails the whole story of the fall in the mud until Chunhang has nowhere to hide, while Nanxiang composes a mocking couplet pairing famous lovers of antiquity with an actor on stage and a scholar trotting after a carriage. Huifang retaliates by reciting, at a breath, the absurd chain of offices Liu Wenze once invented for Gao Pin, ending in Minister of the Music Bureau and Duke of the Shi State, first class, hereditary. A drinking game follows on the character "Xi," each player required to find its first through eleventh occurrences in the Four Books; men speak out of turn, cite two lines at once, and are fined five, nine, even eighteen cups, with Lanbao, Yulin, and Huifang drinking on others' behalf. Midway a messenger comes from Huifang's house: the two drunkards have gone home, but a Cantonese gentleman named Xi Shiyi is waiting there, richly attended by four servants, having come to pay his respects to her fame. She sends word that she will not be back today — and so this new caller's name arrives at her door for the first time. The party breaks up after the second watch; Huifang leaves first, and Nanxiang, seeing Lanbao and Yulin home, stops at Wang Xun's gate for a drink of water and goes in to sit with Wang Xun and Yan Zhongqing.`},15:{zh:`史南湘回到王恂、颜仲清处，将田春航与苏蕙芳患难订交、诗酒相怜的情景细细说来，二人艳羡之余，也知春航从此或能收心。梅宅这边，西席李性全已选安徽知县，魏聘才替他张罗盘缠，李元茂则打算寄籍京中。聘才趁忌辰独往樱桃巷，会叶茂林、打茶围，竟在杜琴言处说动一番相思，约定次日带梅子玉相见；子玉一夜不眠，正待赴约，却传来父亲梅士燮放江西学差的喜讯，府中贺客盈门，相会之期遂被官场大事隔断。

临行前，颜夫人提醒士燮将二女儿与王家的婚约补足礼数，孙亮功作媒，王文辉回礼丰厚；席间孙亮功又看中衣冠焕然的李元茂，经仲清推波助澜，为他与孙家女另伏一桩婚姻。子玉闷坐无趣，往访刘文泽、史南湘，反被二人取笑与琴言"定情"，强拉去樱桃巷。琴言偏被刘大人带往春喜园，众人转入对门妙香堂拜陆素兰。

素兰一见便猜出子玉姓名，又说琴言自正月初七后日日来访，只因自己与子玉相似，常错唤他作"庾香"，借他作一幅小影以慰相思；子玉听得酸泪暗吞，受素兰赠湘妃竹纸扇而归。到三月初十，梅士燮出京赴任，亲友在旗亭饯别，子玉等送至前站宿店，次日洒泪叩别。徐子云送行后回怡园桃花坞，与萧次贤、袁宝珠、金漱芳赏花行令；宝珠、漱芳仿袁绮香所刻酒令掷骰作句，或绿暗红稀，或打破锦屏风，妙语迭出。花影酒痕之间，众人又谈起子玉、琴言与春航、蕙芳两段深情，次贤预言这相得之后，未来必有许多苦恼缓缓生出。`,en:`Shi Nanxiang returns to Wang Xun and Yan Zhongqing and recounts, with relish, the new intimacy between Tian Chunhang and Su Huifang, leaving both listeners envious and half-convinced that Chunhang may finally reform. In the Mei household, tutor Li Xingquan has been appointed county magistrate in Anhui; Wei Pincai helps raise his travel funds, while Li Yuanmao schemes to register himself in the capital. Pincai meanwhile visits Cherry Blossom Lane, drinks with Ye Maolin, makes the rounds of the actors' tea tables, and at last reaches Du Qinyan, where he embroiders Ziyu's longing so persuasively that Qinyan agrees to meet him the next day. Ziyu lies awake all night, only for the appointment to be swept aside by great family news: Mei Shixie has been named Academic Commissioner of Jiangxi, and the house fills with congratulatory callers. 

Before departing, Madam Yan reminds Shixie to regularize his second daughter's engagement to the Wang family, with Sun Lianggong as go-between and Wang Wenhui sending handsome return gifts; in the same festive current, Lianggong notices the newly dressed Yuanmao, and Zhongqing quietly helps set in motion a match between Yuanmao and the Sun daughter. Restless, Ziyu visits Liu Wenze and Shi Nanxiang, who tease him mercilessly about having "pledged affection" with Qinyan and drag him to Cherry Blossom Lane. Qinyan is away at Chunxi Garden, so they cross to Lu Sulan's Miaoxiang Hall. 

Sulan guesses Ziyu's name at sight, then reveals the wound at the chapter's heart: since the seventh day of the first month Qinyan has visited him daily because Sulan resembles Ziyu, even slipping and calling him "Yuxiang," borrowing his face as a painted miniature of the beloved. Ziyu swallows tears, accepts a freshly written fan from Sulan, and leaves. On the tenth day of the third month, Shixie departs; friends feast him at a roadside inn, and Ziyu escorts him to the first station before weeping home. Xu Ziyun, returning from the farewell, enters the peach grove at Yiyuan, where Xiao Cixian, Yuan Baozhu, and Jin Shufang drink among blossoms and compose new dice-game forfeits in the style of Yuan Qixiang's printed set. Amid wine, peach light, and clever lines, the company speaks of the two great attachments now unfolding — Ziyu and Qinyan, Chunhang and Huifang — and Cixian quietly predicts that such perfect sympathy will not come without future suffering.`},16:{zh:`梅士燮赴任之后，内有颜夫人掌家，外有许顺经理，井井有条。子玉仍旧读书，经籍之外兼研诸子百家，花晨月夕则与刘文泽、颜仲清辈明窗净几，把酒清谈、题诗分韵；徐子云一月也过访几次，只是他局面阔大，干谒纷纷，反不及子玉这般清闲自在。魏聘才却在梅宅一无所事：梅士燮远宦江西要两三年才回，王文辉又大模大样毫无关切，他自忖"长安虽好，非久恋之乡"，不如另觅一个居停主人攀鳞附翼，遂进城去找富伦。富伦在对门贵芬家中打牌，聘才顺势拜了贵大爷，席上又认识两人：一个是华府的老篾片阎简安，与老公爷三十年交情而心窄嘴臭；一个是号梅窗、行八的杨梅窗，富伦的干兄弟，借看风水为名而实不通堪舆，专一贪财勾通。杨梅窗与聘才一拍即合，当场便要换帖；阎简安却势利透顶，两下相厌。

散后聘才把心事说与富伦，富伦便荐他到"舅太爷"华公子府中帮办杂务，说那里只须陪酒陪戏、写些不要紧的书札，会弹唱更合心意，只要多戴高帽便可。次日富伦果然去说妥，华公子一口应允，吩咐林珊枝传谕总办议定束修伙食。聘才向子玉辞行，子玉吃惊挽留，李元茂也劝他别去吃钉板，他只说各人有各人的打算；颜夫人念其能办事，仍送了四十两银子饯行。次早两辆车来接，富伦亲送入府。但见照墙三丈、石狮八尺，头门到二门一箭之遥，甬道百步方至大厅，长廊迭阁、碧瓦琉璃，每一重门各有总管登记出入。西花厅中鸭绿绒毯、王铎题匾、王维山水、文徵明草书，锦天绣地，看得聘才心生畏惧。因公子身上不爽快，便安置他到东花园与画工笔人物的张笑梅、画山水花草的顾月卿同住，另有书启先生王卿云。

是晚公子送来一桌四十余样的酒席，众人直饮到二更；次日又一一见过总管、账房、司阍、司厨、库房各色人等。侯门如海，聘才初来只敢小心恭维——自此他进了华府，日后多少事端便由此生出。这边子玉思念了几日，四月中旬芍药盛开，他忽忆起陆素兰待他的情分，检出古砚、香墨、墨兰册页与团扇，又用小楷写了送春二律，亲自送去。素兰谢过，却叹一口气道破琴言的心事：自怡园同席以后，他便不大招呼人，说话总要扯到梅花上去，请萧次贤画了四幅梅花，又向徐子云讨南边织来的折枝梅绸缎做了袍袄；连师傅都说他近来无缘无故酸酸楚楚。子玉听得泪下，半晌才把途中所闻、会馆看《惊梦》、怡园为假琴官所戏、屏后呜咽诸事一一说出，末了却问：我为他是知他底蕴，他为我又晓得我什么？素兰亦为之凄然，说度香有言"人各有缘，此中系天定"，又叹《国风》好色而不淫，正是庾香、玉侬之谓。二人同往秋水堂，门前歇着五六辆车，只得退回打听：琴言卧病在床，师傅长庆正请部里几位经承吃分子饭；再探时前客虽散，又来一班不相识的人硬要叫琴言，长庆推说不在家，他们竟闯进房去抢了烟枪，扬言要到兵马司出首，闹得满堂是人。子玉长叹一声，托素兰致意琴言保重，又说度香处须常去走动，约定改日到素兰这里相会。归途上迎面撞见王通政王文辉，慌得要下车，只勉强应对几句——这一日访友不遇，反遇见了丈人，愈觉纳闷。`,en:`With Mei Shixie gone to his post, Lady Yan manages the household within and Xu Shun without, and all runs smoothly. Ziyu keeps to his books, ranging beyond the classics into the philosophers, and on fine mornings and moonlit evenings sits with Liu Wenze and Yan Zhongqing over wine and rhymes; Xu Ziyun calls a few times a month, though his own establishment is so grand and his callers so many that he enjoys less leisure than these quieter friends. Wei Pincai, meanwhile, has nothing to do. Mei Shixie will be two or three years in Jiangxi, Wang Wenhui is grand and indifferent, and Pincai concludes that fine as the capital is, it is no place to linger without a patron. He drives into the city to find Fu Lun, who is at cards across the road at Gui Fen's, and so pays his respects to Eldest Master Gui as well. Two other players are there: Yan Jian'an, an old hanger-on of the Hua household, thirty years a friend of the old duke but narrow and foul-tongued; and Yang Meichuang, eighth of his line, Fu Lun's sworn brother, who trades on a reputation as a geomancer while understanding nothing of the art and everything of profit. 

Yang Meichuang takes to Pincai at once and proposes they exchange cards of brotherhood; Yan Jian'an, snobbish to the bone, despises him, and the feeling is mutual. Afterwards Pincai opens his mind to Fu Lun, who offers to recommend him to his wife's uncle, Young Master Hua — the post requires only attendance at wine and theatre and the writing of inconsequential letters, and since Pincai can sing it will suit all the better; flattery is the whole secret. Fu Lun makes the arrangement next day, Hua agrees readily, and Lin Shanzhi is told to settle the salary with the steward. Pincai takes his leave of Ziyu, who is startled and presses him to stay, while Li Yuanmao warns that he is walking onto a bed of nails; Pincai answers that every man must reckon for himself. Lady Yan, valuing his usefulness, sends him off with forty taels. Two carriages come at dawn, and Fu Lun escorts him in person. The screen wall is thirty feet high, the stone lions eight; it is a bowshot from the outer gate to the second and a hundred paces more to the great hall, through galleries and tiered pavilions under glazed tiles, with a steward at every gate to record who passes. In the west flower hall — duck-green carpet, a plaque by Wang Duo, landscapes by Wang Wei, cursive script by Wen Zhengming — the sheer magnificence makes Pincai afraid. The young master being unwell, he is lodged instead in the east garden with Zhang Xiaomei, who paints figures in fine line, and Gu Yueqing, who paints landscape and flowers, along with the secretary Wang Qingyun. 

That evening a banquet of more than forty dishes arrives from the master, and the company drinks to the second watch; next day Pincai is presented to the stewards, the counting house, the gatekeepers, the kitchen, and the storerooms. The gate of a noble house is deep as the sea, and for now he keeps small and flatters everyone — but from this day Wei Pincai is inside the Hua mansion, and much will come of it. Ziyu, missing him for some days, finds the peonies at their height in mid-fourth month and remembers Lu Sulan's kindness; he selects an old inkstone, two boxes of scented ink, an album of ink orchids, and a round fan, copies out two farewell-to-spring poems in small script, and takes them himself. Sulan thanks him, then sighs and lays bare Qinyan's condition: since the evening at the Yi Garden he has hardly spoken to anyone, steers every conversation round to plum blossom, has had Xiao Cixian paint him four plum studies, and begged from Xu Ziyun bolts of southern silk figured with plum sprays to be made into gowns; even his teacher says he has turned inexplicably melancholy. Ziyu weeps, and at last tells the whole story — what Pincai reported on the road, the performance of Waking from a Dream at the guild hall, the trick played on him with the false Qinguan, the sound of stifled weeping behind the screen — and ends by asking what the boy can possibly know of him, when he at least knows the boy's history. Sulan, moved to tears herself, quotes Duxiang's saying that each has his own destined affinity, and observes that the Airs of the States, praising desire without licence, fits Yuxiang and Yunong exactly. They walk to the Autumn Water Hall, but five or six carriages stand at the door and they must turn back and send to enquire: Qinyan is ill in bed while his teacher Changqing entertains clerks from the ministry. A second enquiry brings worse news — those guests gone, a party of strangers has arrived demanding Qinyan, refused to leave when told he is out, forced their way into Changqing's room, seized his opium pipe, and threatened to denounce him to the police, so that the house is full of idlers and uproar. Ziyu sighs, asks Sulan to urge Qinyan to take care of himself and to keep calling on Duxiang, and arranges to meet him here another day. Driving home he runs headlong into Wang Wenhui's carriage and must scramble to get down and pay his respects — a day of friends not found and a father-in-law encountered instead.`},17:{zh:`子玉自素兰处归来，月明花静，正自怅望，高品、史南湘、颜仲清、王恂联袂而至。众人先说日间在高品斋中会了田春航与苏蕙芳，赞湘帆果是同道，与媚香相处的光景令人艳羡；高品便借"惧内"大作文章，说湘帆守定约法三章，连王恂、史南湘辈也牢守闺房，不奉将令不敢妄离一步，满座哄然。继而高品提出：十五日是蕙芳生日，不如凑一公分，在刘文泽的倚剑眠琴之室设席，为春航、蕙芳同贺。点名之间，王恂先漏了徐子云、萧次贤，众人补上；南湘又故意提醒"大会群花，倒忘了一个花王"，这才添上杜琴言，并及秦琪官，共成十人。子玉听见琴言名字，心头一震，散后独坐磨墨，写给度香、静宜的请帖竟成一篇骈俪小启，"怜渠蕙质，堕彼梨园；会我竹林，数他花信"，字字香艳。

次日文泽在家布置，并知会诸花辰刻毕集，只是琪官害眼、琴言病未痊愈，未必能来；席间酒令则议来议去，只说到时见景生情。此时的春航已足不出户，蕙芳每日必来一次，或清谈或小饮，时进箴砭；他一意研经挥翰，名动京师而耻于干谒，宁居萧寺；蕙芳又替他结交了度香、静宜、文泽、南湘、仲清、王恂诸人，仲清昔日相激原是激励，经高品剖明，又添赠五十金，两下更觉相好。及至开筵，中堂高悬群仙高会之图，长廊摆列十余盆蕙花。蕙芳华服先至，恭行大礼，说"今日大罗天上，众仙齐集，使蕙芳辈鸡犬偕升"；子玉与他初次觌面，彼此暗暗赞叹，蕙芳心中直以瑜亮并生相许。徐子云、萧次贤携袁宝珠、王兰保并八个书童翩然而来。高品又打趣春航迟到，说他莫非又滑倒在车辙里，惹得满座大笑，蕙芳脸红。众人不肯推寿翁寿母上坐，仍旧叙齿分作三席，由金漱芳、李玉林、林春喜、袁宝珠、王兰保、陆素兰、苏蕙芳、王桂保分头伺候。

酒酣之际，人人心畅，独子玉念着琴言卧病，药炉半烬，深闭绿窗，只推醉倚炕而卧；萧次贤说昨日诊脉，是积劳兼忧郁，痰中已有血点，非静养数月不能痊愈，子玉听得更觉烦闷。仲清提议留下翰墨，次贤应允作图，众人遂以年齿为序联句，从"玉树歌清晓莺乱"起，一路转韵，南湘一句"惟觉花心尚少真，蛱蝶有雄谁细辨"分清了人与花的界限，文泽以"鸳鸯虽小总相亲"工稳相对；高品屡出恶谑被群旦围啐，王恂一句"蝉娟疑窃嫦娥药"也遭他抢白；子玉心不在焉，只写下"顾盼曾回玉女眸"，暗指那一出《惊梦》，众人却不解其故；末了仍由子玉以"雪花飞上琼枝艳"作结，一气呵成。诗成又将人比花：宝珠为牡丹，蕙芳为芍药，素兰为莲花，玉林为碧桃，漱芳为海棠，兰保为玫瑰，桂保为芙蓉，春喜为兰花；子云说琴言色艺过佳而性情过冷，又酷爱梅，比作梅花最称，且不属庾香更属谁，说得子玉脸红；琪官则议定比作杏花。因说到菊有黄白，文泽提起东园新来一个从广西随千总入京、又被弃置的妓女，人称白菊花，高品便把她的来历一一说破。临散又订下十八日袁宝珠移寓之约，南湘夸口一客不到便罚他做一天东。是日之后，蕙芳踵门叩谢，独子玉已病。到十八日群贤毕集，自午至子，行令猜枚，清歌檀板；张仲雨也来趋奉，花谱中添了琪官，惟子玉与琴言二人皆未痊愈，南湘果然被罚，又在自己家中再叙一日。`,en:`Ziyu comes back from Sulan's into a night of moonlight and motionless flowers, and is still gazing out when Gao Pin, Shi Nanxiang, Yan Zhongqing, and Wang Xun arrive together. They begin with laughter: that day at Gao Pin's rooms they met Tian Chunhang and Su Huifang, and found Xiangfan truly one of their own, his life with Meixiang a thing to envy. Gao Pin turns it into a discourse on henpecked husbands — Xiangfan bound by a three-article covenant, Wang Xun and Shi Nanxiang never stirring from their quarters without orders — and the room dissolves. Then the real proposal: the fifteenth is Huifang's birthday, so let them pool a subscription and hold the feast at Liu Wenze's Chamber for Leaning on a Sword and Sleeping by the Qin, honouring Chunhang and Huifang together. Calling the roll, Wang Xun forgets Xu Ziyun and Xiao Cixian, who are duly added; then Nanxiang observes that a great assembly of flowers has somehow omitted its flower king, and Du Qinyan is added too, with Qin Qiguan, making ten. At Qinyan's name something turns over in Ziyu, and after the others go he sits alone grinding ink until his invitation to Duxiang and Jingyi has become a small parallel-prose rhapsody — pity for an orchid nature fallen into the theatre, a summons to a bamboo-grove fellowship, a counting of the flower-calendar winds. Next day Wenze prepares the rooms and the performers are told to gather at dawn; but Qiguan has an eye ailment and Qinyan is not recovered, so neither is certain, and the question of drinking games is left to the moment. 

Chunhang by now never leaves his rooms: Huifang comes once daily to talk or drink and to offer correction, while he grinds away at the classics, famous in the capital yet ashamed to solicit patrons, content to lodge in a temple. Through Huifang he has come to know Duxiang, Jingyi, Wenze, Nanxiang, Zhongqing, and Wang Xun; and since Gao Pin explained that Zhongqing's earlier scorn had been meant to goad him, and another fifty taels arrived, the two are warmer than ever. At the feast a painting of the immortals' assembly hangs in the hall and a dozen pots of orchid stand along the gallery. Huifang arrives first in full dress and makes a formal obeisance, saying that today the great heaven is crowded with immortals and even the dogs and fowl of his sort may rise with them. He and Ziyu meet for the first time, each privately marvelling, Huifang deciding that here is a rival talent worthy of his own Tian. Xu Ziyun and Xiao Cixian sweep in with Yuan Baozhu, Wang Lanbao, and eight page boys. Gao Pin teases the late Chunhang by wondering whether he has slipped into a wheel rut again, and Huifang colours. Refusing to be seated above the rest, the guests of honour take places by age at three tables, waited on by Jin Shufang, Li Yulin, Lin Chunxi, Yuan Baozhu, Wang Lanbao, Lu Sulan, Su Huifang, and Wang Guibao. As the wine goes round every heart lifts but Ziyu's: thinking of Qinyan ill behind closed green windows with the medicine stove burnt low, he pleads drunkenness and lies down on the couch. Xiao Cixian reports that he took the boy's pulse the day before — exhaustion compounded by grief, with blood already in the phlegm, and months of quiet needed — and Ziyu, listening, sinks further. 

Zhongqing proposes that the day leave some record; Cixian agrees to paint it, and the nine men compose a linked poem in order of age, opening on jade trees and dawn orioles and turning rhyme after rhyme. Nanxiang draws the crucial line — that the flower's heart lacks truth, and who can tell a male butterfly? — which fixes the difference between flowers and people; Wenze answers it neatly with mandarin ducks that are small but always paired. Gao Pin's coarse couplets are shouted down by the performers, and Wang Xun's line about stealing the moon goddess's elixir earns a rebuke of its own. Ziyu, barely present, writes only of eyes turning back in a glance — an allusion to Waking from a Dream that no one else understands — and is given the closing line, where snow alights on a blossoming branch and draws the whole poem together. Then they match each performer to a flower: Baozhu the peony, Huifang the herbaceous peony, Sulan the lotus, Yulin the emerald peach, Shufang the crab-apple, Lanbao the rose, Guibao the hibiscus, Chunxi the orchid. Ziyun says Qinyan's art is too fine and his temper too cold for anything but plum — which he loves besides — and asks to whom plum could belong if not to Yuxiang, at which Ziyu blushes; Qiguan is settled as apricot. Talk of yellow and white chrysanthemums leads Wenze to mention a new courtesan at the East Garden, brought from Guangxi by a lieutenant and then abandoned, known as White Chrysanthemum, whose history Gao Pin proceeds to supply in full. Before parting they fix the eighteenth for Yuan Baozhu's housewarming, Nanxiang boasting that he will stand a day's feast for every guest who fails to come. Huifang calls next day to give thanks, but Ziyu has fallen ill. On the eighteenth the company duly assembles from noon to midnight over forfeits, guessing games, and song; Zhang Zhongyu joins the eager crowd and Qiguan is well enough to appear, but Ziyu and Qinyan are both still unwell — so Nanxiang pays his forfeit and entertains them all again at his own house the next day.`},18:{zh:`杜琴言病势缠绵，闭门谢客，只有同班几个相好时来宽慰；袁宝珠、陆素兰传来梅子玉那日的光景与"千万保重"的话，他听了反更伤感，怡园也久已屏迹。作者趁此写出梨园师傅一生四变：少年丰姿美秀，人所钟爱，名之为兔；二十以后相貌渐蠢而犹搔头弄姿、极意骚浪，名之为狐；三十以后嗓哑须出，唱不成戏，便买孩子教一年半载打发出去赚钱，赚得多的当儿子看待，赚不来的朝打暮骂，出师时开口三千五千吊，钱一到手仍旧赶出门去，连旧衣也不给一件，若无老婆还要徒弟伴宿，凶恶更胜猛虎；及至时运退了，只在班里打旗跑龙套，便只好比作狗。琴言到京四个月，徐子云已在他身上花去白银数千，所以长庆待他分外殷勤——若病上一年半载，只怕也就变了脸。另一边，魏聘才进华府满一月，宾主竟不曾见面，几次请见皆以有事辞之；府中门客家人数百，豪奴架子极大，喜庆要派分子，撇兰聚赌、车夫厨子打抽丰，样样都得应酬，赔累已多，与顾月卿、张笑梅尚合式，与阎简安、王卿云则两下相厌，终日不过"两餐老米饭，一枕黑甜乡"。他出城探望，见子玉卧病未愈，又寻不着李元茂，遂转到伏虎桥张仲雨处。

二人先去戏园听了几出陈腐旧戏，远远望见李元茂同孙嗣徽在对楼，仲雨只笑这对郎舅是天生一对废物。转上酒楼小酌，聘才诉说华府冷落，仲雨便替他讲一篇富贵场中的处世学：上等如陈眉公、李笠翁，以书画诗文哄得王侯动色，如今没有了；二等须有"一团和气、二等才情、三斤酒量、四季衣服、五声音律、六品官衔、七言诗句、八面张罗、九流通透、十分应酬"十样，句句还得添一个"不"字——和气不变、才情不露、酒量不醉、衣服不当、音律不错、官衔不做、诗句不荒、张罗不断、通透不短、应酬不俗；三等则只是"童"与"半通、会足恭、巴结内东、奴才拜弟兄、拉门面靠祖宗、钻头觅缝打抽风"。落到实处，他教聘才先打通华公子寸步不离的亲随林珊枝，再以小便宜笼络华光宿身边八龄班诸童，日日把好话吹进主人耳里，谓之"放线雀儿"；顺其性而留一分在后，谓之"收劣马"；不求一事而献些小忠小信使其放心，谓之"钓金蝉"；至于阎简安、王卿云一流不中用的人，应酬反被要紧人看轻，不如不睬。聘才听得心折，戏称要具门生帖拜他为师。归途上他远远跟着李元茂、孙嗣徽，到了东园——茅茨土墙的娼寮门口挤满闲人。两个脑满肠肥的妇人坐在长凳上招手，唱着"只要京钱二百文"的浪曲，又编出"一个儿脸麻，一个儿眼花，瞎眼鸡同着癞虾蟆"来骂那两人，聘才听懂了几乎失笑。

继而帘内走出一个素净俊俏、鬓边插花的南方女子，捧过琵琶弹了一套《昭君怨》，又自弹自唱身世：从前是鸳与鸯，如今是参与商，千山万水追寻，只落得一枝残菊傲秋霜；石公坝伤心，画眉塘丧命，全湘沅湘、瓜州扬州、淮徐济兖一路北来，只不晓那薄幸儿郎在何处藏——声调凄惨，把聘才听得流下泪来。这便是高品所说从广西新来的白菊花。其时孙嗣徽的荷包已被剪绺割去，只好咬文嚼字地叹"君子无故，玉不去身"；李元茂也说起去年在戏园门口跌交被人扶起、银子随即不见的旧事，听者暗笑。原来白菊花是梧州人，嫁过一个不长进的姓宋的，后与湖广籍张姓营员誓同偕老，去年张营员奉差进京，她便带了盘费与一个小丫头追赶，误了路径出了广西，盘费用尽，又得了假信说他补了千总不得出京，遂卖衣搭船北上；及到京时，那人早已差竣回乡，她只得沦落卖笑。

开门户的陶妈妈家中原有三个姑娘，最好的是扬州人玉天仙，近日接着广东巨富奚十一——此人家有千万，兄弟十人皆作道府，老太爷现任提台，家开洋行，正是京城人眼中的大冤桶；只是他一天要吃一二两鸦片，几个姑娘既吃不惯黑土烟，言语又不投机。陶妈妈便把同是广西人的白菊花接来认作义女，专为笼络他。奚十一一听是广西人便满心欢喜，步行而至，见她娇如花、柔如水，语音又相合，大为得意；她烧烟的手段极好，能挑得五寸长的面条烟供他一口吸尽。他一连宿了七八天，日费数十吊，做衣打首饰，连老鸨帮闲无不打把式，他也从不吝啬。菊花动了从良之念，奚十一本为物色小星而来，两下正合；老鸨舍不得这注财源，两头挑唆，说他家有几十房小星、恋新弃旧，又说菊花有本夫、性子不好，二人却全然不信。末了奚十一叫官媒作媒，赏了陶妈妈五十金，买丫头、雇老妈，将白菊花领回做了姨奶奶——她受尽狼狈之后，总算走上了一条自以为安稳的路。`,en:`Du Qinyan's illness drags on. He shuts his door and sees only a few friends from his own troupe; when Yuan Baozhu and Lu Sulan bring word of how Mei Ziyu looked that day and his plea that Qinyan take care of himself, the message deepens rather than soothes his grief, and the Yi Garden has long gone unvisited. Here the narrator anatomises the four transformations of a theatre master. In youth, lovely and desired, he is called a rabbit. After twenty, coarsening yet still simpering in fine clothes and playing the wanton on stage, he is a fox. After thirty, his voice gone and his beard grown, unable to perform, he buys children, trains them a year or so, and sends them out to earn — treating the profitable ones as sons and beating and cursing the rest, demanding three or five thousand strings at their graduation and then turning them out without even an old coat, and, if he has no wife, making them share his bed: for cruelty he outdoes a tiger. And when his luck fails at last and he is carrying flags among the extras, he can only be called a dog. Qinyan has been in the capital four months, and Xu Ziyun has already spent several thousand taels on him, which is why Changqing treats him so well — though a year's illness would doubtless change that face. Meanwhile Wei Pincai has completed a month in the Hua mansion without once meeting his employer, every request for an audience put off with pleas of business. 

Among several hundred retainers and servants the arrogant ones expect deference; every celebration means a subscription, and grooms, cooks, and hangers-on come round for their share, so that his outlay mounts steadily. He gets on with Gu Yueqing and Zhang Xiaomei, is cordially disliked by Yan Jian'an and Wang Qingyun, and passes his days on two meals of old rice and one good sleep. Going out of the city he finds Ziyu still ill and Li Yuanmao's door locked, and drifts on to Zhang Zhongyu at Fuhu Bridge. They sit through several stale operas — spotting Li Yuanmao and Sun Sihui across the gallery, whom Zhongyu dismisses as a matched pair of fools — then adjourn upstairs to drink, where Pincai pours out his disappointment. Zhongyu answers with a complete science of advancement. The first rank — men like Chen Meigong and Li Liweng, whose brush and verse made princes court them — no longer exists. The second requires ten things: an even temper, second-rate talent, three catties of wine, four seasons of clothes, five tones of music, a sixth-rank title, seven-character verse, address on all eight sides, knowledge of the nine schools, and complete sociability — each with a negative attached: the temper must not alter, the talent not show, the wine not intoxicate, the clothes not go to the pawnshop, the music not slip, the title not be taken up, the verse not run to seed, the address not falter, the knowledge not fall short, the sociability not turn vulgar. The third rank needs only to have sat the boy's examination and to be half-literate, obsequious, ready to flatter the master, to swear brotherhood with servants, to trade on ancestors and squeeze what it can. In practice, he says, Pincai must first win over Lin Shanzhi, who never leaves Hua Guangsu's side, and buy the boys' troupe with trifles, so that good words reach the master daily — that is flying the kite. Yield to a man's temper but always hold one part back — that is reining the vicious horse. 

Ask nothing, offer small proofs of loyalty until he trusts you — that is angling for the cicada. As for useless men like Yan Jian'an and Wang Qingyun, courting them only lowers you in the eyes of those who matter. Pincai is so impressed that he offers, in jest, to enrol as a formal disciple. Walking back he trails Li Yuanmao and Sun Sihui to the East Garden, where a crowd blocks the door of a thatched brothel. Two gross women on a bench beckon and sing a ditty promising heaven for two hundred cash, then improvise another about a pockmarked face and a pair of weak eyes, a blind cock and a warty toad — which Pincai alone understands to be aimed at his companions. Then a plainly dressed southern woman with a flower at her temple comes out, takes up a pipa, plays Zhaojun's Lament, and sings her own history: once mandarin ducks, now two stars that never meet; a thousand hills crossed for nothing; a single ravaged chrysanthemum braving the autumn frost; grief at one dam, near death at another, down all the rivers and through every checkpoint to the capital, with no notion where the faithless man has hidden. Her voice is so desolate that Pincai weeps. 

This is White Chrysanthemum, the newcomer from Guangxi that Gao Pin had described. Sun Sihui discovers his purse has been cut away and can only lament in classical tags that a gentleman does not part with his jade; Li Yuanmao recalls how he fell at a theatre door the year before, was helped up, and found his silver gone. Her story is this: born at Wuzhou, married to a worthless man named Song, she pledged herself to an officer surnamed Zhang from Huguang; when he was sent north last year she followed with a little money and one maid, missed the road out of Guangxi, ran out of funds, and was told falsely that he had been made a lieutenant and could not leave the capital, so she sold her clothes for passage — only to arrive and find him long since gone home. Madam Tao, who keeps the house, already has three girls, the best of them Yu Tianxian of Yangzhou, and has lately hooked Xi Shiyi — a Cantonese worth millions, with ten brothers in prefectural posts, a father commanding a province, and a trading house of his own, in short the fattest pigeon in Beijing. But he smokes one or two ounces of opium a day, the girls cannot manage the black paste, and the dialects do not match; so Madam Tao takes in White Chrysanthemum, a fellow Cantonese, as an adopted daughter expressly to hold him. Delighted to hear of a Guangxi woman, Xi Shiyi walks there on foot, finds her lovely as a flower and soft as water and her speech his own, and is enchanted; she rolls him pipes so skilfully that a five-inch thread of paste vanishes in a single breath. He stays seven or eight nights at some tens of strings a day, ordering clothes and jewellery and tipping everyone in the house without a murmur. She begins to think of leaving the trade, and since he came looking for a concubine the match suits both — only Madam Tao, loath to lose the income, works on each in turn, telling her that he keeps dozens of concubines and tires of each in a month, and telling him that the woman has a husband living and will never settle. Neither believes a word. In the end Xi Shiyi engages an official matchmaker, pays Madam Tao fifty taels, hires a maid and a nurse, and takes White Chrysanthemum home as his concubine — after so much hardship, a road she takes for safety.`},19:{zh:`魏聘才照张仲雨的法门行事，先向林珊枝献尽殷勤，又在八龄班诸童身上赔尽辛苦。珊枝原是联锦部有名的小旦，后被华公子重价买进，八龄班皆其所教，音律最精；一日他教玉龄唱曲，恰是聘才最得意的一支，聘才在旁帮腔，五音不乱、唇齿分明，把珊枝惊倒，自此便在华光宿面前朝夕称赞。华公子最信珊枝，心上遂有了这个人。聘才又今日送扇明日送荷囊，把苏、扬来的小戏子哄得称心，对管总办事的更巴结周到，不到一月人人说好，连张笑梅、顾月卿也不及他的鞠躬尽瘁，阎简安、王卿云则更不必说。一日探得华公子出门，他便约张笑梅出城访冯子佩。子佩是早丧严亲的宦家子弟，母亲钟爱，任他游荡歌场，家业已渐萧条，书斋里残书笔砚、箫管琵琶、刀箭杂物堆得满桌；人却袅娜多姿，一见便与张笑梅搂抱厮闹。杨梅窗随后闯入，因是忌辰不能听戏，四人便上酒楼。

席间议叫相公：聘才自恃已是华府中人，偏点杜琴言，杨梅窗点李玉林，张笑梅点蓉官——不料琴言、玉林皆称病不来，聘才只得改叫琪官，梅窗另叫二喜。二喜一到，听说聘才如今住在华公府，"哎呀"一声，态度顿改，京师人情随势转舵，于此可见。及问起玉林、琪官何以不来，二喜便讲出一段骇人的原委：广东奚十一在京候选，本带数万银子要捐大官，用去了只捐得知州；他初到京便看中登春班的春兰，每出戏做十几副行头，首饰皆金，师傅又拿话呕他赚他，出师又花五千吊，二人天天一炕吹烟一桌吃饭，一碗菜说咸了便连碗砸掉，几百吊的皮褂说风毛不好便顺手撕碎。既有春兰仍不肯放过别人，只要中意便一味硬来：他有个洋漆描金的木桶，口小底大，内藏机括，中层一块板上开两孔，外有铜锁；哄人俯身去捡桶中金银翡翠，两手一伸进去便被锁住，退不出、提不起，他便不问愿否强行施暴，事后知机的多讨些东西，不知机的与他争闹，反被诬为讹诈，送到坊里拘禁挫辱，还要师傅拿百十吊去赎。李玉林为人忠厚，被他一套衣裳一对镯子并赏师傅四十吊动了火，几番相请皆不遂意，末了灌了酒骗他看桶；玉林早听过风声不肯上前，他便仗力硬按，扯脱了裤子，亏得跟班硬闯进来才罢，玉林哭骂而出，他反倒把人送了坊。

当天傍晚他又叫了琪官，琪官不知就里，两手果然伸进桶去；他正要如法炮制，被琪官一腿踢中要害，疼得蹲了下去，琪官又不顾疼痛用力乱扭，把机括扭坏，两手刮得血淋淋才脱身。琪官告知袁宝珠，宝珠气极进怡园禀了徐子云，子云大怒，连夜托人从坊里要出玉林，又要禀明巡城御史搜桶治罪；不知谁走漏了风声，奚十一既怕闹事又已银钱告罄，便在吏部报了"资斧不继"出京，据说躲往天津，只怕过些时还要回来。众人听罢又惊又快，冯子佩痛骂，杨梅窗却打趣问他是否见过那宝贝桶子，二喜也被聘才、梅窗你一句我一句地取笑，红着脸拧人。聘才心里暗想：不料琴言、琪官既唱了戏还这般有骨气。

散后梅窗拉着子佩往胭脂巷玉天仙家去了。另一头，潘其观自被苏蕙芳灌醉哄骗之后，一直懊恼：既想收拾他又怕他交游广，淫心未断，又心疼那二百吊，还疑心张仲雨与蕙芳串通，唠唠叨叨说些影射话，把仲雨也得罪成了对头。他往保定查铺两月回来，与老婆闹了几场，无处出气，便独自一人不带仲雨，傍晚径到蕙芳门口，恰遇他从春航处归来。蕙芳吃了一惊，只得殷勤让进，先赔话解释那日是华公子传去实在短伺候；潘三却说"二百吊钱倒买张老二吐了我一脸酒"，又声称已戒了酒。蕙芳心知今日难脱，索性发狠留他，亲自到厨下备了三荤三素与一壶黄酒，说不喝酒也要应个景儿；潘三终究忍不住，一壶又添一壶，醉后故态复萌，抱住他脸上乱嗅，口口声声只要他"有一点心"，什么都依，连银号都肯给。蕙芳一面周旋，一面正色相责：我今年才十八，既已出师，求你留我一点脸；又反将他一军，说听张仲雨讲潘三爷最是大方，中意的人千儿八百都肯，怎么在我面上才花二百吊就要捞本。潘三无言可答，只好再以银钱笼络，许他随时到铺子里取钱。蕙芳瞧见他腕上一只十两重的金箍，便故意拿徐子云所赏的全绿翡翠镯子给他看，叹一声"可惜没个金的配他"，潘三心疼半晌，终究抬着膀子说"你要就给你罢"。镯子刚上手，他便拦腰抱住，抠摸不已，蕙芳无法可施，只得拿他衣襟上的小牙梳替他梳胡须敷衍。正在危急之际，忽听外面人声脚步，嚷道坊官查夜；潘三是个财主，最怕查三问四，慌得从黑影里一溜烟跑了出去。`,en:`Acting on Zhang Zhongyu's instructions, Wei Pincai lavishes attention on Lin Shanzhi and takes endless trouble over the boys' troupe. Shanzhi was once a noted dan of the Lianjin company before Hua Guangsu bought him at a great price; he trained the boys himself and knows music to the bone. One day, teaching Yuling an aria that happens to be Pincai's own favourite, he hears Pincai join in from the side — every tone true, every syllable distinct — and is astonished. From then on he praises the man morning and evening, and since Hua Guangsu trusts Shanzhi above all and knows he seldom praises anyone, Pincai secures a place in his master's mind. Fans one day and embroidered pouches the next win over the Suzhou and Yangzhou children, and such assiduous courtesy to stewards and clerks that within the month everyone speaks well of him — more warmly than of Zhang Xiaomei or Gu Yueqing, let alone Yan Jian'an and Wang Qingyun. Learning that Hua Guangsu is out, Pincai takes Zhang Xiaomei to call on Feng Zipei, a young gentleman who lost his father early, was indulged by his mother into a career of pleasure, and whose fortune is visibly thinning; his study is a heap of tattered books, brushes, flutes, a pipa, swords, and arrows, while he himself is so willowy that he and Zhang Xiaomei fall at once into an embrace. Yang Meichuang bursts in after them, and since the day is a memorial day when theatre is forbidden, the four go to a wine shop. Choosing performers, Pincai — trusting his new standing — sends for Du Qinyan, Meichuang for Li Yulin, Zhang Xiaomei for Rongguan; but Qinyan and Yulin both plead illness, so Pincai calls Qiguan instead and Meichuang calls Er Xi. Er Xi arrives, hears that Pincai now lives in the Hua mansion, and audibly changes his manner — the capital's regard turning with the wind. 

Asked why Yulin and Qiguan are absent, he tells a shocking story. Xi Shiyi came to the capital with tens of thousands to buy high office and got only a sub-prefecture; he fastened first on Chunlan of the Dengchun troupe, making a dozen sets of costumes for every role and gold ornaments besides, paying five thousand strings to buy out his apprenticeship, sharing pipe and table with him daily, smashing a dish for being too salty and tearing a fur coat worth hundreds of strings because the trim displeased him. Yet Chunlan did not stop him pursuing others by force. He keeps a lacquered and gilded barrel, narrow-mouthed and broad-based, fitted with a hidden catch: an inner shelf pierced with two holes and a lock outside. He invites a boy to reach in for the gold or jade he has thrown down, and when both hands are through, the catch closes; the barrel cannot be lifted or the hands withdrawn, and he does as he likes. Those with sense afterwards demand more presents; those who protest are accused of extortion and handed to the ward office, where friendly officers hold and humiliate them until a master pays a hundred strings to redeem them. Li Yulin, an honest soul, was courted with a suit of clothes, a pair of bracelets, and forty strings for his teacher; when repeated invitations got nowhere, Xi Shiyi plied him with wine and tried to show him the barrel. 

Yulin had heard the rumours and would not approach, so Xi Shiyi simply pinned him down and pulled off his trousers — and only let go when Yulin's attendant forced his way in. Yulin went out cursing and weeping, and Xi Shiyi had him committed to the ward office. That same evening he sent for Qiguan, who knew nothing and put both hands in; as Xi Shiyi moved on him, Qiguan kicked him squarely between the legs, and while he crouched in agony the boy wrenched at the mechanism until it broke, freeing his hands raw and bleeding. Qiguan told Yuan Baozhu, Baozhu went furious to the Yi Garden, and Xu Ziyun, hearing also of Yulin, sent that night to have him released and prepared to petition the city censor to seize the barrel and prosecute. Someone carried the warning; short of money and afraid of a case, Xi Shiyi pleaded exhausted travelling funds at the Board of Civil Office and left the capital, reportedly for Tianjin — though he will surely be back. 

The company is by turns appalled and delighted; Feng Zipei curses, Meichuang asks slyly whether he has inspected the famous barrel himself, and Er Xi, teased in his turn, reddens and pinches his tormentors. Pincai privately marvels that Qinyan and Qiguan, being what they are, should have such spine. Afterwards Meichuang carries Zipei off to Yu Tianxian's house in Rouge Lane. Elsewhere, Pan Qiguan has been brooding since Su Huifang drank him under the table: he wants revenge but fears the boy's connections, his appetite is undiminished, he grudges the two hundred strings, and he suspects Zhang Zhongyu of conspiring in the trick, dropping insinuations until Zhongyu becomes an enemy in earnest. After two months inspecting his cloth shops at Baoding and several quarrels with his wife, he arrives alone at Huifang's door one evening, just as Huifang returns from Chunhang's. Concealing his alarm, Huifang ushers him in and explains that the last time it was Young Master Hua who sent for him; Pan retorts that his two hundred strings bought only Zhang the Second's vomit in his face, and announces that he has given up wine. Seeing no escape, Huifang resolves to brazen it out, goes himself to the kitchen for six dishes and a pot of wine, and observes that even a reformed man must honour the occasion. Pan drinks one pot and then another, and drunk becomes himself again, clutching at Huifang and swearing that if the boy will only show him a little heart he shall have anything, a bank included. Huifang parries and then answers gravely: he is eighteen and out of his apprenticeship, and asks only to be left a little face. Then he turns the argument round — Zhang Zhongyu says Pan Third is open-handed, good for a thousand on anyone he fancies, so why, having spent two hundred here, is he already trying to recover his stake? Pan has no reply and falls back on money, inviting him to draw on the shop whenever he likes. Noticing a ten-ounce gold band on Pan's wrist, Huifang produces the flawless green jade bracelet Xu Ziyun gave him and sighs that it wants a gold one to match; Pan winces, hesitates, and finally holds out his arm. The bracelet is barely on before he seizes Huifang round the waist, and Huifang, out of stratagems, takes the little ivory comb from Pan's lapel and busies himself combing the man's beard. At that moment voices and footsteps sound outside with a cry that the ward officer is making his night round — and Pan Qiguan, a rich man with a horror of questions, bolts through the dark and out of the gate.`},20:{zh:`原来蕙芳下厨房时便已算定潘其观今日不肯罢休，预先叫家人假扮坊官、又请了两个坊卒，到歪缠不清时嚷将进来；果然色大胆小的潘三中计而逃。虽赚得一只金镯，蕙芳仍恐他明日再来，只好到田春航寓中躲避几天。潘三一路丧气而归，因惧内不敢公然在外胡闹，只打算慢慢再拿银钱买转他的心。这边梅子玉自怡园一别，与杜琴言再未会面：琴言的病时好时发，不进园子唱戏，偶尔力疾到怡园一走；子玉的病本由忧闷而起，到怡园偏值琴言不来，到秋水堂又逢师傅请客或有人坐着，又不便再去寻陆素兰，只得怅恨缘悭。琴言在家并不知子玉来过几次，又听说他害病，愈觉悲酸，却因从未到过梅宅不便自去。作者点明症结：长庆只认得钱多，不认得人好——子玉是逢场作戏的读书人，在琴言身上从未花过一个钱，长庆自然不肯拉拢。

此时高品应河间知府之聘去修志，刘文泽被岳母接了同往直隶总督衙门，旧日名士已少了三人，只剩徐子云、萧次贤、史南湘、颜仲清、田春航、王恂六人往来。五月初一恰是王文辉生日，家中亲友毕集，颜夫人等女眷也去了，王恂与仲清便脱不开身；子云、次贤去拜寿时约了南湘、春航同回怡园看龙舟、赏榴花。四人换了便服乘马入园，沿峭壁转上青石羊肠小道，古藤碍首、香草钩衣，又顺围墙经过一带红阑修竹，登三十余级石磴到缥缈亭，见几十株苍松黛色参天，树杪微露碧瓦朱楼。春航叹其文心之妙不让庐山香炉峰，南湘品为幽邃而不险仄，子云说这都是萧次贤手笔，布置时数易其稿。转下石磴便是停云叙雨之斋，壁上一联"拜石有时具袍笏，看云无处不神仙"，署款竟是华光宿；南湘失惊于其词翰，子云说他天分极高只是工夫稍浅，为势位所误，春航则说单看此联便可见其胸次之雅、意气之豪。

正说着，水榭那边荡出两只花艇：王兰保掖起罗衫、盘了辫发，鬓边倒插一枝榴花，手持紫竹篙一面撑一面赶鸭，惊得鸳鸯乱飞，白鹭掠檐而去，次贤笑说这才真是"打鸭惊鸳鸯"。船中走出袁宝珠、金漱芳、苏蕙芳、陆素兰四人，连兰保共是五枝名花。众人登舟游赏，遥望桃坞、杏村、梨院、牡丹香国与梅子青黄的梅崦；素兰细说桂岭、兰径、菊畦、稻庄与鹤栏鹿棚的所在。及至吟秋水榭，三层楼阁建在水中：第一层十二间作六面样式，面面玻璃雕窗，隔作阴阳明暗十二处；第二层四面式样，望见柳阴中泊着三丈多高的龙舟，舟身彩画青龙，五六层架子上尽是绫锦伞盖旗幡、孔雀珍珠银针诸伞，剪彩装成楼台庭院，人物皆以走线行动，顶上立一只扎成的金毛孔雀，二十四人荡桨，内设八音班奏粗细十番——这是次贤在江苏见过后画出图样选匠制造的；第三层三面式样，一色香楠木，地高气爽，凭阑可收怡园八九分全景。众人下楼入席，宾主四人与名花五人依齿而坐。龙舟盘旋阁前，家人放起五千一串的百子爆竹；忽有四个短衫赤膊、红巾扎额的健仆上前叩头，鼓响一声齐翻筋斗入水，原来是夺标：家人抛下白鸭与红漆葫芦，四人或俯或仰、或沉或浮，游龙戏水般争夺，看得满座欢笑。次贤便以此设令：取园中所结的漆葫芦，各人抽一根《水浒传》人物酒筹，注上名号，把筹封进葫芦抛入水中，捞着谁的便由谁行令。

于是次贤得"李逵大闹浔阳江"，南湘得"武松醉夺快活林"而连胜三拳又打了通关，子云得"宋江怒杀阎婆惜"，笑问座中谁是阎婆惜，素兰红了脸把嘴一呶推给宝珠，宝珠反笑他上当——筹上不饮的才是阎婆惜。轮到蕙芳偏是"潘金莲雪天戏叔"，要露胸搭肩、以残酒相劝、末了作怒容自饮，并坐左邻正是春航；他先错叫了宝珠一声"叔叔"，笑得宝珠喷了一桌，众人逼迫不过，只得勉强行完，兰保还打趣说这是捉弄姓潘的报应。兰保得"鲁智深醉打山门"，漱芳得"金翠莲酒楼卖唱"须弹琵琶敬酒，春航得"一丈青捉王短虎"要与蕙芳牵红巾饮三个交杯，臊得蕙芳直说难星过完；不料宝珠得的是"王婆楼上说风情"，须以扇打西门庆、又要西门庆跪捧莲钩敬酒，恰恰轮着子云屈了一膝，南湘一句玩笑更引得漱芳笑闪了腰；末一根是素兰的"梁山泊群雄聚义"，合席各饮三杯收令。酒到红日沉西方才吃饭。饭后南湘提议趁晚霞去看小赤城的榴花，众人复坐船过红桥，从神仙洞穿出杏楼、桃坞，但见一二百株榴花回绕如城，红霞闪烁、流火欲燃，间以黄白玛瑙诸色。天晚将别，子云说园中各处对联尚须添设几副，今日被龙舟耽误，改日再请一游，并约梅子玉、颜仲清同来；南湘、春航应允而去，五位名旦也各自散了。`,en:`It emerges that while Huifang was in the kitchen he had already reckoned that Pan Qiguan would not be shaken off, and had arranged for his servants to impersonate a ward officer, with two constables hired for the part, to raise the alarm at the critical moment. Timid as he is lecherous, Pan Qiguan duly fled. The gold bracelet is won, but Huifang, expecting him back, goes to hide for some days at Tian Chunhang's lodging; Pan, too afraid of his wife to make a public scandal, resolves to buy the boy's goodwill by degrees instead. Since the evening at the Yi Garden, Mei Ziyu and Du Qinyan have not met once. Qinyan's illness comes and goes, he no longer performs, and only occasionally drags himself to the garden; Ziyu's own sickness began in melancholy, and whenever he goes to the garden Qinyan is absent, while at the Autumn Water Hall he finds the teacher entertaining or strangers sitting about — and he cannot keep calling on Lu Sulan. Qinyan, for his part, never learns how often Ziyu has come, hears only that he is ill, and cannot decently present himself at the Mei house. The narrator names the obstruction plainly: Changqing understands money, not merit, and since this young scholar has never spent a cash on Qinyan, the teacher sees no reason to help. The circle has thinned, too — Gao Pin has gone to compile a gazetteer for the prefect of Hejian, and Liu Wenze has been carried off by his mother-in-law to the governor-general's office in Zhili — leaving Xu Ziyun, Xiao Cixian, Shi Nanxiang, Yan Zhongqing, Tian Chunhang, and Wang Xun. 

The first day of the fifth month is Wang Wenhui's birthday, and with the house full of relations and Lady Yan among the women guests, Wang Xun and Zhongqing cannot get away; so Ziyun and Cixian, having paid their respects, carry off Nanxiang and Chunhang to the Yi Garden for the dragon boats and the pomegranates. The four change into plain clothes and ride, climbing a goat-track of blue stone where old vines catch at their hats, past red railings and bamboo, then up thirty-odd steps to the Pavilion of the Misty Void, where dozens of ancient pines rise black-green and a corner of tiled roof shows above the treetops. Chunhang says the artistry rivals the Incense Burner Peak of Mount Lu; Nanxiang calls it deep without being vertiginous; Ziyun says it is all Xiao Cixian's work, redrafted many times. Below stands the Studio of Pausing Clouds and Narrating Rain, hung with a couplet on saluting stones in court dress and finding immortals wherever clouds are watched — signed by Hua Guangsu. Nanxiang is startled by the calligraphy; Ziyun says the man's natural gifts are very high and only his application shallow, spoiled by rank and wealth; Chunhang replies that the couplet alone reveals an elegant mind and a bold spirit. As they speak, two flower-boats swing out from the water pavilion: Wang Lanbao, gown tucked up and braid coiled, a pomegranate blossom stuck at his temple, poles along and chivvies the ducks until the mandarin ducks scatter and a white egret skims the eaves — which Cixian calls beating the ducks and startling the lovebirds. 

Out of the cabins come Yuan Baozhu, Jin Shufang, Su Huifang, and Lu Sulan, five famous flowers counting Lanbao. They board and drift, looking out at the peach slope, the apricot village, the pear court, the peony kingdom, and the plum slope where the fruit hangs half green and half yellow, while Sulan describes the cassia ridge, the orchid path, the chrysanthemum field, the rice farm, and the crane and deer pens. The Chanting-Autumn Water Pavilion stands in the lake in three storeys: the first of twelve rooms on a hexagonal plan, every face glazed and carved, divided into twelve light and shadowed compartments; the second four-sided, from which they see the dragon boat moored under the willows, thirty feet high, its hull painted as a green dragon and its five or six tiers loaded with brocade canopies, banners, and parasols of peacock feather, pearl, and silver needle, with cut-silk palaces and temples whose figures move on strings, a gilt peacock at the summit, twenty-four rowers, and a band playing within — all built from drawings Cixian made after what he had seen in Jiangsu; the third three-sided and panelled in fragrant nanmu, high enough that eight or nine parts of the whole garden lie open to view. They descend to table, four hosts and guests with five performers seated by age. The dragon boat wheels before the hall, five thousand firecrackers are let off in a string, and four grooms in short jackets and red headbands come up, kowtow, and at a drumbeat somersault backwards into the lake — for the prize-taking. Ducks and lacquered gourds are thrown in, and the four twist and dive after them, to the delight of the table. Cixian turns this into the game: gourds grown in the garden are opened, each guest draws a Water Margin tally and marks it with his name, the tallies are sealed inside and thrown into the lake, and whoever's gourd is retrieved must perform his forfeit. Cixian draws Li Kui making havoc at Xunyang; Nanxiang draws Wu Song seizing Happy Grove, wins three bouts running, and drinks a full circuit besides; Ziyun draws Song Jiang killing Yan Poxi and asks who at the table is Yan Poxi, at which Sulan blushes and passes it to Baozhu, who laughs that Sulan has caught himself out, since the tally says Yan Poxi is the one who does not drink. Huifang's tally is Pan Jinlian teasing her brother-in-law in the snow, requiring an open collar, a hand on the shoulder, wine offered from his own lips, and a final cup drunk in feigned anger — with Chunhang seated on his left. 

He first calls Baozhu "brother-in-law" by mistake, making him spray the table, and only completes the forfeit under general coercion, while Lanbao observes that this is what comes of tricking a certain Pan. Lanbao draws Lu Zhishen drunk at the monastery gate; Shufang draws Jin Cuilian singing in the wine shop and must play the pipa; Chunhang draws the Ten-Foot Green capturing Dwarf Tiger Wang and has to drink three cross-cups with Huifang under a knotted kerchief, until Huifang declares his run of bad luck over — only for Baozhu to draw Dame Wang telling of romance upstairs, which requires him to strike Ximen Qing with a fan and to be offered wine by a kneeling Ximen Qing holding his slipper, a part that falls to Ziyun, who bends a knee while Nanxiang's joke makes Shufang laugh until he wrenches his back. The last gourd is Sulan's, the heroes swearing brotherhood at Liangshan, and the whole table drinks three cups to close. They eat as the sun goes down. Afterwards Nanxiang proposes seeing the pomegranates at Little Red Wall by the evening light, so they take the boat back over the red bridge and out through the grotto past the apricot tower and peach slope, where one or two hundred pomegranate trees ring the ground like a wall of red cloud, shot through with yellow, white, and agate. As they part, Ziyun says several pavilions still want couplets, that the dragon boat has consumed the day, and that he will invite them again — with Mei Ziyu and Yan Zhongqing — to finish the task. Nanxiang and Chunhang agree and drive off, and the five performers disperse.`}},n={21:{zh:`魏聘才在华府住了月余，上下周旋得法，又蒙华公子看顾，银钱宽展，便渐渐小人得志：内有顾月卿、张笑梅，外有杨梅窗、冯子佩，朝欢暮乐，所见所闻无非钻营势利，遂心肥胆大。从前在梅宅有梅士燮在家自然畏惧，梅子玉所交又都是名士，如马困槽枥不能泛驾；如今却是任意所欲，无所忌惮。一日他雇了十三太保玻璃热车，把四儿也打扮起来，意气扬扬出城探病。子玉病了月余，意懒神疲，饮食大减，颜夫人只当他读书伤了心血，参苓调理总不见效——原来这是七情所感，草木无从解劝，除非琴言亲来畅谈一昼夜方可霍然。聘才把华公子、林珊枝与八龄班夸得上天下地，又说连琴言也不及；子玉一听便动了心事，问他既亲见其人绝世无双，何以又说有多少胜他的。聘才便打比方：琴言的相貌虽好，性情过冷，如高枝之花攀折不到，如冬夜之月寒到肌骨，不如近手的春花、三秋的明月又好看又不冷。

子玉却正色驳他：花无人折则自保其芳，月无人看则更形其皎；难攀之花遇珍禽仙露愈显其好，寒天之月映寒梅白雪愈见其清；琴言的生香活色人所能知，而挚意深情人罕能喻——一席话无意间竟把聘才归入了"俗人异趣"，聘才心上不悦，只得勉强称是。子玉又托他带话：切莫说我有病，只说不要紧，几天就好；又劝琴言常找陆素兰谈谈，郁闷一开自然痊愈。聘才辞出，暗想庾香既爱其人何不常叫来畅叙，何必闷出病来，径往樱桃巷而去。恰值长庆出门，他直闯进去：小院无人，绿窗深闭，庭前一树黄梅红绽半边，檐下白鹦鹉忽然叫道"客来了，莫要进来"。房中一股幽香药味，壁上悬着萧次贤画的四幅红梅与徐子云所书四幅篆字，炕几胆瓶里插一枝梅花，琴桌上横着一张梅花断纹的瑶琴，连内房软帘上绣的也是各色梅花。琴言穿着湖色纺绸夹袄出来，比前消瘦，正似雪里梅花。

他听得"出城"二字，只当子玉搬了家，忙问搬在何处；聘才见他问起庾香便高兴、对自己却冰冷，心中可恶，索性捏造起来：先冷笑说庾香近来不记得你了，继而绘声绘色说今日进去时听见笑声，云儿高喊"有客来了"，庾香卧在床上满脸通红、答非所问，帐后有响动，他借捡扇子揭帘一看，见一双靴子与白花绉绸的衫子边儿，出门时又见蓉官的跟人在门房说话，因此断定是蓉官；末了还叹惋他撇了旧情，玷污了干净身子。琴言听得心酸落泪，虽半信半疑，却又忍不住追问；聘才再进一步，说他既与庾香相好就不该常在怡园，难怪人家有醋意，还说那张琴也已转送了人。琴言又羞又气，几乎哭出来，起身入内，吩咐小使请魏少爷回府。聘才大怒，转念又怕糟蹋了庾香的心上人反招见怪，只忿忿上车，暗誓要慢慢收拾他。琴言卧床垂泪，左思右想不能明白，忽记起素兰那日的话未曾说完，便遣人去请。素兰一到，先笑他除了庾香再无事寻我，随即说出实情：前日怡园看龙舟，度香请庾香而他因病未到，说他病了一个多月，脸上清瘦了好些，十天前还到过怡园；又讲一件笑话——那日萧次贤把田春航替苏蕙芳做的小序给庾香看，度香在旁赞"愈艳愈好，愈浓愈好"，庾香竟误听作问他玉侬与玉艳孰优，脱口答道"玉侬自然在玉艳之上，玉艳虽好尚逊瑶卿、媚香一筹，而玉侬则玉树琼花，非人间花谱可以位置"，说得度香、次贤大笑，他自己也窘得支吾遮饰。

琴言听到此再忍不住，泪如线穿，把胸前一片纱都湿透了，又顿足痛骂魏聘才无缘无故造言生事。素兰劝住了，又说些外间新闻：那日行酒令时连尖利的媚香也吃了闷亏；媚香为潘其观纠缠不清，已躲到湘帆处几天，如今吩咐下人只回不在家，又在床后开了一个通厨房的门以备躲避。素兰再讲一桩：春阳馆的东家因不认得一位姓魏的师爷，写账时得罪了他，那人醉后摔笔推砚砸碎一柜家伙，写票子时写的是"华公府师老爷"；次日兵马司一枝火签一条链子把掌柜的锁去，亏得求了张仲雨花几十吊讲情，只打了二十嘴巴才放出来，还送了两桌酒席。琴言道，管他是不是，横竖叫魏聘才的总不是好东西。素兰又笑他满屋满身都是梅花，是睹物怀人；却不知庾香身上的荷包、扇络无一不是琴的样式，连扇面画的也是一正一反两张琴——说得琴言又哭了。临别素兰嘱他：这人既进了华公府，小人得志必要作威作福，恶人自有造祸之才，不可不防；又说过几天想个法子，请庾香来会他一面。`,en:`A month and more in the Hua mansion, courting everyone high and low and enjoying both his master's favour and a fuller purse, Wei Pincai begins to swell. Indoors he has Gu Yueqing and Zhang Xiaomei, outdoors Yang Meichuang and Feng Zipei, and a daily round of pleasure in which everything seen and heard concerns influence and advantage. At the Mei house, with Mei Shixie present, he had been kept in check, and Mei Ziyu's friends were all scholars and gentlemen — a horse penned at the manger cannot bolt. Now nothing restrains him. One day he hires a fashionable glazed carriage, dresses up Si'er, and rides out grandly to visit the invalid. Ziyu has been ill above a month, listless and off his food; Lady Yan supposes hard study has drained him and doses him with ginseng to no effect, for the sickness comes of feeling, which no root or bark can argue with, and nothing would cure it but a day and a night of talk with Qinyan. Pincai praises Hua Guangsu, Lin Shanzhi, and the boys' troupe to the skies, adding that even Qinyan does not equal them — which touches Ziyu at once, and he asks how a man who has seen that peerless face can speak so. Pincai offers his comparison: Qinyan's looks are fine but his nature is cold, a flower on a branch too high to pluck, a winter moon whose brilliance chills the bone, less lovable than blossom within reach or the mild moon of autumn. 

Ziyu answers gravely that a flower no one plucks keeps its own fragrance, and a moon no one watches shines the whiter; that a flower beyond reach shows best among rare birds and immortal dew, and a winter moon best above plum and snow; that Qinyan's living beauty anyone may see, but his depth of feeling few can construe. Without meaning to, he classes his guest among the vulgar, and Pincai, stung, can only agree. Ziyu entrusts him with a message: say nothing of my illness, or if he asks, say it is slight; and tell him to talk often with Lu Sulan, since lifting the gloom will speed his recovery. Riding away, Pincai reflects that a man who loves someone should simply send for him rather than sicken in silence, and goes straight to Cherry Blossom Lane. Changqing is out, so he walks in: an empty courtyard, green windows shut, a plum tree hung with yellow fruit half flushed red, and under the eaves a white parrot that suddenly announces, "A guest has come — do not go in." The room smells of medicine and faint perfume; on the walls hang four red-plum paintings by Xiao Cixian and four sheets of seal script by Xu Ziyun; a plum spray stands in a vase, a qin with plum-bark cracks lies on the table, and even the inner curtain is embroidered with plum. Qinyan comes out in a lake-blue jacket, thinner than before, like plum in snow. Hearing the words "outside the city," he takes it that Ziyu has moved and asks anxiously where. Pincai, nettled that the boy brightens at Ziyu's name and freezes at his own, begins to invent. 

He laughs coldly and says Ziyu no longer remembers him; then, pressed, describes arriving to hear laughter, Yun'er calling out loudly that a guest had come, Ziyu flushed on his bed and answering at random, a stirring behind the curtain, and how, stooping for a dropped fan, he glimpsed a pair of boots and the hem of a white crepe gown — and how, leaving, he saw Rongguan's servant gossiping at the gatehouse. He ends by lamenting that Ziyu should throw away such devotion and soil himself so. Qinyan's tears fall though he half disbelieves, and he cannot stop questioning. Pincai presses further: since Qinyan is so much at the Yi Garden, no wonder there is jealousy — and adds that the qin has been given away to someone else. At that Qinyan rises, wounded and furious, goes into the inner room, and sends word that the gentleman may return home. Pincai leaves in a rage, restrained only by the thought that harming Ziyu's beloved would earn Ziyu's anger, and swears to settle the account by degrees. Weeping on his bed, Qinyan remembers that Sulan's account was once cut short and sends for him. Sulan arrives, teases that Qinyan never wants him except about Ziyu, and then tells the truth: at the dragon-boat party Duxiang had invited Ziyu, who was too ill to come, having been sick above a month and grown thin, though he had called at the garden ten days before. 

And there was a joke — Xiao Cixian showed Ziyu the preface Tian Chunhang wrote for Su Huifang's birthday, and when Duxiang praised it as the richer the better, the more voluptuous the better, Ziyu misheard it as a question about which he preferred, Yunong or Yuyan, and blurted out that Yunong naturally stands above Yuyan, that Yuyan for all his merit falls short of Yaoqing and Meixiang, and that Yunong is a jade tree and a flower of heaven not to be placed in any earthly register at all — at which the two men laughed and Ziyu covered his confusion badly. Qinyan weeps until the gauze at his breast is soaked, then stamps and curses Wei Pincai for inventing slanders out of nothing. Sulan calms him with other news: at the drinking game even the sharp-witted Meixiang was caught out; and Meixiang, harried by Pan Qiguan, has hidden several days at Xiangfan's, now instructs his servants to say he is never at home, and has had a door cut behind his bed into the kitchen for escape. Then Sulan tells another story: the manager of the Chunyang Restaurant, not recognising a certain Master Wei, offended him over the writing of a bill; the man, drunk, threw down the brush, shoved the great inkstone off the counter and smashed a whole set of ware, and signed the note in the name of the Hua mansion. Next morning the ward office came with a warrant and a chain; only by begging Zhang Zhongyu and spending tens of strings did the manager escape with twenty blows on the face — and two banquets besides. Qinyan says it hardly matters whether it is the same man, since anyone called Wei Pincai is certain to be a scoundrel. Sulan then laughs that Qinyan's room and clothes are all plum, an object standing for a person — and adds that Ziyu's purse and fan-cords are all shaped like a qin, and his fan painted with two of them, one upright and one reversed; at which Qinyan weeps again. Leaving, Sulan warns him that a mean man newly in power will certainly abuse it, that wickedness has a talent for making trouble, and that Qinyan must be on his guard — and promises that in a few days he will contrive a meeting with Ziyu.`},22:{zh:`陆素兰被琴言催得无计，寓中人杂，酒楼又不便倾诉，终于想出泛舟运河之计：在大东门外雇一只精洁画船，搬去自家玩器笔砚花卉，只约琴言一人，再借徐子云名义修书请梅子玉。子玉正对着子云所赠瑶琴消磨长日，见封面字迹既不像子云，也不像萧次贤，拆开才知是素兰相招，且暗许一见玉侬，一时心花大开，病去九分，即刻回帖应约。素兰持信去报喜，才进二门便听见一片喝骂：两个凶汉捶台拍桌，骂琴言不过一个小旦，装病摆架子，扬言要毁他的窝、揍他师傅曹长庆；家人低声下气，奉茶装烟，直送出四吊钱才把这两尊瘟神劝走。素兰隔窗偷看被瞧见，几乎被追上。原来一个是华光宿府中的车夫，一个是官厨小子，都是魏聘才花八吊钱买来的。素兰奔到琴言房中，蓝纱帐里一片呜咽。

琴言说出死志：求他转求徐子云，把自己葬在怡园梅崦的梅树下；再不然烧成灰，往山高水深处顺风吹散，省得留一个苦命的痕迹在人间。才十六岁，已活得不耐烦了。二人对哭半个时辰，素兰自伤才貌之人天所必妒，转而察出闹事者的来路，琴言方恍然大悟：魏聘才造谣于前、遣人于后。素兰以子玉的回信解劝，琴言看了又看，才微微一笑。是夜四更风雨雷电大作，琴言梦见刚要上船便被恶汉揪住，惊出一身冷汗；醒来又听檐下鹦鹉学舌道「昨日的人又来了」。

次日子玉冒着残雨早起，捡出江绸湖绸、零碎玩物与十几两碎银预备酬谢素兰，却被赤脚披衫的李元茂缠住借钱，误了时辰，直到午初才赶到船上。素兰有意相试，先说玉侬病重不能来，子玉如冰水淋身，只叹好事多磨；素兰又说另有一个貌似玉侬的可作替身，子玉正色不肯，道当日怡园已被唐突过一次，何必再叫婢学夫人。话未了，后舱转出一个如花似玉的人来——正是琴言，比前清减，从前国色天姿，如今却落花无言、人淡如菊。三人抱头痛哭，然后说破魏聘才的种种阴谋，子玉气得神色惨淡，誓与之绝交。酒摆上来，愁云渐散：一个看对面是瑶柯琪树、秋月冰壶，一个看对面是蔷薇濯露、芍药笼烟，只恨鲁阳无戈，留不住那一轮红日。忽有一船横过，急管繁弦，三个袒裼的汉子怀抱小旦对口喝酒——素兰指出正是缠扰苏蕙芳的潘其观，怀里坐的是登春班的玉美。一水之隔，两种"乐"字相照：这边是相对忘言的清愁，那边是狂笑如奔流的浊欢。日落催归，子玉分赠礼物、散赏跟人，在大东门执手而别，琴言道往后相见不在香畹处便在怡园。子玉回家已上灯，颜夫人问起，只得随口支吾。`,en:`Pressed by Qinyan day after day, Lu Sulan can find nowhere private enough: his own lodging is too crowded, a restaurant too public for hearts to be emptied. At last he hits on a day's drifting up the canal. He hires a trim painted boat outside the Great East Gate, moves aboard his own curios, brushes, inkstones and flowering plants, invites Qinyan alone, and writes to Mei Ziyu in Xu Ziyun's name. Ziyu, wearing out the long day over the qin Ziyun gave him, notices that the hand on the envelope is neither Ziyun's nor Xiao Cixian's; opening it, he finds Sulan's invitation with its veiled promise of Yunong, and nine-tenths of his illness lifts on the spot. He answers at once that he will certainly come. Sulan carries the reply to Qinyan's house and, barely inside the second gate, hears cursing: two bruisers pounding the table, jeering that Qinyan is only a boy actor giving himself airs, swearing to wreck the place and thrash his master Cao Changqing. The servants bow, pour tea, pack the water-pipe, and finally buy them off with four strings of cash. Sulan, spying through a window crack, is seen and barely outruns them. 

The pair, it emerges, are a carriage driver from Hua Guangsu's mansion and a boy from the official kitchen, hired by Wei Pincai for eight strings. Sulan runs to Qinyan and finds him sobbing behind blue gauze curtains. Qinyan speaks of dying: let Sulan beg Xu Ziyun to bury his bones beneath the plum trees of the Plum Slope at Yiyuan, or else burn him and scatter the ash on the wind where the mountains are high and the water deep, so that no trace of an unlucky life remains to trouble anyone. He is sixteen, he says, and already weary of living. They weep together half an hour; Sulan reflects that heaven envies whoever is given beauty and talent, then reasons out where the ruffians came from, and Qinyan sees the whole design at last — Wei Pincai's slanders first, his hired men after. Sulan quiets him with Ziyu's letter, which Qinyan reads and rereads until he smiles faintly. 

At the fourth watch wind, rain, thunder and lightning break over the city; Qinyan dreams he is seized by the ruffian just as he steps aboard, and wakes in a cold sweat to hear the parrot under the eaves announce, "Yesterday's man is here again." Ziyu rises early through the last of the rain, packing Jiangsu and Huzhou silks, small treasures and a dozen taels of loose silver to thank Sulan — only to be waylaid by Li Yuanmao, barefoot and half-dressed, who hums and haws his way around to borrowing money, so that Ziyu reaches the boat only at noon. Sulan tries him first: Yunong is too ill to come. Ziyu takes it like cold water poured over him and can say only that good things are much thwarted. Then Sulan offers a substitute of similar face; Ziyu refuses gravely — Yiyuan once played that trick already, and he will not have a maid imitate the lady. Before he finishes, a figure like flowering jade steps out of the after-cabin: Qinyan himself, thinner than before, once a peerless bloom and now a fallen blossom that says nothing, pale as a chrysanthemum. The three weep in one knot, and then the truth of Wei Pincai's plotting comes out; Ziyu, ashen, swears to have done with him. Wine is set out and the cloud lifts by degrees: each finds the other a jade branch, an autumn moon in a jar of ice, a rose washed with dew, a peony veiled in mist, and both wish they had a spear to drive the red sun backward. A boat crosses their bow with shrill pipes and hurrying strings, three men stripped to the waist drinking mouth to mouth with young actors in their laps — Sulan identifies Pan Qiguan, who has been hounding Su Huifang, and Yu Mei of the Dengchun troupe on his knee. One stretch of water divides two kinds of pleasure: on this side a quiet sorrow too full for speech, on that a laughter like a torrent going down rapids. Sunset drives them back; Ziyu distributes his gifts, tips the servants, and parts from Qinyan at the Great East Gate, where Qinyan says that hereafter they will meet either at Xiangwan's or at Yiyuan. Ziyu reaches home by lamplight and answers Madam Yan's questions with whatever comes to hand.`},23:{zh:`本回上下两截，各写一个"欲"字的丑处。上截属李元茂。他从梅子玉的账房借得八吊大钱，先在烟铺换了十张票子，装在槟榔口袋里挂在衣衿上，志得意满；去寻母舅孙嗣徽扑了空，与孙嗣元话不投机，便信步逛到东园，心里还惦记着从前那个弹琵琶的妇人。他戴上眼镜，弯腰探头，看中一个体肥脸粗、眼波流转的三十来岁妇人；门边两个闲汉——一个挽着揪儿把玩麻鹰，一个穿蓝布衫——你一言我一语，说包管替他守门，一推把他推进门去，反手扣上。屋里先有汉子索钱，元茂只有票子，递出一吊只当能找零，那人看也不看便塞进皮抽屉。

草帘一掀，腥气扑鼻，正在情热之际，忽听街门大响，两个闲汉扭打进来，四个人八只手把他剥个精光，一哄而散。元茂上天无路，只得裹了草帘蹲在破屋里高喊小使王保；那孩子才十三四岁，早躲在树后啼哭。看热闹的挤满门口，有人笑道这是好嫖的报应。衣裳取来穿上，他还假作体面，回头恐吓要送兵马司。原来那妇人与两个闲汉本是一路，专候这样呆头呆脑而衣履光鲜的外行。

此事他有冤无处诉，偏偏人丛中有认得他的，知他住在梅宅、又是孙亮功未过门的女婿；后来王保打破茶碗挨了两掌，索性把这段丑事说了出去，孙亮功也就风闻了。下截随孙嗣徽转出姬亮轩：江苏金匮人，久办刑钱，因品行不端，近省竟寻不出馆地，只得携资进京钻营。作者借此把作幕的分作数等：有科名不遂而改途、慈祥济世、正大光明的上幕，十中不过二三；有胁肩谄笑、以亲戚同乡结党、开口只论束修多寡、到馆则奉成案为圭臬的劣幕；更有《四书》只读得一半、拜师学幕而吃喝嫖赌招摇撞骗的下下之流——姬亮轩正是最末一等。是日嗣徽请他听凤台班的戏，又携伶人凤林入酒楼雅座。姬亮轩身边有个十五六岁的徽州少年巴英官，本是剃头铺学徒，十千钱买来改名装扮，日间主仆称呼，夜间妻妾侍奉；巴英官气忿站在廊下，凤林最伶俐，几声"巴二爷"便哄住了。席上一个满口之乎者也而不通，一个满口下作而自得：凤林说班中师傅一年可挣三四千吊，姬亮轩伸出舌头，说比刑钱束修还多，懊悔当初不曾学戏；又自夸这一个少年兼作"驴子与小妾"，月给八百钱，一年不到二十千，比叫相公便宜十倍；末了竟以坐船坐车作譬，说到粪车与木耳，秽亵不堪，连凤林也红了脸罚他三杯。回末作者自解：世间实有此等人，若只写美人名士的好色不淫，而不写几个极淫的，便不成五色之文、八音之律。`,en:`The chapter falls into two halves, each exhibiting a different ugliness of appetite. The first belongs to Li Yuanmao. Having got eight strings of cash out of the Mei counting-house through Mei Ziyu, he changes five of them into ten paper notes at a tobacconist's, stows them in the betel pouch hanging at his lapel, and swells with confidence. He calls on his uncle Sun Sihui and finds him out, sits briefly with Sun Siyuan, whose contradictions he cannot bear, and then drifts toward the East Garden quarter, still thinking of the woman with the pipa he once saw there. Spectacles on, stooping and craning, he settles on a fleshy, coarse-faced woman of thirty with roving, watery eyes. Two idlers at the door — one with his hair in a topknot, toying with a little hawk, the other in a blue cloth shirt — take turns coaxing him, promise to stand guard, then shove him through the doorway and bolt it behind him. 

A filthy man appears demanding payment; Yuanmao has only notes, hands over one worth a string expecting change, and watches it vanish into a leather drawer unexamined. Behind the straw curtain the air is rank; and just as his heart is hottest the street door crashes, the two idlers come brawling in, and four men with eight hands strip him bare and scatter. With no hole to hide in, Yuanmao winds the straw curtain round himself, crouches in the hovel and shouts for his page Wang Bao — a boy of thirteen or fourteen who had wandered off to play and is now weeping behind a tree. A crowd packs the doorway; someone laughs that this is what whoring earns. When his clothes are fetched he dresses, walks out with borrowed dignity, and threatens over his shoulder to have the culprits handed to the ward office. The woman and the two idlers were of course one gang, waiting precisely for a well-dressed simpleton with no experience. Yuanmao can complain to nobody, but people in the crowd know him for a resident of the Mei house and Sun Lianggong's betrothed son-in-law; later Wang Bao, slapped for breaking a teacup, tells the whole story, and word reaches Sun Lianggong himself. 

The second half follows Sun Sihui to his kinsman Ji Liangxuan, a man of Jinkui in Jiangsu, long employed as a legal-and-fiscal secretary, whose character is so bad that no post can be found for him in the nearer provinces; so he has come to the capital with money and goods to buy his way into notice. The narrator pauses to sort the secretarial profession into its kinds: at the top, scholars whose examination luck failed, who take up the work late and practise it uprightly, guarding both their employer's name and the people's welfare — two or three in ten at most; below them the mean sort, all bowed shoulders and ingratiating smiles, who form cliques of kinsmen, countrymen and old connections, sell recommendations, ask first how large the fee is, and once installed treat precedent as scripture and let hired copyists do the work; and lowest of all, men who have read half of *The Four Books*, apprentice themselves to a "teacher" for the price of one banquet, and then live by drinking, gambling, whoring, and swindling — Ji Liangxuan's own class exactly. That day Sihui takes him to the Fengtai troupe's performance and then to a private upstairs room with the actor Fenglin. Ji keeps a handsome page of fifteen or sixteen from Huizhou, Ba Yingguan, bought out of a barber's shop for ten thousand cash, renamed and dressed up: master and servant by day, husband and concubine by night. Yingguan sulks jealously in the corridor until the quick-witted Fenglin soothes him with a few cries of "Second Master Ba." At table one man talks unintelligible Classics and the other unblushing filth. Fenglin mentions that a troupe master clears three or four thousand strings a year; Ji puts out his tongue in astonishment — more than a secretary's fee — and says he regrets not having learned opera instead. He then boasts of his page as at once donkey and concubine: messenger by day, company by night, relief in emergencies, eight hundred cash a month and under twenty strings a year, a tenth of the cost of calling for actors. He ends with the notorious comparison of boats and carts, night-soil carts and wood-ear fungus, until even Fenglin reddens and fines him three cups. The author closes by defending the filth: such people do exist, and a book that shows only beauties and gentlemen who love beauty without lechery, and never the utterly lecherous, would be no pattern of five colours, no harmony of eight tones.`},24:{zh:`颜仲清与王恂在家闲话，把连日新闻一件件抖开。先是张仲雨传来的：开银号的潘其观自三月起便想认苏蕙芳作干儿子，蕙芳佯作殷勤，先借他二百吊钱，待他要敬皮杯时假装鱼骨鲠喉，再把一干人灌得烂醉，安置同宿客房；潘其观滚下床来，尿在自己鞋里，醒后查问，只说蕙芳被华光宿叫去了，闹了一夜也只得回家；四月再来纠缠，又撞上假查夜的，吓得丢下一只金镯逃走。其次是李元茂东园裹草帘的丑事，已由张仲雨的人目睹传开。再次是魏聘才自进华府便变了相：闹春阳馆，累店家挨了二十嘴巴，又买通华府车夫与厨下小子去糟蹋杜琴言。末一件却是喜信——梅子玉与琴言、陆素兰同游运河一日，两人的病竟都好了。由新闻转入议论，才是本回正文。

王恂不解：子玉与琴言不过正月初六看《惊梦》的一面之缘，何以缠绵至此？仲清答得极透：这一段至正之情的情根，偏由魏聘才这不正之人种下——当日同船北上，聘才细数琴言的好处，子玉听熟了便日日思想；及见其色艺冠群，又闻其孤高冷淡，爱中生敬，敬中愈生爱；再得徐子云作氤氲使，以假试真，才把两副肺腑打通。子玉一怒而琴言感愈深，琴言一哭而子玉爱弥甚，虽只一面，已如三生前定。论到田春航与蕙芳，则起于色而终于敬，正如子云、相如同工异曲；至于潘其观，仲清断言他要认干儿子恰是其无情处——若得银号而不遂欢心，必恨蕙芳入骨；情字若走到守钱虏心上，天上情关也须另请情仙执掌。仲清又逐一品题：徐子云爱博而不泛、气盛而不骄，是情中主人；萧次贤野鹤闲云而纯静过人；史南湘恃才傲物而用情恳挚；高品如云行水流，辞锋虽利，独于所好不加一刻薄语；刘文泽与王恂和平浑厚；自己则不肯轻用情，只把情借与人用，成人之情即以成己之情——王恂笑称这是朱家、郭解一流的"情中之侠"。至于魏聘才、冯子佩、潘其观是情中之蠹，奚十一以木桶暗害相公是情中之盗，孙嗣徽、孙嗣元则根本不知情为何物。正说得有味，李玉林同王桂保来了，带来怡园的消息：六月初六荷花生日，华光宿要来逛园，徐子云与萧次贤商定，从各班挑人合成一个大成班，另谱新戏。

戏目取毛声山在《琵琶记》评语中拟而未作的《补天石》诸目——《李陵返汉》《燕丹灭秦》《诸葛延年》《明妃归汉》，次贤又添四本：《金谷园绿珠投楼》《马嵬驿杨妃随驾》《李谪仙夜郎奉诏》《杜拾遗金殿承恩》。仲清极称这四本为天下才子佳人吐气，并为杨妃辨诬：祸在国忠，何罪玉妃；洗儿之说新旧《唐书》皆不载，不过因元微之一句轻薄词句以讹传讹。旦脚十人是袁宝珠、苏蕙芳、陆素兰、王兰保、金漱芳、林春喜、李玉林、王桂保并杜琴言、玉艳，王恂笑称"十美班"。饭后行《贴翠令》，酒酣时王恂请二人品评同侪：宝珠不慕势利而意气自豪，蕙芳一尘不染而心怀好胜，素兰和而不流、婉而有致，兰保雅俗咸宜，漱芳恬静妥贴，春喜心灵口敏，桂保泛应有余、风流自赏，琴言别有会心、海枯石烂而节操不移；玉林自谦无可取，桂保便替他补上"芳洁自守，不亢不卑"。说到蓉官、二喜、玉美之流，桂保比作助纣为虐的旁门左道，道玉林之事是蓉官攻出来的，袁琪官之苦是二喜作成的——由此牵出奚十一以木桶暗算琪官、琪官性烈毁桶而出、双手刮得稀烂的惨事，也带出奚家由洋商而军功、坐拥千万而以鸦片致富的来历。桂保又替琴言担心：他见一个得罪一个，心上却只有一个梅子玉。二更方散。`,en:`Yan Zhongqing and Wang Xun sit at home and unpack the week's news item by item. First, from Zhang Zhongyu: Pan Qiguan, who keeps a silver bureau, has been trying since the third month to make Su Huifang his adopted son. Huifang played warm, borrowed two hundred strings of cash from him, and when Pan wanted a lip-to-lip toast feigned a fishbone in his throat; then he got the whole party blind drunk and bedded them down together in the guest room. Pan rolled off the bed, urinated into his own shoe, and on waking was told only that Huifang had been summoned by Hua Guangsu; he raged all night and went home with nothing. Returning in the fourth month, he ran into a faked night patrol and fled, dropping a gold bracelet. Next, Li Yuanmao's straw-curtain disgrace at the East Garden, witnessed by Zhang Zhongyu's man and now everywhere repeated. Next, Wei Pincai, who has changed beyond recognition since entering the Hua mansion: the uproar at the Chunyang Restaurant that cost the manager twenty blows on the face, and the bribing of a Hua carriage driver and a kitchen boy to abuse Du Qinyan. Last, a piece of good news — Mei Ziyu spent a day on the canal with Qinyan and Lu Sulan, and both invalids have recovered. From gossip the chapter turns to its real subject, an anatomy of feeling. Wang Xun cannot understand it: Ziyu and Qinyan met only once, on the sixth of the first month, over a performance of *Waking from a Dream*. 

Zhongqing's answer is exact. The root of this most upright love was planted by a most unupright man — Wei Pincai, who came north on the same boat and catalogued Qinyan's excellences until Ziyu thought of him daily before ever seeing his face. Then came the sight of his unrivalled art and the report of his proud, cold nature, so that love bred reverence and reverence bred more love; then Xu Ziyun played matchmaker, testing the false against the true, and opened one heart into the other. Ziyu's anger deepened Qinyan's gratitude, Qinyan's tears deepened Ziyu's love, and a single meeting became a bond fixed three lives ago. Tian Chunhang's love for Huifang, by contrast, began in desire and ended in reverence — the same craft in a different key. As for Pan Qiguan, Zhongqing insists that wanting Huifang for an adopted son is the very proof of his heartlessness: had he spent his silver and been refused his pleasure, he would have hated Huifang to the bone. If feeling ever lodged in a miser's breast, heaven's gate of passion would need a new keeper. Zhongqing then appraises them all: Xu Ziyun loves widely without diffuseness and is spirited without arrogance — the master of feeling; Xiao Cixian is a wild crane among idle clouds, unmatched in purity; Shi Nanxiang is proud of his gifts yet wholly sincere in affection; Gao Pin moves like cloud and water and, sharp-tongued as he is, will not say one cruel word of anyone he loves; Liu Wenze and Wang Xun are peaceable and generous. He himself will not spend feeling lightly, but lends it to others, completing their loves and so completing his own — at which Wang Xun laughs that he is a knight-errant of feeling in the line of Zhu Jia and Guo Jie. Wei Pincai, Feng Zipei, and Pan Qiguan are the worms of feeling; Xi Shiyi, who traps young actors in a wooden barrel, is its bandit; Sun Sihui and Sun Siyuan simply do not know what feeling is. 

Li Yulin and Wang Guibao arrive with news from Yiyuan. On the sixth of the sixth month, the lotus birthday, Hua Guangsu is to visit the garden, and Xu Ziyun has agreed with Xiao Cixian to pick the best of every troupe into one grand company and stage newly written plays. The titles come from *Stone to Mend Heaven*, the set Mao Shengshan proposed in his commentary on *Story of the Pipa* but never composed — *Li Ling Returns to Han*, *Prince Dan Destroys Qin*, *Zhuge Yan Nian*, *Bright Consort Returns to Han* — to which Cixian has added four more: *Green Pearl Throws Herself from the Golden Valley Garden*, *Consort Yang Follows the Carriage at Mawei Post*, *Li the Banished Immortal Receives the Edict at Yelang*, and *Du the Remonstrator Honored in the Golden Hall*. Zhongqing praises these last as vindication for every gifted man and beautiful woman, and defends Consort Yang in particular: the catastrophe was Yang Guozhong's, not hers, and the tale of the bathing of the infant appears in neither the old nor the new *Book of Tang*, having grown out of one frivolous line by Yuan Weizhi. The ten female leads are Yuan Baozhu, Su Huifang, Lu Sulan, Wang Lanbao, Jin Shufang, Lin Chunxi, Li Yulin, Wang Guibao, Du Qinyan, and Yuyan — a "Troupe of Ten Beauties," Wang Xun says. After dinner they play the *Affixing the Kingfisher Command*, and over wine Wang Xun asks the two actors to appraise their colleagues: Baozhu scorns influence yet carries himself proudly; Huifang is spotless, luminous, and privately competitive; Sulan is harmonious without drifting, gentle without weakness; Lanbao suits high company and low alike; Shufang is calm and apt in speech; Chunxi is quick of mind and tongue; Guibao meets all comers and admires his own dash; Qinyan understands what others cannot construe and would keep his integrity though seas dried and stones crumbled. Yulin claims nothing for himself, so Guibao supplies it: fragrant and self-contained, neither haughty nor servile. Of Rongguan, Er Xi, Yu Mei and their sort, Guibao says they belong to the heterodox school that abets tyranny — it was Rongguan who exposed Yulin, and Er Xi who engineered Yuan Qiguan's suffering. That leads to the cruelty itself: Xi Shiyi's barrel trap, and Yuan Qiguan, too fierce to submit, breaking out of it with both hands flayed raw. With it comes Xi's pedigree — a foreign-trade fortune turned into military rank, ten million in property, opium money underneath it all. Guibao ends by worrying over Qinyan, who offends everyone he meets and keeps room in his heart for one man only, Mei Ziyu. They break up at the second watch.`},25:{zh:`六月初六荷花生日，怡园为华光宿设宴演新戏。刘文泽因其父刘侍郎升了礼部尚书，星夜自保定赶回，是日辰刻先到。书中于此细写怡园：周围三四里，小山堆以太湖石，大山以土为脚，护以花木，衬以亭台；山洞暗设桔槔戽水倒喷，便成飞瀑；池通外河，回环随势；园内另设射圃、球场、渔庄、稻舍、酒肆、茶寮，皆园丁开设，使执事人不必出外旷业，这是徐子云的用心。庭院二十四处，认真游赏须八九日方尽。正中含万楼取"含万物而化光"之意，东边垂杨之外便是吟秋水榭，荷花开满一池。子云向文泽说明今日客少之故：梅子玉旧病复发，史南湘醉后呕血，田春航因未会过华公子不肯来，王恂在家守着夫人临盆，颜仲清见众人皆辞也辞了，高品尚未回京，只剩张仲雨与萧次贤。仲雨新捐了六品，总共花去四千，还是起盛银号的潘其观替他垫了五百；文泽笑他将来必名利双收，子云接口道"上司一定欢喜，只是百姓吃苦些"，仲雨也不讳言，说自己这官原是花本钱来的。华公子乘椅轿而至，随从二十余人；两家父辈本是军营旧交，子云长他五岁，遂以兄事之。

席上十旦捧牙笏参见，华公子独于杜琴言留意，说他"举止似乎未熟，然而秀外慧中，觉有出尘之致"。开场《李陵返汉》《明妃入关》，继以《仪郎奉诏》，把李谪仙的魂魄都做了出来；第二本《杨妃入蜀》则国忠伏诛、六军踊跃，明皇自吹玉笛，妃子歌《清平》，宫人作《回风》之舞，八位女仙自扎成的峨嵋山顶御风而下，百和龙涎香烟缭绕，竟把一座戏台放在彩云端里。华公子喝采不绝，赏钱八十吊，又命林珊枝捧出一盘梅花、如意、菱角式的金锞分赏十旦；自家的八龄班也上来唱了《群仙高会》。未末移席吟秋水榭，十旦扮采莲女荡舟花间，唱《采莲歌》，声如子夜清歌，望如湘君游戏。华公子兴犹未尽，要"重歌《金缕》，再舞《霓裳》"，子云说苏蕙芳与陆素兰的《独占》、袁宝珠与琴言的《惊梦》《寻梦》皆绝妙无双，可惜雷同；文泽便提议二人同唱，遂有琴言自东、宝珠自西同演《寻梦》一段奇景：一个如华月初升、春云冉冉，一个如娇花欲放、晓露犹含，两声相和，如碧树间么凤雏凰。华公子拍案叫绝，说从此才信人间真有绝色，悔从前把嫫母无盐也置之金屋；又评二人"五雀六燕，轻重适均，离之则独绝，合之则两全"。宝珠为公子赏识而益加精神，琴言心里却不愿，只因惹不起，又见华公子眉目略似子玉，索性把他权当作子玉在楼上，照正月那日的情景重演一回。林珊枝眼看要关城，又暗含醋意，几番以目示意，子云会意劝驾。

华公子临行执琴言之手，赞他"可夸京城独步"，解下汉玉双龙佩相赠，又请子云许可，另以一佩赏宝珠。客散后子云命众人换了便衣，廊前四盏素灯下重开一席。众人皆劝琴言领情：文泽说星北之倾倒不下于子玉，次贤说外间传闻不可信，不宜太冷淡。琴言越想越气——度香明知他与子玉相好，竟也说这等话——竟淌下泪来。张仲雨醉后冷笑，说难道赞也赞坏了，人家费尽心力还巴结不到这一赞；琴言忍不住顶了回去：我不会巴结人，也不像那自己巴结了还要教人巴结的。一句话说得仲雨变色，只碍着子云暂忍。子云托词他醉了，命宝珠携他入内；宝珠取出两块玉佩比了比，自己的略小一些。这一句硬话，日后却生出无数苦况来。`,en:`On the sixth of the sixth month, the lotus's birthday, Yiyuan entertains Hua Guangsu with the new plays. Liu Wenze, back from Baoding by forced stages since his father Vice-Minister Liu rose to Minister of Rites, arrives first, in the early cool. Here the garden is set out in full: three or four li round, its small hills piled of Taihu stone and its great hills earth-footed and crowned with peaks, screened with flowering trees and set off by pavilions; in a grotto a hidden well-sweep throws water upward into a flying cascade; the pond draws from the outer river and winds wide or narrow as the ground allows. Within are an archery ground, a ball court, a fishing hamlet, rice cottages, a wine shop and a tea booth, all kept by the gardeners so that the staff need never idle away outside — Xu Ziyun's own idea. Of the twenty-four courtyards, a serious visitor would need eight or nine days to see them all. At the centre stands the Hanwan Tower, named for containing all things and transmuting them into light; east of the weeping willows lies the Yinqiu Water Pavilion, its pond one sheet of lotus. Ziyun explains why the company is thin: Mei Ziyu has relapsed, Shi Nanxiang has vomited blood after drinking, Tian Chunhang will not come because he has never met Hua, Wang Xun is at home waiting on his wife's confinement, Yan Zhongqing withdrew when the rest did, and Gao Pin is not yet back — leaving only Zhang Zhongyu and Xiao Cixian. Zhongyu appears in the sixth grade, his rank freshly bought at a total of four thousand, five hundred of it advanced by Pan Qiguan of the Qisheng silver bureau. Wenze predicts he will reap both fame and profit; Ziyun adds that his superiors will certainly be pleased and only the people will suffer; Zhongyu cheerfully agrees, since an office bought with capital must be made to pay. Hua arrives in a chair with twenty and more attendants — the two fathers were comrades in the same campaign, and Ziyun, five years the elder, is treated as a brother. 

Ten young leads present themselves with ivory tablets, and of them all Hua fixes on Du Qinyan, remarking that his bearing is not yet practised but that he is elegant without and clever within, with an air from beyond the dust. The programme opens with *Li Ling Returns to Han* and *Bright Consort Enters the Pass*, then *The Envoy Receives the Edict*, which conjures up the very soul of Li the Banished Immortal. The second piece, *Consort Yang Enters Shu*, executes Yang Guozhong, rouses the six armies, and carries the emperor to Mount Emei, where he plays the jade flute himself while the consort sings *Clear Peace* and a palace lady dances the *Whirling Wind*; then eight female immortals ride the wind down from a scaffolded Emei wreathed in ambergris smoke, until the whole stage seems lodged in coloured cloud. Hua's applause never stops. He rewards eighty strings of cash and has Lin Shanzhi bring out a tray of gold ingots shaped like plum blossoms, ruyi sceptres and water-caltrops to divide among the ten; his own boys' troupe then comes up to perform *Immortals' Grand Assembly*. In the late afternoon the party moves to the Yinqiu Water Pavilion, where the ten, dressed as lotus-gatherers, pole among the flowers singing the *Lotus-Picking Song*, a sound like midnight airs, a sight like the Lady of the Xiang at play. Still unsatisfied, Hua calls for the *Golden Thread* to be sung again and the *Nishang* danced again, so that each may show his art. Ziyun observes that the incomparable pieces are Su Huifang and Lu Sulan's *Exclusive Possession* and Yuan Baozhu and Qinyan's *Waking from a Dream* and *Pursuing the Dream* — a pity they overlap. Wenze proposes the two sing together, and so comes the marvel of the doubled *Pursuing the Dream*: Qinyan entering from the east like a bright moon newly risen, his steps drifting spring cloud; Baozhu from the west like a flower on the point of opening with dawn dew still in it, his steps slender willows swaying; the two voices answering like young phoenixes among jade trees. Hua strikes the table and cries that only now does he believe in perfect beauty, repenting that he ever housed plain women in embroidered rooms; and he judges them evenly matched, each unrivalled alone and each completed by the other. Baozhu, warmed by such notice, performs the better for it; Qinyan is unwilling but dares not offend, and, seeing something of Ziyu in Hua's face, resolves to pretend that Ziyu himself sits in the gallery and to sing as he sang on that day in the first month. 

Lin Shanzhi, watching the gates and privately jealous, signals until Ziyun takes the hint and speeds his guest. At parting Hua takes Qinyan's hand, calls his singing unmatched in the capital, and tears off a Han-jade double-dragon pendant for him, then asks Ziyun's leave to give another to Baozhu. When the guests have gone the company changes into plain clothes and sits again under four glass lamps on the verandah. All of them press Qinyan to feel honoured: Wenze says Xingbei's admiration is no less than Ziyu's, Cixian that the rumours about Hua are unreliable and Qinyan should not be so cold. Qinyan grows angrier the longer he thinks — that Xu Ziyun, of all people, knowing what binds him to Ziyu, should talk so. His tears fall. Zhang Zhongyu, drunk, sneers that praise can hardly spoil a man and that others toil for years without earning such praise; and Qinyan answers that he does not want praise and cannot fawn, being unlike those who fawn themselves and then teach others to fawn. Zhongyu changes colour and swallows it only out of deference to his host. Ziyun covers the moment by calling Qinyan drunk and sending Baozhu in with him; inside, Baozhu holds the two pendants side by side and finds his own the smaller. That one hard sentence will breed sorrows without number.`},26:{zh:`华光宿掌灯后才回府，向苏浣香略说了怡园光景，便到星栊卧室来。十个"珠"字婢一色白罗衫、麻姑髻、素馨花，因随主母到家庙进香才改的装：明珠捧上冰梅汤，掌珠拿了鹅毛扇轻轻打着，荷珠替他换了轻纱衫。他细开一张消暑粥的方子——盛暑是发散时候，防风露、薄荷露少用，玫瑰、香稻、荷花、桂花诸露多加，茯苓、莲子、琼糜、燕窝诸粉各依分两，文武火慢熬，一时二刻不得见铜器，还须亲手做，不许经老婆子的手。廊下铜炉银吊慢熬着粥，婢子们笑闹推挤，说到秋天"收房"的话，滚作一团。爱珠提绛纱灯去传林珊枝。公子要遣人请魏聘才到留青精舍说话，珊枝知道不过是看戏闲谈，便以园门已锁、开三重门须到二更为辞挡了回去。公子却问起：那些相公为何总不进府？珊枝答，朔望原是清早就来的，门上只说爷未起身便挡了驾，班子里请安也不挂号簿。

又问琴言是谁的徒弟，答是曹长庆的——正是珊枝旧日的师傅，当年因性气不合，被长庆挑换了登春班的绣芳，绣芳出师得了八千吊，长庆才买下琴言。公子再问那出《寻梦》谁强，珊枝答宝珠唱得熟，琴言初次尚生，第二次略好；公子却说论相貌还算琴言，身上像有仙骨。掌珠只顾听着，一扇子脱手落地，明珠嗤笑，公子便说改日叫那班人进来唱一天，还要十婢扮生与他们合串。次日公子直睡到巳正才起。他在华夫人房中用饭，说八龄班万不及人家一分，又说夫人的相貌像苏蕙芳，夫人听了不悦。书中于此点明：这一对夫妻本是琴瑟相和，公子好奢而不淫；夫人几次要替他收房，他反说一作了妾便无趣，不如伺候几年配个青年美貌的，是件功德；又说夫人如牡丹，杂花种到牡丹台上，不是效颦邻女，便是婢学夫人——所以任他繁华，夫人绝不生疑。这一顿饭直谈到午后，公子又在夫人床上睡着，竟把魏聘才忘在留青精舍。

聘才奉召之喜如金殿传胪，一路对林珊枝老珊长、老弟短，进门只顾细看那镂空嵌宝的窗棂屏门，却足足饿等了两个时辰，茶把肠子洗得精空，肚里咕噜如饿鸱叫；珊枝在洗红轩外等得也饿，还是明珠让了些残馔，爱珠故意刁难不肯通传，末了荷珠进去回了。公子出来赔了不是，命捧上八样点心，一面说自己费心买的八个伶人本想压倒外边，如今反被外边压倒。聘才立刻迎合：好花不在多，拣顶好的买几个便是；心里却另有一本账——琴言心上只有梅子玉，一旦进了府，此生休想再见；他那古怪脾气，长久必被厌弃，正好弄他进来受两年苦，以出胸中恶气。公子碍着徐子云，说这两个都是度香所爱；聘才便说度香真爱的是袁宝珠，琴言不过是幌子，他师傅长庆最爱钱，一说必肯。公子终被说动，只叮嘱"可行则行"，又不甘落在度香之后。

次日聘才带了四儿出城，邀上叶茂林同去，路上遇见张仲雨，附耳说了许多，聘才只点头道"进来了还由得他"。到长庆家，长庆先淡淡相待，一听是华府师爷便满面添花，开出烟灯请他躺下，夸琴言一面之赏便得十个金锞、并公子的玉佩，值七百吊、二百吊不等。及至说破来意，长庆便哭穷：孩子去年十月才买来，一教就会，才十五岁，出师还有五年，一家二十余口全靠他一人。叶茂林劝以华府之势与日后执事顶戴，聘才劝以少年发身难保，转眼便是白丢；长庆只推要问琴言愿否。聘才索性揭他的短：除了徐子云谁喜欢他？他为梅少爷病成那样，人人厌他，若指望靠梅少爷发迹，除非公鸡生蛋。一句正戳中长庆的心病。及至开价，长庆算准华公子是出名的冤大头，暗想五六千两总讲得出口；聘才摇头道男孩子哪值上万，四五千已尽。长庆摇头装哭，聘才便勃然作色，说等你去发大财，过几天自己送上门来时便一钱不值，拂袖上车，只低声约叶茂林在六合馆相候。长庆终舍不得这棵摇钱树，说要问过徒弟，两日后再回话。聘才无可奈何，只得回府托林珊枝转禀，说没有找着长庆，改日再去。`,en:`Hua Guangsu reaches home well after lamp-lighting, gives Su Huanxiang a brief account of Yiyuan, and goes through to the Star-Latticed Chamber. The ten pearl-named maids stand in matching white silk, hair in Magu knots with a ball of jasmine at the front, having changed dress to follow their mistress to the family shrine. Mingzhu brings iced plum broth, Zhangzhu plies a goose-feather fan, Hezhu changes him into gauze. He dictates a recipe for a cooling porridge with fastidious precision — high summer being a dispersing season, less mint and fangfeng dew, more rose, fragrant-rice, lotus and cassia; poria, lotus-seed and bird's-nest powders by weight; slow fire; no bronze vessel may touch it for an hour and a quarter; and it must be made by her own hands, not passed through the old women's. Under the eaves a silver pot simmers over a brazier while the maids tease and tussle over which of them will be taken into the bedchamber come autumn. Aizhu carries a crimson lantern to summon Lin Shanzhi. Hua wants Wei Pincai fetched to the Liuqing Study; Shanzhi, guessing it is only theatre gossip, objects that the gates are locked and three of them would have to be opened, and the errand is dropped. Hua asks instead why the actors never come to his mansion. 

On the first and fifteenth they do come early, says Shanzhi, but the gatemen turn them away saying the master has not risen, and troupe visits are never entered in the register. Whose disciple is Qinyan? Cao Changqing's — Shanzhi's own former master, who once traded him to the Dengchun troupe for Xiufang after a quarrel, and who bought Qinyan only after Xiufang graduated for eight thousand strings. Which of the two was better in *Pursuing the Dream*? Yuan Baozhu sings with more practice, Shanzhi answers; Qinyan was raw the first time and better the second. But in looks, says Hua, Qinyan has the advantage — the boy seems to have immortal bones in him. Zhangzhu, listening too hard, lets the fan slip out of her hand; Mingzhu snorts; and Hua promises to have the troupe in one day and make his ten maids play the male roles opposite them. He sleeps late, and takes breakfast in Madam Hua's room, telling her that his own boys' troupe is not worth a tenth of theirs, and that she resembles Su Huifang — which she does not enjoy hearing. Here the novel pauses on the marriage: the two are genuinely devoted, and Hua, extravagant as he is, is not lecherous. Several times his wife has offered to let him take a maid as concubine, and he has refused, saying a maid once made concubine loses all her charm, and it is a better deed to keep them a few years and then marry them to handsome young men; besides, were his wife plain he might look elsewhere, but as she is, any concubine set beside her would be a common flower planted on a peony terrace. So however lavish he grows, she never suspects him. 

The meal runs past noon, Hua falls asleep on her bed, and Wei Pincai is left forgotten in the Liuqing Study. Summoned to breakfast, Pincai had been as elated as a man called up in the Golden Hall, flattering Lin Shanzhi the whole way; now he sits two full hours among the openwork carved windows and jewel-inlaid screens, tea washing his stomach empty until it growls like a hungry owl. Shanzhi, waiting outside the Xihong Pavilion, is fed leftovers by Mingzhu, while Aizhu refuses to carry word in and Hezhu at last does. Hua comes out apologizing, orders eight kinds of pastry, and confesses that the eight singers he bought to crush all rivals have themselves been crushed. Pincai instantly agrees that fine flowers need not be many, only the finest — while privately reckoning that Qinyan's heart holds nothing but Mei Ziyu, that once inside these walls he will never see him again, that his strange temper must in time disgust his new owner, and that two years of such misery would settle the grudge nicely. Hua objects that both boys are Xu Ziyun's favourites; Pincai answers that Ziyun's true favourite is Yuan Baozhu, that Qinyan is only a screen, and that his master Changqing loves money above everything and will jump at the offer. 

Hua yields, saying only that it should be tried and dropped if refused — and grumbling that even to buy him now is to come a move behind Duxiang. Next morning Pincai takes Si'er out of the city, collects Ye Maolin, and meets Zhang Zhongyu on the road, who whispers a great deal in his ear; Pincai only nods and says that once the boy is inside, the matter will hardly rest with him. Changqing receives them coolly until he hears that this is the Hua mansion's Master Wei, whereupon he blossoms, opens the opium lamp, and boasts that a single meeting earned Qinyan ten gold ingots and the young lord's own jade pendant, worth seven hundred strings and two hundred more. When the purpose is named he pleads poverty: the boy was bought only last October, learns everything at a touch, is barely fifteen with five years to graduation, and twenty-odd mouths depend on him. Ye Maolin urges the weight of the Hua name and the prospect of a post and a button; Pincai urges that boys of fifteen may spoil within a year and leave the investment worthless. Changqing keeps saying he must ask the boy. So Pincai strikes at the sore place: apart from Xu Ziyun, who likes Qinyan? He fell ill for Young Master Mei, everyone is sick of him, and if he means to rise through Young Master Mei, roosters will lay eggs. Changqing, whose complaints of temper were only bargaining, has nothing to answer. At the price he reckons Hua a famous soft touch and hints at five or six thousand taels; Pincai shakes his head — no boy is worth ten thousand, four or five is the limit. Changqing shakes his head and pretends to weep; Pincai flares up, tells him to go and get rich and warns that in a few days he will bring the boy to the door himself and find him worth nothing, then sweeps out to his carriage, murmuring to Ye Maolin to meet him at the Liuhe Restaurant. Changqing, unable to part with his money tree, will only promise an answer in two days. Pincai, out of moves, goes back and has Lin Shanzhi report that Changqing could not be found and that he will call again another day.`},27:{zh:`魏聘才碰了软钉子回来，盘算再唆使那车夫、厨子上门吵闹，只须隔几天下手，免得看出破绽。笔锋随即转到奚十一：他在长芦盐务里躲了一月，恰逢洋船到岸，汇了两万银子并五千货物，重新阔起来；木桶事已冷，便回京照旧胡闹。他官名奚正绅，因岭南口音"十一"近"土"，人又叫他奚老土。相与最密的是张仲雨与潘其观：仲雨在帐局子里迎逢，早探明奚家底细，及奚十一告贷时替他向潘其观银号借了一万，九扣之外又私扣一千，奚十一到手只八千。作者比较二恶：潘其观虽丑，却胆小、惧内、又怕被讹，心淫而胆怯；奚十一恃财恃势，无法无天，人号"烟熏太岁"，且自忖在京不过一年半载，不闹个淋漓尽致便不像个公子。他新聘的刑钱朋友，正是第二十三回那位坐粪车的姬亮轩，由孙亮功转托王文辉荐来，年修一千二百两；姬亮轩几次把巴英官送与东家，英官伺候过大老爷，反在师爷面前骄纵起来。

一日张仲雨在奚寓早饭，蓉官、春林侑酒，奚十一一饭之间两次入内，众人挤眉弄眼。蓉官问起一个姓魏的如今何等体面，仲雨说他在华公府当师爷发了财；姬亮轩问华府是否除皇帝就数他家，仲雨说单庄头就有四十几个，一年租课抵得一府钱漕。谈锋一转说到秋水堂的杜琴言，蓉官、春林齐说他架子大：终席不言，不斟酒，不请安，赏他东西也不谢，连袁宝珠、苏蕙芳都没有这样。奚十一夸口必要一试，仲雨冷笑道：别说你奚老土，就是徐子云、华光宿两位大公子，也常受他的气，你连他的屋都进不去。这边琴言自怡园回来日日啼哭，一恐将来进府应酬不来，二恨每逢热闹独不见梅子玉；曹长庆进来说破聘才买人之意，琴言放声大哭，说师傅若不要他，他也不到别处去，只求一死了事。长庆出门前请了陆素兰来陪他。

素兰这一番剖白，是全书写"情"最透彻的一段：他先说华公子未见得坏，进去混几年未必无益，只苦在见不着子玉；继而分作四层——子玉年少，事发必受家教之责，怨且归你；你心里有他，脸上却不可教人看破；你屡屡冲撞徐子云，久而久之他必心冷，那时师傅要在你身上讨还，比进华府更苦；到那步田地，子玉纵有泪也无力，你身在外如驴儿推磨，心在内如道士炼丹，才真要死了。琴言答以"重情而略事，行吾心之所安"，说到极处不过捐生以报；素兰便正色驳他：你死了不算忠臣烈士节妇义夫，明白人说你情痴，糊涂人说你呆子；子玉上有父母、下有功名子嗣，痛过之后终究要说一句镜花水月到眼皆空，只留你在阴司孤孤单单，那时还唱得成《阳告》么？一席话说得琴言破涕而笑。琴言又抱怨袁宝珠：若正月初六那出《惊梦》是他去唱，自己便不上台；前日的《寻梦》又偏要合演，他不肯让，自己也不敢输，才招出这场祸来。正吃着对门王兰保送来的西瓜，前面已闹翻了天。原来奚十一带着姬亮轩、春兰、巴英官坐车来访，被回说不在家，先怀三分怒；长庆权衡再三，不肯拿琴言应酬，却把大帽子压他，只说华公子已把人叫进府唱戏，又殷勤替他脱衫、开烟灯，才把火气按下；随后竟叫回天福、天寿两个师弟去陪烟。春兰、巴英官看不入眼，四处闲逛，从半掩的小门进去，隔着绿纱窗缝，看见床上坐着两个绝色的人。

回去一说，奚十一如火烧心，掀翻众人直闯进内，撞倒长庆，踢开锁着的房门，扯下帐子、掀翻桌子，又揪住长庆连打五个嘴巴，扬言明日再来搜。琴言与素兰早从后门逃到王兰保家。事后众人聚议：聘才之怨未解，又添一个奚十一。王兰保献出一条妙计——明日就送琴言进华府，一文身价不要，只说孩子不懂规矩，权且进去伺候两月，好不好再说；将来有事仍可告假；华公子见他不能在外唱戏，必有厚赏，那时师傅有财有势，闲人再不敢上门；若先争身价，人便死死活活都是府里的了。素兰拍手叫妙，长庆心服，琴言至此亦无可奈何。当晚琴言移住素兰家；次日奚十一果然又来，遍搜不见，恶狠狠而去。`,en:`Rebuffed at Changqing's, Wei Pincai goes home calculating that another visit from the carriage driver and the kitchen boy, delayed a few days so the hand behind it will not show, should settle the matter. The narrative then turns to Xi Shiyi in person. A month in hiding at the Changlu salt administration ended when a fleet of foreign ships came in; his family being foreign traders, he remitted twenty thousand taels and five thousand in goods and is rich again, and with the barrel scandal grown cold he returns to the capital and his old habits. His formal name is Xi Zhengshen, but since in his southern speech "the Eleventh" sounds like the word for earth, people call him Xi the Earth. His two intimates are Zhang Zhongyu and Pan Qiguan. Zhongyu, who haunts counting-houses, had taken his measure early, and when Xi ran short arranged a loan of ten thousand from Pan Qiguan's bureau — discounted a tenth, with another thousand skimmed off for himself, so that Xi received eight. The narrator weighs the two villains: Pan, repulsive as he is, is timid, hen-pecked, and afraid of being fleeced, so his lechery is all appetite and no daring; Xi, armoured in money and rank, is lawless, nicknamed the Smoke-Blackened Grand Duke, and reasons that since his stay in the capital will be short, anything less than utter debauchery would make him look like no gentleman at all. His newly engaged legal secretary is none other than the night-soil-cart philosopher of chapter twenty-three, Ji Liangxuan, recommended through Sun Lianggong and Wang Wenhui at twelve hundred taels a year; Ji has lent his page Ba Yingguan to his employer more than once, and the boy, having served the master, now gives himself airs before the secretary. 

One morning Zhang Zhongyu breakfasts there with Rongguan and Chunlin to pour wine, while Xi twice retires within during a single meal and the company exchange glances. Rongguan asks about a certain Wei now grown so magnificent; Zhongyu explains that he is secretary in the Hua mansion and has made money. Ji asks whether the Hua house really ranks next after the emperor; Zhongyu answers that its forty-odd estate stewards bring in more rent in a year than a whole prefecture's tax. The talk turns to Du Qinyan at Autumn Water Hall, and both actors complain of his airs: silent through a whole banquet, never pouring wine, never bowing, not even thanking a giver — not Yuan Baozhu nor Su Huifang is half so proud. Xi boasts he will try him, and Zhongyu answers coldly that even Xu Ziyun and Hua Guangsu, great gentlemen both, put up with the boy's temper, and that Xi would not get inside his door. Meanwhile Qinyan has wept every day since Yiyuan, dreading the summons into the mansion and grieving that Mei Ziyu is never at any of the gatherings. When Cao Changqing comes in and explains Pincai's offer, he cries aloud that if his master no longer wants him he will not go elsewhere but simply cease to be. Changqing goes out, sending for Lu Sulan to sit with him. 

Sulan's argument is the book's clearest reasoning on feeling. Hua, he says, is not obviously bad, and years there might do no harm but for the loss of Ziyu; then he lays out four consequences — that Ziyu is young, and if the matter comes out he will be disciplined and Qinyan blamed; that Qinyan may keep his heart for Ziyu but must not let his face declare it; that his repeated coldness will in time chill Xu Ziyun, whose support alone keeps his master from squeezing him, and that the squeezing would be worse than the Hua mansion; and that when it came to that, Ziyu could offer nothing but tears, leaving Qinyan's body outside like a donkey at the mill and his heart within like a Daoist over his crucible. Qinyan replies that he weighs present feeling above future event, acts as his heart finds peace, and at the last will give his life to complete it — whereupon Sulan turns grave. Dying would make him no loyal minister, no martyr, no chaste wife; the clear-sighted would call him a love-fool and the muddled a simpleton; and Ziyu, who has parents to repay, a career to make, and no heir yet, would grieve and then conclude that all of it was flowers in a mirror and moonlight on water, leaving Qinyan alone in the shades with no one to hear him — could he sing *The Piteous Appeal* down there? At this Qinyan laughs through his tears. He also blames Yuan Baozhu: had Baozhu sung *Waking from a Dream* that sixth day of the first month, he would never have gone on stage at all; and the recent disaster came of the doubled *Pursuing the Dream*, where neither would yield and so he was forced to excel. They are eating watermelons sent over by Wang Lanbao from across the lane when uproar breaks out in front. Xi Shiyi has arrived with Ji Liangxuan, Chunlan, and Ba Yingguan, been told the boy is out, and taken offence. 

Changqing, unwilling to offer Qinyan, plays the great card instead — the young lord has already taken the troupe into the Hua mansion to sing — and pacifies him with the opium lamp and his own hands at his gown, then produces his other two disciples, Tianfu and Tianshou, to attend him. Chunlan and Yingguan, disgusted by what follows, wander into the inner court, push open a half-shut gate, and through a crack in the green gauze see two exquisite figures seated on a bed. Their report sets Xi ablaze. He throws off the boys who try to hold him, charges inward, knocks Changqing sprawling, kicks in the locked door, tears down the bed curtains and overturns the table, then seizes Changqing and slaps him five times, swearing to come back and search the place tomorrow. Qinyan and Sulan have already fled by the back gate to Wang Lanbao's. In council afterwards, with Pincai's grudge unsettled and Xi Shiyi now added, Wang Lanbao proposes the one workable plan: deliver Qinyan to the Hua mansion tomorrow, refuse every penny of purchase money, and say only that the boy does not know the rules and may serve two months on trial; leave can still be asked for him afterwards, and Hua, seeing that he can no longer sing outside, will certainly be generous, so that Changqing will have both money and protection and no ruffian will dare come to the door — whereas to bargain a price is to sell him body and soul. Sulan claps his hands, Changqing is convinced, and Qinyan, after this day, has nothing left to say. That night he moves to Sulan's house; the next day Xi Shiyi comes back as promised, ransacks the place, finds nothing, and departs snarling.`},28:{zh:`曹长庆挨了打，只得同叶茂林到华府向魏聘才负荆请罪，愿即日送人，分文身价不要。聘才自以为前番发怒果然奏效，托林珊枝转禀。华光宿问何以不要身价，聘才答说他怕孩子不懂规矩，日后有错还可领回；华公子点头应允，却声明既进了府，无论领价与否，外面一概不准陪酒唱戏，又命账房每月给长庆二百两。杜琴言至此自知避无可避。他想见梅子玉一面，又怕相见徒增断肠、耳目又多；欲写信，则方寸已乱，万语千言无从下笔，末了取出平日拭泪的那方罗帕，另包四味药裹在里面，托陆素兰面交。

次日入府，华公子细细打量，十分满意，即拨在留青精舍；又领他到苏浣香处叩见，夫人见他弱质婷婷、毫无优伶习气，也说了个"好"字。琴言暗中洒泪，过了几日见公子行事尚正经，才略觉放心；独对魏聘才只作息夫人不言之状。子玉一日到素兰处，遇见王兰保与袁琪官在座。素兰拿出那个无字纸包，帕中滚出四味药来，子玉先疑是诀别之物，几乎哭倒；素兰忙说玉侬无恙，把聘才买人、奚十一大闹、众人劝驾、连夜避祸、送进华府的始末细细说了。四味药是牵牛、独活、芍药、防己，还是琪官的跟人认得——他曾在药铺当过伙计。素兰解"独活"为离你而独居，兰保解"防己"为自守其身，子玉却把整个哑谜读通了：芍药一名将离，是说已经离了；既离便只有独活；独活于高墙之内，自然刻刻小心、守身如玉；至于牵牛，别无他解，必是约他七月七日牛女相会之期见一面。素兰拍手称是，约他七夕来住一天。琪官初见子玉，慕其丰标，因说魏聘才当日在船上如何下作，又咬牙骂起奚十一来。子玉回家便觉四肢不舒，次日竟大病：语言无次，一日数哭，梦中或唤玉侬，或唤香畹，或痛骂奚十一、魏聘才。

颜夫人只当他中了邪，延医求签许愿，十余日毫无起色；颜仲清、王恂常来探视，心里明白却无法可施。夫人疑"玉侬"是个女子，唤云儿细问，云儿不肯招，直到取了板子才跪下说实话：少爷所往皆是那小旦琴言处，见面多是哭；五月里说往怡园，其实是同两个小旦游运河；前日在那小旦家得了一个纸包，回来就成了这个样子。夫人又问李元茂，元茂只说去年常听魏聘才夸赞琴言，正月初六会馆团拜看戏之后便留心起来，余事须问魏聘才。于是差许顺往华府相请。聘才见夫人愁容，先说了些江西家信、场期高中的套话，及听说病源，一时良心发动，脸上竟露愧色；随即改口自表功劳：他正因世兄为一个小旦荒废诗书，才怂慂华公子买他，如今人已入府，从此可以杜绝痴心。夫人反倒谢他，又求他设法叫琴言出来一见。聘才便层层作难：那师傅在钱眼里过日子，一年二千四百银的进项，非钱不行。

夫人心痛儿子，许了二百两。聘才回府却另有一副面孔：他知华公子的脾气断不能明说，只死缠林珊枝，许他两匹新花样库纱。珊枝原有自己的心事——琴言进来半月，连华夫人都疼他，常赏东西，说这孩子老实不像唱戏的；珊枝进府三年，恐怕被他压下去，正想设法挤他，因此一口应承，只说是自己作主，不许提聘才。次日恰值华公子身子不爽，在夫人房里不出来。琴言听说子玉病重，眼泪汪汪，随聘才重门叠户地出来，坐在放下帘子的车里，四儿坐车尾。到梅宅径入书房，许顺诧异而不敢拦。颜夫人一见琴言便吃一惊：玉骨冰肌，华光丽质，梳了头便是千金小姐，竟像未过门的媳妇王琼华，本来一肚子怒气，不觉化作笑容。琴言战战兢兢磕头，如实供说：苏州人，父母双亡，被族中叔母卖出，去年冬天到京，在联锦班；正月初六姑苏会馆唱戏，第一次见着少爷，就像从前认识一般；元宵在怡园看灯，最爱那"落花人独立，微雨燕双飞"的灯谜，徐子云以一张瑶琴为彩，说谁猜着便请谁相见，恰是少爷猜着；隔两日请他吃酒，才算相识；第二次便是素兰邀游运河——半年之内统共两面。夫人听得称奇，说既如此，何以他心心念念只是你？琴言道，不但小的觉得像旧识，少爷见了也这样说，彼此都说不出缘故。夫人反生怜意，命他进房去劝慰，自与聘才坐在外间静听，许顺也跟到卧房外细听。`,en:`Beaten and out of choices, Cao Changqing goes with Ye Maolin to Wei Pincai's study in the Hua mansion to beg pardon, offering to deliver the boy at once and take not a penny. Pincai, certain his own display of temper produced this, sends word through Lin Shanzhi. Hua Guangsu asks why no price is wanted; because, says Pincai, the master fears the boy does not know the rules and wishes to be able to take him back. Hua consents, but rules that once inside these walls, bought or not, Qinyan may never again pour wine or sing outside, and orders the counting-house to pay Changqing two hundred taels a month. Du Qinyan knows there is nowhere left to hide. He longs to see Mei Ziyu once, yet fears the pain of it and the many watching eyes; he tries to write and finds his mind in pieces and ten thousand words refusing the brush. 

At last he takes the silk handkerchief he has so often dried his tears with, wraps four medicines inside it, and gives it to Lu Sulan to deliver. Next day he enters the mansion; Hua studies him with great satisfaction and installs him in the Liuqing Study, then brings him to Su Huanxiang, who, seeing a slight and graceful figure without a trace of actorly manner, says one word: "Good." Qinyan weeps in private, and after some days, finding his new master's conduct correct enough, is a little reassured — though before Wei Pincai he keeps the silence of Lady Xi. Ziyu, calling at Sulan's, finds Wang Lanbao and Yuan Qiguan there. Sulan brings out the unlabelled parcel; the handkerchief falls open and four medicines roll out. Ziyu, taking them for a dying man's keepsake, nearly collapses, and Sulan hurriedly assures him that Yunong is alive, then tells the whole story: Pincai's offer, Xi Shiyi's rampage, the council of friends, the flight by night, the delivery to the Hua mansion. The drugs are morning glory, angelica, peony and stephania, identified by Qiguan's servant, who once worked in a pharmacy. Sulan reads "angelica" — literally living alone — as separation; Lanbao reads "stephania," which is written as guarding oneself, as a promise of chastity; but Ziyu construes the whole rebus: the peony's other name means about to part, so the parting is done; parted, he can only live alone; living alone behind those walls, he will guard himself like jade; and the morning glory, the Herdboy star, can only mean the seventh night of the seventh month, when Herdboy and Weaving Maid meet. Sulan claps and engages him to stay the night of the festival. Qiguan, meeting Ziyu for the first time, admires him greatly, and tells him how vilely Pincai behaved on the boat north, then curses Xi Shiyi through his teeth. Ziyu goes home aching in every limb and by the next day is gravely ill — speech disordered, weeping several times a day, calling in his sleep for Yunong, for Xiangwan, and cursing Xi Shiyi and Wei Pincai. Madam Yan, supposing him bewitched, tries physicians, divination and vows for ten days together with no effect; Yan Zhongqing and Wang Xun visit often, understand perfectly, and can do nothing. Suspecting "Yunong" to be a woman, she questions Yun'er, who denies everything until the rod is fetched and then kneels and tells all: that his master's outings were to the young actor Qinyan, that their meetings were mostly weeping, that the errand to Yiyuan in the fifth month was really a day on the canal, and that a paper parcel received at the actor's house had brought on this state. 

Li Yuanmao adds only that Pincai used to praise Qinyan, and that Ziyu grew attentive after the guild-hall gathering on the sixth of the first month. So Xu Shun is sent to fetch Pincai. Confronted with the mother's grief, Pincai's conscience stirs and shame shows in his face; then he recovers and claims the credit — seeing his friend ruining health and studies over an actor, he had urged Hua Guangsu to buy the boy, and now that the boy is immured the infatuation can die. Madam Yan thanks him, and begs him to contrive a visit. Pincai piles up difficulties: the master lives inside the eye of a coin and draws two thousand four hundred a year; nothing moves without money. Aching for her son, she promises two hundred taels. Back at the mansion he wears another face entirely. Knowing his master's temper makes an open request impossible, he works on Lin Shanzhi instead, promising two bolts of new-patterned silk. Shanzhi has his own reason: in half a month Qinyan has been petted even by Madam Hua, who gives him presents and calls him honest and unlike an actor, and Shanzhi, three years in the house, means to squeeze out a rival. 

So he agrees, on condition that the responsibility be called his own and Pincai's name never mentioned. The next morning Hua is unwell and keeps to his wife's room. Hearing that Ziyu is dangerously ill, Qinyan follows Pincai out through gate after gate with tears standing in his eyes, riding inside the carriage with the blind down and Si'er at the back. At the Mei house Pincai walks straight to the study; Xu Shun, astonished, dares not stop them. At first sight of Qinyan, Madam Yan is startled: jade bones and ice flesh, a bearing so noble that dressed as a woman he would pass for a great house's daughter — indeed he resembles her son's betrothed, Wang Qionghua — and her stored anger turns unaccountably into a smile. Trembling, Qinyan kowtows and answers truthfully: a Suzhou boy, both parents dead, sold off by a clan aunt, come to the capital last winter with the Lianjin troupe. On the sixth of the first month he sang at the Suzhou Guild Hall and saw the young master for the first time, and it was as though they had known each other before. At the Lantern Festival he went to Yiyuan to see the riddles, and loved best the one on the couplet about a man standing alone amid falling blossom and swallows flying in pairs through fine rain; Xu Ziyun set a jade qin as its prize and promised to introduce whoever solved it, and the solver was the young master; two days later he was invited to drink and Qinyan called to attend. That was their meeting; the second was Sulan's day on the canal. Twice in half a year, and no more. Madam Yan wonders aloud that so little should breed so much, and Qinyan answers that it was not only he who felt they had met before — the young master said the same, and neither of them could explain it. Pity replaces suspicion. She sends him in to console her son, and sits outside with Pincai to listen; Xu Shun, who has understood the case only now, follows to the bedroom door to listen too.`},29:{zh:`杜琴言进梅宅时满拟要受一场凌辱，不料颜夫人非但不加呵叱，反有怜恤之意，还命他去劝慰。到卧房时但见帘帏不卷、几案生尘、药鼎烟浓、香炉灰烬，楠木小床垂着白绡帐。梅子玉半睡半醒，口中先念《长恨歌》的"七月七日长生殿，夜半无人私语时"——原来他梦中已是七夕，正与琴言在素兰处煮茗谈心；继而叹"黄泉碧落两难寻"，又惦记大雨与相隔甚远的船；末了竟说出一句"独活、防己之下，应须添一味当归"，把那个哑谜答了回去。颜夫人隔帘听着，两泪交流。琴言泪落满他一脸，几次低唤，子玉却冷笑道"你已试过了我一回，难道我还认不得你"，只当又是一个假琴言。魏聘才叫云儿绞了热手巾替他净面，扶起靠着叠好的锦被；颜夫人不肯进房，怕儿子愧惧。琴言把身子挪向亮处，侧脸相对，子玉这才心头一跳，问了又问，直到听见"真真是真的"，忽然狂笑一声扑在他肩上，笑个不住，满屋的人都笑了。琴言为治他的心病，破例说了些谎：说华公子极正经，府中上下待他都好，不用唱戏，不过在留青精舍当个伺候书房的书童；说太太已准他常来，还说等老爷回来要商量买他进来服侍少爷。子玉素知他不说谎，听了便四平八稳，欢喜得手舞足蹈，跨下床走了两步，说自己不过受了些暑气，又叫云儿拿东西来吃。

琴言临别再三叮嘱，子玉也劝他从此与聘才和好，又嘱他见太太时说话须留意。颜夫人在外看得又惊又叹：这一对小人儿一个哭一个笑，并没说什么，若不是亲眼所见，任谁都要胡猜。她命许顺秤了二百两银子交与聘才——那正是聘才向她讹来的谎账，回府便锁进钱柜；又赏琴言银镶小刀、大小荷包、罗帕、洋表、梅花小锭与金面牙扇。回府时门上大簿子已记了一笔。林珊枝先吓唬他们，说公子传了两三回不见人，已差人去长庆家查问；待琴言急得落泪、聘才连连作揖，才笑说早已打点停当，叫那人城外逛一逛回来复命就是。随即又勒索起来：说那两匹库纱花样不好，他新近在沙回子处看中一个二两重的金丝花篮和一把嵌翡翠盖的时大彬宜兴壶——那壶倒茶不滴一点，不放茶叶冲下开水也是淡绿好茶——请聘才买来奉送，连门簿上那一笔也求他抹去。到了留青精舍，珊枝又追问琴言：无缘无故怎会害病？见你戏的又不止他一个。说得琴言臊得无可回答。聘才却把这段奇闻在城外各处传扬，各班相公添枝加叶，越传越不成话。

王文辉在席上从蓉官、二喜口中听见，心里老大不快：子玉才十八岁，正是好花含蕊的时候，偏偏去年将爱女许了他，有了外遇，将来琴瑟便难专好。他盘问王恂，王恂含糊遮掩；陆氏夫人却先替女儿吃起醋来，把子玉数落一顿，连王文辉也带累挨了训。王琼华听母亲说了又说，低头不语，只想着"才人行短"，转念又记起哥哥、姊夫都称子玉正经多情，或者他所爱的不过是那份颜色罢了，便红了脸，手拈衣带呆坐出神。子玉自琴言来后不到十日精神复旧，只是见了母亲总有些惶恐；颜夫人体贴，绝口不提琴言。他终究放心不下，借看聘才为名又去了一趟，由珊枝把琴言叫出来谈了一个多时辰。华府人多嘴杂，此事很快传到华光宿耳中。公子动了气，叫珊枝上来责问几句，从此不准琴言出门，把他派到洗红居，交十珠婢看管，不与外人通问，与拘禁无异。幸而众婢多情爱好，倒着实照应；而琴言年纪虽小，性情异样，守身如玉，连十珠婢也防着，公子看他老诚，索性把他当个丫鬟看待。至此莫说走出来，连林珊枝不奉呼唤也进不去。琴言日间与众婢略说几句，夜来只独守孤灯，怨那秋风秋雨而已。`,en:`Du Qinyan enters the Mei house braced for humiliation, and instead finds Madam Yan not only forbearing but kind, and is sent in to console the invalid. In the sickroom the curtains hang undrawn, dust lies on the desk, the medicine tripod smokes, the censer is all ash, and a small nanmu bed is veiled in white gauze. Mei Ziyu, half asleep, is murmuring lines from the *Song of Everlasting Sorrow* — the seventh night of the seventh month in the Hall of Long Life, the hour when no one overhears — for in his dream the festival has already come and he sits brewing tea with Qinyan at Sulan's; then he sighs that neither the Yellow Springs nor the blue void will yield her up; then frets about the downpour and the boat lying so far off; and at last, answering the herb-rebus in his sleep, says that after angelica and stephania there ought to be added one dose of danggui — the herb whose name means ought to return. Listening beyond the curtain, Madam Yan weeps. Qinyan's tears fall over the sleeper's face, and when he calls, Ziyu only laughs coldly: you tested me once already, do you think I would not know you — taking him for another counterfeit. Wei Pincai has Yun'er wring out a hot cloth, wipes his face, and props him against folded brocade; Madam Yan will not come in, lest her son be shamed. Qinyan shifts toward the light and turns his face, and Ziyu's heart jumps; he asks and asks again, and when he hears "truly, it is truly true," he bursts into wild laughter and pitches forward onto Qinyan's shoulder, laughing until the whole room laughs with him. To cure the sickness Qinyan tells his first lies: that Hua Guangsu is entirely correct, that everyone in the mansion treats him well, that he need not sing but serves in the Liuqing Study like a study-boy, that Madam Yan has licensed him to come often, and that when the master returns there is talk of buying him to wait on the young gentleman. Knowing that Qinyan never lies, Ziyu is settled past all shaking; he climbs down, takes two steps, declares it was only a touch of summer heat, and calls for something to eat. 

At parting Qinyan urges him earnestly, and Ziyu urges him in turn to be reconciled with Pincai, and to weigh his words before Madam Yan. Outside, she marvels: one weeping, one laughing, and nothing said between them — had she not seen it herself, anyone would have imagined the worst. She has Xu Shun weigh out two hundred taels for Pincai, the sum he has extorted with a lie, which goes straight into his cash chest at the mansion; and she gives Qinyan a silver-mounted knife, purses large and small, a handkerchief, a foreign watch, small plum-blossom ingots, and an ivory fan with gold leaf. At the gate their return is entered in the register. Lin Shanzhi first frightens them — the young lord called for Qinyan three times and has sent a man to Changqing's to verify the story — and only when Qinyan is in tears and Pincai bowing does he laugh and admit he has already told the man to stroll about outside the city and report that it was indeed the master's birthday. Then he collects his price: the two bolts of silk are the wrong pattern, but at Sha the Muslim's he has seen a gold-wire flower basket of two taels' weight and a Shi Dabin Yixing teapot with a kingfisher-jade inset lid — a pot that pours without a drop spilt and yields pale-green tea from plain boiling water — and Pincai may buy him both, and have the register entry erased besides. Back at the Liuqing Study, Shanzhi presses Qinyan: how does a man fall ill over nothing, when others see you act and do not sicken? — and Qinyan, scarlet, has no answer. Pincai meanwhile publishes the wonder all over the outer city, and rival actors embroider it out of recognition. 

Wang Wenhui hears the tale from Rongguan and Er Xi at a banquet and is deeply displeased: Ziyu is eighteen, a bud not yet open, and only last year he promised his favourite daughter to him; an outside attachment bodes ill for the harmony of the marriage. He questions Wang Xun, who answers evasively and defends his cousin, and remains unsatisfied. Madam Lu is jealous on her daughter's behalf before the girl is even married, and delivers a lecture that catches her own husband in its sweep. Wang Qionghua, hearing it repeated, sits with lowered head, thinking that a man of talent is short on conduct — then remembering that her brother and brother-in-law both call Ziyu upright and deeply feeling, and wondering whether it is only beauty he loves, she blushes, twists her sash, and stares at nothing. Ziyu is himself again within ten days, though shy before his mother, who never once mentions Qinyan. Still uneasy, he uses a call on Pincai as pretext and goes again, and Shanzhi brings Qinyan out to him for above an hour. In a house of so many mouths the story soon reaches Hua Guangsu, who is angry, rebukes Shanzhi, forbids Qinyan the gate, and moves him to the Xihong Lodge under the eyes of the ten pearl-maids, cut off from every outside message — imprisonment in all but name. The maids, being warm-hearted, look after him kindly, and another boy would have thought it paradise; but Qinyan, young and singular, guards himself like jade even among them, so that Hua, satisfied of his gravity, treats him as one more maid and forbids only the outside world. Now there is no walking out, and even Lin Shanzhi cannot come unsummoned. By day Qinyan exchanges a few words with the maids; by night he keeps a solitary lamp and quarrels with the autumn wind and rain.`},30:{zh:`杜琴言擅自出府一事，华光宿虽未究其细底，只责他不知规矩，姑免其罚，却把他拨到内室，从此里外不通。他独坐水晶山畔，对着几丛凤仙垂泪：花在本根相偎相倚，自有天然机趣，纵有风吹雨打也无损根本；一被折入金盆玉盎，剪枝摘叶、绳拴线缚，合了人的眼睛，减却花的颜色，与把人拘禁得笑不敢笑、哭不敢哭何异？何况人还不如花——花今年谢了明年再开，人却一年不似一年；他日被人厌恶时，便如落花之飘茵落圂，沾泥带水，无所归结。又想到自己那日为宽子玉之心撒的谎，倘教他知道如今音信断绝，只怕旧病复发。八月十二，丹桂满院，华公子记起六月在怡园许下的秋凉之约，因十五是家宴，便定十四夜请徐子云、萧次贤、刘文泽，并声明夜宴。是时正值秋闱二场：主考正是刘文泽之父大宗伯，副主考王文辉新迁学士，陆宗沅、杨方猷、周锡爵、孙亮功一齐分房，名士回避了大半；徐子云、史南湘已是前科举人，萧次贤高尚不仕，只有田春航、高品入场，梅子玉与王恂、刘文泽、颜仲清皆例应回避。

子玉在家闷坐，琴言杳无音信，几次想去访聘才，又厌华府豪奴气焰，终于不去。西园之胜，原是老公爷的画友谢笠山经营十二年，浓淡疏密各得其宜；华公子爱繁华，改得一味雕琢绚烂，不留一分朴素。地方二十亩，一道玉带河六折六桥，长廊曲榭，岸边号"小苏堤"；假山两种，小者太湖石嵌空玲珑，大者黄石高至数丈，苍藤绿苔缠护。张仲雨一早进来，午后同魏聘才随林珊枝闲游：穿神仙洞、过丹桂，看凹字形七间的"归鸿小渚"和华公子的亲笔小跋；再上藏书楼，两所作丁字形，缥缃万卷，芸香袭人；又见壁挂瑶琴、几列彝鼎的水阁，湘竹几椅，退光漆面，仲雨叹此地洗尽繁华，珊枝说公子虽爱热闹，其实也喜清静；末了是白石平台上的玻璃水榭，云母屏风，一缸金鱼养着四尺高的水晶山。这日之宴分三处：日间恩庆堂演戏，酉戌之间小平山看杂技，入夜留青精舍演灯戏。

恩庆堂上五大名班合演，随后袁宝珠、苏蕙芳、陆素兰、金漱芳、李玉林、王兰保、王桂保、林春喜、袁琪官等九人添上全福班一个，合演《秦淮河看花大会》，幽闲妖冶各极其致；华公子笑说这是抄袭子云旧文，子云却赞没有一人雷同，萧次贤因说如今秦淮冷落，马湘兰的相貌也不过中等。子云不见琴言，问起，公子只说他脾气虽古怪却老实，如今派在内书房。及至十旦上来敬酒，子云问苏蕙芳田春航的场中文字，文泽便揭他天天焚香祷告；华公子追问，蕙芳斟酒相拦，张仲雨又笑他"事急借人作护身符，如今倒忘恩负义"，说得蕙芳脸红，华公子想起前情，动了怜念，便把话岔开。饭后众人穿廊过院，壁上一块六尺高的祝枝山草书木刻，跟班一推竟是暗门，门后绿阴满目、水声潺潺。琴言这才由珊枝领出来见客：低眉垂首，全无从前高傲神气，隔了两月，倒像相逢陌路；子云问了几句，几番要问子玉，终于缩住。

看过像声、《龙图公案》与变戏法，月色将上，红灯十数对引路，五彩绸坊悬起数百盏画灯，庭心八枝锡地照打成花卉，火树银花一般。饮到二更，冯子佩忽然半夜闯来，说今日随妻舅归自荣到乌大傻家拜寿，公摊九十吊叫了全福班，归自荣串《独占》，自己却被乌大傻死缠去唱《活捉》——正做到进场时，刑部一张票子、一条铁链把乌大傻锁了去，满堂哭喊而散。张仲雨一猜便着：乌大傻造假房契替归自荣借了六百吊，钱是两人分用，如今独他倒运。华公子劝子佩登台，子佩说不是相公巴结谁，子云说顾曲登场原是风流自赏；及至配戏，聘才所会的《折柳》《赏荷》《小宴》《琴挑》《偷诗》，子佩一概不会，他会的却是《前诱》《后诱》《反诳》《挑帘》《裁衣》一路，末了还是八龄班一个孩子低声提起，才定了《活捉》。子佩出场竟幽怨可怜，喑呜如泣，轻云随足、淡烟抹袖，纤音摇曳得灯火为之不明，众人如不相识，华公子离席赞他"居然像个好妇人"。此时刘文泽与蕙芳低语的却不是子佩，而是归自荣：此人江西籍而寄籍直隶，进过一名秀才，少年标致，在京八年不归，谎称断弦娶了乌大傻之妹，又纳叶茂林之女为副室，别居城南；叶女奁资颇厚，两年花尽，夫妻交谪，她便倚门卖俏，自荣先怒后安，竟明目张胆当起忘八，还雇了伙计在家，而口中犹常称某给事为业师。华公子不喜与闻这些，只顾看戏出神，忽命捧上大玉杯，送到子云面前。`,en:`Hua Guangsu never inquires into the details of Du Qinyan's unlicensed outing; he merely charges him with not knowing the rules, waives the punishment as a first offence, and moves him to the inner apartments, where nothing passes in or out. Sitting alone by the crystal rockery, Qinyan weeps over a clump of balsam. Flowers left leaning on one another at the root have a natural life, and wind and rain hurt only the season, not the stock; but plucked into a golden basin, pruned, stripped, bound with cord to please a human eye, they lose the very colour that was praised — how is that different from penning a person up until he dares neither laugh nor cry? And people are worse off than flowers, for a flower that falls this year opens again the next, while a person only declines; and when he is at last found tiresome he will be a fallen petal blown into the mire, soiled and belonging nowhere. He thinks too of the lies he told to quiet Ziyu, and fears that the silence now closing over them will bring the illness back. By the twelfth of the eighth month the courtyard is heavy with cassia. Remembering the promise made at Yiyuan in the sixth month, and the family feast falling on the fifteenth, Hua fixes the fourteenth for Xu Ziyun, Xiao Cixian, and Liu Wenze, specifying a night banquet. 

It is the second session of the autumn examinations: the chief examiner is Liu Wenze's own father, the Minister of Rites, the deputy is Wang Wenhui, newly raised to Academician, and Lu Zongyuan, Yang Fangyou, Zhou Xijue, and Sun Lianggong all sit as section examiners — so half the known scholars must withdraw. Xu Ziyun and Shi Nanxiang already hold their degree, Xiao Cixian holds himself above office, and only Tian Chunhang and Gao Pin go in, while Mei Ziyu, Wang Xun, Wenze, and Yan Zhongqing are barred by kinship. Ziyu sits at home with no word of Qinyan, thinks more than once of calling on Pincai, and cannot face the insolence of the Hua servants. The West Garden was laid out over twelve years by the old duke's painter friend Xie Lishan, light and dense in right proportion, until Hua Guangsu, loving splendour, reworked it into unbroken carving and colour with no plainness left. Twenty mu; the Jade Belt River winding through six bends under six bridges; long galleries and water pavilions; a bank called the Little Su Embankment; rockeries of two kinds, small ones of fretted Taihu stone and great yellow-stone crags tens of feet high, netted with old vine and moss. Zhang Zhongyu comes early, and after noon tours the place with Wei Pincai behind Lin Shanzhi: through a grotto and past cassias to the seven-bay Returning Geese Islet with Hua's own inscription; up into the library towers, two blocks set like the character for T, thousands of volumes breathing the scent of insect-repelling herb; then a water pavilion hung with jade qins and ranked with ancient bronzes, its furniture of speckled bamboo under dull lacquer, of which Zhongyu says that here all splendour is washed away, and Shanzhi answers that his master loves quiet as well as noise; and last a glass-topped summer pavilion on a white stone terrace, with mica screens and a great jar of goldfish holding a crystal hill four feet high. 

The day has three settings: plays at Enqing Hall, acrobatics at Xiaopingshan toward evening, lantern plays at the Liuqing Study by night. Five great troupes perform; then nine of the famous ten — Yuan Baozhu, Su Huifang, Lu Sulan, Jin Shufang, Li Yulin, Wang Lanbao, Wang Guibao, Lin Chunxi, Yuan Qiguan — with one of the Quanfu troupe added, give the *Flower-Viewing Assembly on the Qinhuai*, secluded grace and open coquetry each carried to its limit. Hua calls it a rehash of Ziyun's old piece; Ziyun answers that no two of them are alike; and Xiao Cixian observes that the Qinhuai is desolate now, and that even Ma Xianglan's looks were middling. Missing Qinyan, Ziyun asks after him, and is told only that the boy's temper is odd but honest and that he now serves in the inner study. When the ten come up to pour wine, Ziyun asks Su Huifang how Tian Chunhang's examination essays went, and Wenze betrays that Huifang burns incense and prays for him daily; Hua presses for the story, Huifang blocks Ziyun with a cup, and Zhang Zhongyu laughs that having once used a great name as a shield he is now ungrateful — at which Huifang colours, and Hua, remembering something, feels a stir of pity and lets the subject drop. Afterwards the party threads corridors and courts to a six-foot panel of Zhu Zhishan's cursive set in a wall, which an attendant pushes open: it is a hidden door, and beyond it green shade and the sound of water. 

Only here does Shanzhi bring Qinyan out — eyes lowered, all his old pride gone, so that after two months he and Ziyun meet almost as strangers; Ziyun asks him a few things, twice moves to ask about Ziyu, and each time stops. After vocal mimicry, a recital from the *Cases of the Dragon Diagram*, and conjurers, the moon rises; a dozen pairs of red lanterns lead them out beneath a five-coloured silk archway hung with hundreds of painted lanterns, past eight pewter standing lamps wrought into flowers with the light inside the blossoms, fire-trees and silver flowers. They drink to the second watch, and then Feng Zipei bursts in at midnight with his story: he had gone with his brother-in-law Gui Zirong to a birthday at Wu Dasha's, where ninety strings were pooled for the Quanfu troupe and Gui performed *Exclusive Possession*, while Wu badgered Feng into playing *Capturing Alive* with him — and at the very moment of the capture, men came in with a warrant from the Board of Punishments and led Wu away in chains, the women wailing and the party scattering. Zhang Zhongyu guesses at once: Wu forged a house deed to raise six hundred strings for Gui, the two split the money, and now Wu alone pays. Hua urges Feng onto the stage; Feng retorts that he is no actor with anyone to flatter, and Ziyun answers that treading the boards is a pleasure taken for its own sake. The repertories will not meet: Pincai offers *Plucking a Willow Branch*, *Appreciating the Lotuses*, *The Small Banquet*, *Flirting with a Zither*, *Stealing the Poem*, and Feng knows none of them, his own being *Prior Enticement*, *Later Enticement*, *Counter-Deception*, *Lifting the Curtain*, and *Cutting Cloth* — until a boy of the Baling troupe murmurs that Pincai has been seen in *Capturing Alive*, and that is settled on. Feng's performance astonishes them: plaintive and sobbing, light cloud at his feet and thin mist brushed from his sleeves, a thread of sound that seems to dim the lamps, so that Hua leaves his seat to say that the man is truly like a beautiful woman. Meanwhile Liu Wenze and Huifang, whispering, are not discussing Feng at all but Gui Zirong — a Jiangxi man registered in Zhili who took a licentiate's degree, handsome, eight years in the capital without going home, who pretended his wife had died in order to marry Wu Dasha's sister, then took Ye Maolin's daughter as a second wife in a separate house south of the city; her dowry was large and gone in two years, and when poverty set them quarrelling she began displaying herself at the door, old acquaintances resumed their visits, and Gui, angry at first and then finding the money easy, kept the trade openly and even hired an assistant, though he still speaks of a Supervising Secretary as his teacher. Hua dislikes such talk and ignores it, absorbed in the stage, and calls for a great jade goblet to be carried to Ziyun.`}},t={31:{zh:`冯子佩、魏聘才下台后，华光宿笑说子佩"真像个阎婆惜"，若化了女身也是个不安分的；徐子云则请聘才闲时到怡园讲论音律。闹到子正撤席止戏，独不见杜琴言——林珊枝回说身子不快，其实他每逢热闹便触动心事，又瞧不上冯、魏二人串戏，托病去了。华公子命众旦脱去衣帽、依齿序坐，说要看看他们戏曲之外的一长。徐子云、刘文泽当场分派：苏蕙芳长于诗，袁宝珠长于丹青，王兰保长于舞剑，陆素兰长于书法，李玉林长于填词，王桂保长于灯谜诙谐，金漱芳长于品箫，林春喜长于吹笙，可惜琴言又病了，他会一套《平沙落雁》。萧次贤便"点将"：琴言与漱芳琴箫相和，宝珠作画，蕙芳、素兰、玉林题诗，珊枝与春喜笙琶竞奏，桂保猜谜说笑，末了兰保舞剑。琴言双锁蛾眉勉强和弦，半年不弹，指下生涩，第四段第三句几乎想不起来；漱芳的箫却和平圆润，因他每三日必受萧次贤一课。华公子摇头说"琴声不佳，箫声倒好"，子云替他分解，琴言抚今追昔，几乎落泪，默默退后，子云、次贤看着也恻然。宝墨那边，宝珠一时不得主意，蕙芳替他出题：小手卷上淡写两三处楼台，加丛林修竹，上画一月，用花青烘出彩云烟雾，题作《良宵风月图》。宝珠大喜，用王麓台笔法，三分工七分写，不到半个时辰便成。

华公子赞不绝口，说这般才貌真羞死从前那班爱钱的相公。蕙芳题一绝："良宵灯月赏秋光，丝竹纷纷斗两厢。我道嫦娥畏岑寂，遣风吹送上华堂。"举座拍案，刘文泽改第三句为"想是嫦娥怕孤寂"，觉得更淡远。素兰亦成一绝，子云嫌两首同用"嫦娥"，改作"广寒宫殿层层启"。李玉林填《一痕沙》小令，华公子把"集群贤"改"会群仙"、"乱啼声"改"只三更"。张仲雨与聘才暗暗吃惊：向来疑他们有人代笔，今日面试才知句句自出。冯子佩不去凑趣，只把一盘桂花用红线结成花球，挂在华公子衣襟上。继听珊枝的琵琶、春喜的笙合奏《月儿高》，指法如雨洒芭蕉，声韵如滩头流水。

桂保猜谜，一猜便中华公子隐的"风花雪月"；子佩与聘才各出一个爆竹谜，被华公子笑作"放不响的爆竹"。晓风既起，兰保扎袖掣剑，到后来只见一道寒光，连人也不见了。红霞满天，正是中秋，众客告辞。十六日，王恂、颜仲清同史南湘来看子玉。子玉病愈已久，知琴言深锁华府不可复出，便作看破红尘之状，歌场舞席一概不与，只对着茗碗香炉独坐，其实情丝未断，一念及便涕泪潸然。三人拉他同到田春航、高品寓中，苏蕙芳、李玉林正在座。谈起闱中文字：春航之文人人看好，高品却把自己那篇不足五百字的短文抖出来，子玉赞其"笔老格高"，南湘断言春航必中魁，高品则要碰上荒疏房考才有指望；高品扯碎文稿，说"得失自有一定"，又赌气说"我总中一个给你看"。

席间有客来找高品，久谈不出，春航说近来找他的多是俗陋不堪之人，小使还见管家拿进好几封银包，仲清、南湘便猜出他穷极了在作代笔的买卖。随后蕙芳忽然正色请客"要分子"，众人当是顽话；待菜摆齐、安席已毕，他从靴掖里取出一篇《香雪先生传》与两本《香雪遗稿》，才说破缘故：香雪先生本是名士出身，因不第焚弃笔砚，入班教曲，就是教他读书教他唱戏的业师；蕙芳一身才技皆其所授，情如骨肉。先生病故多年，旅榇寄在城南寿佛寺无人过问；如今寡妻弱子千里访来，到京才知人已不在。蕙芳倾囊二百金相助，又请萧次贤画像征诗；其子元佐年十三，贫不能入塾，却在人家书塾窗外听讲，默记于心，《五经》已熟一半。他说到伤心处呜咽起来，满座动容，齐说此等高义今人所难：颜仲清作诔，王恂作挽诗，史南湘作墓志，田春航另作一传，梅子玉作赞，高品作祭文，又议定刻成一集、量力凑分。蕙芳叩头相谢，慌得众人齐来扶起，从此人人视他为畏友，连顽笑也不肯了。计徐子云二十四两、刘文泽十六两、萧次贤十二两，六人各八两，共成百金；刻印百部的六十两由蕙芳独任；同出香雪门下的袁宝珠、王桂保、金漱芳、陆素兰四人又凑百金，连蕙芳所出共四百金，足够母子与老仆雇舟由运河还乡。二十九日，南湘、仲清骑马出城至寿佛寺：草深一尺，颓垣败井，佛像倾欹，殿角坍塌，落叶成堆，三间小屋里堆着二三十具灵柩。众人一奠：蕙芳上香，素兰奠酒，漱芳执壶，宝珠上菜，桂保焚纸，春航、南湘、高品同行一礼，五旦叩头代谢。事毕给了和尚一吊钱，转往锦秋墩浩然亭，四围远眺，数十里城池村落尽在目前。众人由荒寺谈到鬼，宝珠讲出怡园"闹鬼"的笑话——原是一只大马猴顶着熏笼偷果子，唬得两个小使各说见了一种鬼，闹了两天才拆穿。九人席地围坐，田春航想起去年潦倒、几至沟壑，若非蕙芳成就，纵有满腹珠玑也无今日，对着西风衰飒忽然泪落；蕙芳亦忆及若非香雪提携早已十死八九，放声一哭，满座悲酸。史南湘痛饮一大碗，对远山舒啸，清风四起，林木为摇；高品自笑胸中空空洞洞，说孙登之啸不过形狂、阮籍之悲亦云气馁，提议联句，于是高、史、颜、田四人合成一阕《贺新凉》，末句"诗唱秋坟聊当哭"、"且一醉，莫僝僽"。天欲雨，众人匆匆收拾，南湘搭蕙芳的车，仲清搭素兰的车，到家时已萧疏细雨。`,en:`When Feng Zipei and Wei Pincai come off the stage, Hua Guangsu jokes that Zipei was truly Yan Poxi to the life and would be no respecter of rules if he were born a woman, while Xu Ziyun invites Pincai to visit Yiyuan and talk music. Wine games run to midnight before the tables are cleared, and Du Qinyan is nowhere to be seen: Lin Shanzhi reports him unwell, though in fact festivity always reopens his grief, and having no opinion of Feng and Wei as actors he had begged off. Hua now has the young performers seated by age in ordinary clothes, saying he wishes to see what each can do besides act. Xu Ziyun and Liu Wenze assign the parts: Su Huifang excels in poetry, Yuan Baozhu in painting, Wang Lanbao in swordplay, Lu Sulan in calligraphy, Li Yulin in lyrics, Wang Guibao in riddles and wit, Jin Shufang on the xiao, Lin Chunxi on the sheng — and it is a pity Qinyan is ill, for he knows a suite of *Wild Geese Alighting on Level Sand*. Xiao Cixian deploys them like a general: Qinyan and Shufang to join qin and flute, Baozhu to paint, Huifang, Sulan and Yulin to inscribe it, Shanzhi and Chunxi to contest on pipa and sheng, Guibao to set riddles, and Lanbao to close with the sword. Qinyan takes up the strings with knitted brows; half a year unpractised, his fingers are stiff and he nearly loses the third line of the fourth section, while Shufang's flute is round and serene, for Cixian has taught him every third day. Hua shakes his head — the qin is poor, the flute is good — and Ziyun makes excuses for him, but Qinyan, measuring what he was against what he is, retires almost in tears, and both Ziyun and Cixian are moved. At the painting table Baozhu is at a loss until Huifang supplies the subject: a short handscroll with two or three towers lightly drawn, a grove and bamboo, a moon above, and indigo-washed cloud and mist — to be called *Fine Night, Wind and Moon*. Baozhu paints it in Wang Lutai's manner, three parts careful and seven parts free, and finishes within the half hour, to Hua's delight, who says such gifts shame the money-loving actors of former days. 

Huifang inscribes a quatrain on lamps and moon and the two wings of music, imagining the moon goddess so afraid of loneliness that she sends the wind to blow her into the hall; the table applauds, and Wenze suggests a gentler turn for the third line. Sulan matches it with another, and Ziyun, to avoid repeating the goddess's name, alters his opening to the Palace of Broad Cold. Yulin fills in a lyric to *A Trace of Sand*, and Hua improves two phrases. Zhang Zhongyu and Pincai are secretly astonished: they had always supposed these poems were ghost-written, and here is proof. Feng Zipei, taking no part, threads a tray of cassia flowers on red silk into a ball and hangs it from Hua's lapel. Then Shanzhi's pipa and Chunxi's sheng play *Moon Rises High*, the fingering like rain on plantain leaves, the tone like water over a shoal. Guibao guesses Hua's poem-riddle at once — wind, flower, snow and moon — while Zipei and Pincai each offer a firecracker riddle, and Hua laughs that a firecracker which will not go off is no use to anyone. As the dawn wind rises Lanbao ties back his sleeves and draws, until at the end there is only a streak of cold light and no man visible. Red cloud fills the sky; it is the Mid-Autumn day, and the guests take leave. 

On the sixteenth, Wang Xun and Yan Zhongqing bring Shi Nanxiang to see Ziyu, long recovered, who — knowing Qinyan locked beyond reach — affects to have seen through the world, shuns theatre and banquets, and sits alone with his tea and censer, though the thread is uncut and he weeps whenever the thought returns. They carry him off to Tian Chunhang and Gao Pin's lodging, where Su Huifang and Li Yulin are sitting. The talk is of examination essays: everyone expects well of Chunhang, while Gao Pin produces his own, under five hundred characters, which Ziyu praises for an old brush and a high frame; Nanxiang predicts Chunhang will head the list and Gao Pin will pass only if his section examiner is careless. Gao Pin tears up the draft, says gain and loss are fixed, and swears he will pass all the same. A visitor calls him out for a long while, and Chunhang remarks that the men who seek him lately are a coarse sort, and that his servant was seen carrying in several packets of silver — from which Zhongqing and Nanxiang infer that poverty has driven him into writing examination papers for hire. Then Huifang gravely announces that he is standing the dinner and really does want a subscription. The table is laid with ceremony; and from his boot he draws a *Biography of Master Fragrant Snow* and two volumes of the *Posthumous Writings of Fragrant Snow*, and explains. Fragrant Snow was a scholar of standing who, failing the examinations, burned his brushes, joined a troupe as a singing master, and taught Huifang both his letters and his art; all that Huifang can do came from him, and they were as kin. 

He died years ago, his coffin left unclaimed in the Shoufo Temple south of the city; now his widow and young son have come a thousand li to find him and learned only on arrival that he was long dead. Huifang has emptied his purse of two hundred taels and asked Xiao Cixian for a portrait and a call for poems; the son Yuanzuo, thirteen, too poor for school, listens at other people's schoolroom windows and has half the Five Classics by heart. Speaking, he breaks down, and the whole table rises: such righteousness, they say, is rare in this age. Yan Zhongqing will write the dirge, Wang Xun the elegies, Nanxiang the epitaph, Chunhang a fresh biography, Mei Ziyu an encomium, Gao Pin the sacrificial address; and the pieces are to be cut and printed as a collection, each contributing as he can. Huifang kowtows and has to be lifted up, and from that day everyone treats him as a friend to be revered, and will not even joke with him. Xu Ziyun gives twenty-four taels, Liu Wenze sixteen, Xiao Cixian twelve; the six friends give eight each, making a hundred; the sixty taels for a hundred printed copies Huifang pays alone; and Yuan Baozhu, Wang Guibao, Jin Shufang and Lu Sulan, who also studied under Fragrant Snow, add a hundred more, so that four hundred taels in all send the widow, the boy, and an old servant home by canal boat in comfort. On the twenty-ninth Nanxiang and Zhongqing ride out to the Shoufo Temple: grass a foot deep, broken walls and ruined wells, the images tilted, a corner of the great hall fallen in, leaves drifted in heaps, and in three small rooms some thirty coffins stored. They make their offering — Huifang burning incense, Sulan pouring the libation, Shufang holding the flask, Baozhu setting out the dishes, Guibao burning paper money, while Chunhang, Nanxiang and Gao Pin bow together and the five performers kowtow their thanks. A string of cash to the monk, and they ride on to Jinqiu Mound and the Haoran Pavilion, from which tens of li of walls and villages lie open to view. Talk of the ruined temple turns to ghosts, and Baozhu tells the comic tale of the Yiyuan haunting — a big monkey stealing hoarded fruit and bolting with a fumigating basket on its head, so that two pages each swore to a different sort of ghost until the creature came back and settled the question. The nine of them sit on mats on the ground; and Chunhang, remembering how near destitution he came last year and how little his learning would have availed but for Huifang, weeps into the autumn wind. Huifang, thinking how he would have died ten times over but for Fragrant Snow, weeps aloud, and the whole party with him. Nanxiang drains a great bowl and whistles at the far hills until the trees shake, and Gao Pin, professing himself hollow within, says that Sun Deng's whistling was mere wildness and Ruan Ji's grief a failure of spirit, and proposes linked verse — whereupon he, Nanxiang, Zhongqing and Chunhang complete between them a lyric to *Congratulations on the New Cool*, ending that a poem sung at an autumn grave will serve instead of weeping, and that one may as well get drunk and stop fretting. Rain threatens; they pack in haste, Nanxiang riding back in Huifang's carriage and Zhongqing in Sulan's, and a thin drizzle is falling by the time they reach home.`},32:{zh:`秋雨连绵七八日，初八方晴，初十出榜，初九一早便报起来。是夜田春航睡不着，起身到高品房中夜话：他说自己名心本淡，中不中无妨，只对不住苏蕙芳半年期望；又叹科名虽不足贵，古今才人却断无不由此而起。高品则骂今科主考房官把人回避得干干净净。说到问卜，高品讲起当年在江宁请人起梅花数，得泰卦五爻"帝乙归妹，以祉元吉"，那年又恰是乙未，他自以为十拿九稳，谁知鬼神最会哄人；春航道：心之所欲，象即呈焉，那是人心上起的象，不是卦中之象。二人煮茗谈到五更。这边蕙芳亦一夜不安，巳正才起，忙遣人打听，又推了城中登高之约。午初回报：已报过四十名，田老爷不在内，倒是那开窑子的归自荣中在三十四名。蕙芳听了气道："忘八都中了，还了得！"袁宝珠、陆素兰、李玉林接连遣人来问信，闹得他坐立不安。偏这时胡裁缝送了一盘枣糕上门，先说潘其观的女婿中了、赏了一百吊，宏济寺的高老爷中在八十一名；随即央求他收自己第三个儿子三喜为徒——那孩子学了三四个月连剪刀也拿不起，一天要四五十钱买糖果，倒会唱许多乱弹；又诉裁缝之苦：无本钱，赊料贵，替人垫钱，七折八扣，五七个伙计老米饭酸菜汤，一日两吊钱的开销。

蕙芳厌烦，只说自己将要改行，且劝他送到乱弹班去，昆腔学一辈子还未必得人一声好。上灯时蕙芳才驱车到宏济寺贺高品，却见冷冷清清：高品与春航对坐下棋，春航一把掳了棋子说"输了"。高品说报条上写的是江南监生，与自己对不上帐。史南湘、颜仲清随后闯来道喜，说已报一百八十余名。南湘讲起自己当年在湖北候到四更才报，原来五魁是后填的；仲清也说上科被报录的误作南元，空喜欢了半夜。直到二更后忽然一片声嚷进来："田老爷大喜，中的是南元！"报条写明"中式第二名，田春航，年二十三岁，江南上元县附贡生"。众人添酒再饮，又遣人骑快马去看榜。五更人回，春航果然第二；高品急问自己，那人说八十一名是"高品三，年四十岁，江南淮安府山阳县监生"，气得他呸了一声。取《题名录》细看，果无其名。

蕙芳指出榜上认得的两个：三十四名归自荣即叶茂林的女婿"窑子归"，六十三名花中桂即潘其观的女婿"杠花"，其父本是抬杠出身，如今开了几处杠房。高品长叹，索性直说了：一千六百两银子卖掉一个举人，这"杠花"就是他替中的，由张仲雨过手，明日便要讨账；他自知此事作不得，只因家中有急事而囊空如洗，又存着"或者名利双收"的侥幸，如今倒也罢了，至少不必认抬杠的作年伯、婊子作年嫂——又打趣春航，说照年谊算来，潘其观竟成了他的年丈，说得众人大笑。天明春航去谒房师座师，一连忙了半个月：他出于孙亮功门下，刘侍郎与王文辉虽未见过他，早从儿子书房里见过他的笔墨，倾倒已久，今中南元十分欢喜，从此与刘文泽、王恂又成世谊，独与孙家弟兄淡淡而已。高品收清枪银一千六百两；张仲雨在花家原讲定二千四百，从中扣去八百，又索谢仪二百，净得千金，随即由藩经历加捐正指挥，在坊里当起差来；高品于十月初二回苏州去了。另一头，乌大傻八月十四日被刑部锁去，原是归自荣急欲借钱，商借他的房契抵押，许其分用；大傻的契早已押出，只得另造伪契押得六百吊，自分二百吊。谁知新旧两个财东叙账时对出房址，才知两年前已押八百吊、还欠四个月利钱，遂告到部里。大傻本是天文生，祖上作过官，如今只剩数顷荒田几间破屋，幸而是白契并非私造印信，其堂母舅现任刑部司官，尚有照应；他想供出归自荣，无奈契是自己出名，反与归自荣毫无干涉，白革了功名，限期赔偿。拿人那日，皂隶陆升与归家同住一条胡衕，明明看见归自荣在乌家吃酒；及见他报了举人，越想越不明白。十月初三他在衙门前小茶馆遇见绍兴籍的贴写葛逢时，问明乡试八月初八头场、十一二场、十四三场，点名清早、午未封门，二更天还在场外的，必是头二场犯了贴例贴出来的——或烧了卷子，或污了墨，或交白卷，既未完场，便是大主考的儿子也不能中。

葛逢时听罢劝他细访，若真没进场，必有顶名代替，"我们见景生情，大家可以发些财"。陆升果然从归家撵出的一个奶妈子口中访得明白：那日归自荣因城外人眼多，躲在城里看戏，由一个马回子经手，请了一个丁忧的浙江廪生代考，许银一千两，先付润笔一百，他却只付四十，马回子又中饱十两，廪生只得三十两，天天上门吵闹，连马家大门都打破了。陆升上门讹诈一千两不成，反被啐了一脸，索性逢人便说；葛逢时替他写了呈子递进部去。马回子与廪生星夜逃走，部中审了两次，归自荣抵赖不得，据实供明，革去举人监押，俟拿到代枪之人再定案。此案一出，落第生监鸣鼓而攻，把他城外那些丑事一总揭出；部里一位老司官游戏笔墨，作了一篇四六勘语，满城传遍，从"家本书香，父曾攀桂；心耽铜臭，性爱游花"骂到"面无皮而岂解包羞"，末了"先除巾服""照例充军烟瘴""大书以示衣冠，泄众人之公忿"，归自荣自此成了衣冠禽兽。刘文泽的家人抄了一张来，恰好史南湘、颜仲清都在。仲清说这是天理昭彰，若无那皂隶一闹又有谁知；春航却说漏网者固多，冤枉者亦复不少——袁宝珠说过，教他画画的南京人金粟本是磊落名士，初到京时寄居某显宦家，跟班与其内眷有私，竟把相如文君的事疑到他身上，那显宦明于责人昧于责己，怀恨借端另案罗织，又带累几个名士一并斥革。文泽以唐六如、吴汉槎为例，说名自在，斥革何损；春航则道买卖人的儿子、家人的内亲一样中举，不过有幸有不幸罢了。正说着，史南湘的家人来报老爷放了道，南湘便先辞去。`,en:`Seven or eight days of autumn rain end in a clear eighth; the list is due on the tenth, and the criers begin at dawn on the ninth. That night Tian Chunhang cannot sleep and goes into Gao Pin's room to talk. His own appetite for fame is slight, he says, and passing or failing matters little — except that it would betray half a year of Su Huifang's hoping; and though examination rank is nothing to boast of, no man of talent in any age has risen without it. Gao Pin curses this year's examiners, whose kinship has disqualified half the city. Talk turns to divination, and Gao Pin recalls a plum-blossom prognostication at Nanjing that gave him the fifth line of the hexagram Tai — Di Yi gives his daughter in marriage, blessing, supreme good fortune — in a year whose cyclical name seemed written into the very words, so that he had thought himself certain, and learned that the spirits are expert liars. Chunhang answers that the mind is the most spiritual of things: what it wants, it sees; the image rose in the man, not in the hexagram. They brew tea until the fifth watch. Huifang, sleepless too, is up only at mid-morning, sends a man for news, and refuses an invitation to climb for the Double Ninth. By noon the word comes back: forty names read out, no Master Tian — but the brothel-keeping Gui Zirong stands thirty-fourth. Even a cuckold-keeper has passed, says Huifang, disgusted. Yuan Baozhu, Lu Sulan, and Li Yulin all send to ask, and he cannot sit still. 

At this moment the tailor Hu arrives with a tray of jujube cake, reports that Pan Qiguan's son-in-law has passed and paid a hundred strings in messengers' money, and that the Master Gao at Hongji Temple is eighty-first — and then makes his petition: his third son cannot hold a pair of shears after four months' training and eats forty or fifty cash a day in sweets, but is presentable and has picked up any number of luantan arias by ear, and might Huifang take him as a disciple? Tailoring, he complains, has no capital in it: dear materials on credit, money advanced for customers, bills discounted and disputed, five or seven workmen fed on coarse rice and pickled soup at two strings a day. Huifang, out of patience, says he is on the point of leaving the profession himself, and that a luantan troupe would suit the boy better, since a lifetime of Kunqu may still not earn one word of praise. Only at lamp-lighting does he drive to Hongji Temple to congratulate Gao Pin, and finds the place silent, Gao Pin and Chunhang at chess until Chunhang sweeps the pieces away. The notice, Gao Pin says, names a Jiangnan licentiate, and the details do not match. Shi Nanxiang and Yan Zhongqing arrive with congratulations and the news that a hundred and eighty names have been read. Nanxiang recalls waiting until the fourth watch in Hubei only to find himself first of all, since the top five are entered last, and Zhongqing recalls being wrongly reported as second on the list and rewarding the criers for nothing. After the second watch a shout comes through the gate: Master Tian has taken second place, Nanyuan — the slip naming him at twenty-three, a tribute student of Shangyuan county. More wine is called for, and a servant is sent on a fast horse to read the list itself. At the fifth watch he returns: Chunhang is indeed second; and the eighty-first is one Gao Pinsan, aged forty, of Shanyang county in Huai'an. Gao Pin spits. The *Title Register* confirms that his name is not there. 

Huifang can identify two of the successful: the thirty-fourth, Gui Zirong, Ye Maolin's son-in-law and keeper of a brothel; and the sixty-third, Hua Zhonggui, Pan Qiguan's son-in-law, whose father rose from carrying coffin-poles and now owns several undertakers' shops. Then Gao Pin makes his confession: sixteen hundred taels have sold a degree, and it was he who sat for the undertaker's son, brokered by Zhang Zhongyu, with payment to be collected tomorrow. He knew it was indefensible; but with urgent family business in the south and nothing in his purse he had gambled on either failing honestly or taking both name and profit — and at least, he says, he need not now call a coffin-bearer his examination uncle or a whore his examination aunt; and he teases Chunhang that by the same reckoning Pan Qiguan has become his examination father-in-law. By morning Chunhang is off to pay respects to his examiners, and a fortnight of banquets follows. He emerged under Sun Lianggong in the fourth section; Vice-Minister Liu and Wang Wenhui, who had never met him but had long admired his writings seen in their sons' studies, are delighted, and he becomes a family connection of Liu Wenze and Wang Xun, though relations with the Sun brothers stay cool. Gao Pin collects his sixteen hundred; Zhang Zhongyu, having agreed twenty-four hundred with the buyer, kept eight hundred and squeezed two hundred more as a gratuity, clearing a thousand, with which he buys promotion to a ward captaincy and takes up office; and Gao Pin leaves for Suzhou on the second of the tenth month. Meanwhile the case of Wu Dasha unfolds. Gui Zirong, needing money, had asked to pledge Wu's house deed and share the proceeds; the deed being already pledged, Wu forged a second and raised six hundred strings, keeping two hundred. The old creditor and the new happened to compare accounts, recognized the same property — pledged two years before for eight hundred strings, with four months' interest overdue — and sued. Wu, an astronomy student from a decayed official family left with a few qing of waste land and some broken rooms, was saved only by the deed's being unsealed rather than a forgery of an official seal, and by an uncle serving at the Board; he tried to implicate Gui and found that since the deed was in his own name Gui was untouchable, so he lost his student status and was given a term to repay. On the day of the arrest the runner Lu Sheng, who lives in the same lane, had plainly seen Gui drinking at Wu's house; and when Gui's success was announced he could not make sense of it. 

On the third of the tenth month, in a teahouse before the yamen, he puts the question to the Shaoxing copyist Ge Fengshi, and learns the schedule: the sessions of the eighth, eleventh, and fourteenth, roll-call at dawn and the gates sealed by early afternoon; and that a man still outside at the second watch must have been posted out for a fault in an earlier session — a burnt sheet, a blotted one, a blank one — his paper never reaching the copyists, and no such man could pass, not even the chief examiner's son. Ge advises him to investigate, since a substitute must have sat in his place, and there is money in it for them both. From a wet-nurse whom Gui dismissed for theft, Lu learns everything: that Gui hid in the city to watch a play because there were too many eyes outside it; that a broker called Ma the Muslim engaged a Zhejiang stipendiary student in mourning to sit for him at a thousand taels, a hundred down; that Gui paid only forty, of which Ma skimmed ten, so the student got thirty taels for winning a man a degree and now besieges Ma's house daily, having already broken his gate. Lu's attempt to extort a thousand taels earns him a faceful of spittle and a public quarrel, so he swears to sue, Ge drafts the plaint, and it is filed. Ma and the student flee by night; after two hearings Gui admits everything, is stripped of his degree and held pending the substitute's capture. The scandal rouses every disappointed candidate in the city, and all of Gui's private life is published; then an elderly departmental officer with a taste for parody composes a verdict in parallel prose which circulates everywhere, moving from a family of books and a father who plucked the cassia to a man with no skin left on his face to blush with, and ending with the stripping of cap and gown, banishment to the malarial frontier, and a public proclamation to vent the general indignation. Liu Wenze's servant brings a copy home, with Shi Nanxiang and Yan Zhongqing present. Zhongqing calls it heaven's plain justice — and asks who would ever have known but for one runner's noise. Chunhang answers that many slip the net and many are wronged: Yuan Baozhu has told him that the Nanjing scholar Jin Su, who taught him painting, is a man of open character who lodged with a certain grandee on first coming to the capital; his servant had an affair with a woman of the household, the scandal was imputed to the master, and the grandee — sharp in judging others and blind to himself — nursed the grudge until he could build a separate case that stripped Jin Su and several other scholars of their standing. Wenze cites Tang Bohu and Wu Zhaoqian, whose names outlived their disgrace; Chunhang replies that merchants' sons and servants' kin pass too, and it is all luck. As they talk, Shi Nanxiang's servant comes to say that his father has been appointed a circuit intendant, and Nanxiang takes his leave.`},33:{zh:`史南湘之父放了大名道，南湘随任出京，要到明年会试才回，京中又少一个知己。腊月中旬，王文辉又来对颜夫人说，不是冬底便是春初要与梅子玉毕姻；颜夫人推说须寄信江西、候回信再定，子玉因此连王宅也不大去了。徐子云新补了缺，衙门有事，也不能天天在园。一日雪消风静，子玉本要进城访魏聘才、打探杜琴言消息，行不到半里忽又踌躇，索性折回；出城时车马骆驼塞路，隔着玻璃窗一望，对面车上竟是袁宝珠，便被邀到他寓中。那"小琅嬛室"芳香不染纤尘：两盆水仙开足，古铜瓶插着天竹与腊梅，壁上悬的都是前人名迹，绝无世俗纱帽之作；地罩左边是宝珠自己的倚竹图小照，右边四幅花卉，正是教他画画的金粟手笔。子玉因赞他在金粟失意之后反更亲密、旅费皆赖他周全，不亚于苏蕙芳之待田春航，一句无心之言竟触动宝珠心事，眼眶一红，长叹一声"同是天涯沦落人，相逢何必曾相识"，泪落下来；子玉忙指窗外之竹，说岁寒时与唐花较量，方见其节同松柏，宝珠才破涕为笑。宝珠留饭，子玉却提议煮雪烹茶，请陆素兰来作个清谈：铜盘上安一尺来高的古铜茶炉，鼎足圆身，焰炭生炭并加，一炉活火直燃；蔚蓝大磁瓯外，另有宜兴提梁刻字大壶盛着雪水。宝珠亲手把四种名茶并作一瓯，三人分饮，清香泌入心脾，都说"不像一种茶味"。说到琴言，宝珠道：华府未必是他安身立命之处——徐子云那样待他尚且不能得他欢心，何况那里步步不离规矩，闲散惯的人如何过得？八月十四那日出来伺候，分明是勉强；素兰也说他如今见了旧人都生生的，总是忧郁不开。

随后素兰说出一桩新闻：魏聘才宿娼被拿，已送刑部。那日他请富三爷在蓉官寓里喝酒，富三先进城去了，聘才便叫蓉官请了个媳妇玉天仙，就在蓉官处过夜；二更时吏目郁泰孙查夜进来，本是相识，聘才还拉他入座取笑，那人翻脸道"就是总宪坐在这里我也拿得他"，一条链子把三人锁走，聘才簇新衣服被拴在车尾跟着跑。后来使了一千六百吊、写了字据、找了铺保，作了一套假供，改名"李三才"才开释；不料巡城御史访得吏目诈赃卖放，把案提上去送了刑部。素兰说，从前他指使赶车的糟蹋玉侬，如今是加倍奉还了。子玉回家，颜夫人正因梅士燮的家书而不悦——信中把他的事一一说破，还责她不能教训。子玉看得两颊通红，一言不发；夫人冷笑道：知道害怕，何如当初不作？以后学好不学好由你，你父亲回来未必依你。又听说聘才下狱，气骂一声"下作东西"，却仍打发梅进到王文辉处求他到部里说个人情。王文辉冷笑道：那魏聘才我一见就知道不是个东西，你们老爷偏要留他；刑部并无相好，只好转托人碰他的运气。

末了夫人一句"这是喜欢到相公家里去的榜样"，臊得子玉满面通红，好几天不敢再作妄想。聘才在监里许了蓉官与玉天仙许多银子，串通口供，反说是吏目借银不遂、挟嫌设局，又诈了五百吊改供；玉天仙更供他半月前发帖请分子未得而怀恨。两堂对质，口供相符；华光宿虽不愿管，心里到底不安，着人到部托情，遂从轻发落：吏目革职，聘才杖二十，玉天仙逐出境外，蓉官释放。聘才反欣欣然进城，向门上夸口说司官老爷们都与他相好，司狱又是至交。到第三日，林珊枝双手捧了一封银子放下便走，封面写着"赆仪二百两"——聘才心头一跳，才明白过来。候了两日无人再来，只得写一封谢札，托词梅宅有事要他回去照料，开春再来；门上回话说公子已看过，说回梅宅很是，因有事不及亲送。聘才心如死灰，当日搬出。

他在华府不过十个月，除修金外颇有遗赠，捐从九品花去四百余金，衣服浪费二千金，除新得的二百外尚存三百，且捐的名次靠前，约半年可选，因此胆壮心豪，租了宏济寺田春航住过的房子，高车大马阔起来，也不到梅宅去看望，只与蓉官、玉天仙日日征歌斗酒。富三爷原为他的事在部里打听多日，无路可通，后知华公子已托了情才放心；及闻他辞馆出来，一早出城去寻。到寺门口，正见一个披青布老羊皮袄的刑部禁卒在那里撒村：说自己一天到晚买长买短、生炉子烧炕，许的一百吊如今只给三十吊，"过了河就拆桥，保佑你别进来"。富三爷进去，见聘才与蓉官迎出，便劝他到自己家里过年，免得庙里冷清；聘才谢而不受。富三爷此来另有心事：他的直隶州已经顶选，眼下两个缺，一个湖北一个贵州，湖北好而贵州极苦，本该轮到他，偏来了个压班的投供。聘才便荐寺中的唐和尚——其兄弟正是吏部文选司经承——又背地形容这位"唐大哥"：算什么出家人，吃喝嫖赌样样精明，吹唱也好，还会专医杨梅疮。话未完，唐和尚推门进来：五短身材，青绉羊皮僧袍，黄丝绦，灰绒窝，满面阴骘纹，一双色眼，先攀富三爷之父的旧交，说神座旁那副对子还是二十九年前太爷亲笔，随即拍胸担保湖北一缺"包管作脸儿"。少顷小和尚得月捧水烟进来，十五六岁，青绸灰鼠暖兜、藕色狐皮僧袍、大红镶鞋，生得十分标致；富三爷看得两眼发直，末了笑说错认作大悲庵的姑子，说得聘才大笑，小和尚涨红了脸。唐和尚自道从前被师兄拘束，如今舒服——那师兄不吃不喝紧守租子，反被侄儿骗得干干净净，临终两手空空，身后事还是他办的；"人生在世，乐得吃，乐得顽"，如今都是酒肉和尚，守什么清规。席上蓉官与他插科打诨，秽语相攻；得月喝了几杯酒，脸红得越显娇媚。直到申末烟瘾发过才散，富三爷进城，又把那件事重托了唐和尚。`,en:`Shi Nanxiang's father is made intendant of the Daming circuit, and Nanxiang leaves the capital with him, not to return before the metropolitan examination; the circle thins again. In the middle of the twelfth month Wang Wenhui presses Madam Yan to conclude Mei Ziyu's marriage at the turn of the year, and she puts him off until a letter can go to Jiangxi and be answered — after which Ziyu keeps away from the Wang house. Xu Ziyun, newly appointed to a substantive post, can no longer spend his days in the garden. One still, thawing day Ziyu sets out to call on Wei Pincai for news of Du Qinyan, loses heart within half a li, and turns back; and in a jam of carts and camels outside the gate he sees Yuan Baozhu through the glass and is carried off to his lodging. The Xiaolang Chamber is fragrant and without a speck of dust: two bowls of narcissus in full flower, an old bronze vase holding nandina and wintersweet, walls hung only with genuine work of former masters and nothing of the vulgar official kind, and in an alcove, on the left a small portrait of Baozhu leaning on bamboo, on the right four flower panels by Jin Su, who taught him to paint. Ziyu praises him for growing closer to Jin Su after his disgrace and bearing all his travelling costs, a loyalty no less than Su Huifang's to Tian Chunhang — and the careless words touch a wound. Baozhu reddens, sighs out Bai Juyi's line about two exiles at the world's edge who need no earlier acquaintance, and weeps; Ziyu hurriedly points to the bamboo outside, whose integrity shows only when set against forced hothouse flowers in the cold, and Baozhu smiles through his tears. Instead of dinner Ziyu proposes melting snow for tea and sending for Lu Sulan. The equipage is set out: a great bronze tray, an ancient tea stove a foot high with tripod feet and a small round mouth, live coals heaped on fresh, and beside a deep-blue porcelain bowl a lidded Yixing pot with a carved inscription full of snow water. Baozhu blends four famous teas into one bowl with his own hands, and the three of them agree it tastes like no single tea at all. 

Of Qinyan, Baozhu says the Hua mansion cannot be where his life comes to rest: Xu Ziyun, who treats him as he does, has never won his heart, and in that house every step is ruled by regulation, unbearable to one used to freedom — his attendance on the fourteenth of the eighth month was plainly forced. Sulan adds that he is stiff now even with old friends, and never out of his melancholy. Then Sulan tells the season's scandal: Wei Pincai has been taken with a prostitute and sent to the Board of Punishments. He had been entertaining Fu the Third at Rongguan's rooms; Fu went into the city, and Pincai had Rongguan send for a woman, Yu Tianxian, and stayed the night. At the second watch the clerk Yu Taisun came on patrol — a man he knew, and had drunk and watched plays with — and Pincai laughingly pulled him toward a seat, until Yu turned cold and said that if the chief censor himself sat there he could take him too. All three were chained, and Pincai, in new clothes, was tied to the tail of a cart and made to run behind it. Sixteen hundred strings, a written bond, and a shop guarantor bought a false deposition under the name Li Sancai and his release — until a patrolling censor discovered the extortion and the falsified record and sent the case up. Sulan observes that the man who once hired carters to torment Yunong is being repaid with interest. At home Ziyu finds his mother out of temper: Mei Shixie's letter has come, setting out everything his son has done and blaming her for failing to correct him. 

Ziyu reads it scarlet and speechless, and she says coldly that if he can be frightened now he might better not have done it; that he may reform or not as he pleases; and that his father may be less indulgent on his return. Hearing of Pincai's arrest she curses him for a shameless creature — and still sends Mei Jin to ask Wang Wenhui to put in a word at the Board. Wang laughs shortly: he knew at first sight that Wei Pincai was worthless, and it was his old friend who insisted on keeping him; he has no connections there and can only pass the request along. Her parting shot — that this is what comes of frequenting actors' houses — leaves Ziyu crimson, and for days he dares not indulge a single longing. In prison Pincai buys Rongguan and Yu Tianxian with promises and matches their depositions: it was the clerk who invited them, tried to borrow a thousand taels, was refused, and took his revenge with soldiers already waiting, then extorted five hundred strings to alter the record; Yu Tianxian adds that he had sent round an unpaid subscription notice a fortnight earlier and bore a grudge. The stories agree at two hearings; and Hua Guangsu, disinclined to act yet uneasy, sends to intercede, so the sentence is light — the clerk cashiered, Pincai given twenty strokes, Yu Tianxian expelled, Rongguan freed. Pincai comes back to the mansion in triumph, boasting to the gatemen that every official there was his friend and the jailer his intimate. On the third day Lin Shanzhi sets down a heavy packet — the young lord's gift — and is gone before thanks can be spoken; the wrapper reads "parting gift, two hundred taels." Two days pass with no one calling. 

He writes a letter of thanks pleading business at the Mei house and promising to return in spring, and the gatekeeper brings back word that the young lord thought returning to the Mei house very proper and was too busy to see him off. His heart goes cold and he moves out that day. Ten months in the Hua household have not been unprofitable: with gifts beyond salary he has spent four hundred on a ninth-rank title and two thousand on clothes and pleasure, and still has three hundred besides the parting gift, with a place near the head of the selection list and a post likely within half a year. So he takes the Hongji Temple rooms Tian Chunhang once occupied, keeps carriage and horse, never calls at the Mei house, and passes his days with Rongguan and Yu Tianxian over wine and song. Fu the Third, who had spent days trying to find a channel at the Board and was relieved to learn that Hua had interceded, rides out early to find him — and at the temple gate hears a Board jailer in a sheepskin coat cursing that after fetching and carrying, lighting stoves and heating the kang, the hundred strings promised him have shrunk to thirty: there is no doing kindness in official service, they pull down the bridge once across the river, and he hopes never to see the man inside again. Fu urges Pincai to spend the new year at his house rather than a cold temple, and is politely refused. He has his own worry: his prefectural appointment stands at the head of the list, and of the two vacancies — Hubei, which is good, and Guizhou, which is wretched — Hubei should be his turn, but a senior candidate has filed. Pincai recommends the temple's own Tang Heshang, whose brother is clerk of the Selection Department, and describes him beforehand: no monk at all, expert in eating, drinking, whoring and gambling, a decent singer, and a specialist in curing the pox. Tang comes in on the words — a short, thick man in a blue crepe lambskin robe and yellow sash, his face full of sinister lines and his eyes lecherous — claims an old friendship between his own master and Fu's father, points to a couplet by that father carved twenty-nine years ago beside the shrine, and guarantees the Hubei post on the spot. Then his novice Deyue brings in a water pipe: fifteen or sixteen, in a squirrel-fur hood and lotus-coloured fox-lined robe with bright red shoes, and so handsome that Fu stares until he covers it with a joke about having mistaken him for a nun from the Dabei Convent, at which Pincai roars and the boy turns scarlet. Tang gives his own history cheerfully: while his senior monk lived he was kept in check, but that man neither ate nor spent, clutched his rents, was cheated of everything by a nephew, died empty-handed, and had his funeral paid for by Tang himself — so one may as well eat and enjoy, since all monks nowadays are wine-and-meat monks and discipline is a fiction; he has never concealed that he has tasted all four vices. At table Rongguan trades obscenities with him, and Deyue grows prettier with a few cups of wine. They break up at the end of the afternoon when the opium craving takes the monk, and Fu the Third rides back into the city, having entrusted his business to him again.`},34:{zh:`李元茂寻遍店铺才找到宏济寺，带来两桩消息：王家已写信给梅士燮，明年二三月要与梅子玉完姻；孙家也天天催他自己的亲事。他半年没接家中一钱，年下欠了四十多吊馆子账，衣服全当在铺里，开口便向魏聘才借四十两。聘才索性从靴掖里抖出一叠账贴：裁缝二百余吊，馆子庄子二百余吊，零星几十吊，通共五百余吊，加上寓中浇裹与新年花消非八百吊不能过去，还反劝他去与孙家两位舅子商量；元茂叹道那两个宝贝天天白吃白喝，从没作过一回东。元茂厚着脸留宿，吹烟、要果子、要点心，把四儿支使得团团转。三更时唐和尚请聘才过去密议：吏部两个缺同日到，湖北在前，只消把贵州挪到前头便成——横竖一倒转也是作弊；他兄弟说与富三爷素无交情，不犯把这大情白送人，贵州一任抵不得湖北一年，索价三千吊，合银不过一千二百两，而那缺一到任就有两万银子的现成规矩。又叮嘱先把银票存进铺子、写明日子，免得事后长短。

次日聘才进城，把三千两的口气报作二千四百两，富三爷应了；无奈已是二十六，银号里说年下银根紧，勉强开了一千四百两，聘才还教分作三张写；另两家不肯，富三爷只得回家与三奶奶商量，拿四十两金叶、一对金镯并零星金器约六十两，到生铺子换出一千两的票子，也分作三张。聘才回寺交割，唐和尚笑说"这二百两是我们两人应得的，只给他一千就够了"。元茂仍赖着不走，青天白日在庙里开灯吹烟，聘才发作了一顿，末了被他缠不过，取出一只三两六钱的扭丝金镯，叫他拿去当一百多吊。二十九日富三爷果然得了湖北，欢天喜地来道谢；票子交与经承时又被扣去二百"拉纤提缆"之费，唐和尚独得，另将二百中的一百分与聘才，聘才前后实得一千三百两，账目一清，过年便阔绰起来：除夕接了玉天仙到寺中守岁，请唐和尚过来，早早关了山门，一夜花炮不绝；唐和尚怕元旦有人来行香撞见，天未明便催车送她回去。初一拜年，初二听戏，初三大排筵席，请冯子佩、杨梅窗、乌大傻一班浮浪子弟并一群下作相公，连闹五日去了三百吊。初九又开赌：聘才作头家，玉天仙坐内室收头钱；一人传两、两人传三，第三日引来奚十一与潘其观两个大赌客。

头一日聘才赢二百余金、收头钱一百八十两；次日潘其观坐庄，两叵罗松江锭足足一千两，五十滩下来输个精光还倒贴一百余两，聘才输三百，唐和尚赢一百，冯子佩赢四百，奚十一独赢八百五十余两，赏了小旦、小使五十余两，又看上小和尚得月，赏他十个中锭。唐经承提醒门外有像坊里人的在交头接耳，聘才已是惊弓之鸟；唐和尚却说不妨，聘才住房旁边便是两三亩菜园，种菜的另有门户，院中原有一重门被他封锁着，一旦有事开了门从菜园出去，是极旷野的地方。聘才顺势收场，只说要为将行的富三爷饯别，定在十四。是夜他与冯子佩同榻，说出满腹牢骚：华光宿、徐子云一班人挥金如土，偏把相公们看得与自己一样；田春航一辈书呆又抬得越高，连句笑话也说不得；那些相公装假斯文，油不油醋不醋，不是与这个同心便是与那个知己，见了人连安也不请、奴才也不称，倒要讲究琴棋书画——身分都被这班书呆子作坏了。冯子佩帮腔说，若长此下去谁还叫他们？随即荐了两个又便宜又听使唤、相貌可与袁宝珠、苏蕙芳并肩的人：卓天香与张翠官——却原来是"整容班"，即软篷子里的小剃头。十四日聘才发帖请客，贵芬病了、张仲雨有事，便补上冯子佩与唐和尚，蓉官陪富三爷，天香、翠官坐着大骡车来。

潘其观一见便笑"你们怎么也跑来了"，奚十一说"魏大爷要开篷子作掌柜的了"，富三爷这才明白他们不是班子里的。风水先生杨八也踏雪而来，说替内城朋友看了一天阳宅，又被邀去看地，"不过是想外放"；富三爷打趣他小心像乌大傻那件事，看完了人家找上门来。冯子佩忽然头晕呕吐，先回去了。入夜点起聘才新制的玻璃灯屏，上画二十四出春画，奚十一与潘其观指着画互相取笑，富三爷看到末一幅两个和尚，大笑说"真是对景挂画"，唐和尚只合掌念"罪过"。席上奚十一灌得月皮杯，一手不老实；杨八抱着天香颠簸，又打听他妹子的相貌；富三爷则拉过翠官问他愿不愿跟到湖北，翠官说学徒三年已满，去便去得，靠在他怀里叫"亲爸爸"；杨八在旁吃醋，索性求富三爷在告帮单上再添一笔，富三爷写了三十两，他便连连道谢。杨八初学吹烟，被胡子挡着口涎直流，签子乱戳，皮袖子在灯上烧了一块，惹得哄堂。末了奚十一烟尽，巴英官气忿忿把大金盒一扔滚到灯边，得月去取时碰翻了灯，玻璃罩摔碎，油溅了奚十一一脸。聘才说自己房里还有几两老土，遣四儿去取——四儿到院门口，锁虽开着，门却被里面一块石头顶住；进屋一看，大皮箱少了一个，炕上两个拜匣、一个衣包也不见了，忙嚷出来："老爷！被窃了！"`,en:`Li Yuanmao runs Wei Pincai to earth at the Hongji Temple with two pieces of news: the Wang family has written to Mei Shixie to hold Mei Ziyu's wedding in the second or third month, and the Sun family presses daily for his own. Not a cash has come from home in half a year; he owes forty strings at restaurants, his clothes are all in pawn, and he opens by asking for forty taels. Pincai draws a sheaf of bills from his boot instead: two hundred odd strings to tailors, as much again to restaurants and shops, tens more in oddments, above five hundred in all — and with household costs and the new year he cannot get through under eight hundred; let Yuanmao try his brothers-in-law, and Yuanmao sighs that those two treasures eat and drink free and have never stood a dinner in their lives. Yuanmao stays the night regardless, smoking opium and calling for fruit and pastries until Si'er is run off his feet. At the third watch Tang Heshang calls Pincai in to a scheme: the two vacancies reach the Board on the same day with Hubei first, and it needs only the moving of Guizhou to the head of the list — reversing them being no more a fraud than leaving them; his brother owes Fu the Third nothing and will not give away so large a favour, since a whole term in Guizhou is not worth a year of Hubei; the price is three thousand strings, a bare twelve hundred taels against the twenty thousand in established perquisites that come with the post. Get the draft lodged at a shop with a date on it, he adds, or people renege afterwards. Next day Pincai reports the figure to Fu the Third as two thousand four hundred taels and is accepted; but it is already the twenty-sixth, and the silver-bureau manager, pleading the year-end squeeze, will issue only fourteen hundred — which Pincai has written as three separate notes. 

Two other bureaus refuse, so Fu goes home, consults his wife, and turns forty taels of gold leaf, a pair of bracelets and sundry gold, some sixty taels in all, into a thousand-tael draft at an unfamiliar shop, again split three ways. Back at the temple Tang laughs as he takes the papers: two hundred of it belongs to the pair of them, and a thousand will do for his brother. Yuanmao, still there, is smoking in broad daylight in a temple, which earns him a scolding; but he cannot be shaken off, and gets a twisted gold bracelet of three taels six to pawn for a hundred strings. On the twenty-ninth Fu the Third duly receives Hubei and comes to give thanks; when the drafts pass to the clerk another two hundred is docked as a towing fee, which Tang keeps, giving Pincai one hundred of the remainder — so that Pincai clears thirteen hundred taels, settles every debt, and keeps a lavish new year. On the last night of the year Yu Tianxian is brought to the temple, Tang is invited to sit up, the gates are shut early and firecrackers go all night; before dawn Tang hurries her away lest worshippers see her. The first day is for calls, the second for the theatre, and on the third a great banquet gathers Feng Zipei, Yang Meichuang, Wu Dasha and a crowd of low performers for five days and three hundred strings. On the ninth the gambling opens, Pincai as banker and Yu Tianxian in the inner room taking the house cut; by the third day the room is packed and two heavy purses have arrived, Xi Shiyi and Pan Qiguan. 

The first day Pincai wins two hundred odd taels and a hundred and eighty in house money; the next, Pan Qiguan banks with two baskets of Songjiang ingots, a full thousand taels, and after fifty rounds loses all of it and a hundred more, while Pincai drops three hundred, Tang wins a hundred, Feng Zipei four hundred, and Xi Shiyi eight hundred and fifty, of which he scatters fifty among the boys and pages and gives the young monk Deyue ten ingots, having taken a fancy to him. Tang's brother the clerk warns that two men were whispering at the gate who looked like ward officers, and Pincai, twice shy, takes fright; but Tang points out that beside his rooms lies a vegetable garden of two or three mu with its own gate, and a connecting door he had locked for propriety's sake, through which one may escape into open country. Pincai closes the tables anyway, pleading a farewell dinner for Fu the Third on the fourteenth. That night, sharing a bed with Feng Zipei, he empties his grievance: Hua Guangsu, Xu Ziyun and their kind scatter money like great lords and yet treat actors as equals; Tian Chunhang and the bookish set have hoisted them so high that one cannot make a joke; and the actors themselves put on a false refinement, neither oil nor vinegar, forever being one man's soulmate and another's confidant, never bowing, never calling themselves servants, and insisting on painting, calligraphy, chess and the qin — their standing quite spoiled by scholars. Feng agrees that if it goes on nobody will call for them at all, and recommends two who are cheap, biddable, and as handsome as Yuan Baozhu or Su Huifang: Zhuo Tianxiang and Zhang Cuiguan — of the "grooming troupe," which proves to mean apprentice barbers from a canvas-awning stall. On the fourteenth the invitations go out; Gui Fen is ill and Zhang Zhongyu engaged, so Feng Zipei and Tang fill the places, Rongguan is called to attend Fu the Third, and the two barbers arrive by mule cart. Pan Qiguan laughs to see them — how did you two get here? — and Xi Shiyi says Master Wei is opening a barber's stall, at which Fu the Third understands at last that they are not of any troupe. 

The geomancer Yang Eight arrives late from a day of inspecting a house and grave sites for men in the inner city who want provincial posts, and Fu warns him not to end like the Wu Dasha business, where the clients came looking for him afterwards. Feng Zipei is suddenly sick, flushed and feverish, and is sent home. At dusk Pincai's new glass lamp-screen is set out, painted with twenty-four erotic scenes; Xi and Pan trade jokes over it, and Fu roars at the last panel of two monks that here is a picture matched to its setting, while Tang joins his palms and murmurs about sin. At table Xi forces wine on Deyue with a hand where it should not be; Yang Eight bounces Tianxiang on his knee, drinks mouth to mouth with him and asks coarse questions about his sister; and Fu the Third asks Cuiguan whether he will come to Hubei, at which the boy — his three years' apprenticeship up, and free to go — leans against him calling him his own dear father. Yang Eight, jealous, asks instead for another subscription toward buying himself a posting, and is given thirty taels and told not to sulk. His first attempt at the opium pipe is a disaster of moustache, drool, a blocked stem, and a fur sleeve burnt through on the lamp. At the last Xi Shiyi's box runs empty, Ba Yingguan throws it down in a temper so that it rolls to the lamp, and Deyue in reaching for it overturns the lamp, smashing the glass shade and splashing oil across Xi's face and lynx coat. Pincai remembers some old opium in his own room and sends Si'er for it — and Si'er finds the courtyard door unlocked but wedged from within by a stone, and inside, one great leather trunk gone, and with it two document cases and a bundle of clothes. He runs out shouting that they have been robbed.`},35:{zh:`月明如昼，众人开了封锁的院门到菜园里搜寻，在西北角只寻着一只空箱，内剩几件棉衣，地下一块洋表已踏得粉碎；种菜的老夫妇吓得发抖，那新雇的伙计却不见影踪。唐和尚疑他通同引贼，把老夫妇绑了交街兵看守。魏聘才清点：箱内七件细毛衣服、十五两金子、二百两银子，拜匣内三十余两散碎银、二两鸦片并几件玉器，衣包内是大毛衣服，通共一千余金；所幸讹富三爷的银票另放在别处。天已开城，众客纷纷告辞，蓉官偏在此时才坐车来，见了卓天香、张翠官，冷笑一声说"既然大家要散，我也回去，还要叫剃头的剃头呢"，一弯腰竟自去了，两个剃头的甚是局促，聘才各赏四两银子。

次日报了失单，坊里审出种菜的实系不知情；那伙计姓蔡，年底新来，本在二荤铺打杂，睡后即不见，遂知会九城严缉，送部备案。这边正月十三至十五，王恂、颜仲清、刘文泽、田春航都在怡园赏灯，梅子玉去了一天，想起去年此日初见杜琴言，今年竟如隔世，独自伤感。十七日王恂、仲清飞札来说：明日是袁宝珠生日，徐子云请名士名旦辰刻齐集怡园。萧次贤把席设在新辟的红茶仙馆——那地方原是梅崦之前、海棠春圃之后的空地，只一亭子，两块英州灵石，一高一丈二尺，一高四尺余；大石畔一株合抱的玉兰，小石边一株六尺高的千层宝珠山茶，开出千朵红花。就着两树起了二十四间房，墙上嵌十二个花神；次贤取那山茶与宝珠同名之意，故择此处，又嫌平日酒菜不能翻新，三日前亲手检点器皿，每样菜开一张做法，油盐酱醋各有分量，配成三十二样。是日到的名旦除宝珠外只有苏蕙芳、陆素兰、李玉林、金漱芳四人，名士则怡园主客之外有刘文泽、颜仲清、王恂、田春航、梅子玉，共十二人。风转东南，梅崦数百枝梅花齐放，俨然瑶台雪圃。子玉在廊上见新嵌一方石刻，是首五言绝句："春已随年转，花如人返魂。

料他惜花客，坐月到黄昏。"子云说破：这是琴言近日怀梅崦之作，宝珠抄了出来，他便刻上，也是望梅止渴的意思——"真是花是人非，吾兄尚忆去年否"，几句话说得子玉悲酸难禁，默默走开；宝珠说已搜得琴言二十余首，他却总不肯给人看。席上器皿件件新奇：四角四把银壶，壶心装着银桔槔与银索，左旋则酒自流出，右旋即止；又有两只镀金小杯，底座如钟轮，下装四个小车轮，说敬谁便走到谁面前，一滴不洒。众人称奇而不解，蕙芳先看破座中一枚极小的指针，子玉继而悟出，还看出次贤"近处斟浅、远处斟满"，是要借重量致远，满座叹服他二人聪慧。先行刘文泽的令：十二人各飞一句唐诗，依次嵌一至十二的数目，数到第几字便由第几人饮，自"梅花柳絮一时新"起，历"愿陪鸾鹤回三山""刺绣五纹添弱线""门前才下七香车""宝扇迎归九华帐"，直到"南陌青楼十二重""娉娉袅袅十三余"，末由蕙芳以"年初十五最风流"收令。次贤又取出一筒牙筹，大筹刻"百美捧觞"四隶字：每人掣一枝，得一美人名，先集唐诗二句，把名字上下两字分嵌上下句；再集《毛诗》二句凑成花名——首字相凑为并头花，末字相凑为并蒂花，一末一首相接为连理花，居中相对为含蕊花，参差不对为参差花，各有自饮与贺酒之数。

众人嫌苦，子云便另出一"甜令"给五旦：两颗骰子，么为月、二为星、三为雁、四为人、五为梅、六为天，掷出何色便集曲文两句，一句一色，次序不得颠倒。于是一席两令，交相辉映。文泽掣"玉环"，得"玉钩帘下影沉沉，环佩空归月下魂"，又以"鸡既鸣矣，冠绥双止"凑成鸡冠并头花；仲清掣"朝云"，成"朝朝暮暮阳台下，云雨荒台岂梦思"，以"我其夙夜，妻子好合"凑夜合并蒂花；王恂凑出"奉时辰牡，颜如渥丹"的牡丹，被仲清取笑"菜还没上一半，烧猪倒先拿出来"，又被次贤指出记错了作者，罚酒一杯；田春航掣"红拂"，子玉掣"洛神"，成"还似洛妃乘雾去，更凝神女弄珠游"，众人推为一日之冠，又以"月出皎兮，季女思饥"凑成月季并头花，共贺三杯；次贤掣"夜来"，以"既见君子，吉日庚午"凑成子午花，而是日恰是庚午；子云掣"莲香"，久久不肯轻说，末了用温飞卿《采莲曲》二句，被次贤指出一字不对，宝珠听了却看他一眼，若有所思。那边骰令更见天巧：蕙芳掷星梅得《访素》之句，素兰掷月人得《琴挑》之句，玉林掷《闻铃》《絮阁》，漱芳"月移花影，疑是玉人来"，蕙芳又以《偷诗》"恨无眠残月窗西，更难听孤雁嘹呖"惊倒子玉；素兰掷重四，一句《窥浴》的"两人合一付肠和胃"，仲清拍案叫作天籁；宝珠掷重二，念《密誓》的"问双星，朝朝暮暮，争似我和卿"，众人一齐看他，他红了脸垂头微哂；末了漱芳掷重四，仍用《窥浴》的"意中人，人中意"作结，把满座人都收在里头，众人依字数各贺六杯，方才吃饭。名士们纷纷认输，说词出佳人之口，句句贴切，一杯罚酒也没有，倒被他们讨了几十杯贺酒去。亥末席散，五旦直送到园门。次贤对子云说，明日要把这两个令刻出去传到外间，也教人费些心思，免得总是猜拳打擂的混闹。`,en:`Under a moon bright as day the household opens the sealed courtyard door and searches the vegetable garden, finding in the northwest corner only an emptied trunk with a few cotton clothes left in it, and on the ground a foreign watch trampled to fragments; the old gardener and his wife shake with fright, and the newly hired hand has vanished. Suspecting collusion, Tang Heshang has the couple bound and given into the street watch's keeping. Wei Pincai reckons his loss: seven fine-fur garments, fifteen taels of gold and two hundred of silver from the trunk, thirty-odd taels of loose silver, two taels of opium and some jade from the cases, heavy furs from the bundle — above a thousand taels in all, though by luck the drafts squeezed out of Fu the Third lay elsewhere. The gates open, the guests scatter, and Rongguan arrives by carriage only now; seeing Zhuo Tianxiang and Zhang Cuiguan he laughs coldly, says that since everyone is leaving he may as well go too, having a barber to send for, bows, and departs — leaving the two barbers awkward and Pincai four taels the poorer for each. 

Next day the loss is reported; the ward office finds the gardener innocent, and the hand, one Cai, hired at the year's end from an eating-house, went missing after lying down, so the nine gates are alerted and the case sent up. Meanwhile, from the thirteenth to the fifteenth, Wang Xun, Yan Zhongqing, Liu Wenze, and Tian Chunhang have been at Yiyuan among the lanterns; Mei Ziyu went for one day, and remembering that on this day last year he first saw Du Qinyan — a year now like another life — grieved alone. On the seventeenth a note comes: tomorrow is Yuan Baozhu's birthday, and Xu Ziyun gathers scholars and performers at the garden at the hour of the dragon. Xiao Cixian sets the table at the newly opened Red Camellia Hall — waste ground until last year, in front of the Plum Slope and behind the crabapple court, holding only a pavilion and two Yingzhou spirit-stones, one twelve feet and one four; by the greater stands a magnolia more than an armspan round, by the lesser a six-foot thousand-layered camellia of the kind called Precious Pearl, bearing a thousand red flowers. Twenty-four rooms were raised around the two trees, and twelve flower-deities set into the walls; Cixian chose the place because the camellia bears Baozhu's own name, and, weary of unvarying food, spent three days going over the vessels himself and writing out a method for every dish, the oil, salt, sauce and vinegar all measured, until he had composed thirty-two. Several performers are engaged elsewhere, so besides Baozhu only Su Huifang, Lu Sulan, Li Yulin, and Jin Shufang come; with Liu Wenze, Yan Zhongqing, Wang Xun, Tian Chunhang, Mei Ziyu and the two hosts they are twelve. The wind turns southeast, and hundreds of plum trees at the Slope open at once like a terrace of jade snow. On the gallery Ziyu finds a newly inset stone bearing a quatrain: spring has turned with the year, the flowers are like a soul come back, and he fancies that flower-loving guest sitting under the moon until dusk. Ziyun explains that it is Qinyan's recent poem remembering the Plum Slope, copied out by Baozhu and carved by himself — plums gazed at to quench thirst — and asks whether his friend remembers last year; and Ziyu, unable to bear it, walks away without a word, while Baozhu says he has collected above twenty of Qinyan's poems, which their author will never show. 

The vessels are all new-made marvels: four silver pots at the corners, each with a silver well-sweep and chain inside, so that a turn of the lid to the left starts the wine and a turn to the right stops it; and two small gilt cups on clockwork bases with four little wheels, which roll to whichever guest is named without spilling a drop. Nobody can find the trick until Huifang notices the tiny needle set in the base like a compass; Ziyu works it out next, and adds that Cixian pours shallow for a near seat and full for a far one, using weight to carry distance — at which the table concedes them both the day. Wenze's game comes first: twelve Tang lines, each containing the numbers one through twelve in order, the position of the numeral naming the drinker, running from "plum blossom and willow catkin, all new at once" through wishing to ride the phoenix back to the three peaks, five embroidered threads, the seven-scented carriage, the nine-flowered curtain, to the twelve storeys of the green tower and the slender girl of thirteen, until Huifang closes it with the fifteenth day of the new year being the most beguiling of all. Then Cixian produces an ivory tube whose great slip is carved with four clerical characters, the Hundred Beauties Bearing Cups. Each guest draws a beauty's name, matches it with two Tang lines that embed its two characters, and then builds from the *Mao Shi* a pair of lines whose characters compose a flower: joined at the head, joined at the stem, twined at the root, holding a bud in the middle, or standing uneven — each pattern with its own tally of cups to drink and cups to be toasted. The company protests at the difficulty, so Ziyun sets the five performers an easier game with two dice: one is moon, two star, three wild goose, four person, five plum, six heaven; whatever falls must be answered with two lines of opera in the right order. 

So the table runs two games at once. Wenze draws Yuhuan and produces a jade hook and a girdle-pendant returning empty under the moon, then a cockscomb from the *Mao Shi*; Zhongqing draws Morning Cloud and matches dawn and dusk beneath the terrace with rain and cloud on the ruined height, then a silk-tree flower; Wang Xun contrives a peony from two lines so oddly juxtaposed that Zhongqing jokes the roast pig has arrived before half the dishes, and Cixian catches him misattributing his Tang line and fines him a cup. Chunhang draws Hongfu; and Ziyu, drawing the Luo River Goddess, gives lines of the goddess riding away on mist and the river nymph at play with her pearls, which the table judges the finest of the day, and then a China rose out of the *Mao Shi*, worth three cups. Cixian draws Night-Come and finds a flower whose name is the cyclical pair of the very day; Ziyun, drawing Lotus Fragrance, holds back a long while and is finally caught by Cixian on a single mismatched tone in his lines from Wen Tingyun's *Lotus-Picking Song* — at which Baozhu glances at him, thinking his own thoughts. On the other side the dice bring out still finer things: Huifang from *Visiting Su*, Sulan from *Flirting with a Zither*, Yulin from *Hearing the Bells* and *The Pavilion of Gossamer*, Shufang with moonlight moving the flower shadows as if a beloved were coming; then Huifang again, from *Stealing the Poem*, on sleepless resentment at the waning moon west of the window and the unbearable cry of a lone goose, which leaves Ziyu abashed. Sulan throws a double four and answers with a line from *Peeking at the Bath* about two people sharing one set of bowels, which Zhongqing strikes the table to call the music of heaven; Baozhu throws a double two and quotes *The Secret Vow* — asking the paired stars whether morning and evening together can match him and his love — at which everyone turns to look, and he reddens and bows his head; and Shufang closes with another line from *Peeking at the Bath*, the one in the heart and the heart in the one, which gathers every person present inside it, and is honoured with six cups apiece before dinner. The scholars concede defeat outright: words from a beauty's mouth, they say, and not one forfeit paid all evening, while the performers have collected scores of toasts. The party breaks up at the end of the hai hour, the five seeing them to the garden gate; and Cixian tells Ziyun he means to have both games carved and circulated outside, so that people may be made to exert their wits instead of brawling over finger-games.`},36:{zh:`元宵之夜华光宿命杜琴言与八龄班同演，琴言身子不快又感伤旧事，演到情深处竟真哭起来；华公子只当他无故生悲，痛斥了一番，几日不叫他上去。正月二十七日，他独坐留青精舍后院的太湖石畔，对着两株绿萼梅、一株红梅出神：想徐子云待他，即有伺候不到处也不形于色、不藏于心，反百般体贴；而华公子喜时与子云彷佛，不合意时不是发烦便是挑斥，元宵那日竟与诸奴一同诟斥。主儿多叫一回，同伙便多恨一回；主儿多赏一回，同伙便多骂一回。他恨奚十一无故闹上门来，才把自己逼落此地；又想魏聘才虽不是好人，倒还说过一两句道着心事的话；至于林珊枝，俨然半个主子，须小心奉承才罢。若得做个清白人，饥寒一世也甘愿；不然先做戏子后做奴才，还有谁看得起？正伤心间，珊枝进来说破：师傅曹长庆昨夜睡下，今早已死了，公子准他回去送殓，只嘱不要耽搁。琴言竟认不得出府的路，由小使指点着走出头门，长庆师娘的表弟伍麻子已带着车等在照墙边。

到家时篷已搭起，天寿迎出，师娘在里头呜咽。长庆衣衾已具，帕子蒙面；琴言跪在床前痛哭。师娘说他并无病，吹烟到三更还讲了好些话，醒来一摸已冷；又诉苦：一辈子挣的钱不知用到哪里去了，去年过年就不宽余，两个师弟十天倒闲八天，临终又没一言半语，外头的往来一概不知，胡衕外两所房子收不得多少租，衣衾棺木搭篷勉强办了，到买地下葬只怕就拮据。琴言把随身一包银子——大小十五锭约五六十两，都是府中年下的赏赐——尽数送她。师娘掂了掂，点了数，收进抽屉，说自己无儿无女，将来还要靠他。袁宝珠、苏蕙芳、陆素兰随后来吊，一屋子低声议论：素兰劝他总要告一个月假，等出殡才回，不然对不住师娘；蕙芳索性劝他告长假不去，"究竟你也不是卖与他们的"；宝珠说那里好倒也好，只是拘束，同事中没一个知心的人。说到林珊枝，蕙芳最不平：当日唱乱弹时不过油腔滑调哄那些不会听戏的人，一进华府就像做了官，见了同辈大模大样，偏又与自己同岁；宝珠说他如今要人叫"三爷"，叫"三哥"便爱理不理，只在八龄班面前充老手，当年王兰保要打他的旧恨至今记着。琴言也道好笑：他自己就是长庆的徒弟，今日偏说"你的师傅死了"——难道出了师便不算师傅？问起魏聘才，蕙芳说他如今更阔，住在宏济寺，同奚十一、潘其观、杨八一班人天天开赌宿娼，连张仲雨都不与他往来了；问起梅子玉，宝珠便把怡园那日的酒壶、走杯与刻在梅崦的那首诗、子玉见诗时的神色一一说了，琴言听着心酸。

众人约他叙聚，他说师傅新丧于理有碍，末了议定三日后。王桂保、李玉林、金漱芳也来了，直等送殓才散。伍麻子往华府代告一月之假，公子准了，还捎回一个衣箱。接三那日请了叶茂林张罗，各班脚色、街邻与馆子里的人挤了满屋。次日宝珠传信：怡园的雅集改在后日辰刻。琴言闲坐无事，想去看子玉——记得去年颜夫人待他的光景，谅无妨碍；转念又怕独自登门须向门房通报，万一碰了钉子反进不去，那家的人除云儿外一个也不认得。想来想去，索性先到宏济寺找魏聘才同去。

谁知一进院子，满屋子的人：奚十一、潘其观、唐和尚并小和尚得月，还有两个班里的。聘才当众夸他是"第一个有名的杜大相公"；唐和尚合掌乱拜，潘其观趁势一把拉住他的手，说"我的琴大太爷，今日真是有缘千里来相会"，琴言含羞含怒挣脱；奚十一按住他两肩笑道：去年夏天我来找你，你分明在家不肯见，后来与你师傅闹起来，你从后门跑了，这才进的华府——这不是我作成你的么？今日见了，该谢谢我。琴言方知是他，战战兢兢。听得琴言要聘才陪他到梅宅去，奚十一便定下计策：先假意告辞，把聘才拉到一边，说自己实在爱他，明请必定不来，只求聘才留他吃饭，自己再闯进来同坐一坐，"虽不能怎样，也就完了这件心事"；聘才明知他的脾气，却却不过情面，只叮嘱不许撒村动粗。奚十一先打发人送来四样菜两样点心，托词"从前不知道，冒犯了，深自懊悔"，聘才便代做主人，赏了来人五百钱。他又飞车回寓，吹了三十口大烟，盘算送什么礼：怀里的八大件钢镶表值二百吊，究竟不够体面，索性到白菊花房里开箱取了那对京中一时买不出来的翡翠镯子，也不盖箱盖便走，只说"比一比颜色就拿回来"。潘其观在唐和尚房里听得此计，垂涎到咬牙捶颈。

席上聘才百般说些"庾香近来不出门赴席，常托我劝你放宽了心，慢慢结交华公子自然可以常见面"的话，只为拖住他等人。奚十一一到，便执壶擎杯直送到琴言嘴边，又拉住不许他走，说"你家公子是我嫡嫡亲亲的世叔，你既是他心爱的人，就算我的小兄弟"；夹一条海参过来还带着秽语，琴言眉稍微竖，聘才只得用脚踢他。潘其观与唐和尚随后闯进，潘其观抢过琴言的杯子一口干了，把杯口在嘴唇上擦了一转，斟了半杯双手送来——琴言忍无可忍，接过便装作失手，"当"的一声砸得粉碎，只说一句"我冒失了"。唐和尚更不知好歹，抬出华府是本寺大施主的旧情，把酒杯顶在光头上双膝跪下，两手靠在他膝上不住念佛，笑得满座捧腹，琴言无法只得喝了一口，又怕人喝他的剩酒，索性干了。及至起身要走，奚十一推住、和尚抱腿，琴言才厉声正色道："今日请教各位，待要怎样？"末了奚十一取出锦匣，一对透水全绿的翡翠镯光华射目，潘其观伸舌头说城里一千吊也找不出来；琴言正色说断不敢受，又说自己从不带镯子，无心伸出一只手来示意，奚十一误当他肯了，一把攥住膀子用力一拽，琴言站立不稳跌进他怀里，被他抱住闻了脸，硬套上一个。趁他去取第二只时琴言挣脱，泪流满面，也顾不得吉凶祸福，哭喊道："我又不认识你！我如今改了行，你还当我相公看待，糟蹋我，我回去告诉我主人再来和你说话！"跑到院子里除下镯子用力一砸，断成三段，没命地去了。奚十一暴跳如雷要追，被聘才用头顶住死命劝住，潘其观与唐和尚还在旁边火上添油。巴英官却捡起碎镯，在掌中拼好，笑说花三钱银子镶成三截，自己也带个三镶翡翠镯——"一千吊的镯子，如今倒直三千吊了"，气得奚十一直骂。琴言上了车放下帘子，一路掩面悲泣，到家便脱了外褂卧下，只怨自己发昏去找聘才，惹出这场祸来，蒙头哭了半日，几乎要寻短见。`,en:`On the night of the Lantern Festival Hua Guangsu orders Du Qinyan to perform with the boys' troupe; unwell and full of old griefs, he weeps in earnest when the part turns sorrowful, and Hua, taking it for gloom without cause, rebukes him roundly and does not call for him again for days. On the twenty-seventh of the first month he sits alone by the Taihu stones in the courtyard behind the Liuqing Study, gazing at two green-calyx plums and one red one. Xu Ziyun, he remembers, never let a lapse of service show in his face or lodge in his heart, and answered it with kindness; Hua in good humour is much the same, but when displeased is either irritable or fault-finding, and at the festival abused him among the servants. The oftener the master calls for him, the more his fellows hate him; the oftener he is rewarded, the more they curse him. He hates Xi Shiyi, whose unprovoked assault drove him into this house; allows that Wei Pincai, bad as he is, once said a word or two that touched his case; and finds Lin Shanzhi half a master in himself, to be flattered carefully or feared. If he could only be a clean man he would accept a lifetime of hunger and cold — but who respects one who was first an actor and then a servant? Into this grief Shanzhi comes with the news: his master Cao Changqing lay down last night and was dead this morning, and the young lord permits him to go and see to the encoffining, provided he does not linger. Qinyan does not even know the way out and must be guided past the gatehouse, where Wu Mazi, his teacher's wife's cousin, waits with the familiar carriage. 

At the house the matting shed is already up, Tianshou runs out, and the widow is sobbing within. Changqing lies dressed with a cloth over his face, and Qinyan kneels and weeps. There was no illness, she says: he smoked until past the third watch and talked as he lay down, and when she woke he was cold. Then her troubles — a lifetime's earnings gone nobody knows where, last new year already tight, the two junior disciples idle eight days in ten, no last word from him and no account of his outside dealings, so that neither what is owed nor what is owing can be known; the two houses beyond the lane fetch little rent; shroud, coffin and shed are managed, but ground and burial will pinch. Qinyan gives her the whole packet of silver he brought, fifteen ingots great and small, some fifty or sixty taels of new-year gifts from the mansion; she weighs it, counts it, puts it away, and says that being childless she must depend on him. Yuan Baozhu, Su Huifang, and Lu Sulan come to condole, and the talk runs low and frank. Sulan urges him to ask a full month, until after the burial, or he wrongs the widow; Huifang says he should ask indefinite leave and never go back, since he was after all not sold to them; Baozhu allows the place is good enough, only confining, with not one congenial soul among the staff. On Lin Shanzhi, Huifang is bitterest: when he sang luantan he was all slick patter for people who could not tell good singing, and since entering the Hua house he carries himself like an official and looks down on his own kind — though he is Huifang's own age. Baozhu adds that he now expects to be called Third Master and barely answers to Third Brother, swaggers only before the boys' troupe, and still nurses the grudge from the day Wang Lanbao nearly thrashed him. 

Qinyan finds his own grievance comic: Shanzhi is a disciple of this very house, and yet said "your master is dead" — as though graduating cancelled a master. Of Wei Pincai, Huifang reports that he is grander than ever at the Hongji Temple, gambling and whoring daily with Xi Shiyi, Pan Qiguan, Yang Eight and their sort, until even Zhang Zhongyu has dropped him. Of Mei Ziyu, Baozhu describes the day at Yiyuan, the self-pouring pots and rolling cups, the poem carved at the Plum Slope, and Ziyu's face when he read it — and Qinyan's heart turns over. They press him to a gathering; he objects that his master is only just dead, and it is settled for three days on. Wang Guibao, Li Yulin, and Jin Shufang come too, and all stay for the encoffining. Wu Mazi carries the request for a month's leave, which is granted, with a trunk of clothes sent back. On the third-day rites Ye Maolin manages the arrangements, and the room is packed with players of every troupe, neighbours, and restaurant men. Next morning Baozhu sends word that the Yiyuan gathering is put off a day. 

Idle, Qinyan thinks of visiting Ziyu, remembering Madam Yan's kindness — then hesitates, since going alone he must announce himself at the gatehouse and may be turned away, and he knows nobody in that household but Yun'er. So he decides to collect Wei Pincai first, and walks into a room full of men: Xi Shiyi, Pan Qiguan, Tang Heshang with his novice Deyue, and two others from a troupe. Pincai presents him grandly as the most famous of all young performers; the monk bows over joined palms; Pan Qiguan seizes his hand, calling him his own great master and saying that a thousand li have brought them together, until Qinyan wrenches free in shame and fury; and Xi Shiyi presses him down by both shoulders and says he owes him thanks — for it was he who came looking last summer when Qinyan would not appear, he who quarrelled with the master and sent him fleeing out the back gate and into the Hua mansion. Hearing that Qinyan wants Pincai's company to the Mei house, Xi frames his plan: he takes his leave, draws Pincai aside, admits he loves the boy, knows an open invitation would be refused, and asks only that Pincai keep him to dinner so that he may burst in and sit with him — nothing more, and hardly an outrage. Pincai, who knows the temper he is dealing with, cannot refuse and only stipulates no coarseness and no rough handling. Xi sends over four dishes and two pastries with a coached message of apology for past offence, and Pincai plays host and tips the messenger five hundred cash. Then Xi races home, smokes thirty pipes, and considers his gift: the eight-piece steel-set watch at his waist is worth two hundred strings but not grand enough, so he opens White Chrysanthemum's box, takes out a pair of jadeite bracelets not to be bought in the capital, and walks off without shutting the lid, saying only that he means to compare their colour. Pan Qiguan, told the plan in the monk's room, drools and pummels his own neck with impatience. At table Pincai spins soothing talk — that Yuxiang goes to no parties or plays now, and keeps sending word that Qinyan should set his mind at rest and cultivate Hua Guangsu so that meetings may become easy — all to keep him seated. 

Xi arrives, pours, and pushes the cup to his lips; when Qinyan rises to go he holds him fast, arguing that the young lord is his own nephew by seniority, so that his favourite is a younger brother to Xi himself; then offers him sea cucumber with an obscenity that makes Qinyan's brows go up and earns Xi a kick under the table. Pan Qiguan and the monk burst in next; Pan snatches Qinyan's cup, drains it, wipes the rim along his lip, refills it half full and holds it to his mouth — and Qinyan, taking it, lets it slip and smashes it, apologizing for his clumsiness. Tang is worse: invoking the Hua mansion as the temple's great patron, he balances a cup on his shaven head, kneels with both hands on Qinyan's knees, and chants the Buddha's name until the room is helpless with laughter and Qinyan drinks one mouthful — then drains the rest rather than let anyone drink his leavings. When he stands to go, Xi pushes him back and the monk clasps his legs, and at last he says levelly: I ask you, gentlemen, what is it you want of me? Then Xi produces the brocade case, and a pair of clear all-green bracelets flashes out; Pan puts out his tongue and swears a thousand strings would not find their like in the city. Qinyan says gravely that he dare not accept and never wears bracelets, and without thinking holds out a bare wrist to prove it — which Xi takes for consent, gripping his arm and pulling so hard that the slight body falls into his lap, where he is held, sniffed, and fitted with one bracelet. Reaching for the second, Xi loosens his hold; Qinyan tears free in tears and cries out, past all calculation of consequences, that he does not even know this man, that he has left the profession, that to treat him still as an actor is to defile him, and that he will tell his master and settle it afterwards. He runs into the courtyard, pulls off the bracelet, and dashes it into three pieces before fleeing. Xi rages and would give chase, but Pincai holds him back bodily, offering himself to be beaten instead, while Pan and the monk feed the fire. Ba Yingguan gathers the fragments, fits them together in his palm, and says with a straight face that for three qian of silver he will have them mounted in three bands and wear a triple-set jadeite bracelet himself — a thousand-string bracelet now being worth three thousand. Qinyan rides home behind drawn blinds with his face covered, lies down without undressing, curses his own folly in ever going to Pincai, and weeps under the quilt half the day, near to wanting his life over.`},37:{zh:`琴言恨了半夜，天明尚未醒，被小使推醒，说怡园徐子云来叫，梅子玉已先到了。他头晕眼花，照镜见面貌清减，勉强上车。这日是二月初一，园中梅花未遍，茶花、玉兰正开。刘文泽、王恂是座师世兄，田春航正会同年团拜，颜仲清感冒，两处都辞；王恂辞了那边，清早同子玉先来。梅崦本作梅花样式，五间一处共五处，长廊曲槛钩连，绿萼红蕊围绕，外边望去只见一片香雪。子云倡议作十日之叙：不拘人多人少，谁空闲谁来，他若不在，萧次贤也尽可作主人。次贤因说起京中种种消遣——打牌掷骰、押宝摇摊、打锣鼓、看戏法、听盲词，甚而自家包头开脸登台唱一出，再不然青楼曲巷打情骂俏——"非但我不能，诸公谅亦不好"；子云便说天下事莫乐于饮酒看花。王恂偏出难题："人中花与花中花孰美？"子云答各有美处，又说自然人贵花贱；王恂笑道：满园都是花，谈了半日不见一个人中花来，可见你爱花不爱人。

少顷袁宝珠、王桂保到，说联锦五包堂会、联珠四包堂会，陆素兰、王兰保处处有戏，是再不能来了。午正琴言方至，比去年高了好些，一身素淡衣裳走入梅林，人花一色。子云特特要他坐首席，子玉作陪，桂保次之，苏蕙芳来了又坐在次贤之上，宝珠坐在子云之上，逊者罚酒十杯；王恂笑说外边馆子若依这坐法，便该倒贴开发。宝珠看出琴言眼睛哭肿，子云叹他多情，说长庆待他也不算好，还哭得这样。谁知琴言另有悲伤：他想魏聘才必要造谣，说他在那里陪酒、受了奚十一的镯子，不如自己说明。先问子玉几时见的聘才，子玉说去年十月见过一次，只有大年初一天明在门外打门留了个片子；琴言便骂那人始终不是个东西，随即把昨日的事细细说出——奚十一故意先走，聘才撵开潘其观，转眼送菜来，随后奚十一、潘其观、唐和尚先后闯进，席间百般戏侮，末了硬套镯子，他砸碎逃出。

子玉气得说这是聘才设的计。桂保笑说他若遇见奚十一，也要叫他看看桶子，另叫个赶车的顽顽他；次贤更以"苦肉计"取笑蕙芳，被蕙芳斟酒罚了一杯。子云问子玉与琴言同席几回，子玉说一年之内才两回，琴言笑说三回，次贤便揭破那次瞒人私逛运河。子云要请他二人作十日之欢，子玉只说恐怕福薄灾生。席上先行桂保的"一字化三"令：大加一点是太、移上去是犬；蕙芳以王、玉、主对之，桂保便笑她连上"犬"字，倒把主人骂了；此后木本未、水永、十士干、卜上下、白自百次第而出，蕙芳一个"杳查香"最得赞赏，王恂说到"曰田"便顿住，子玉说透上去是由，子云说拖下来是甲，次贤说一头往上一头往下便是申，满座大笑。蕙芳又说起今日见着一位六旬开外的老名士，是刘文泽的业师，姓氏他记作"瞿"；子云一想便知是屈道翁，子玉说他名本立，虽不相识，却常看父亲裱成册页的几封来札，论史疑难处独出卓见。次贤说道翁今年六十，曾与其亡兄同举孝廉方正，在江西作知县，去年题升通判，此来当是引见；他虽古执，笔墨却极游戏，著作之外还有《忘死集》《醒睡集》两种，集词为词、集曲为曲，末后还有对戏目的对子。

论及侯石翁，次贤断言道翁高于石翁：石翁才虽大而格不高、驳杂不纯，道翁才亦不小而格纯正，是可传之作，连石翁也很佩服他。由此子云便出"对戏目"之令：八人分作四对，互出互对，一字不工罚一杯，巧对绝对贺一杯。于是《题曲》对《教歌》，《埋玉》对《拾金》，《踏月》对《扫花》，《偷鸡》嫌纤小，改出《访鼠》以就《伏虎》之正大，《看袜》对《借靴》，《醉妃》对《醒妓》。蕙芳出一个绝难的：《女盗》一名《牝贼》；次贤想了想，以《势利》一名《势僧》相对——"势"之对"牝"，一男一女，子玉抚掌称绝，蕙芳心服，真喝了三杯。此后《拔眉》对《开眼》，次贤又说《刺目》更好；《扶头》对《切脚》，众推与《拔眉》《刺目》并称双绝；《折柳》对《扫松》，《扫秦》对《挡汉》，秦、汉与扫、挡皆天然；桂保被一个《花鼓》难住，只得认罚半杯。将散时子玉出三字的《飞熊梦》，琴言对《伏虎韬》；琴言再出四字的《卖子投渊》，子玉对《思亲罢宴》，满座拍案——子云笑说"情见乎词"，方才庾香才说回去迟了恐怕伯母见问，这正是本地风光。一日共对了三十二副，是六十四个戏目，喝了几十杯酒，各有醉意。次日又添了文泽、春航与几位名旦，再叙一日；陆素兰又单请子玉、琴言二人清谈小叙，更为有趣。一连三日，子玉心满意足而人也乏了。恰好史南湘到京，徐子云便要请屈道翁作一个诗酒大会。`,en:`Qinyan lies awake half the night in bitterness and has to be shaken out of sleep with word that Xu Ziyun has sent for him and Mei Ziyu is already at Yiyuan. Dizzy and hollow-eyed, seeing in the mirror how thin he has grown, he forces himself into the carriage. It is the first of the second month; the plums are not fully out, but the camellias and magnolias are. Liu Wenze and Wang Xun, as sons of the chief examiners, are wanted at Tian Chunhang's gathering of new graduates, and Yan Zhongqing, with a cold, has declined both invitations; only Wang Xun begs off the other party and comes early with Ziyu. The Plum Slope is laid out in the shape of a plum blossom — five clusters of five rooms, joined by long galleries and winding rails, ringed with green calyx and red fragrance, so that from outside one sees no buildings at all, only a sheet of scented snow. Ziyun proposes a ten-day gathering: no fixed company, whoever is free may come, and if he himself is out, Xiao Cixian will play host. Cixian runs through the city's amusements — cards and dice, wagering at bao and tan, gongs and drums, conjurers, blind balladeers, or hiring a troupe and then painting one's own face to go on stage, or the courtesan lanes with their painted quarrels — and concludes that not only can he not, he doubts any of them cares for such things; and Ziyun answers that nothing on earth is better than wine and flowers. Wang Xun sets his teasing question: which is lovelier, a flower among people or a person among flowers? Each has its beauty, says Ziyun; and pressed, that people are precious and flowers cheap — at which Wang Xun laughs that a garden full of flowers has produced no human flower in half a day, so their host must love flowers and not people. Yuan Baozhu and Wang Guibao arrive with the explanation: five private engagements for one troupe and four for another have swallowed everyone, and Lu Sulan and Wang Lanbao cannot come at all. 

Qinyan appears at noon, grown taller since last year, walking into the plum grove in plain pale clothes so that person and flower are one colour. Ziyun insists that he take the head of the table with Ziyu beside him, Guibao next with Wang Xun, and Su Huifang, when she comes, above Cixian and Baozhu above Ziyun, on pain of ten cups for anyone who defers — at which Wang Xun says that a restaurant seating its guests so would have to pay them to eat. Baozhu notices that Qinyan's eyes are swollen, and Ziyun sighs over so much feeling for Cao Changqing, a master who never treated him well. But Qinyan's grief is another matter. Reckoning that Wei Pincai will certainly put it about that he served wine there and accepted Xi Shiyi's bracelet, he decides to tell it himself. First he asks when Ziyu last saw Pincai — once last winter, he learns, and one banging on the gate at dawn on New Year's Day, a card left and nothing since — though Pincai had claimed three meetings this year. Then the whole story comes out: Xi Shiyi leaving on purpose, Pincai clearing Pan Qiguan out of the room, the dishes arriving with an apology, Xi Shiyi, Pan Qiguan and Tang Heshang bursting in by turns, the mockery at table, the bracelet forced onto his wrist and smashed in the courtyard. 

Ziyu, furious, says the plan was Pincai's own. Guibao jokes that if he met Xi Shiyi he would show him a barrel and set a carter on him; Cixian teases Huifang about a stratagem of self-wounding and is fined a cup for it. Ziyun asks how often Ziyu and Qinyan have sat at one table: twice in a whole year, says Ziyu; three times, says Qinyan — and Cixian exposes the secret day on the canal. Offered a ten-day festival for the two of them, Ziyu says only that he fears his luck is too thin to bear it. Guibao opens the games with one that turns a character into three: add a stroke, then move it. Huifang's version yields the character for host, which Guibao points out follows Guibao's own for dog, so that between them they have called their host a dog. Others follow — root and branch, water and eternity, ten and shield, divination and above and below — and Huifang's transformation of "dim" into "examine" into "fragrance" wins the most praise, while Wang Xun stalls halfway and the others finish his character for him three different ways, to general laughter. Then Huifang mentions meeting an old scholar of sixty and more, Liu Wenze's teacher, whose surname he has misremembered; Ziyun identifies Qu Daoweng at once, and Ziyu says that although they have never met, he often reads the letters on disputed points of history that his father had mounted as an album, each with an insight nobody else has had. 

Cixian adds that Daoweng passed the examination for the filial and upright in the same year as his own late brother, served as a magistrate in Jiangxi, was promoted last year, and must have come up for his audience; that he is old-fashioned but playful with the brush, and besides his serious work has two collections of jeux d'esprit, *Forgetting Death* and *The Awakening from Sleep Collection*, full of centos and, at the end, couplets made of play titles. Weighed against Hou Shiweng, Cixian rates Daoweng higher: Shiweng's talent is great but his standard low and his work mixed, while Daoweng's is pure and will last — as Shiweng himself acknowledges. That sets the second game: eight players in four pairs, each setting a title for his partner to match, a cup for every character out of true and a toast for anything exquisite. *Inscribing the Song* is answered by *Teaching Song*, *Burying the Jade* by *Finding Gold*, *Treading on the Moon* by *Sweeping Flowers*; *Stealing the Chicken* is judged too petty beside *Subduing the Tiger* and exchanged for *Visiting the Rat*; *Looking at the Stocking* draws *Borrowing the Boot*, and *The Drunken Consort* draws *Sobering the Courtesan*. Then Huifang sets a cruel one, *Female Thief*, known also as *Hinny Bandit* — and Cixian answers with *Power and Profit*, known also as *Powerful Monk*, so that the male member answers the female, at which Ziyu claps and Huifang drinks his three cups. *Plucking the Eyebrow* draws *Opening the Eye*, with *Piercing the Eye* proposed as better still; *Supporting the Head* draws *Cutting the Foot*, which the table ranks as a second marvel beside the first; Ziyu's *Plucking a Willow Branch* draws *Sweeping the Pine*; and Qinyan's *Sweeping Away Qin* meets Ziyu's *Checking Han*, dynasty against dynasty and verb against verb. Guibao is beaten at last by *Flower Drum*, which he cannot match, and pays his forfeit. At the close Ziyu offers a three-character title, *The Flying Bear Dream*, and Qinyan answers *The Crouching Tiger Scroll*; then Qinyan sets a four-character one, *Selling One's Son to Cast Himself into the Abyss*, and Ziyu answers *Longing for One's Parents, Abandoning the Feast* — at which the table strikes the boards, and Ziyun observes that feeling shows through the words, since Ziyu had just said he must go before his mother asked for him. Thirty-two couplets and sixty-four titles have been matched in one day, and scores of cups drunk. The next day Wenze and Chunhang and several more performers join for another gathering; then Lu Sulan invites Ziyu and Qinyan alone for a quieter and finer one. Three days together leave Ziyu content and worn out — and just then Shi Nanxiang reaches the capital, and Xu Ziyun sets about inviting Qu Daoweng to a great assembly of poetry and wine.`},38:{zh:`徐子云在梅崦请屈道翁，陪客是史南湘、颜仲清、刘文泽、田春航、梅子玉；王恂因李元茂招赘孙家吉期已定，被孙亮功托去料理，不能来。名旦因道翁好静只叫四个：袁宝珠、苏蕙芳、陆素兰，金漱芳有恙，另知会了杜琴言。书中于此细叙屈公身世：湖北武昌人，三闾大夫之后，学贯天人；父由宏词科授翰林院检讨，未满三十而卒，那时他才四岁；十六岁入学即丁祖父忧，服满又丁祖母忧，服阕后太夫人又相继去世，一连九年之忧，已二十五岁。娶闵氏，贤慧无双；此后笔耕餬口，历燕赵吴越并滇黔，为诸侯幕客二十年，纵横万余里，爱其才品者比之杜少陵、孟东野。两试不第，馆于刘侍郎家教过刘文泽两年，又被华光宿请去教书三年，后举孝廉方正，铨得江西一个苦缺知县，任满题升南昌通判；去年夫人病故，只剩孑然一身，随身唯一个跟了五六年的长随刘喜有良心，行李虽薄，金石玩器名书古画却装了好几箱。到京后刘侍郎赠二百金，华公子出城拜访又送三百金，他转手便买书买帖，又分送贫寒旧交，所余无几。

道翁初见颜仲清骨秀神清，见子玉温然玉立、秀外慧中，暗喜"梅铁庵可为有子矣"，又说他父亲在江西严拒情面、杜绝苞苴，一省人比之司马光、文彦博。四旦上前请安，道翁细看一番，说要凭神骨举止口音断人清浊休咎：说苏蕙芳是个好出身、非平常人家子弟，幼年却受尽苦楚，一二年内必有一番作为、就要改行；说素兰、宝珠大致相仿，皆是"出污泥而不滓"；独看琴言便说"这位有些不像"，身有傲骨，断不能与时俯仰，一腔心事百不合宜，又取他的手看了说：有文在手，趁早改行，虽非富贵中人，恰是清高一路；这片心与人两样，不愿意的一点委屈也受不得，愿意的却死而无怨，若遇忠孝节义之事，能行人所不能行——只怕寿元不足，须从宽厚上用心，修身以俟，也可挽回造化。琴言听得句句说到心坎，反待他亲厚起来。席间道翁又说起自己在江西的政绩：那里"东乡有一虎，西乡有一狼"，土豪与蠹役相勾结，三年换了七任知县，盗案命案二百余件，他费了半年心力办掉五人，此后便太平无事。子云见上菜的家人衣上爬着一个虱子，道翁便考子玉此字何书为古：子玉举阮籍《大人先生论》，南湘举《史记》，道翁则说更古的在《商子》——古有"虱官"，仁义礼乐之官谓之虱，仁义生于人心，犹虱生于人垢；南湘、子玉皆拜服。次贤趁势索看他少年游戏之作《醒睡集》，又把前日众人对戏目的单子取来，道翁看了连声称好，说四位竟是我辈，"可惜老夫早生四十年"。

继而仲清问碑板，道翁从《衡岳碑》七十七字的辗转翻刻讲到周武王铜盘铭之伪、石鼓文之聚讼，断汉隶以《孔庙礼器碑》为第一；春航问《兰亭》，他便析欧临之定武本与褚摹之绢本，历叙契丹辇石、薛家翻刻剔损五字为识、靖康失去而弘治间复出于天师庵的始末；又评淳化、大观、绛帖、潭帖之高下，谓淳化气运朴厚、大观光彩浮动，比之诗则盛而渐晚。子玉问李杜优劣，道翁说诗以性情所近，元微之粗率颓唐，于李岂能相近，自然尊杜贬李；世人只喜太白天才横逸，他却独爱《乌栖曲》一路，末了断以"李诗可以绍古，而杜诗可以开今"。子玉又问历代韵书分合，他从沈约以越音定八方之音说起，历数《广韵》二万六千余字、《集韵》五万三千余字与后来的删并，说"韵之失不在二百六部之分，而在一百七部之合"，子玉避席称谢。南湘问太乙数能否验国运灾祥，道翁历举阳九百六之数应验与不应验的两串朝代，末了断然道："数不敌理。理生于自然，数若有预定。故圣人言理不言数，数止理中之一端耳。

"南湘赞为快论。次贤故意问一个琐屑之理：《越绝书》说慧种生圣、痴种生狂，何以愚夫蠢妇倒生出绝慧绝美的儿女？道翁引《齐民要术》种梨之法与鹘生三子一为鸱之类作答，又说愚夫愚妇外貌虽蠢，七情六欲之间未必样样都蠢，从此解了一点灵气，也未可知，说得众人大笑。子云问古来形容美人何处最妙，道翁推《硕人》为第一，说它字字绝妙而不着一衬帖假借之语，扫去烘云托月之法；宋玉《神女赋》以白日屋梁作比已属不伦，曹子建《洛神赋》又袭其意以游龙比美人，更不善体会；至于"增之一分则太长"云云，细核起来亦有语病。刻划尽致者，则莫如《杂事秘辛》量女莹身体一段，虽文章秽亵而精细无匹。由此又辨缠足之始：子玉问"约缣迫袜"是否即缠足，道翁说不过略为缠束，与今日必使尖如莲瓣者不同；蕙芳问那七尺一寸如何算，道翁便以汉尺折今尺，核出通身不过四尺八寸许，与蕙芳差不多还略高些，次贤笑说或者当日量错了一寸，众人皆笑。末了道翁提起京中一位极博雅之人：南京巨族，姓金名栗号吉甫，论世家与子云、华光宿不相上下，考据学问品行当今数一数二，著有一部说平倭寇的说部，把两个逆首骂得痛快，只是时运太坏未能大用其才。宝珠忙接口道"何幸此君今日竟遇知己"，素兰在旁揭出：宝珠的画画弹琴皆是此君所教，前月还同逛了两天翠微山，其待此君不亚于蕙芳之待田春航。子玉也说曾在宝珠处见其笔墨，大有唐六如光景；道翁说不特笔墨似六如，命宫磨蝎也似六如，明日还要去拜他，子云忙求引见。这一日道翁足足讲了一日，人也乏了；二更时宝珠向子云讨扇子求题，子云索性取四柄出来，于是宝珠拂几、蕙芳移研、素兰磨墨、琴言润毫，道翁乘兴按各人的大概各题七律一首，半行半草一笔虞世南，并落双款。`,en:`Xu Ziyun invites Qu Daoweng to the Plum Slope, with Shi Nanxiang, Yan Zhongqing, Liu Wenze, Tian Chunhang and Mei Ziyu to keep him company; Wang Xun is absent, having been asked by Sun Lianggong to manage Li Yuanmao's now-fixed wedding into the family. Because the old scholar likes quiet, only four performers are called — Yuan Baozhu, Su Huifang, Lu Sulan, and, since Jin Shufang is unwell, Du Qinyan. Here Qu's history is set out. A man of Wuchang in Hubei and a descendant of the Lord of the Three Wards, his learning joins heaven and man; his father, a Hanlin examiner by the special composition examination, died before thirty, when the boy was four. He entered the district school at sixteen and immediately went into mourning for his grandfather, then for his grandmother, then for his mother — nine years altogether, so that he was twenty-five before he was free. He married a wife of matchless good sense, then lived by his brush for twenty years as a private secretary through Yan, Zhao, Wu, Yue, Yunnan and Guizhou, ten thousand li of travel, admired as a latter-day Du Fu or Meng Jiao. Twice failing the examinations, he tutored in Vice-Minister Liu's household, where he taught Liu Wenze for two years, and was afterwards engaged for three years by Hua Guangsu; then, recommended as filial and upright, he was given a poor magistracy in Jiangxi and promoted at the end of his term. 

His wife died last year, leaving him alone with no kin, a light baggage, several chests of bronzes, rubbings, paintings and calligraphy, and one honest retainer, Liu Xi, who has served him five or six years. In the capital Vice-Minister Liu gave him two hundred taels and Hua Guangsu came out of the city to call and gave three hundred — most of which he has already spent on books and rubbings or given away to poor old friends. Meeting Yan Zhongqing he notes fine bone and clear spirit; meeting Ziyu, warm as jade and clever within as without, he thinks privately that Mei Shixie may be called a man with a son, and tells him that in Jiangxi his father's refusal of favours and of bribes has made the whole province compare him to the great ministers of the Song. When the four performers come to bow, he studies them and says he judges by frame, bearing and voice rather than complexion: Su Huifang, he declares, was well born and no ordinary family's child, suffered bitterly in youth, and within a year or two will do something notable and leave the profession; Sulan and Baozhu are much alike, lotuses unstained by their mud; but Qinyan strikes him as different — there is pride in his bones, he will never bend with the times, and his heart is at odds with everything around him. Taking his hand, he reads there that he should change his calling early, that wealth is not his portion but purity is, that he can bear no slight in what he does not want and would die without complaint for what he does, and that in a matter of loyalty or honour he could do what others cannot — only his allotted years may be short, and he must use his heart generously and cultivate himself, by which fate may yet be turned. Every word touches Qinyan where he lives, and he warms to the old man. At table Qu describes his own record in Jiangxi, where a tiger in the eastern township and a wolf in the western — local bullies in league with rapacious clerks — had run through seven magistrates in three years and left two hundred cases of robbery and murder, until he spent half a year and broke five men, after which the district was quiet. 

Ziyun, noticing a louse on a servant's new coat, gives Qu an opening to test Ziyu on the word's oldest occurrence; Ziyu offers Ruan Ji's *Essay on the Great Man*, Nanxiang the *Records of the Grand Historian*, and Qu caps both from the *Book of Shang*, where the offices of benevolence and ritual are called lice — since benevolence is bred of the human heart as lice are bred of human filth. Cixian coaxes him about *The Awakening from Sleep Collection* of his youth and produces the sheet of matched play titles from the other day, which the old man praises so warmly that he wishes he had been born forty years later. Then Zhongqing asks about steles, and Qu traces the seventy-seven characters of the *Mount Heng Stele* through its successive recuttings, dismisses the Zhou bronze-basin inscription as forgery, reviews the disputes over the stone drums, and ranks the *Confucius Temple Ritual Vessels Stele* first among Han clerical script. Chunhang asks about the *Orchid Pavilion*, and Qu distinguishes Ouyang Xun's Dingwu cutting from Chu Suiliang's silk copy, following the stone through the Khitan sack, the Xue family's counterfeits and the five characters they mutilated for a mark, its loss in the Jingkang disorder and its reappearance in a hermitage under the Ming; then he grades the four great model-books, calling one plain and rich in vital force and another glittering on the surface, as poetry passes from its height into its late season. Asked to weigh Li Bai against Du Fu, he answers that men prefer the poet nearest their own temperament, and that a coarse and slack writer could never be near Li; that while everyone praises Li's soaring gift, he himself loves best the *Raven-Roost Song* manner; and he concludes that Li's verse continues antiquity and Du's opens what comes after. On the rhyme books he begins with the objection that Shen Yue fixed the sounds of all quarters by the speech of one, counts the twenty-six thousand characters of the *Expanded Rhymes* against the fifty-three thousand of the *Collected Rhymes*, and holds that the fault lies not in the old division into two hundred and six categories but in the later reduction to one hundred and seven — at which Ziyu leaves his seat to thank him. Nanxiang asks whether the Taiyi numbers can really foretell a dynasty's fortune, and Qu recites two strings of reigns, one where the cycles fit and one where they conspicuously do not — tyrants who fell in no fatal year, usurpers who rose in none — and concludes that number cannot outweigh principle: principle arises of itself, number pretends to be fixed, and therefore the sages spoke of principle and not of number, number being only one branch within it. 

Nanxiang calls it a judgment that clears an ancient doubt. Cixian then poses a homely puzzle from the *Book of Yue Annals*, that clever seed breeds sages and foolish seed madmen — so how do dull parents produce brilliant and beautiful children? Qu answers from the *Essential Techniques for the Common People*, where ten pear seeds yield two pears and the rest wild stock, and from the old lore of birds that hatch one of another kind; and adds that dull husband and dull wife may not be dull in every one of the seven passions, and that whatever spirit is loosed there may be what takes shape — at which the table laughs. Asked where the ancients described beauty best, Qu gives first place to the *Stately Lady*, whose every phrase is exact without one borrowed or supporting word, dispensing with the trick of painting clouds to set off the moon; Song Yu's *Rhapsody on the Goddess* compares her only to sunlight on a roof beam, which is no comparison, and Cao Zhi's *Rhapsody on the Goddess of the Luo* borrows the fault and likens a beautiful woman to a coiling dragon; while the famous line about one fraction too tall and one too short will not survive examination. For sheer anatomy, he says, nothing equals the passage in *Miscellaneous Secret Affairs* measuring the girl Ying — indecent, but unmatched in precision. That leads to the origin of bound feet: Ziyu asks whether the tight silk and stocking of the passage is not already binding, and Qu answers that it was only a wrapping to keep the foot from spreading, not today's compression into a lotus petal; and when Huifang asks how a height of seven feet one inch is to be understood, Qu converts Han measure into the foot of today and arrives at something over four feet eight — much Huifang's own height, and a little more — until Cixian jokes that the ancient measurer may simply have added an inch, and everyone laughs. At the last Qu mentions a man of vast learning now in the capital: of a great Nanjing family, surnamed Jin, named Su, styled Jifu, whose lineage stands with Ziyun's and Hua Guangsu's, whose scholarship and character are second to none, and who has written a work of fiction about the pirate wars that curses its two rebel chiefs magnificently — but whose luck has been so bad that his gifts have never been used. Baozhu says quickly that the man has at least found one who knows him, and Sulan discloses that Baozhu's painting and qin-playing were both taught by him, that the two spent two days at Cuiwei Hill last month, and that Baozhu's devotion is no less than Huifang's to Tian Chunhang. Ziyu adds that the brushwork he saw at Baozhu's has much of Tang Bohu in it, and Qu replies that not only the brush but the horoscope resembles Tang Bohu's — he means to call on him tomorrow, and Ziyun at once begs an introduction. The old man has talked a whole day and is tired. At the second watch Baozhu asks Ziyun for a fan to be inscribed, and Ziyun produces four; so Baozhu dusts the table, Huifang shifts the inkstone, Sulan grinds the ink and Qinyan wets the brush, and Qu writes for each of them a regulated verse suited to their characters, half running and half cursive in the manner of Yu Shinan, with both signatures beneath.`},39:{zh:`李元茂招赘孙家的吉期定在二月初十。年内李性全寄了几百两银子来，又具禀帖托王文辉；王文辉不耐烦作媒，都交王恂代劳，元茂则央了魏聘才代制一切。聘才闹了一个多月，输的花的不少，窃案又未破，正自烦闷，因当初原是他与王文辉作媒，便借此常到王宅走动，又趁势追讨去年那只金镯；元茂说父亲只寄三百两，办事尚且不够，又笑说"横竖出在内人身上"，聘才说使老婆的钱不算汉子，元茂道"又不是当忘八来的钱"。过礼那日聘才不得不到梅宅，因几个月不去又出过事，甚是局促；颜夫人淡淡问了几句，梅子玉记着杜琴言前日所说，也不似从前亲厚。元茂的彩礼由梅进代办：钗环簪镯、彩缎衣衫之外，又借了颜夫人的珠冠玉带、补服朝珠、蟒衣绣裙，共铺十六盒，扎了亭子，也还像个局面。吉日梅宅备两桌酒，颜仲清、王恂、子玉陪着新郎打趣：仲清说"今夕真个到了群玉山头"，王恂说"一路荣华到白头"，子玉念了近视眼的洞房诗"犹道灯前相对影，愈揉双眼愈模糊"；元茂道近视也有好处，新人丑些便看不清楚，只要不像他那两位内弟的相貌就好。新娘本是孙亮功前室所生，继母不甚钟爱，一切从简，女客只有陆宗沅的夫人带了小女儿来；姬亮轩在那里假热闹。

闹房时仲清要看新人，孙嗣元跑到床前死把住帐门，连说几个"看"字才挣出"不得"；孙嗣徽满口"夫妇居室，人之大伦"，王恂便对以"无感我帨兮，无使龙也吠"。姬亮轩初见子玉，一味逢迎，说他东家极好相与，子玉私问王恂才知是奚十一，登时一腔忿恨不再理他；仲清追问他与孙家究竟怎样的亲，他便扯出"舍亲的舅嫂是我表嫂之表妹"一串新亲旧亲，聘才笑说这个青只好算个蛋青。孙嗣徽引《孟子》讲"亲亲"，孙嗣元结结巴巴驳他"螃蟹没有血，甲鱼还有血，王八也是你亲戚了"，满屋大笑。仲清便讲海龙王放生的笑话：王八认龟丞相为本家、蛇将军为亲戚，因王八不会生儿子须请蛇代生，故本家亲戚都算得；蛤蟆则说人人是本家、个个是亲戚，因他是人溺余精所生——龙王大惊，快些放他去，免得攀出蛤蟆亲戚来。嗣徽与亮轩明知是骂，却回答不出。王恂又讲瞎妓辨客的笑话：一个作八股的秀才、一个办命案的刑名、一个近视眼的呆子，各有各的手法；帐子里也透出一丝笑声。轮到聘才，他本意只取笑元茂的近视，却讲了两个近视兄弟赌赛眼光的故事——哥哥认错当铺招牌，末了各去看自己老婆，一个说嫂子满头白发是"天老"，一个说弟妇下身的毛也是白的。

话音未落，帐中新娘破口大骂，掷出一物砸破两个菜碗，众人面面相觑，急急散去。原来这位姑娘正是"天老"：满头白发，连眉毛寒毛皆白，北边叫天老，南边谓白羊子；又性情泼悍，年已三十，四远驰名无人聘娶，才将就送与元茂。当夜元茂只道她是替自己出气，欢喜非常，剪亮蜡花揭开红巾一照：雪白桃花脸，乌云也似一头黑发，喜得他骨软筋酥。事后新娘递过一块帕子，他记着"元红"之说揌在枕边预备次日验看；再一度之后满身是汗，随手摸着那帕子，把满头满脸抹了个干净。次早新娘另在一间房里梳头，元茂被伴婆拉去拜见泰山长亲；回来偷偷取出帕子一看，只见一条条不像漆不像油也不像墨，翻遍床褥再寻不着别的，闻着有些油香汗气，末了在黑迹之外看出一点淡淡红影，便恍然自解：红的是元红无疑，黑的定是昨日有人把黑油涂在他帽子里捉弄他。新娘那边也在纳闷：这女婿团头大脸倒还平正，只是脸上隐隐一条一块，像煤黑子擦不干净，想必洗脸不用胰子。过了几日谢媒拜客，王恂、仲清问他新人如何，他翻来覆去只说头发，什么"白丝细发""银丝鹤发"，人人听不懂。

他自己却渐渐生疑：脸上天天沾黑油，帕子又是那样，她还绝早关门在别屋梳头。他不敢问，只想若头发是黑的，或者下身的毛是白的，索性夜里悄悄拔了一根，用纸包好揌在辫顶；次早打开一看却是漆黑的，便放了心——却不知那一根原是从他自己身上擦下来的。约过半月，新娘梳头时觉冷，叫丫鬟取半臂，元茂跟进去，只见一个身穿大红紧身的人披着三尺余长的银丝细发、两道淡金色眉毛，唬得他当是遇了鬼，细看才认出衣裳；一口气直冲上来，说"原来如此，我该倒运，娶了一个妖精，这是《西游记》上的不老婆婆"。新娘又羞又气，哭骂道：我是千金小姐，留赘你一个白身人，头发白些，那一样不如你？嫌我便休了我！乒乒乓乓砸了一地。原来她这几天用香油调了灯煤，再和柿漆，先梳好了再细细刷上，比人的还光还亮，只是每日须用皂荚水加硼砂明矾洗净、晾至半干再梳挽，要费一个时辰；那日略迟些，因此败露。元茂负气跑到魏聘才处住了两天，说破缘故，聘才才恍然大悟，原来那日的笑话竟说到板眼里去了。孙亮功大怒，各处寻着人劝回，元茂犹不肯进房。到晚间灯下再看，黑白分明依旧是个美人，他心思一动，想出个绝妙主意：与其天天上漆，费力不便又染得他一脸黑油惹人笑话，不如学小旦上装用网巾一扎，把那一头银丝罩在里面，再不然索性剃掉。新娘不肯剃，却依了网巾，当晚就买了网巾、髻子、燕尾来，熬皂荚水洗出两大盆靛青似的黑水，又刮去鬓脚眉毛，戴上网巾，加了假髻，扎上燕尾，额上一支翠翘，画了眉，前后两面镜子一照，竟像个醉杨妃。元茂闻了闻头，擦了擦脸，只余一点油气，不再落色，喜孜孜地支开丫头，一场怄气就此化作洞房和好。`,en:`Li Yuanmao's marriage into the Sun family as a live-in husband is fixed for the tenth of the second month. His father, Li Xingquan, has sent several hundred taels and a formal note to Wang Wenhui, who, having no patience for matchmaking, hands everything to Wang Xun; and Yuanmao begs Wei Pincai to manage the outfitting. Pincai, a month deep in losses with the burglary still unsolved, takes the errand as a pretext for frequenting Wang Wenhui's house, since he had been a matchmaker in the first place — and uses the occasion to press for the gold bracelet lent last year. Yuanmao pleads that three hundred taels will barely cover the wedding, and jokes that the debt will come out of his wife; Pincai says that spending a wife's money is not manly, and Yuanmao retorts that at least it is not a cuckold's earnings. On the day of the betrothal gifts Pincai must call at the Mei house, where after months away and a public scandal he is uncomfortable; Madam Yan questions him coolly, and Mei Ziyu, remembering what Du Qinyan told him, is no longer warm. Mei Jin assembles the gifts — pins, rings, bracelets and silks, plus Madam Yan's borrowed pearl coronet, jade belt, rank-badge robe, court beads, python robe and embroidered skirt — sixteen trays under a carried canopy, which make a respectable show. On the day itself the Mei household sets two tables, and Yan Zhongqing, Wang Xun and Ziyu tease the bridegroom in verse: tonight, says Zhongqing, he reaches the Mountain of Massed Jade; splendour all the way to white hair, says Wang Xun, not knowing what he says; and Ziyu quotes the near-sighted man's bridal poem, in which the harder he rubs his eyes the blurrier his bride becomes. Yuanmao answers that short sight has its advantages — if she is plain he will not see it — and that he asks only that she not resemble his two brothers-in-law. 

The bride is a daughter of Sun Lianggong's first wife and no favourite of her stepmother, so everything is done plainly, with Lu Zongyuan's wife and her little girl the only women guests, and Ji Liangxuan supplying counterfeit jollity. When Zhongqing tries to see the bride, Sun Siyuan plants himself against the bed curtain and stammers out that it cannot be looked at; Sun Sihui declaims that husband and wife in their chamber are the great bond of humanity; Wang Xun caps him from the Songs with the line about not disturbing her kerchief and not setting the dog barking. Ji Liangxuan, meeting Ziyu for the first time, presses him to come and visit his employer, who is the easiest of men — and when Ziyu learns from Wang Xun that this means Xi Shiyi, his anger closes the conversation. Asked exactly how he is related to the Suns, Ji produces a chain of cousins by marriage that Pincai calls a very watery shade of blue. Sun Sihui quotes Mencius on loving one's kin, and Sun Siyuan stammers a rebuttal that crabs have no blood while turtles do, so the cuckolds must be his relations too, at which the room roars. Zhongqing tells a fable of the Dragon King releasing creatures, in which the turtle claims the Turtle Minister as clansman and the Snake General as kin, since turtles cannot beget and must borrow the snake, so that the offspring is turtle by house and snake by seed; and the toad claims all mankind as relatives, being generated from what men pass in the street — at which the Dragon King hurriedly lets him go before the kinship is proved. Sihui and Ji know themselves meant, and cannot answer. Wang Xun follows with the tale of the blind courtesan who identified three customers by their methods — a licentiate composing an eight-legged essay, a legal secretary taking inventory of scars, and a near-sighted fool whose eyebrows tickled her — and a stifled laugh comes from inside the bed curtains. 

Then Pincai, meaning only to mock Yuanmao's eyesight, tells of two near-sighted brothers who wager on their vision and end by describing each other's wives: the elder, finding his wife sifting flour, reports that she is white-haired from birth, and the younger answers that a white head is nothing, since his own wife's body hair is white as well. Before the laugh has finished the bride bursts out cursing behind the curtain and hurls something that smashes two dishes, and the company scatters in confusion. For the bride is exactly that: white of hair, brow, and body, of the kind the north calls a heaven-born white one; fierce-tempered besides, thirty years old, famous for miles around, and unmarriageable — which is why she was put off on Yuanmao. That night, believing she had defended him, he dismisses the servants in delight, trims the candle, lifts the veil, and sees a snow-and-peach-blossom face under cloud-black hair, and is weak with joy. Afterwards she hands him a handkerchief, which he tucks by the pillow to examine in the morning for the token of virginity; but after a second bout, sweating under new cotton quilts, he gropes for it and wipes his face and head with it. She rises at dawn to dress her hair in a separate room, and he is carried off to pay respects to his father-in-law; and when at last he unfolds the handkerchief in private he finds streaks that are not lacquer, not oil, and not ink, searches the bed for another and finds none, smells oil and sweat, and finally, spotting a faint pink at the edge of the black, satisfies himself that the red is the proof he wanted and the black some prank of the wedding guests, oil smeared inside his hat and carried to the cloth by his own sweat. She, for her part, studies him and decides he is passable but for the streaks and blotches on his face, like a coalman imperfectly washed, and concludes that he does not use soap. For days afterwards, when Wang Xun and Zhongqing ask about his bride, he can speak of nothing but her hair — fine white silk, silver crane threads — to everyone's bewilderment. 

His own suspicion grows: the daily oil on his face, the handkerchief, the closed door and the early rising. Not daring to ask, he reasons that if the head is black perhaps the body is white, and pulls out a single hair by night, wraps it in paper, and hides it in his queue; and finding it jet black in the morning, is reassured — the hair having in fact come from his own head. Half a month later the truth breaks. Feeling chilly at her toilet, she sends a maid for a jacket; Yuanmao follows the maid in, and sees a figure in a red bodice with three feet of silver-fine hair and pale gold eyebrows, takes it for a ghost, recognizes the clothes, and explodes that he has married a monster, the Never-Aging Granny out of *Journey to the West*, and she wanting a husband too. Shamed and furious, she weeps and curses him: she is a rich house's daughter who took in a man with no standing, her hair is a little white, and in what else is she not his equal? Let him divorce her, then — and she smashes what comes to hand. Her method comes out: sesame oil mixed with lamp-soot and persimmon lacquer, painted on after her hair was dressed, glossier than anyone's — but needing to be washed out daily with soap-pod water, borax and alum, dried half-dry, and re-coiled over a full hour; and that morning she was late. Yuanmao storms off to Pincai's for two days, tells the story, and Pincai understands at last that his joke had gone straight to the mark. Sun Lianggong, furious, has him hunted down and brought back, and he will not enter the room until evening, when by lamplight she is black and white and beautiful again and his resolve softens. Then he produces his brilliant solution: rather than lacquer her head daily, at such labour and inconvenience, and blacken his own face into a public joke, let her bind it in a hairnet as the young female-role actors do, hiding the silver inside — or shave it off entirely. She refuses the razor but accepts the net; a net, a false chignon and a swallow-tail are bought that night, soap-pod water boils out two great basins of indigo-black, the temple hair and eyebrows are shaved, the net goes on with the real hair coiled inside, the chignon is pinned, the swallow-tail tied, a kingfisher ornament set on the brow and the eyebrows painted — and between two mirrors she looks like Consort Yang half-drunk. Yuanmao smells her hair and rubs his cheek against hers, finds only a trace of oil and no more staining, sends the maids away, and the quarrel ends in the bridal chamber after all.`},40:{zh:`奚十一碰了杜琴言的钉子，无计可施，只得逢人便造谣言，说琴言在外陪酒还要跟他；那只镯子，他哄白菊花说自己失手砸了，被巴英官揭穿，闹了一场，许下将来另配才罢。此人一味弃旧怜新：先买春兰，不到半年就冷了；去年得巴英官如获至宝，如今又弄上得月与卓天香，把英官也疏远了。英官与春兰闲话，说破那只木桶的机关：西洋造法，口小底大，里头叮叮当当地响，扔进一样东西叫人伸手去取，中间一层板上开两个洞，各只容一只手，两手一齐伸进去便被机簧锁住，再也退不出来；春兰头一次就上了这个当，后来被人告发才把桶劈了——听说还有好人家的孩子受骗之后回去上吊投水的。英官又说奚十一如今赏也稀少，"这个人偏不生疮，烂掉了倒大家干净"；春兰劝他仍跟姬亮轩，英官说那位更肉麻小气，两个黄牙又有狐骚臭，还不如当日在剃头铺里打条辫子也有好几百钱的舒畅。谁知一语成谶。这日奚十一独到宏济寺，唐和尚与魏聘才都不在，见得月朝里睡着便动了火，不听他连说两声"来不得"；原来唐和尚见得月气色不正、指甲发青，知他中了毒气，才用一剂攻毒泻火的药，正泻得厉害，奚十一竟当面承受了个满身狼藉。他扫兴出来，撞见卓天香，二人便在聘才屋里坐着。

天香是来借钱的：张翠官前日被人诬赖偷了烟壶，捆起来要送北衙门，跟去的人赔了八十吊写了欠票才放出来，如今当尽衣服才凑得六十五吊，还短十五吊；奚十一一口应承，写票子到潘其观铺子里去取。偏偏天香先前在哈回子铺里与那小回子有过瓜葛，那人新染杨梅毒尚未发出，已过给了天香；天香此时正焦辣难受，便与奚十一又闹了一回。两毒并发，由肾经直入心经，奚十一当晚便如火烧，下身肿得酒杯大。白菊花吓了一跳，请了自称专医此症的唐和尚来，仍用泻药，毒深打不下去，三日更重；另请的医生要二百金包医，却越治越烂，到后来烂得像蜂巢一般，臭不可闻，他连躺也躺不得，只能不穿裤子坐在凳上，两脚搁在小凳中间垂着那一件烂物。白菊花不敢埋怨，只求神许愿；换了两三个医生，末了还是唐和尚用至宝丹敷住，才保住一寸有余，收功之后没头没脑、肉小皮宽，行房是再也不能了。这是奚十一的淫报。潘其观的报应来得更巧。

他店里有个十六岁的小伙计许老三，生得标致，潘其观久想弄他不得；正月十五众伙计回家过节，他单留下老三在小账房喝酒，趁他醉后强奸，又预先从剃头铺讨了些短发揌了进去，指望他日后痒起来自来就他。老三醒来顾着脸面不敢声张，却把此事告诉了姐夫周小三——那是替潘其观赶车的，颇有血性。他要去吵闹，老三止住，回家与两个哥哥并姐姐商量。许老二脱了衣裳就要打架，大哥要请中人讲理，独许三姐说：既无伤痕，验也无从验，不如叫他也受用一回，吃个说不出的闷亏。三姐才十九岁，生得标致又千伶百俐，"若做了男子倒是个有作为的"。计策定了：由周小三请潘其观到家赴妻子生日，中途假称有事出门，留下三姐周旋。潘其观见她瓜子脸、凤眼、大元宝头，一双三寸金莲，早已魂消；三姐先支开老三上大街打酒，又故作推拒，摸着他腕上的金镯不放，潘其观便解下八两重的镯子相送，并许了加她丈夫的工食。

方要入内，周小三持刀突入，一把揪住，扬言要送官断离；三姐假哭，又暗地飞了个眼色。一番讨价还价，末了议定先付三百吊的银票，此后每月再给六十吊。潘其观千恩万谢地去了，三姐临别还低低说了句"明日"。次早潘其观支开周小三下乡，兴冲冲赶去，才脱了衣裳上炕，周小三便领着两个舅子持刀执绳闯入，把他按倒捆了。许老二一面痛骂"肛了我的兄弟，还想血我的妹子"，一面依样把药与头发揌了回去，三姐在旁笑得打颤；大哥数落道：你强奸我兄弟犹可，还放了东西叫他一世成病，今日不过还个礼。潘其观讨还银票与金镯，周小三只说"你回去与我打官司就是了"，三姐更笑说要打官司须早些讲，好教她习学口供。他一人如何斗得过四个，只得抱头鼠窜；三姐还在背后笑喊，叫他明日再来。事后三姐分派那三百吊：一百吊给丈夫作本钱，从此不必赶车；两个哥哥各三十吊；余下一百四十吊留给老三日后开铺生息——"这钱是因他来的，自然他应多些"。又说三弟的毛病要紧，听得荞麦面能除肚里的毛，叫他们做些半生的荞麦汤圆包上糖，与他吃两天试试。`,en:`Rebuffed by Du Qinyan and out of stratagems, Xi Shiyi can only spread the story that the boy served him wine and wanted to attach himself to him. As for the bracelet, he told White Chrysanthemum he had broken it by accident, and when Ba Yingguan gave him away there was a scene, quieted only by a promise of a replacement. The man tires of everything: he bought Chunlan and cooled toward him within half a year, prized Ba Yingguan last year, and now, having taken up Deyue and Zhuo Tianxiang, neglects Yingguan too. Talking with Chunlan, Yingguan gets the secret of the notorious barrel: made in the western fashion, narrow at the mouth and wide at the base, chiming inside, with something dropped in for the victim to reach after; a partition below has two holes, each admitting one hand, and once both are in a catch closes on them and no withdrawal is possible. Chunlan was its first victim; the thing was destroyed only after somebody informed — and boys of decent families, it is said, went home from it to hang or drown themselves. Yingguan adds that presents have grown scarce and that a man like this will not even get a sore — better he rot and be done with; and when Chunlan suggests returning to Ji Liangxuan, he answers that the secretary is meaner and more sickening still, with his two yellow teeth and his smell, and that he was happier as a barber's apprentice earning a few hundred cash a day. The wish is granted almost at once. That day Xi goes alone to the Hongji Temple, finds Tang Heshang and Wei Pincai out and Deyue asleep with his face to the wall, and will not be stopped though the boy twice tells him it cannot be done — for Tang, seeing Deyue's colour bad and his nails blue, had dosed him with a violent purge to drive out an infection, and Xi receives the whole of it. 

Disgusted, he comes out and meets Zhuo Tianxiang, and they sit in Pincai's rooms. Tianxiang has come to borrow: Zhang Cuiguan was accused the other day of stealing a snuff bottle, tied up and threatened with the north yamen, and only bought free for eighty strings on a written bond; every garment they own is pawned, and they are still fifteen short. Xi promises a draft on Pan Qiguan's shop. But Tianxiang has just been with a young man at a beef-seller's who was newly infected and did not yet know it; itching and miserable, he turns to Xi for relief. Two poisons meet, pass from the kidney channel into the heart, and by night Xi is burning, swollen to the size of a wine cup and running. White Chrysanthemum is horrified and sends for Tang, who advertises himself as a specialist in exactly these complaints and prescribes another purge; the poison is too deep to be driven down, and in three days it is worse. Another physician guarantees a cure for two hundred taels and does more harm, until the sore is like a wasp's nest and the stench unbearable. Xi cannot lie down; he sits trouserless on a stool with his legs propped on two smaller ones and the ruined member hanging between. White Chrysanthemum, not daring to reproach him, prays and makes vows and nurses him through two or three more doctors, and in the end it is Tang's costly pills that save something over an inch of him — shapeless when it heals, small in the flesh and loose in the skin, and forever useless. 

So much for Xi Shiyi's lechery. Pan Qiguan's reckoning is neater. In his shop was a handsome assistant of sixteen, Xu Laosan, whom Pan had long wanted and never got. On the fifteenth of the first month, with the other clerks gone home for the festival, he kept the boy back to drink in the little counting room, forced him when he was drunk asleep, and packed in clippings begged from a barber's shop so that the itching should afterwards drive him back of his own accord. Waking violated, Xu could not cry out for shame — but he told his brother-in-law Zhou Xiaosan, who drives Pan's carriage and has blood in him. Zhou would have made a scene; Xu stopped him and went home to his two brothers and his sister. Xu Laoer was for going straight round with his fists and the eldest for arguing it out through the man who found the place; only Third Sister saw the truth of it — that with no wound there can be no inquest, and that the thing to do is to let Pan enjoy something himself and swallow a loss he can never name. She is nineteen, handsome, and quick enough that had she been born a man she would have made something of herself. 

The plan is laid: Zhou invites Pan to his house for his wife's birthday, is called away in the middle of dinner, and leaves Third Sister to entertain the guest. Pan, seeing the melon-seed face, the phoenix eyes, the great ingot-shaped coiffure and the three-inch feet, is lost from the start. She sends Xu Laosan off to the main street for stronger liquor, plays at refusing, and lingers over the gold bracelet on his wrist until he takes off all eight taels of it and promises her husband a raise besides. Then Zhou bursts in with a knife, seizes him, threatens the magistrate and a divorce, while Third Sister weeps for form and passes Pan a wink behind her husband's back. After bargaining, it is settled at a draft for three hundred strings down and sixty strings a month thereafter; Pan goes home thanking them, and as he leaves she whispers one word: tomorrow. Next morning he packs Zhou off to the country and hurries round, and has scarcely undressed when Zhou walks in with both brothers, knives and a coil of rope, and he is pinned and bound on the floor. Xu Laoer strikes him, cursing him for what he did to one sibling and meant to do to another, and repays him in exactly his own coin, hair and drug and all, while Third Sister laughs until she shakes and the eldest brother delivers the sentence: the rape might have been borne, but he left something behind that has made a sick man of the boy for life, and today is only the return of a courtesy. Pan asks for his draft and his eight-tael bracelet; Zhou tells him to go and sue; Third Sister says that if he means to sue he had better say so early, so that she can rehearse her deposition. One man against four, with his own body burning, he can only bolt — while she calls after him to come again another day and she will find something to scratch his itch. Afterwards she divides the three hundred strings: a hundred to her husband for capital so that he need drive no more carriages, thirty to each brother, and a hundred and forty put by for Xu Laosan to set up a shop, since the money came through him and his share should be the largest. And she adds that his complaint must be seen to — buckwheat, she has heard, will carry hair out of the body, so let them make him half-raw buckwheat dumplings with sugar in them and try it for two days.`}},i={41:{zh:`杜琴言告假逾期未归，华光宿心上惦记，却仍照旧过他的富贵春日。这日他在归鸿小渚倚阑垂钓，池石脚下嵌着铜皮，流水过处琤琮如琴筑；一尾红鲤游来吃落花，吞了钓饵，一拽却把丝线扯断，连钓吞去了半截。他一时高兴，命人撑小船过来——那船三丈余长，漆得光亮，船头一亭，中舱一张花梨圆桌，绿油布顶垂着白绫飞沿。又遣小丫鬟去请苏浣香，夫人带了明珠、花珠、荷珠、赠珠过来，早已叫老婆子备下食品。夫妇同上小船，花珠与明珠一个望前一个望后，把船在水中打起转来，花珠手一脱，溅了自己一脸水，荷珠笑个不住，换了荷珠才顺。清风微皱，穿过小桥，桥边山石上古藤蒙茸垂到水面，底下水声戛玉；苏堤上杨柳桃花红绿相间，绕过潭水房山，到了修竹深处开满碧桃的留仙院。

彼时蝴蝶成群，众婢捉来，从小丫鬟头上拔一根发拴住两只，看它们双双飞舞。华公子说赏花不可无酒，又要以百花露酿的竹叶春祭花；夫人问用什么作供，寻常蔬果与虔诚特祭不称。于是取钥匙开了博古厨，抽屉里一个紫檀匣中是管夫人画的花蕊夫人小像，后有赵集贤小楷录的花蕊夫人《宫词》，堪称双绝；夫人提议再配马四娘的兰花，公子摇头，说不如用李香君那柄《桃花扇》的册页，再加上夫人亲绣的《玉台新咏序》。三样宝贝陈在廊前，博山炉焚了百合香，一觞汉玉盛酒。二人不拜，只各拣一株最好的——公子红碧桃，夫人白碧桃——把酒浇在根上。夫人又命取一大瓶酒来，说见丫头们眼馋，也叫她们顽顽；公子笑说她们祭花却要拜的。

于是十珠各执小杯，穿花来去乱浇，宛然一群穿花蝴蝶，末了齐齐拜了四拜。花下铺开锦褥，夫妻对坐小酌，夫人便命她们接唱《桃花扇》的《访翠》《眠香》两出，两套十一枝，短的并作一枝，恰好一人一枝。宝珠起唱，公子赞此曲可追《玉茗堂四梦》，夫人也说此后传奇少有能与匹敌的；明珠漏了一句被公子听出；掌珠一面唱一面把帕子打个结，望荷珠脸上掷去，惹得满座喝采；爱珠接唱《眠香》，公子叹这样的妙曲该教白香山的樊素来唱，夫人笑说她们也还将就，比外头班中生旦强得多。唱毕，夫人提笔品第：以宝珠为第一，说他"风神跌宕、文秀温存"，次爱珠、赠珠、掌珠、蕊珠、珍珠、花珠、荷珠，明珠居末。花珠不服，说自己应在蕊珠、珍珠之上；夫人一语道破：你要显己之长压人之短，添出腔调来，那是戏曲不是清曲；清曲要唱得雅，洗尽铅华方见清真本色，你唱惯了搭白的戏曲，一时洗不干净。花珠这才心服，又问珊枝如何，夫人说珊枝也是戏曲，"倒是琴言虽然生些，还得清字意"。

一句话勾起公子的心事，说这孩子实在有些古怪，待他也算好了，看他心上总像有些委屈，如今告假一个多月也不进来；夫人道：他大抵是个高傲性子，不肯居人下，但既落到这个地位也就无法，所谓"做此官，行此礼"，若妄自高傲便真是胡涂人了——公子笑而不语。赏赐分作两等：一等钗环，二等香粉。廊外两个老婆子早把那瓶冷酒偷吃了一半，一个醺醺地靠着山石要睡，一个还厚着脸皮向爱珠讨转赏，被小丫鬟香儿取笑得干瞪眼，另一个只叹"人老珠黄不值钱"。公子随口念起《牡丹亭》"良辰美景奈何天"两句，夫人便说《游园惊梦》固然旖旎，只是柳梦梅似乎配不上丽娘；说到《元人百种曲》，公子说只可唱不可读，词章直该一火焚之，夫人却翻进一层：那些曲本未唱时或者原好，都因唱的人要合工尺，处处点金成铁，如今不过是个工尺字谱，文理顺逆、气韵雅俗一概不讲；偏又曲文好的没人会唱——自《九宫谱》一定，人人只会改字换音，不会移宫就谱，实是世间一件缺事。公子听得叫妙，便要就着《梁州序》的工尺另填新词：他先成一阕，末句"休负了，金樽浅"；夫人和了一阕，末句"只羡鸳鸯不羡仙，休负了，金樽浅"，公子朗吟数遍拍案叫绝，说这五十七字如香云缭绕、花雨缤纷，就是《桃花扇》中也无此丽句。宝珠、爱珠念熟了却错板，夫人叫他们把工尺注在旁边，果然字字对腔。公子又戏说这两阕定了他夫妇的生旦，还要多填几阕成一套《赏花》，叫十珠扮作他二人串一出《祭花》；夫人笑说那便与《赏荷》一般俗了。夕阳斜照，红碧桃如霞如锦，白的成了粉色，二人缓缓回房。晚间公子到洗红轩，终于唤林珊枝来问：琴言是正月二十四日告的假，如今已是三月初二，过了七八日还不回来；珊枝支吾说想必有事，公子便吩咐明日差人出城去问他几时进来。`,en:`Du Qinyan's leave has run out without his returning, and though it nags at Hua Guangsu he goes on with his spring. Fishing over the rail at the Returning Geese Islet, where copper strips set into the rocks make the current chime like a zither, he watches a red carp come nosing among the fallen petals, take his bait, and break the line, swallowing half the hook. On impulse he calls for the little boat — three zhang long, brightly lacquered, a pavilion at the bow, a rosewood table amidships, a green oilcloth roof with a white silk valance — and sends a maid to fetch Su Huanxiang, who arrives with Mingzhu, Huazhu, Hezhu, and Zengzhu, having already had food packed. On the water Huazhu pulls while Mingzhu pushes, the boat spins helplessly, Huazhu splashes her own face and Hezhu laughs until they change places. Then the passage: old vines trailing from the bridge rocks to the water, the cold clink of the current beneath, the willows and peaches of the Su Embankment, and at last, past Tanshui Fangshan, the Liuxian Court deep in bamboo and full of flowering peach. Butterflies hang everywhere; the maids catch two big ones and tether them with a hair plucked from a little maid's head to watch them dance in pairs. No flowers, says Hua, without wine — and he proposes a sacrifice to the peach with the bamboo-leaf spring brewed from a hundred flowers' dew. 

When his wife asks what offerings could suit so deliberate a rite, he has the curio cabinets unlocked; a sandalwood case yields a portrait of Lady Huarui painted by Madam Guan, with her *Palace Lyrics* copied beneath in Zhao Mengfu's small regular script — two marvels in one scroll. His wife proposes an orchid painting; he prefers Li Xiangjun's leaf from *The Peach Blossom Fan*, and adds his wife's own embroidered *New Songs from the Jade Terrace Preface*, praising her needle as unsurpassed. The three treasures are set out in the gallery with a boshan censer of lily incense and a Han jade goblet of wine; and instead of bowing, husband and wife each choose the finest tree — his a red peach, hers a white — and pour the wine at its roots. Seeing the maids' eyes follow the jar, she sends for a larger one so that they may play too, and Hua notes that they, at least, must bow. So the ten scatter with little cups among the blossoms like a flock of butterflies, and bow four times together. Brocade mats are spread beneath the trees, and over the flower wine she sets them to sing the scenes *Visiting Cui* and *Sleeping amid Fragrance* from *The Peach Blossom Fan* — two suites of eleven arias, the short ones combined, one apiece. 

Baozhu opens, and Hua declares the text fit to follow the *Four Dreams of the Jade Tea Hall*, while his wife holds that hardly anything written since can stand beside it. Mingzhu drops a line and is caught; Zhangzhu knots her handkerchief as she sings and flicks it into Hezhu's face, to applause; and when Aizhu begins *Sleeping amid Fragrance*, Hua sighs that such an aria deserves Bai Juyi's own singing girl, and his wife answers that these will do, being far better than the sheng and dan of any troupe outside. Afterwards she takes the brush and ranks them: Baozhu first, for a free spirit and a tender elegance that crowns the ten, then Aizhu, Zengzhu, Zhangzhu, Ruizhu, Zhenzhu, Huazhu and Hezhu, with Mingzhu last of all. Huazhu protests that she sang better than two placed above her, and gets an answer that is real criticism: she wanted to show her own strength and cover others' weakness by adding ornament, and that is stage singing, not pure singing; pure singing must be elegant, washed clean of powder, showing its own true colour, and she, used to arias broken by speech, cannot yet wash it off. Huazhu submits, and asks about Lin Shanzhi — stage singing too, says her mistress, whereas Qinyan, raw as he is, does catch the sense of the word "pure." The name turns Hua to his grievance: the boy is genuinely odd; they have treated him well enough and he always seems to feel wronged; his month's leave is long past and still he stays away. A proud nature, says his wife, not made to stand below others — but having fallen into that station there is no help for it; hold the office and observe its rites, and to be arrogant regardless is simply foolish. Hua smiles and says nothing. 

The rewards are graded: hairpins and rings for the first class, powder and perfume for the second. Outside, the two old serving women have quietly drunk half the wine jar; one dozes against a rock while the other begs Aizhu for her cast-off prizes and is mocked by the little maid Xiang'er, and the first sighs that when a person grows old the pearl yellows and loses its price. Hua recites two lines from *The Peony Pavilion*, and his wife allows that *A Stroll in the Garden and Waking from a Dream* is all softness and warmth but that Liu Mengmei is hardly worthy of Liniang. Of the *Hundred Yuan Plays* Hua says they may be sung but not read, and that the texts deserve burning; his wife goes one better — they may well have been good before they were set to music, and it is the singers, forcing them to the notation, who turned gold into iron, until they are mere note-books with no care for sense or tone; and where the words are good nobody can sing them, since after the *Nine-Palace Score* was fixed men learned only to change the words to fit the tune and never to shift the mode to fit the words, which is a real defect of the age. Delighted, Hua proposes new lyrics to the notation of *Liangzhou Xu*. He writes one first, closing on not wasting the shallow golden cup; she answers with another, ending that she envies the mandarin ducks and not the immortals — and he chants it over and over, strikes the table, and says that even *The Peach Blossom Fan* has no such fifty-seven characters, wreathed like scented cloud and scattered like a rain of petals. Baozhu and Aizhu learn them, lose the beat, and get it perfectly once the notation is written beside the words. Hua jokes that the two lyrics have cast the pair of them as leading man and leading lady, and that with a few more they might make a whole suite, *Viewing Flowers*, and have the ten act it as *Sacrificing to the Flowers* — which his wife dismisses as no better than *Appreciating the Lotuses*. The sun slants, the red peach burns like brocade and the white turns to powder, and they walk slowly back. That evening, at the Xihong Lodge, Hua at last summons Lin Shanzhi: Qinyan took leave on the twenty-fourth of the first month, it is now the second of the third, his month is seven or eight days past, and someone must be sent out tomorrow to ask when he means to come in.`},42:{zh:`葬事既毕，杜琴言身子不快，想安顿几日再进华府，不料长庆师娘一早便进房算起账来：一家一年总要三千吊才够，天福、天寿挣不出什么，他既不再进班子，钱自然出在华府身上，须讲定每月给她多少，"就算省俭些，大约二百吊一月总要的"；又诉自己三十几岁，苏州无家，回不去，开门七件事加上人情使费，不靠他靠谁。琴言听得发呆：当初曹长庆送他进府本为避难，讲的什么条件他一概不知，只听说师父每月到门房领一次，多少也不知；如今师父死了，府里未必照旧，他自己更无一钱，怎敢随口应承？师娘却疑心华府出手极阔，又疑他借师父之死撒手不管，冷笑道："交情越重，钱应该越多；若是不给钱的交情，要他做什么？"又说：他也没有花三千五千两替你出师，若出了师，我自然不能对你讲这些话；索性叫他拿三千两银子来出师，我可以置些产业，倒比零碎的好——两条路凭你走哪一条，总要讲明了才可以进城。一席话说得琴言几乎落泪：他原以为师父一死身子便由得自己，谁知师娘更利害。他想起凡有想不透的事经陆素兰一说便明白，遂带了小厮出门；偏偏素兰不在，转去找袁宝珠也不在。路上却被两个人跟着：一个圆脸混沌、脚下皂靴，一个獐头鼠目、便帽上拖着长红帽纬，一路品评"好一朵鲜花"，逼得他只好低头急走。回房长叹垂泪，又遇伍麻子进来吃着潮烟胡扯：说华府的赶车养马铡草的过年都穿狐皮袄，腰间大皮荷包解开来尽是银锞；说华府银子多得柜里装不下，堆在墙脚，隔两三年要发出来晒，人家趁势把五两的换他十两的，他也不点数，问起来只说"晒干了自然收小"；又说怡园山石底下埋着十缸金十缸银，看金的财神一头黄毛，看银的一头白毛，半夜坐在园墙上拿金银锭子打人，有时运的捡了发财，没时运的捡起来是块黄土还要生病。琴言听得也不觉失笑。

正说着，外头来了两个打茶围的客——原来就是路上那两人：乌大傻与姬亮轩，新近在戏园里认识，跟到门口，姬亮轩认出是琴言的住处，又想起奚十一说他在魏聘才处陪过酒，越发认定；乌大傻只图吃个镶边酒，力劝进来。天福、天寿散戏回来，乌大傻卖弄戏文，说王兰保的《盗令》《杀舟》、王桂保的《相约》《相骂》都是名人家数；天寿认出姬亮轩就是去年陪那位吃烟的老爷来、把师父打翻的那一伙，天福便骂"天报！叫他多病几天"。问起琴言，天福说他如今谁也不见，除了怡园哪一家都不去；伍麻子索性说"我们这个琴官如今是华公府的二爷，二位老爷高兴，叫天福、天寿伺候罢"。姬亮轩几番要溜，先托词小解，才跨出院子便被天寿一把扯住辫子，帽子也掉了；乌大傻却稳坐不动，只顾吃喝。姬亮轩终究挤进琴言房里去了：乌大傻先深深一揖，假意痛悼长庆，还问"那些联幛的公分有我的名字没有"；姬亮轩则满面堆笑替东家道歉，说敝东直爽不会体贴，尚祈包涵。乌大傻眼近视又腹中欠通，偏爱念对子充斯文，把壁上一副对子念错了两字。姬亮轩更吹得没边：说敝东在京里也算个阔老斗，与华光宿、徐子云不相上下，都是世交；前日徐少爷来访不遇，还在他书房坐了半日，再三约他逛园，他因想"未做过外任的人未必知道我们这一席极尊贵"，怕人不肯僭他的坐位，所以没去。

琴言听得坐不住，托词有事走了出来。席上不过八碟干果、四碟小菜、两碗白粥，姬亮轩暗想必是乌大傻讲明了省钱，既是他作东，索性吃他娘的；乌大傻却喝了两壶酒、吃尽小菜与两碗粥，反过来吩咐天寿添菜烫酒，说"今日是我拉他来的，你们巴结得不好，以后他就不肯来了"——姬亮轩这才放心。谁知乌大傻趁他们争着上烟，蹋着破皂靴匆匆先走了。姬亮轩找不着人，脸色顿变，吃完了饭便说今日没带钱，明日补情；天寿说可以送老爷回府去取，他不肯，起身要走，被两个孩子拉住，他便学起奚十一的腔调恫吓，说要照去年的样打他们一顿。伍麻子动了气：酒饭烟茶都是钱买来的，一个大钱不见面倒骂人不开眼；既没带钱，跟你回去领也不算得罪人。姬亮轩那样鸡肋身材木瓜脑袋，装威风也吓不动人，只得由他提着灯笼跟着，一路左绕右绕想遁去而不得。到了寓所开了拜匣，捡出票子来看去看来如割自己的肉，末了拈了一张两吊、又添一张一吊，拿纸捻蘸油送出。

伍麻子虽不识字，一到十的数目却认得，一看便叫住他，问相公开发与酒席钱在哪里；姬亮轩只说"一总在内"，任他不要也随他。伍麻子说摆个酒至少也得二十吊，何况添菜吃饭，三吊钱赏厨房打杂的还不够。及至门房里人出来解劝，说"我们这师爷也太想便宜了，既要乐又舍不得钱"，又说这是奚十一的寓处，伍麻子才知不好惹，只得回去交账。师娘一见三吊钱便骂开了：这些年可曾收过三吊钱？怎么不摔还他臊臊他的脸？两个相公留了两个客，烟茶酒饭闹得乌烟瘴气，还替人提灯笼做跟班；一个汉子连数目字都不认得。伍麻子火星直冒，回说自己见千见万也没见过这两个不爱脸的，一个喝了两碗粥先逃，一个时刻想跑，若不是碍着奚十一去年打上门来的凶势，岂肯只要三吊。次日他去照票，那后添的一吊竟还是假的；再到奚十一寓处找人，反被家人骂了一顿，只得自己赔上一吊交账，唯有咒骂而已。这一日琴言到底找着了宝珠与素兰，把师娘索钱的事细细商量。`,en:`The burial over, Du Qinyan is unwell and means to rest a few days before returning to the Hua mansion — but Changqing Shiniang comes into his room in the morning with an abacus already in her voice. The household needs three thousand strings a year; Tianfu and Tianshou earn nothing worth counting; since he no longer performs, the money must come from the Hua house, and a monthly figure must be fixed — two hundred strings at the very least, even reckoned frugally. She is past thirty, she says, with no home left in Suzhou and no way back, seven daily necessities to buy and social obligations besides: whom is she to depend on if not him? Qinyan is stunned. Cao Changqing sent him into that house to escape trouble, and on what terms he never knew; he only heard that his master collected something monthly at the gatehouse. Now that the master is dead the payments may well stop, and he has not a cash of his own — how can he promise what he cannot deliver? But the widow, suspecting both that the Hua purse is bottomless and that he means to shake her off, answers coldly that the deeper the friendship the larger the money should be, and that a friendship without money is worth nothing; that Hua has not, after all, spent three or five thousand taels to buy him out of his apprenticeship — and if he had, she could not be saying this. Better still, let him produce three thousand taels for a formal release, so that she can buy property instead of taking it in driblets. Either road he pleases, but it must be settled before he goes back into the city. Qinyan is close to tears: he had thought that his master's death made him his own, and finds the widow harder than the master. Remembering that whatever he cannot see through, Lu Sulan can explain in a sentence, he goes out with a page — and finds Sulan out, and Yuan Baozhu out too. 

On the way two men follow him, one round-faced and vacant in black boots, the other with a stoat's head and rat's eyes and a long red tassel on his cap, remarking aloud on the fine flower before them, until he has to hurry home with his head down. There he is trapped by Wu Mazi, who smokes and retails wonders: that in the Hua household even the carters and grooms wear fox fur at new year and carry purses of silver ingots; that the silver overflows the chests and is stacked against the wall and must be spread in the sun every few years, during which people exchange five-tael ingots for ten-tael ones and their master never counts, and when he notices the silver has shrunk they tell him a day's sun dried it smaller; and that under the rockery at Yiyuan lie ten jars of gold and ten of silver, guarded by a yellow-haired god of wealth and a white-haired one who sit on the wall at midnight throwing ingots at passers-by — the lucky carry off gold, the unlucky pick up a clod of earth and fall ill. Even Qinyan laughs. Just then two callers arrive for tea — the very pair from the street: Wu Dasha and Ji Liangxuan, lately acquainted at the theatre, who followed him home, Ji recognizing the house and recalling Xi Shiyi's boast that Qinyan had served wine at Wei Pincai's, and Wu Dasha pressing to go in on the chance of a free dinner. Tianfu and Tianshou come back from the theatre; Wu Dasha airs his connoisseurship, praising Wang Lanbao's *Stealing the Token* and *Killing in the Boat* and Wang Guibao's *The Rendezvous* and *Mutual Scolding*; and Tianshou recognizes Ji as one of the party that came last year with the opium-smoking gentleman who knocked their master down, at which Tianfu says heaven has repaid him and may he stay ill a good while. Asked for Qinyan, Tianfu says he receives nobody and goes nowhere but Yiyuan, while Wu Mazi declares grandly that their Qinguan is now Second Master of the Hua mansion and sees no one, but that Tianfu and Tianshou may attend the gentlemen. Ji tries repeatedly to escape — pleading the privy and bolting across the courtyard, only for Tianshou to catch his queue and pull off his hat — while Wu Dasha sits fast and eats. Ji does force his way into Qinyan's room at last: Wu Dasha bows deeply and mourns Changqing with false warmth, asking whether his name was on the subscription for the funeral scrolls; Ji apologizes oilily for his employer, who is blunt and does not know how to be considerate. Being both short-sighted and unlettered, Wu Dasha loves to read couplets aloud to look learned, and gets two characters of the one on the wall wrong. 

Ji boasts without limit: his employer ranks in the capital with Hua Guangsu and Xu Ziyun, all of them old family connections; young Master Xu called the other day, missed him, sat half a day in his study and pressed him again and again to visit the garden — but he decided against it, since a man who has never held a provincial post might not know how exalted a legal secretary's seat is, and might have senior guests unwilling to give him precedence. Qinyan can bear no more and leaves the room. At table there are eight plates of dried fruit, four small dishes and two bowls of plain congee; Ji concludes that Wu Dasha must have stipulated economy, and that since Wu is standing the treat he may as well eat his fill. Wu Dasha drinks two pots, empties the dishes and both bowls, and then tells Tianshou to add dishes and warm the wine, since he brought Ji and they must do him honour or he will never come again — which settles Ji's mind entirely. But while the boys are quarrelling over the opium pipe, Wu Dasha slips out and shuffles off in his broken boots, comfortably full. Finding him gone, Ji changes colour, finishes his dinner, and announces that he has brought no money and will make it good tomorrow. Tianshou offers to see him home for it; he refuses, rises to go, is held by both boys, and falls back on Xi Shiyi's manner, threatening them with a beating like last year's. Wu Mazi's temper goes: wine, food, tea and tobacco all cost money, not one cash of which has been seen, and it is no insult to walk a man home for his purse. With his chicken-rib frame and papaya head, Ji cannot make the bluff carry, and has to let Wu Mazi light a lantern and follow him, winding through streets in a vain attempt to shake him off. 

At his lodging he opens his document case and picks over his notes as though cutting his own flesh, selects one for two strings, adds one for a single string, and twists a paper spill to carry them out. Wu Mazi cannot read, but from taking Changqing's notes to the bank he knows his numerals, and calls him back to ask where the actors' fee and the wine money are. That is the lot, says Ji, take it or leave it. A party alone costs twenty strings at the least, Wu Mazi retorts, let alone extra dishes and dinner; three strings would not cover what they tip the kitchen. Then the gatekeeper comes out, laughs that their secretary wants his pleasure and grudges the price, and advises Wu Mazi to write off the loss and make no noise — and, learning that this is Xi Shiyi's house, Wu Mazi retreats. The widow, seeing three strings, storms: in all these years has such a payment ever been taken? Why did he not throw it back in the man's face? Two performers entertained two guests with tobacco, tea, wine and food, and he even played footman with a lantern and came back with three strings under his arm — a grown man who cannot read a numeral. Wu Mazi answers hotly that he has seen thousands of notes and never two such shameless creatures, one of whom drank two bowls of congee and fled while the other tried to bolt every minute; and that but for Xi Shiyi's name — the man who last year came looking for Qinyan, threw his master head over heels and smashed the furniture — he would have pressed for ten or twenty. Next morning he takes the notes to be cashed and finds the added one a forgery; going to Xi's lodging to find Ji, he is only cursed by the servants, and must make the string good out of his own pocket. That same day Qinyan at last finds Baozhu and Sulan, and lays the widow's demand before them.`},43:{zh:`琴言一夜无眠，次早请了袁宝珠来。宝珠替他算账：一年二千四百吊，三年便是三千两，而"养膳"二字更没有尽期；华光宿性情不常靠不定，要他出师或者也肯，只是他既出了银子，人便算他的，只怕一辈子终局在那府里，须先立定主意。琴言道，此时虽未出师也由不得自己；宝珠笑说人家只有一"出"，你今有两"出"，不要将来犯了七出。陆素兰随后到，一听便拍手：果然不出所料，索性应承出师，价钱再议。宝珠道你一口答应出师、一口又劈断银价，分明胸有成竹。素兰这才说破：十天前他与苏蕙芳已议过，若四五千吊可以出师，几个相好凑一半也不难。三人便同到蕙芳寓中。蕙芳正看屈道翁新画的册页，末一幅画美人倚阑，阑外落花双燕，正是"落花人独立，微雨燕双飞"的诗意，题着"春色关心燕燕飞，杏花细雨不沾衣"一绝，琴言看了想起当年那个灯谜，怅然出神。

蕙芳听了原委，只说妇道人家的话不作准，又问谁去开口。四人遂同往怡园赏杏。将到东风昨夜楼，先听见楼上笛声清歌：徐子云唱一枝《懒画眉》，萧次贤接唱一枝。上楼一问，才知那是次贤旧友所撰《梅花梦》二十出的曲本，他新从书箱翻出，照《九宫谱》填了工尺，只谱得《入梦》一出；蕙芳劝他自己按谱，切莫交与班中教师，那些人只顺自己的口，去的去添的添，改到不通而后止；说到请屈道翁，次贤笑说他于音律最不讲究，只爱弹琴，还说三代之后乐已亡，所以把《乐记》并入《礼记》。此楼弯弯曲曲数十间，暗通曲达如迷楼，四围杏花，隔山又是桃花坞，云蒸霞蔚。四旦一色小毛外褂，玄狐云狐相映，次贤、子云看得顾盼频频，只说楼外千枝红杏比不上楼中四个玉人。

席间由干荔枝、干江瑶谈起，蕙芳忽然怅怅，问"花能开几日"，次贤答"七十年"——人生七十，活一年开一年；蕙芳又问今年的花留得到明年么，琴言说开花不如不开，宝珠说花谢不如不谢，蕙芳则道：鲜荔枝何等佳妙，干了便酸得可厌，何以形貌变而气味也变？人过几年，也就清而变浊、细而变粗、甘而变酸了。宝珠接口说就是酸些也是妙品，总比俗味强多了，三旦齐声叹息。次贤便以春华秋实相解，又论食品分仙品、神品、逸品、妙品，引《孟子》"口之于味也有同嗜焉"，说那是易牙调的味，如今的厨子只合自己的口味。随后续对戏目：《游湖借伞》对《搜山打车》，《别母乱箭》对《训子单刀》，《闹朝扑犬》对《打店偷鸡》。子云又出摆骰成句之令：宝珠摆"日边红杏倚云栽"，素兰摆"红杏枝头春意闹"，蕙芳摆"一色杏花红十里"，子云说下句"状元归去马如飞"正是田春航的预兆，合席同贺；琴言摆"桃花流水杳然去"，又摆"一一归巢却羡鸦"，次贤瞅了他一眼，暗想今日玉侬出语何以这般颓唐。饭后在留春坞煮茗，宝珠把师娘索价与蕙芳、素兰的主意告诉子云。众人自报能凑之数：蕙芳与宝珠、素兰六百金，王氏弟兄与李玉林、梅子玉四百金，次贤二百，刘文泽三百，王恂、史南湘三百，共一千八百，余下的自然要子云包圆。子云问"难道华星北倒干干净净一文不花"，蕙芳说不必要他出钱——一总要他拿出来他也肯，只是玉侬便得在他家一辈子了。子云点头称是，索性一概不用他们出力："只要你们去讲，讲妥了银子现成，叫他们来领就是了"；又叮嘱以速为妙，免得假期已满进去了再找出来费事。琴言感激得跪下拜谢，泪流不止，子云连忙搀起，说"我看你终非风尘中人，不过一举手之劳，何足称谢"，众人也都为之枨触。

至于谁去开口，蕙芳荐了叶茂林——琴言是他同来的，戏也是他教的，老成会说话；茂林一口应承，却要蕙芳同去："那位庆奶奶嘴里好像画眉哨的一般，我有几分怯他。"定更后二人提灯到了长庆家。那寡妇扎着白包头、穿孝衫而下系绿绸裤，黄瘦长挑，三十来岁，一开口便是满腹苦水：当家的在日，那盏灯边一天十几个人挨次躺下，倒像吏部选缺；如今连狗也没有一个上门；两个傻孩子赔饭赔衣裳；昨日两个生人打茶围，吃完只开发三吊钱。她说想带几千银子回扬州，与兄弟开个大铺子。蕙芳便编出一位江南来的知县：为人极好，只是不大有钱，见了琴言十分赏识，想认作儿子，肯替他出师，三五千两是花不起的，三千吊钱还可打算。寡妇啧啧连声：琴言去年半年就挣了整万吊，三千吊就出师，人家几个月便捞回本钱！茂林辩说若卖去作儿子，攻书上学娶亲，只有赔钱；寡妇索性开口要三千两纹银，否则她还候着华公子——那是有名花钱的主儿，或者一万八千都可以，不然还有徐老爷更好说话。蕙芳这才冷笑着下了一记重手：华公子近来并不喜欢他，非但不肯出师，只怕还要打发他出来——我们常到他府里去，如今不过一间闲房给他住着，也不常使唤；新年叩岁公子每人赏一个元宝，何以独他没有？我见他箱里通共六十几两，还是去年中秋积到如今。徐老爷近来也有些烦了，肯出师早出了，何待今日。

除了这两人，谁给你二百吊一月？婶娘既明白，何不看破些，当自己亲儿子让些下来。寡妇道："杀人一刀，骑马一跑，要爽快；我让二百两，二千八百是不可少的。"茂林见口风松了，便劝定二千四百，说是照应孤儿寡妇、积些阴德，中人分文不取，都贴在正数内。寡妇暗算：万一华府打发出来，这孩子又强不肯唱戏；徐老爷心上人又多；二千四百两已有六千吊，也不算少——终于被这条"软麻绳"捆住，应允了。临了蕙芳还留一手："只怕那边凑不起来，倒叫婶娘见怪。"次早蕙芳到怡园，子云即命总管开了二千四百两的银票，另开五十两赏叶茂林。蕙芳先把五十两送了，再同去交割，只说人家好容易凑得一千九百，自己代借五百，一总一张票子；茂林代写字据交琴言收执。寡妇备了几个碟子相留，蕙芳只叫琴言收拾行李、辞灵谢恩，一同回去，再进城谢华公子。琴言东西不多，蕙芳又拿十吊钱的票子代他分赏众人；他拜了师父的灵痛哭一场，又向师娘拜辞，寡妇也掉了好些眼泪，直送到门口，他才洒泪而别。次日寡妇谢了茂林一百吊，茂林已心满意足。只是琴言命中磨蝎颇多，才出了师，忽又生出一场气恼来。`,en:`After a sleepless night Qinyan sends for Yuan Baozhu, who does the arithmetic for him: two thousand four hundred strings a year is three thousand taels in three years, and "maintenance" has no term at all; Hua Guangsu's temper cannot be relied on, and though he might pay for a release if the mood took him, the release would make Qinyan his, and he might end his life inside those walls — so the decision must be made first. Even unreleased, Qinyan answers, he is not his own; and Baozhu jokes that other people have one release and he wants two, and had better not end with the seven grounds for divorce. Lu Sulan arrives, hears it, and claps: exactly as foreseen — accept the release and argue the price. Baozhu observes that to agree in one breath and cut the figure in the next argues a plan already made, and Sulan admits that ten days ago he and Su Huifang had reckoned that if four or five thousand strings would buy the release, their friends could raise half of it between them. The three go on to Huifang, who is looking through album leaves newly painted by Qu Daoweng; the last shows a beauty at a railing with fallen petals outside and two swallows in the rain — the very conceit of the old lantern riddle — inscribed with a quatrain about spring feeling and apricot rain, and Qinyan stands before it lost in the memory. Told the news, Huifang warns only that a woman's word is not to be trusted and asks who will do the talking. Then all four go to Yiyuan for the apricot. Nearing the East Wind Last Night Tower they hear a flute and singing above: Xu Ziyun through one aria to *Too Lazy to Paint the Brows*, and Xiao Cixian through another. Upstairs they learn it is *Dream of the Plum Blossom*, a libretto in twenty scenes by an old friend of Cixian's, dug out of his book chest and fitted with notation from the *Nine-Palace Score* — one scene, *Entering the Dream*, so far. 

Huifang urges him to score it himself and never hand it to troupe teachers, who cut and add to suit their own mouths until the sense is gone; and when Qu Daoweng is proposed, Cixian laughs that the old man cares nothing for music but the qin, holding that after the Three Dynasties music was lost, which is why the *Record of Music* was folded into the *Liji*. The tower runs to dozens of rooms on two and three storeys, winding and interlinked like the fabled maze, ringed with apricot, with the peach bank beyond a small hill in full bloom — and the four young men in their fur-edged coats, black fox against cloud fox, make Ziyun and Cixian say that the thousand red boughs outside cannot match the four jade figures within. Over wine the talk of dried lychee turns Huifang suddenly grave. How many days can a flower bloom? Seventy years, says Cixian: reckon a man's life at seventy, and he blooms one year for each. This year's flower is not last year's, says Huifang; and can this year's be kept till next? That depends on the keeper, says Ziyun. Better not to bloom, says Qinyan; better not to fade, says Baozhu; and Huifang answers that fresh lychee is exquisite and dried lychee disagreeably sour — why should a change of form change the flavour? So a man after some years turns from clear to muddy, fine to coarse, sweet to sour. 

Even sour, says Baozhu, it is finer than common flavours; and the three of them sigh together. Cixian offers blossom and fruit each in season, and the talk passes to food graded like men into the immortal, the divine, the untrammelled and the exquisite, with *Mencius* quoted on all mouths sharing one taste — true of Yi Ya's cooking, says Cixian, whereas today's cooks suit only their own palates. Then more matched play titles — *Borrowing an Umbrella on the Lake* against *Searching the Mountain, Driving the Cart*, *Parting from Mother amid Flying Arrows* against *Admonishing the Son with a Single Saber*, *Raising a Ruckus at Court, Pouncing on a Dog* against *Attacking the Inn; Stealing the Chicken* — and then Ziyun's game of arranging three dice into a line of verse, where a four may stand for a person, a flower, water or wind as required. Baozhu makes the red apricot leaning on cloud by the sun; Sulan the spring clamour on the apricot bough; Huifang a whole ten li of apricot red, whose companion line about the new zhuangyuan riding away Ziyun declares an omen for Tian Chunhang, so that the table drinks to it together. Qinyan's throws are all of vanishing water and birds returning to the nest, and Cixian glances at him, wondering why everything he says today is so dispirited. Over tea afterwards at Liuchun Wu, Baozhu lays the widow's demand and the friends' scheme before Ziyun. The subscriptions are counted: six hundred from Huifang, Baozhu and Sulan; four hundred from the Wang brothers, Li Yulin and Mei Ziyu; two hundred from Cixian, three from Liu Wenze, three from Wang Xun and Shi Nanxiang — eighteen hundred in all, the rest to fall on Ziyun. Is Hua Xingbei to escape without a penny, Ziyun asks; better not to ask him, says Huifang, for he would pay it all and Yunong would then be his for life. Ziyun agrees, and then refuses their money altogether: none of them is well off, and what is squeezed out now will pinch later; let them do the talking only, and the silver will be ready. And let it be quick, he adds, since the leave has expired and it will be far harder to get the boy out once he has gone back in. Qinyan kneels weeping to thank him and is raised at once — Ziyun, moved, says he has always thought him no creature of the dusty world, and that it costs him only the lifting of a hand. 

For a negotiator Huifang names Ye Maolin, who brought Qinyan north and taught him his parts, is steady and speaks well; Maolin agrees but begs Huifang to come too, since that Mistress Qing's tongue goes like a thrush's whistle and he is half afraid of her. After the first watch the two go by lantern to Changqing's widow. She appears in a white headband and mourning gown over long green silk trousers, sallow, thin and tall, about thirty, and sharp of tongue, and pours out her grievances: while her husband lived a dozen men waited their turn at the opium lamp like candidates for a vacancy at the Board of Personnel, and since he died not even a dog comes to the door; the two boys cost more in food and clothes than they earn; and two strangers who came for tea yesterday ate and drank and paid three strings. She would go back to Yangzhou and set her brother up in a shop, if she had a few thousand taels. So Huifang invents his magistrate: a Jiangnan man newly come, excellent but not rich, who saw Qinyan, admired him greatly, wants him for a son, and will pay for the release — three or five thousand taels being beyond him, but three thousand strings perhaps manageable. The widow clicks her tongue: Qinyan earned a full ten thousand strings in half a year on the stage, and a buyer would recover three thousand in months. Maolin answers that a boy bought as a son must study, be schooled and married, and brings nothing in but expense, and that the gentleman is a philanthropist. She names three thousand taels of fine silver and no less — otherwise she will wait for Hua Guangsu, a famous spender who might give eight or ten thousand, or for Master Xu, who is fond of him and would be easier still. 

Then Huifang strikes: Hua is not pleased with him now and, far from buying his release, may well turn him out; we are in that house constantly, and he has one idle room and is seldom sent for; at New Year the young lord gave each of us an ingot and gave him nothing; his trunk holds sixty-odd taels saved since Mid-Autumn. And Master Xu has grown weary of the business; had he meant to buy the release he would not have waited till now. Apart from those two, who will pay two hundred strings a month? Be clear-sighted, treat the boy as your own son, come down a little, and the two sides will meet: three thousand strings is too little, but two thousand taels I can answer for. Kill a man at one stroke and run a horse in one gallop, says the widow — she does her own hair and binds her own feet, but she does not like knots; she will come down two hundred, and two thousand eight hundred is the least. Feeling the wind change, Maolin proposes twenty-four hundred, framed as charity to a widow and an orphan and a work of hidden merit, with the intermediaries taking no commission but letting it all go into the principal — and, reckoning that the Hua house may indeed cast the boy out, that the boy is stubborn and will not perform, that Master Xu has many favourites, and that twenty-four hundred taels is six thousand strings after all, she is at last tied up by that soft hemp rope of an old man, all sister-this and sister-that, and consents. Huifang leaves himself one thread: he doubts whether the other side can even raise it. Next morning at Yiyuan, Ziyun has his steward draw a draft for twenty-four hundred taels and another for fifty as a present to Ye Maolin. Huifang hands over the fifty first, then goes with him to close the business, saying that the buyer could scrape together only nineteen hundred and that he borrowed five hundred more himself; Maolin writes the deed for Qinyan to keep. The widow lays out dishes to entertain them, but Huifang only tells Qinyan to pack, take leave of Cao Changqing's spirit tablet and thank his mistress, and come away — and then go into the city to thank Hua Guangsu without delay. Qinyan's belongings are few; Huifang produces a ten-string note to be shared among the household in his name; he bows before the tablet and weeps bitterly, and takes leave of the widow, who also weeps and sees him to the gate. Next day she gives Ye Maolin a hundred strings, and he is well content. But Qinyan's stars are crossed, and no sooner is he free than a fresh vexation begins.`},44:{zh:`杜琴言出师的次日，华光宿命林珊枝打发外跟班姚贤出城催他回府。姚贤到长庆家只见伍麻子，说昨日有个江南人替他出了师，已经搬走，住处不知；问师娘，又说一早上坟去了。姚贤气恼出门，恰见两个相公坐车驰过，认得车里像是琴言，便疑他又出去陪酒。半路上撞见两个相好——徐子云的家人孟七与奚十一的家人胡八——三人进馆子小酌。姚贤把话说了，孟七笑说那是讹言：琴言自师父死后天天在怡园，老爷为他请了半月多客；昨日总管还叫他到日新银号开了二千四百两的银票，另一张五十两，交与苏蕙芳去替他出师，方才路上还见他与蕙芳同车回园。又说两家老爷素来相好，常送重价古玩，若要这个人写个帖子去讨便是，何必白花二千四百两。胡八却添出一段：上月他家老爷在宏济寺魏聘才处叫琴言陪了一天酒，还把姨奶奶一对广东买来值一千四百块钱的翡翠镯赏了他；如今老爷病重，他一次也不曾来看，可见没有良心，跟了华公子又想跟徐老爷。

这三个下人各说一半，姚贤回府时顺口又添了好些，还说路上见他与人同车陪酒去了。珊枝先怔了一怔，转念又觉琴言在府里"装腔作势、冷气逼人"，如今连魏聘才、奚十一的酒都陪，正好臊臊他的脸，索性照直去回。他到园中寻公子，却见桃花林下蕊珠跪着背《长恨歌》，背到"梨花一枝春带雨"便接不下去，爱珠在旁笑，众婢起哄——原来这两日公子与夫人拿念唐诗作消遣，背错要罚，那日袁宝珠背李义山《无题》六首只错一字，爱珠背《琵琶行》一字不错。公子听见太湖石后有人偷望，唤出珊枝；珊枝故作吞吐，把姚贤的话细细说了。华公子气得脸都白了，连说"琴言丧尽天良，而度香笑里藏刀，欺人太甚"，扬言要问个明白，还要把琴言撵出京去。珊枝一面婉劝，一面下暗话："断没有将野鸡养成家鸡的"；侍婢也劝：他若真心向着公子，有人替他出师岂有不来禀明的，公子又岂有不肯与他出师之理；何况为一个不要紧的人伤了两代世交的情分，外人也只会说徐老爷抢人、赞公子大量。公子气略平，回房又忍不住向苏浣香说了。

夫人问什么叫出师，听明白后只淡淡道：人家爱替他出师，干我们甚事；琴言到底算个优伶，若闹起来，狎优的名声二家都难免——公子素来敬爱夫人，听了心气又消一半，却终究舍不得，欲行不可、欲罢不能。谁知过了一夜，早上忽又恼起来，命珊枝把琴言的衣箱什物装车，另写一帖，亲送怡园面交徐子云。那日琴言原打算初七再进城——因子云初六要为史南湘、田春航送场，并请屈道翁，约梅子玉、颜仲清作陪——正在梨院与子云、萧次贤、袁宝珠闲话：次贤劝他索性辞了华府住到园里来，子云笑说那还了得，华星北必说我夺其所好；又谈起今年会试与博学宏词两条路，说田春航是当行出色之文、史南湘才气纵横，只怕遇着冬烘考官要受委屈。正说着，门上报华府送箱子来。珊枝进园，琴言一见便躲到屋后。那封信写得极狠：说琴言告假逾期，家人出城才知已由徐子云替他出师并已收用，今将箱笼一并送上，"想琴言断无颜面前来自取"；又说闻此子下流已甚，各处陪酒，惟利是爱，本欲拘回重处，只怕有负尊意，请兄以后严加管束，"弟必留心查察，如有闻见，必为详达，代兄撵逐，勿使名园玷辱"。子云看罢，不白之冤有口难辩，气得两手冰冷，与次贤面面相觑，只冷笑几声；次贤对珊枝说来不及写回字，明日同去当面讲。

珊枝去后，琴言取信一字字看去，忽然一腔怒气直涌上来，眼前发黑，喉中如物噎住，两眼一翻仰面便倒。子云掐人中，次贤抱住摩心，好一盏茶工夫才吐出一口痰苏醒过来，睁眼便泪如泉涌，随即放声大哭——真有三年不雨之冤、六月飞霜之惨，哭了一个时辰仍是有泪无声。子云想去剖辩反长他志气，索性断了这交情：他说这必是林珊枝从中调唆，箱子既送来便不必再去自取，免得再遭羞辱。琴言哽咽道谢，只说自己苦命害了多少人，早该死了；次贤便劝他："你既已出来，从此一无罣碍，就在园中与我作个忘年小友，我将琴棋书画、词赋诗文教你件件精通，将来成个名流，不强如在华府当书童么？"子云更说要索性把他们一班都请进园来住下，不必唱戏，几年后栽培出一班人物，胜过那些不通举人与三等秀才。苏蕙芳赶来，听明缘故反拍手称快，说天下有这么好事，求也求不到，还哭什么；又说自己这两年就想改行，只是无行可改，跟官与唱戏在伯仲之间，做买卖又不在行，非出京不能改图，"总也离不了小旦二字"——袁宝珠说他的心也一样，只不知天从人愿否。是夜三人同宿梨花院，讲到五更才睡。而珊枝回府复命，只说徐老爷拆帖看罢一句话没有，只冷笑一声，箱子也收下了。华公子本指望子云认个不是、自把琴言送来，好消此恨，谁知对方不发一言、公然笑纳，连回字也没有，气得双眉倒竖、脸泛浓霜，一时竟讲不出话来。`,en:`The day after Du Qinyan's release, Hua Guangsu tells Lin Shanzhi to send the outer attendant Yao Xian to fetch him back. At Cao Changqing's house Yao finds only Wu Mazi, who says that a Jiangnan gentleman bought the boy out yesterday and took him away, that his lodging is unknown, and that Changqing's widow is out at the graves. Coming away angry, Yao sees a carriage go by with what looks like Qinyan inside, and concludes he is off to pour wine for somebody. On the road he meets two friends — Meng Qi of Xu Ziyun's household and Hu Ba of Xi Shiyi's — and the three go into an eating-house. Told the story, Meng Qi laughs it off: since his master died Qinyan has been at Yiyuan daily, where their master has been entertaining for a fortnight, and only yesterday the steward had him draw a draft for two thousand four hundred taels, and another for fifty, and hand them to Su Huifang to buy the release; he saw the two of them driving back to the garden not an hour ago. Besides, he adds, the two houses are on such terms, and exchange such costly curios, that a note would have got the boy for nothing — why throw away twenty-four hundred taels? Hu Ba supplies a different half: last month his own master had Qinyan pour wine for a whole day at Wei Pincai's rooms in the Hongji Temple, and gave him a pair of jadeite bracelets bought in Canton worth fourteen hundred dollars; and now that his master is gravely ill the boy has never once called — plainly a creature without gratitude, who serves Hua Guangsu and angles after Xu Ziyun besides. Each of the three knows half a truth, and Yao Xian, reporting back, adds a good deal on his own account, including the carriage and the wine-pouring. 

Shanzhi is startled for a moment, then reflects that in the mansion Qinyan gave himself airs and froze everyone he met, and that if he will now drink with Wei Pincai and Xi Shiyi he deserves to have his face burned — so he goes to report it in full. In the garden he finds Ruizhu kneeling under the peach trees reciting the *Song of Everlasting Sorrow* and sticking fast at the pear blossom in spring rain, with Aizhu laughing at her and the rest joining in: for these two days master and mistress have been amusing themselves by setting the maids to learn Tang poems, with forfeits for mistakes — Yuan Baozhu got through six of Li Shangyin's *Untitled* poems with a single slip, and Aizhu the whole *Song of the Pipa* without one. Hua, catching sight of someone peering from behind a Taihu stone, calls Shanzhi out; and Shanzhi, hesitating for effect, tells everything Yao Xian said. Hua goes white with rage: Qinyan has lost every shred of conscience, and Duxiang hides a knife in his smile and presumes intolerably; he will have it out with him, and drive the boy out of the capital altogether. Shanzhi soothes him with one hand and poisons with the other — nobody ever turned a wild pheasant into a house fowl; and the maids add that if Qinyan's heart had been with his master he would surely have come and reported the offer, and would the young lord have refused to pay for the release himself? And why, for so unimportant a person, wound a friendship two generations old, when the world will only say that Master Xu snatched another man's servant and praise the young lord's magnanimity? Calmer, Hua tells Su Huanxiang. What is this "release"? she asks, and having heard, answers coolly that if someone chooses to pay for it, what is that to them; Qinyan is an actor when all is said, and if a quarrel is made of it both houses will bear the name of keeping performers. Hua, who respects his wife, agrees — and still cannot let it go, unable either to act or to leave off. By morning his temper has risen again: he orders Shanzhi to load Qinyan's boxes onto a cart and carry them, with a letter, to Yiyuan and put them into Xu Ziyun's own hands. 

Qinyan meanwhile had meant to go into the city on the seventh, since Ziyun was giving a dinner on the sixth to see Shi Nanxiang and Tian Chunhang off to the examination hall, with Qu Daoweng invited and Mei Ziyu and Yan Zhongqing to keep him company. He is sitting in the pear court with Ziyun, Xiao Cixian and Yuan Baozhu — Cixian urging him to leave the Hua household outright and live in the garden, Ziyun laughing that Hua Xingbei would certainly accuse him of stealing what another loved; and the talk running on to this year's two roads, the metropolitan examination and the special examination in broad learning, with Tian Chunhang's writing judged exactly of its kind and Shi Nanxiang's brilliant but liable to suffer under a dull examiner — when the gatekeeper announces boxes from the Hua mansion. Qinyan slips behind the house at the sight of Shanzhi. The letter is savage: Qinyan overstayed his leave, and only on sending a servant out did the writer learn that his brother had bought the boy's release and taken him into service; his boxes are therefore returned, since he can hardly have the face to fetch them himself. It adds that the boy is reported utterly debased, pouring wine wherever he is asked and loving nothing but profit, which made the writer's hair stand up; that he had meant to have him seized and punished, and refrained only out of deference; and that his brother should keep him under strict discipline, for the writer will be watching, and on hearing anything will report it and have him expelled on his brother's behalf, so that a famous garden not be disgraced. Ziyun, wronged and unable to answer, goes cold to the fingertips, exchanges a look with Cixian, and laughs shortly; Cixian tells Shanzhi there is no time to write a reply and they will speak to the young lord tomorrow in person. When he has gone, Qinyan reads the letter through, and the anger rises in him until his sight blackens, something closes his throat, his eyes turn up and he falls backward. 

Ziyun presses the point beneath his nose, Cixian holds him up and rubs his chest, and after a full quarter of an hour he brings up phlegm and revives, opening his eyes to a flood of tears and then to open weeping — the grief of three years' drought, of frost in the sixth month — which goes on an hour and ends in silent crying. Ziyun decides that to argue his innocence would only feed the other's pride, and that the friendship may as well be cut: this is Lin Shanzhi's work, he says, and since the boxes have come there is no need to fetch anything and be insulted again. Qinyan thanks him brokenly, saying that his own cursed life has harmed too many people and he should have died long ago; and Cixian answers that being out of that house he is now free of every entanglement, and may stay in the garden as his young friend across the years, to be taught qin and chess, painting and calligraphy, poetry and prose until he is master of them all and becomes a man of name — which is better than being a study-boy in the Hua mansion. Ziyun goes further: he will bring the whole company of them into the garden to live, with no more performing, and in a few years will have raised up a set of men worth a hundred of the illiterate graduates and third-class licentiates. Su Huifang arrives, hears the cause, and claps his hands — such good fortune could not be prayed for, and what is there to cry about? He adds that for two years he has wanted to leave the profession and found no profession to leave it for: attendance on an official is much the same trade, business is beyond him, and in the capital he would be recognized anyway, so nothing can change until he leaves it — for the name of a female-role actor sticks. Baozhu says his heart is the same, if heaven will allow it. The three share the pear court that night and talk until the fifth watch. Shanzhi, reporting back, says only that Master Xu read the letter, said not one word, laughed once coldly, and kept the boxes. Hua had expected an apology and the boy delivered up to appease him; receiving instead silence, acceptance, and not even an answer, he sits with his brows straight up and frost on his face, unable to speak at all.`},45:{zh:`琴言住进怡园，徐子云、萧次贤日日开导，名旦不约而来，牡丹台、芍药圃处处可游，愁闷消去一半。前一日子云已请屈道翁与梅子玉、史南湘、颜仲清、田春航、刘文泽、王恂并诸名旦聚了一天，因南湘、春航次早入场，散得甚早。这日晚饭后，子玉趁着一钩新月带了云儿再来，先到次贤处，却被一个五十余岁、一部长须的湖州古董客王胡子缠住：那人一见便问家世、问科第，自夸二十年走过十四省，去年八月还在江西吉安见过子玉的父亲，消了一个宣炉十匣笔；他此来是想把一部《图书集成》减价三千两消给子云。次贤见子玉不耐，便打发他到已移居海棠春圃的琴言处去。琴言穿着月白夹袄、大红盘花绣履，倚着海棠对太湖石凝思，见了子玉便邀他同看夕照映花。他问：人说海棠有色无香，你可闻见香么？子玉答得极妙：既已占了国色，何必再占国香；你闻见的怕是那边的丁香。海棠自有一种香气，藏在花的肌肤颜色里不肯轻易吐出，须凝眸谛视良久，那清香才自然随人上到鼻孔，也不是人人闻得出来的——琴言攀下一枝细闻，果然点头，子玉便说"此所谓心香"。入屋坐定，琴言说出一段心事：从前在师傅处最苦，点了他的戏，心里就像上法场；及到上场，索性把这身子不当作自己，只当是戏里那个人，任人看任人笑；一下台露了本相又觉抱愧；陪个生人在酒席上更如芒刺在背，看人家自然得很，自己却心口都不听使唤。

及至要进华府，只道不知怎样受罪，进去了倒也不见得怎样，惟有这片心人总瞧不出来——纵格外待得好，究竟拿他当个供人喜笑的优伶。至于度香待他，更没有什么说的。可是"此时身虽安了，心实未安"：从前在火炕里只求早死，如今出来了，心倒比从前更乱；戏是决意不唱，奴才也不再作，却又作什么呢？人待得这样好，他偏愁愁闷闷，倒像不知足——所以这是活路上的烦闷，不是死路上的算计；"生在世间没有一个归着，你教我这心怎能放得开"。子玉说若离了京，人家不知出身，便与平人一样，只恨没有这样一个人带他出京；琴言反问"你怎么倒愿意我出京"，子玉道：我心里愿与你终身相聚、同苦同乐，只恨自己一无能力，又时时虑着老人家回来或再放外任要带我出去，却也不好为着我耽误你一世。琴言说除非等他作了官；子玉道：我不想中博学宏词作翰林，只想得一个外任小官，同你出去，就心满意足了。谈到定更，子云、次贤进来，说起王胡子只是利心太重，那部《古今图书集成》他自己也有一部，不过如聋子的耳朵摆设而已；留他两日，倒要看看他扶乩的本事是真是假。

次日辰刻，子玉衣冠而来。到的有屈道翁、金吉甫与颜仲清、刘文泽、王恂，名旦是袁宝珠、苏蕙芳、陆素兰、李玉林、金漱芳、王兰保、王桂保、林春喜、袁琪官并琴言，连主客共十九人；南湘、春航尚在场中。仙坛设在含万楼上，商彝周鼎、汉盘秦器，百花酿、百和香，中铺净沙。王胡子叩了九个头，焚过风符、云符、鹤符，点李玉林、金漱芳扶乩。乩先旋转画圈，二人各疑是对方作诡；随后写出一首降坛诗"珍珠为辇玉为轮，去请瑶台绛阙真"，自称杜兰香奉金母之命过此。众人推让不肯先问，子云便叫琴言跪下祷告终身，乩上判道："薄命红颜最可怜，杜鹃啼血自年年。再生不记前生事，父子相逢各惘然。

"末句众人皆解不出，再求注解，只得八字："杜郎且退，屈翁上前。"屈道翁上前叩拜，乩又判一诗，并明言：汝前生为江宁府推官，杜郎为汝娇女，十五夭亡，汝伤悼成疾而殁，乃七十七年前事，前因具在，后果将成。琴言红了两颊，道翁亦觉奇异。换琪官、春喜扶乩，来的是坡仙，一气写下长篇古风，把在座逐一嵌入：梅花一枝、瑶琴三尺是子玉与琴言；蕙质兰心而"分香浪费金条脱"是蕙芳，说得他脸上泛红，怕人提起潘其观的旧事；"名园公子"与珠辉宝气是子云与吉甫，宝珠看了心喜；"石崇王恺人争羡"而"豪华怒碎琴，明珠减价珊瑚贱"，众人都知是说华光宿与十珠、林珊枝；此外冲寒之鹤是剑潭，萧颖士之才是静宜，酒狂词客是竹君与卓然，求仙而为红尘所阻是前舟与庸庵，兰香玉树两行则遍及香畹、佩仙、小梅诸人；末段说到汨罗沉冤，"知君一生秉正直，风骨棱棱谢雕饰。娇女含愁化玉郎，石头城下伤春色"——道翁写到这里不禁泪下：他六十无儿又新丧偶，孤苦一身，与琴言相对，各有感伤之态。乩末书"丙寅三月初八日，请得眉山苏子瞻"，随即寂然。承荫堂上开席，子云乘势倡议：两仙皆判玉侬为道翁前生娇女，如今道翁无子、玉侬无父，何不成此仙缘，收为义子——"玉侬虽失足于前，未尝不可立身于后"。

子玉喜动颜色，说这是精金入冶、美玉成器；次贤、吉甫也力赞是数已前定。道翁本已看出琴言虽系优伶却无半点习气，便应允了；琴言想着屈老先生是天下第一等人品，得他教训不枉一世，只是面嫩答不出话，红着脸低头，末了向子云点了点头。于是铺下红毡，刘文泽、颜仲清拉他上前拜了八拜。道翁又说明：我孤苦一身，既认了你就与亲生一样，要教训你，还要随我同去，不知你心上如何？琴言道自己没有父母，岂有不追随的道理。子玉听了未免伤悲，然见他从此入了正路，究竟喜多悲少。道翁当席为他改名：从今须改去本来面目，不应常到外边，在寓里读书习字；出京日期已近，姓名都要改，就依我的姓，改名勤先，留一个琴字在内，号便是琴仙。众人都说改得好。席间王胡子自夸扶乩之能，讲起在扬州盐商家请下神来，把人家闺门里的事都写了出来，吓得主人磕头如捣蒜；王兰保则说杜兰香与玉侬同姓，所以关切得很，才成全了这件好事；袁宝珠说不知自己前生是什么人转世，陆素兰笑他曾自称前生是个尼姑，说得他脸一红，远远看着吉甫一笑。道翁答应明日便搬进园来与琴仙同住；次贤又提起园中匾联多是草创时所定，如今看去似觉草草，非道翁与诸公手笔不可；子云更要在含万楼下刻一篇怡园序，也要借重道翁——次贤笑说他还没有搬进来，你倒先索起房租来了。自此琴言便改姓屈，人称屈勤先，号琴仙，不复叫琴言了。`,en:`Installed at Yiyuan, Qinyan is talked back to life by Xu Ziyun and Xiao Cixian, with performers arriving unasked and the whole garden — peony terrace, peony beds, and the rest — open to him, so that half his gloom lifts. The day before, Ziyun had entertained Qu Daoweng with Mei Ziyu, Shi Nanxiang, Yan Zhongqing, Tian Chunhang, Liu Wenze, Wang Xun and all the performers, breaking up early because Nanxiang and Chunhang were to enter the examination hall at dawn. This evening Ziyu comes again with Yun'er under the new crescent, and at Cixian's is trapped by a Huzhou curio dealer, Wang the Beard — a man of fifty with a beard to his belly, who opens by asking his family and his degree, boasts of twenty years and fourteen provinces, and recalls selling Ziyu's father an incense burner and ten boxes of brushes at Ji'an last autumn; he is here to unload a *Complete Library of Ancient and Modern Books* on Ziyun at a reduced three thousand taels. Seeing Ziyu restless, Cixian sends him on to Qinyan, now moved to the crabapple court. Qinyan is standing in a moon-white jacket and red flowered pearl shoes against a Taihu stone, lost in thought, and calls him to see the low sun through the flowers. People say the crabapple has colour and no scent, he says — do you smell none? Ziyu's answer is exact: having taken the nation's beauty, why claim its fragrance too; what you smell is the lilac beyond. The crabapple keeps a scent of its own hidden in the flesh and colour of the flower and will not give it out lightly; one must look steadily at it a long while, and then the coolness rises of itself into the nostrils — and not everyone can smell it. Qinyan draws a branch down, breathes it, and nods; and Ziyu calls it the fragrance of the heart. Indoors, Qinyan opens his mind. 

Nothing was worse than his master's house: when his play was called he felt he was going to the execution ground, and on stage he made himself not himself, treating his body as the character's, to be stared at and laughed at as though it were no concern of his — and coming off, with his own face back, he was ashamed. To sit beside a stranger at a banquet was thorns in the back; others managed it easily and he wished to learn, and neither heart nor mouth would obey him. He had expected torture in the Hua mansion and found it tolerable, except that nobody could see inside him: however well he was used, he was still an actor kept to amuse. Of Duxiang's kindness nothing need be said. But his body is at ease now and his heart is not. In the fire-pit he only wished to die and never imagined getting out; out of it, his heart is more disordered than before. He will not act again and will not be anyone's servant again — and what then? Everyone is so good to him that his own sadness looks like ingratitude. 

This, he says, is a trouble belonging to the road of living, not to the road of dying; with no place in the world to belong to, how is the heart to open? Outside the capital, says Ziyu, where nobody knows his origin, he would be like anyone else — if only there were someone to take him. Do you actually wish me gone, Qinyan asks; and Ziyu answers that he wishes to be with him for life through hardship and joy, but is useless, and lives in fear that his father will return or be posted away and take him along, and that he must not spoil Qinyan's whole life for his own sake. Not unless you become an official, says Qinyan. And Ziyu: he does not want the special examination and the Hanlin — only some small provincial post that would let him take Qinyan away with him, and he would be content. Ziyun and Cixian come in at the first watch. Wang the Beard's only real fault, says Ziyun, is greed; he already owns a *Complete Library*, which is a deaf man's ears, mere furniture — but he will keep the man two days to see whether his planchette is fraud or not. Next morning Ziyu comes in full dress, an altar being an altar. Qu Daoweng and Jin Jifu arrive first, then Yan Zhongqing, Liu Wenze, Wang Xun; the performers are Yuan Baozhu, Su Huifang, Lu Sulan, Li Yulin, Jin Shufang, Wang Lanbao, Wang Guibao, Lin Chunxi, Yuan Qiguan and Qinyan — nineteen in all with the hosts, Nanxiang and Chunhang still in the examination hall. 

The altar is set in Hanwan Tower among Shang and Zhou bronzes, a Han tripod and a Qin dish, with flower wine, incense, and a tray of clean sand; Wang kowtows nine times, burns talismans of wind, cloud and crane, and appoints Li Yulin and Jin Shufang to hold the stylus. It circles first, each of them suspecting the other of cheating, then writes a descent poem about a chariot of pearl and wheels of jade going to summon the true one of the Jasper Terrace, signed by the immortal Du Lanxiang, passing this way on the Queen Mother's errand. Nobody will ask the first question, so Ziyun makes Qinyan kneel and pray about his life, and the answer comes: a thin-fated beauty is the most pitiable of all; the cuckoo weeps blood year after year; reborn, one remembers nothing of the life before; and father and child, meeting, are strangers to each other. The last line defeats them all, and a gloss is asked; the gloss is eight characters — Du may withdraw, and old Qu come forward. Qu Daoweng bows before the altar, and the stylus gives him a poem and then a plain statement: in a former life he was a judicial officer of Jiangning prefecture, and Du was his beloved daughter, who died at fifteen, whereupon he grieved himself into sickness and died — seventy-seven years ago; the cause is complete and the fruit about to form. Qinyan's cheeks flame; the old man is shaken. Qiguan and Chunxi take the stylus next, and the Immortal of the Slope arrives with a long poem that sets every person present into it in turn: a spray of plum and a three-foot qin for Ziyu and Qinyan; an orchid heart and a gold armlet wasted on scattered fragrance for Huifang, who reddens for fear the old business of Pan Qiguan should be raised; the lord of the famous garden and a radiance of pearl and gold for Ziyun and Jifu, with Baozhu's name woven in to his own delight; the envied splendour of Shi Chong and Wang Kai, and a qin smashed in anger, the pearl cheapened and the coral made worthless — which everyone reads at once as Hua Guangsu, his ten pearl-named maids, and Lin Shanzhi; a crane out of the cold cloud for Jiantan; the talent of Xiao Yingshi for Jingyi; drunken poets who would empty Lake Dongting for Zhujun and Zhuoran; seekers of immortality barred by ten zhang of red dust for Qianzhou and Yong'an; and two rows of orchid fragrance and jade trees taking in Xiangwan, Peixian, Xiaomei and the rest. The last passage turns to the wronged soul of the Miluo: knowing that his life has been upright and his bone-structure angular, disdaining ornament — and that his sorrowing daughter has become a youth of jade, mourning the spring beneath Stone City. 

Writing it out, Daoweng weeps: sixty, sonless, newly widowed and alone, he looks at Qinyan and Qinyan at him, and neither can speak. The stylus dates the poem and names Su Shi of Meishan, and is still. At the banquet in Chengyin Hall Ziyun seizes the moment: both immortals have declared Yunong to have been Daoweng's daughter; Daoweng has no son and Yunong no father; let the old bond be completed by adoption — for a false step in the past does not forbid an upright life afterwards. Ziyu is delighted, calling it pure gold set in the crucible and fine jade cut into a vessel; Cixian and Jifu urge that the thing was fated, or why should the immortal have singled Daoweng out. Daoweng, who has long noticed that Qinyan has not a trace of the professional manner about him, consents; and Qinyan, thinking that to be taught by such a man would redeem a life, is too shy to answer and can only redden, hang his head, and at last nod to Ziyun. Red felt is spread, Wenze and Zhongqing bring him forward, and he makes his eight bows. Then Daoweng states his terms: he is a solitary man, and having adopted him will treat him as his own, will discipline him, and will take him away — is he willing? Having no parents, Qinyan answers, how should he not follow? Ziyu is saddened, and yet on balance glad, since this sets his friend on the right road. At table the old man renames him: from today he must change his outward self, keep away from public places, read and write at his lodging; the day of leaving the capital is near, and his name must go — let him take his adoptive father's surname and be called Qinxian, keeping the character for the qin. Everyone approves. Wang the Beard, in high spirits, tells how at a Yangzhou salt merchant's the summoned spirit began writing out the secrets of the inner chambers until the master kowtowed like a pestle to beg him to stop; and Wang Lanbao observes that Du Lanxiang shares Yunong's surname and was therefore especially concerned, while Baozhu wonders aloud what he was in a former life and Lu Sulan reminds him that he once claimed to have been a nun — at which he colours and glances across at Jifu. Daoweng agrees to move into the garden the next day and share rooms with Qinxian; Cixian raises the question of the plaques and couplets, all fixed when the garden was first built and now looking hasty, which want Daoweng's brush and the others'; and Ziyun asks him besides for a preface to Yiyuan to be carved below Hanwan Tower — at which Cixian laughs that he is collecting rent before the tenant has moved in. From this day Qinyan bears the surname Qu, and is called Qinxian.`},46:{zh:`屈道翁搬进怡园，与琴仙同住海棠春圃，晨夕教他温理旧书、讲究诗词文艺、习学楷字。琴仙天姿颖悟，过目成诵，又锐志攻书，把从前的忧闷都撇开了；半月之间文理大进，字也写好，作对做诗都通顺，父子间亲爱得如同亲生。诸名旦到园来反不好与他盘桓，只在门口略一探望；他足不出户，倒是道翁体贴，叫他出去散散心胸，他答应了仍旧不动，不是写字就是看书，把个潇洒惯的老人家反拘住了，时时要为他释疑问难。一日道翁写成徐子云所托的《怡园序》，自送与子云、次贤斟酌，次贤赞"就使徐、庾复生也不能涂改一字"；论到刻法，道翁说论长久自然是石刻，又荐金吉甫相熟的季十矮子，刻工尚好，只是价大些。子云便裁了八张大宣纸，照含万楼屏风大小送去，请他亲笔自书。

此时田春航、史南湘场事已毕，子云定二十八日辰初集齐，遍游全园：不设筵宴，恐误游兴，只在几处备些小酌茶点，近水处坐船，离水远处步行，务要一日游尽。王胡子住了两日回寓，把那部《图书集成》装了五大车送进园来，子云只得收下，摆在含万楼上，竟填满了五间大楼。是日到的是梅子玉、春航、南湘、颜仲清、刘文泽、王恂，金吉甫因感冒未来。一路走一路改名题联，成了本回的骨干：含万楼原取《易经》"含万物而化光"之义，道翁说此语本言乾道之大，于楼名不甚相宜，况楼上现供赐书，不如改称赐书楼，并亲题长联，上句数玉笈金编、正学《十三经》旁通《廿二子》，下句写笔歌墨舞、"纵横一万里，上下五千年"；承荫堂名不动，添了两副，一副长联，一副集六朝文的八言，都由琴仙端楷写出。戏台前那块"太音之和"留着，檐前一块要换，七字联改作八字，由子玉题了"画堂秋拍"的匾与一副集句，道翁赞道"我说庚香世兄定是不凡的"。

此后牡丹香国改宝香堂、后进题护香廊，春航题联；兰径改风露清吟馆，仲清题"二分水蘸三分竹，一面山栽两面花"；菊畦改黄香东圃，道翁自题"春秋多佳日，风雨近重阳"；松龛改松鹤丹房，南湘集六朝文相应；梅崦因"崦"字不宜入屋，由子玉改作古香林屋，并题一联，琴仙代写，道翁大赞"仙骨珊珊，非吃烟火食所能道"，子玉与琴仙相视皆喜；红茶仙馆既供花神，索性改题为花神仙府，仍由春航题联；梨院改卧云香院，王恂题之。到海棠春圃，道翁当众考琴仙起名，他红着脸说出"春风沉醉轩"，子云拍手，众人齐赞"沉醉"二字入神；他又说昨日偶得上联"一曲惜余芳，娇比玉颜时醒醉"，却对不出下联，次贤说非庚香续成不可，子玉欣然写下"千金买良夜，好酬春色正温柔"。吟秋榭三层，文泽题第一层，南湘题第二层——上有第三层下有第一层，最难着笔——仲清题第三层"君如趁月来游，云移一鹤；我欲乘风归去，桥卧长虹"，南湘先自痛赞，说这断不像第一、二层，实在是最高处。此外停云叙雨轩改练秋阁，次贤题联嵌《山香曲》《水调歌》；桂岭改丛桂山房，子云自集一联；缥渺亭中原有他学生华光宿的旧联，尚可用，子云又添一副；桃花坞改寻源仙墅，仍烦子玉；东风昨夜楼名佳不改，那副长联本是次贤所作，道翁朗吟一遍，也叫琴仙写出；小赤城道翁自题；积翠轩改清凉诗境，道翁笑说庚香既题了温柔乡，也不可不题清凉境；末了稻庄也另题新名，又因文泽、王恂各只得一副，未免不公，再烦二人各撰一副。游到日暮，道翁问园有几里，子云答周围五里，山占一分、水占两分、树木一分、空隙一分，房屋二十几处，除门房马棚厨房外共四百零八间；道翁说够了，太大反觉空旷，"你这个园好在不散，处处精神团聚，一处有一处的结构"。

宝香堂上摆两席，王兰保、金漱芳、袁琪官、林春喜也来了。子云说今日二十四副对子终以道翁的赐书楼、承荫堂最为冠冕；道翁却不肯居功，逐一品题：高超的是剑潭，沉着的是竹君，细腻风光的是庚香，风华绮丽的是湘帆，秀润工稳的是庸庵、前舟，潇洒跌宕的是静宜，就是度香那副集句也落落大方，各自立一帜，无从评定甲乙；又指出这二十四副好在虚字少而实字多，才见得力量，若教外边那些名宿做起来，不知要添多少虚字才凑得拢。末了众人索观那篇序，书童取来，用两个针插在壁上，携灯照读：文中自署书刻石之体说起，历叙帝京形胜、勋阀开园、鸠工三十六月而成风廊水榭四百八间，写园中山水花木、文酒之会与歌台舞榭之盛；继而设为诘难，说玩物丧志、节欲保身，随即以汾阳之业、谢傅之贤、陶士行之童仆千人、信陵之醇酒妇人一一驳回，谓超脱者为才子之情、豪纵者尤少年之气；末段忽转入自身：衰年作吏、憔悴风尘，壮岁束装、羁栖宾客，览洞庭彭蠡之胜、瞻南衡东岱之崇，而今"驽骀犹系盐车，归田何日"，只叹"蓬心将死，经零雨而重苏；桐尾已焦，遇赏音而犹响"，结以"结交以道，文字为缘"。众名士看了，真是游、夏不能赞一词，惟有拜倒而已。道翁自谦一番，只可惜吉甫今日未到，说等他大好了请来看过，再斟酌付刻。直饮到三更方散。`,en:`Qu Daoweng moves into Yiyuan and shares the crabapple court with Qinxian, teaching him morning and evening — old reading gone over again, poetry and prose expounded, regular script practised. The boy is quick beyond expectation, memorizes at a glance, works with a will, and puts his old misery aside; within a fortnight his sense of literature has advanced greatly, his hand is good, and he can turn a couplet or a poem. Father and son grow as close as if born so. The performers who come to the garden no longer sit with him but only glance in at the door; he never goes out, and it is the old man, out of consideration, who urges him to walk about for his spirits — an invitation he accepts and ignores, being always at his book or his brush, so that the once free-and-easy Daoweng finds himself kept indoors resolving difficulties. One day Daoweng finishes the *Preface to the Garden of Ease* that Xu Ziyun asked for, and carries it to Ziyun and Cixian himself; even Xu Ling and Yu Xin come back to life, says Cixian, could not alter a character. Stone, says Daoweng, is the medium for permanence, and he recommends Ji Shi the Dwarf, an acquaintance of Jin Jifu's, a good carver though dear. Ziyun has eight sheets of large xuan paper cut to the size of the Hanwan Tower screens and sent over for the old man to write out himself. 

Tian Chunhang and Shi Nanxiang being now out of the examination hall, Ziyun fixes the twenty-eighth for a tour of the whole garden in one day, with no banquet to waste the light, refreshments laid at several points, boats where there is water and walking where there is not. Wang the Beard, going home after two days, sends in his *Complete Library of Ancient and Modern Books* in five great carts, which Ziyun must accept and which fills all five bays of the tower. The party is Mei Ziyu, Chunhang, Nanxiang, Yan Zhongqing, Liu Wenze and Wang Xun, with Jin Jifu kept away by a cold. The renaming and the couplets make the spine of the day. Hanwan Tower took its name from the phrase in the *Book of Changes* about containing all things and transforming into light — which, Daoweng observes, properly describes the greatness of the creative principle and suits a tower ill, especially one now housing books of imperial bestowal; so it becomes the Tower of Bestowed Books, with his own long couplet running from jade satchels and gold volumes, the *Thirteen Classics* studied and the *Twenty-Two Masters* known by the way, to brushes that sing and ink that dances across ten thousand li and five thousand years. Chengyin Hall keeps its name and gains two couplets, one long and one an eight-word cento of Six Dynasties phrases, both written out in Qinxian's neat regular hand. On the stage the old plaque is kept, the eaves plaque replaced, and the seven-character couplet made an eight; Ziyu supplies both plaque and cento, and Daoweng says he always knew that young Gengxiang was out of the common. 

So it goes on: the peony enclosure becomes Baoxiang Hall with its rear gallery renamed, and Chunhang inscribes it; the orchid path becomes the Studio of Wind, Dew and Pure Chanting, with Zhongqing's couplet of two parts water dipping into three of bamboo and one hillside planted against two of flowers; the chrysanthemum plot becomes the East Garden of Yellow Fragrance, with a small couplet by Daoweng himself; the pine niche becomes the Cinnabar Chamber of Pine and Crane, matched by Nanxiang from the Six Dynasties. The Plum Slope, whose second character will not do indoors, is renamed by Ziyu the Ancient Fragrance Grove House, and his couplet for it — copied out by Qinxian — draws the old man's highest praise, that its bones are an immortal's and no eater of cooked food could have written it, at which Ziyu and Qinxian look at each other in delight. The Red Camellia Hall, housing the flower-gods, is renamed an immortal bower for them, again inscribed by Chunhang; the pear court becomes the Cloud-Reclining Court of Fragrance, inscribed by Wang Xun. At the crabapple court Daoweng examines his son before the company, and Qinxian, blushing, offers the Pavilion Drunk on Spring Wind — at which Ziyun claps and everyone praises the word "drunk" as inspired; and having an opening line but no answering one, he is completed by Ziyu, who writes that a thousand pieces of gold buy one good night to repay the tenderness of spring. The three storeys of the Yinqiu Pavilion take one couplet each: Wenze the first, Nanxiang the second — hardest of all, with a storey above and below — and Zhongqing the third, whose lines about a friend coming by moonlight while a cloud shifts a crane, and himself riding the wind home over a bridge lying like a rainbow, Nanxiang praises as belonging to no storey but the topmost. The rain-and-cloud pavilion becomes the Tower of Distilled Autumn, with Cixian's couplet setting *Mountain Fragrance* against the *Water-Tune Song*; the cassia ridge becomes the Cassia Grove Lodge, with a cento by Ziyun himself; at the Piaomiao Pavilion the existing couplet by Daoweng's old pupil Hua Guangsu is allowed to stand, and Ziyun adds another; the peach bank becomes the Immortal Lodge at the Source, inscribed once more by Ziyu; the East Wind Last Night Tower keeps its name, and its long couplet, Cixian's work, is chanted by Daoweng and copied by Qinxian; the little Red City takes a couplet by Daoweng; the Jicui Studio becomes the Cool Realm of Poetry, since, as Daoweng laughs, having written of the land of tenderness Gengxiang must also write of the region of cool; and the rice farm too is given a new name, with Wenze and Wang Xun each asked for a second couplet, having so far had only one. Toward evening Daoweng asks the garden's size: five li round, says Ziyun, of which hills take a fifth, water two fifths, trees a fifth and open ground a fifth, with some twenty groups of buildings and, excluding gatehouses, stables and kitchens, four hundred and eight rooms. 

Enough, says the old man; more would only be empty; the merit of this garden is that it does not scatter — every place gathers its own spirit and has its own structure. Two tables are laid at Baoxiang Hall, where Wang Lanbao, Jin Shufang, Yuan Qiguan and Lin Chunxi join them. Ziyun holds that of the day's twenty-four couplets Daoweng's two are the most stately; but Daoweng will not take the palm and appraises them all instead — Jiantan lofty, Zhujun weighty, Gengxiang delicate, Xiangfan sumptuous, Yong'an and Qianzhou polished and exact, Jingyi free and unrestrained, and even Duxiang's cento generous and easy — each raising his own banner, and no ranking possible; and he adds the technical point that these twenty-four are strong because they use few empty words and many full ones, whereas the established names outside would need any number of particles to hold their lines together. At last the preface is called for; the boy fetches it, and it is pinned to the wall with two needles and read by lamplight. It begins with the history of inscribed writing and carved stone, moves through the splendour of the imperial city and the founding of the garden — thirty-six months of building, four hundred and eight rooms of galleries and water pavilions — and then through its hills, flowers, literary gatherings and singing halls; it raises the expected objection that such pleasures destroy the will, and answers it with Guo Ziyi's singers, Xie An's music, Tao Kan's thousand servants and Lord Xinling's wine and women, holding that detachment is the feeling of the gifted and abandon the temper of the young; and it turns at the end upon the writer himself — an old man serving as a petty official, worn thin by the dusty road, who set out in his prime and has lodged ever since as another man's guest, who has seen Dongting and Poyang and the sacred peaks of south and east, and who is still a broken hack harnessed to the salt cart with no notion when he may go home: a dying reed revived by a passing rain, a scorched paulownia tail that sounds again when someone who knows music is by. Before this the company can add nothing and simply bow. Daoweng disclaims it all, regrets only that Jifu was not there, and proposes that the piece be shown to him before it is cut. They drink until the third watch.`},47:{zh:`怡园分题之后，众名士读了屈道翁那篇序，无不五体投地，连最不轻易服人的史南湘、田春航也真心拜倒。次日徐子云又请金吉甫入园遍观联额，吉甫也甚佩服；道翁遂以真行字写了十六扇屏风，由吉甫所荐的季十矮子在园中开刻。四月十一日报到：南湘中第二十一名，春航中第三十四名进士，苏蕙芳与王兰保喜得说不出话来。南湘因去年乃翁赴任时卖了住宅，借住刘文泽处；蕙芳出入虽无人拦阻，究竟常要到门房走动，不甚方便，便劝他二人合租一所房子。子云说不必另觅，丛桂山房就有三十几间，尽够住；又说道翁已选了南昌府通判，不日赴任，琴仙是要同去的，索性搬进园来热闹些。蕙芳只说自己还要等一两个月。琴仙听说不日出京，回念旧日相好，出京之后不知何年再叙，留蕙芳坐了半天；蕙芳说他如今在父亲跟前拘拘束束，须到外边才畅快。春航因要接老母与内人北上，终须自寻房子，谢了子云的美意；蕙芳便在鸣珂坊寻得一所，与梅子玉相近，又替他备车马、收管家——赶车的正是周小三，进门后又荐了小舅子许老三，改名许贵，做了跟班，一时局面大变。

另一头，奚十一病后调养月余，性子减了些，身子瘦了些，烟瘾却大了些；及至身体稍健，仍旧不安本分。无奈那物只剩半截，不动时还罢，一动便有一条筋胀得生疼，勉强应酬也不能，白菊花急得暗中流泪，他自己也觉抱愧：才三十岁便成这样，在家乡尚可想个法子，京里未必有这等能手。一日他备了五十两银子，命巴英官拿着到宏济寺谢唐和尚，见寺旁新开了一个饭庄；进门先把得月肩上狠狠拧了一把，骂他害得自己病了一个多月、锯掉半截，得月冷笑回他一句"你不知那里沾了来，倒来冤我"，说到一半便住了口。唐和尚醉醺醺回来，再三推辞才收下银子，随即夸他的新买卖：那所房子原是寺里的，前年租出去吊死过两个人，闹鬼闲了一年，上月春阳馆的东家看中了要开庄子，他出四千吊搭伙，才开三天；一个厨子的烧鸭压倒通京城，还有一样生炒翅子是别处做不来的；更妙的是后头一重门通着魏聘才的住房，酒后要吹两口可以过来，另有两间密室，要相公媳妇都叫得，从这边进去没有人知道。这些话若在从前，奚十一不知怎样高兴，如今却听不得——一动心那处便疼。他索性把苦楚说了，唐和尚大笑，说不如用烂药把那条筋烂掉；奚十一嫌险，只说广东有个接树法子，用狗肾接上，不知京里可有会的。

唐和尚拍手道巧极：方才隔壁吃饭的杨八与另一位师爷，有个苏州亲戚叫阳善修，专门行这一道，配眼珠、配鼻子、配牙皆是假的，独有接那样东西是真的——先上麻药，一劈四瓣，把狗肾嵌进，敷药缠线，一月平复；那狗肾还须趁狗连时一刀砍死两个，从母狗身上取出方才有用，据说人是不疼不痒的。奚十一立逼着去请。那阳善修生得黄肿脸、短小身材，穿件油晃晃的破袖旧绸袄，奚十一见了先有几分瞧不起，只叫和尚代言。那人说得却在行：接法先看本源，再看行货；老年筋力衰的接也白接，本源好的纵烂掉半截，只要有个根子便接得起来。看过之后断定须先把那条筋治直。讲定包修包好二百两，不妥当分文不取，唐和尚作中立了字据，次日先付药银五十两。当晚照方用丁香油调药敷上，以绫带渐渐收紧，三刻工夫那筋果然直了，白菊花摸着比先前长了好些，暗自欢喜。次日行术：炭炉、开水、药吊、旧绸都备齐，先煎一剂参、牛黄、珠粉等药服下，再敷麻药，点一枝五寸长的香候药性走到。其间那人问要不要加长，奚十一说前日不过两寸半、如今三寸，那人许他四寸"也就够了"，白菊花在板壁缝里听见，失口叫了一声"极短也要五寸"，慌得跑了进去，唐和尚忍不住笑，春兰红了脸，巴英官只在鼻子里哼了一声。行术时以青绉纱蒙了奚十一的眼，四刀嵌进四条药线，又将一片鲜肉贴上，通身敷药、缠线、砑光。

白菊花隔着板壁抖得牙齿相碰，春兰、英官吐出舌头缩不进去，唐和尚不忍看，只躺着吹烟。奚十一果然不觉疼，只两腿麻木难动。那人嘱咐每日一剂煎药，午正来上敷药，七日包好；好后两三月内不可动用，公鸡、鲤鱼、羊肉百日内忌口，大好之后吃狗肉倒有益——奚十一笑说狗肉广东人叫地羊，他不知吃过多少了。十余日果然长好，只是刀痕一条条留在上面，一块青一块红，像脸上带着记印，撒溺时仍有些疼。潘其观那边的报应更琐碎难堪。他自受了周小三那一场荼毒，唬出一场大病，二十几天才起得来；偏偏许老二当日塞进去的东西久而生变，痒得难忍，老婆面前又讲不出口。他先打起打更的焦傻子的主意，赏了一碗酒三个饽饽，把病情说了又千叮万嘱不许告人；焦傻子只管点头，其实一句不懂，嚼完饽饽转身要走，被他拉到炕前伏下相求，那傻子看了看，抓过一张包茶叶的纸递过来，说"三爷你自己擦罢，我只会打更，不会擦屁股"，一径去了。他再找旧相识桂枝，请酒许衣，桂枝只当他念旧，及至临事一惊而退，两日皆不济；事后账房里二十九两七钱的银包与墙上一个表都不见了，明知是桂枝拿的，却因是请来治病的，只得忍气吞声，暗恨周小三与许三姐害他。

又过几日，他想起卓天香也是老主顾，叫来商量；说到"红毛风"的旧闻，天香说东光县就有两个四十几岁的人得了这毛病，天天在戏场里找人，至死方休。潘其观急问可有治法，天香想起同行中有人能用指头挖出来，说长毛须经过人精才长。那人一听便笑道：潘三叫你来请我么？这事我早知道——正月里他拿这法子收拾了许老三，许三姐才设计哄他，许老二用他的法子回敬他，都是许老二亲口告诉我的；许老三吃了多少荞麦面又吃泻药也泻不出来，还是我传的法子，听说是三姐用银耳挖替他挖干净的，才不至成了毛病。潘三这个人真不是个东西，极该得这个报应。经天香再三央求，那人开口三百吊："他这个毛病还花三百吊买来的，何况要治好。"又指定要一枝七寸长、笔管粗的金耳挖，二两金子，说银的万使不得。潘其观疑他图这二两金子，便用低银打了镀金充数；治后果然十余日不痒，两次付清三百吊，天香也趁势借去几十吊。谁知那人本是个剃头的，一朝发迹，想把金耳挖打两个戒指，银匠说是镀金的，试金石上一刮果然是银，便恨他欺人，托天香去说：如今这耳挖已成凭据，他要逢人便讲是潘三爷请他挖屁股的，看他一辈子怎样做人。潘其观无奈，又赔了三百吊；此后那人与天香串通，每逢缓急便来找他，稍不应酬便恶言恶语提起旧事，潘其观像写了卖身文契一般，零零星星应酬了好几年，直到那人死了方罢。`,en:`After the day of couplets, the scholars read Qu Daoweng's preface and are prostrated by it; even Shi Nanxiang and Tian Chunhang, who defer to nobody, bow in earnest. Next day Xu Ziyun brings Jin Jifu through the garden to see the inscriptions, and he admires them too; so Daoweng writes out sixteen screen panels in running-regular script, and Ji Shi the Dwarf, whom Jifu recommended, begins cutting them in the garden. On the eleventh of the fourth month the news comes: Nanxiang twenty-first and Chunhang thirty-fourth among the new jinshi, and Su Huifang and Wang Lanbao are speechless with joy. Nanxiang, whose father sold their house last year, is lodging with Liu Wenze; and Huifang, though nobody stops him coming and going, finds the constant business at another man's gatehouse awkward, and urges the two to take a house together. There is no need to look, says Ziyun: the Cassia Grove Lodge has thirty-odd rooms and will hold them both — and since Daoweng has been appointed assistant prefect of Nanchang and will leave shortly, taking Qinxian with him, they may as well move in and make the place lively. Huifang answers that he must wait a month or two himself. Hearing of the departure, Qinxian, thinking of old friends and how many years may pass before another meeting, keeps Huifang half the day; and Huifang observes that under his father's eye he is much constrained and that they must meet outside. Chunhang, who means to bring his mother and wife north, must have a house of his own and declines the offer; Huifang finds him one in Mingke Fang, near Mei Ziyu, and fits him out with carriage, horses and servants — the driver being Zhou Xiaosan, who then brings in his brother-in-law Xu Laosan, renamed Xu Gui, as an attendant, so that the whole establishment changes character. Elsewhere Xi Shiyi, a month and more into his convalescence, is a little quieter, a good deal thinner, and much deeper in opium; and as soon as his strength returns he is as unruly as before. 

But half of him is gone, and though at rest it does not trouble him, at the least excitement a tendon inside cramps and burns, so that he can do nothing at all. White Chrysanthemum weeps in secret, and he is ashamed: thirty years old and reduced to this, and at home in the south a remedy could have been found, whereas in the capital there is probably no such hand. One day he sends Ba Yingguan with fifty taels to thank Tang Heshang at the Hongji Temple, and finds a new eating-house opened beside the gate. Inside he pinches Deyue hard on the shoulder, blaming him for a month's illness and half a man's loss, and Deyue answers coldly that he caught it somewhere else and had better look to his own — then stops in the middle of the sentence. Tang comes back tipsy, refuses the silver twice and takes it, and boasts of his new venture: the building belongs to the temple, and after two people hanged themselves in it two years ago it stood haunted and empty a year, until the proprietor of the Chunyang Restaurant saw it last month and proposed a partnership; Tang put in four thousand strings, and it has been open three days, with a cook whose roast duck beats anything in the capital and a stir-fried shark's fin nobody else can make. Better still, a door at the back opens into the front room of Wei Pincai's lodging, so a man who wants a pipe after his wine may pass through; and there are two private rooms where actors or women may be called with nobody the wiser. 

Once this would have delighted Xi; now he dares not let his mind stir, since stirring hurts. He states his trouble outright, and Tang laughs and offers to burn the tendon away with caustic — which Xi refuses, in case everything should rot with it, mentioning instead the Cantonese grafting method with a dog's kidney. Tang claps his hands: only just now, next door, Yang Eight and another gentleman were telling him of a Suzhou relative, one Yang Shanxiu, who practises exactly this line — his eyes, noses and teeth are false, but the grafting is real: an anaesthetic first, the part split in four, the kidney set in, dressed and bound with medicated threads and sound again within a month; and the kidney must be taken from a bitch by killing a mating pair at one stroke, or it is useless. It is said to be quite painless. Xi has him fetched on the spot. Yang Shanxiu proves to be a short, sallow, puffy-faced man of thirty in a greasy old silk jacket with ragged sleeves, and Xi, unimpressed, lets the monk speak for him; but the man's account is professional enough — grafting depends first on the constitution and then on the state of the part; where the sinews have failed with age it is useless, but a sound constitution with even half gone may be restored so long as a root remains. Having examined him, he rules that the cramped tendon must first be straightened. Two hundred taels, cure guaranteed, nothing at all if it fails; the monk stands surety, a deed is signed, and fifty taels for medicine are to be paid next day. That night the powder is mixed with clove oil, applied, and bound with a silk band drawn steadily tighter, and in three quarters of an hour the tendon is straight; White Chrysanthemum, feeling it, finds it noticeably longer and is privately delighted. The operation follows: charcoal stove, boiling water, decoction pot and old silk; a draught of ginseng, bezoar and pearl; the anaesthetic ointment; and a five-inch stick of incense burned to time its working. Asked whether he wants length added, Xi says he had two and a half inches and now three, and is promised four, "which is enough" — at which White Chrysanthemum, listening at a crack in the partition, blurts out that five is the very least and has to flee inside, while Tang chokes with laughter, Chunlan reddens, and Ba Yingguan snorts. 

Xi's eyes are bound with green crepe; four incisions take four medicated threads; a piece of fresh flesh is grafted on; the whole is dressed, bound and smoothed with an ivory blade. Behind the partition White Chrysanthemum shakes until her teeth chatter, Chunlan and Yingguan stand with their tongues out, and Tang, unable to watch, lies down to his pipe. Xi feels nothing but numbness and cannot move his legs. The instructions are a decoction daily, dressings at noon for seven days, no use of it for two or three months, no cock, carp or mutton for a hundred days — and dog meat afterwards is beneficial, at which Xi laughs that Cantonese call it earth mutton and he has eaten more of it than he can count. In a dozen days it has healed, though the knife-marks remain in stripes of blue and red like brands on a face, and it still hurts to make water. Pan Qiguan's retribution is smaller and harder to bear. Frightened into a serious illness by Zhou Xiaosan's ambush, he was twenty days getting up; and what Xu Laoer packed into him has since begun to itch past enduring, with no possibility of telling his wife. He tries the night-watchman first, a simpleton named Jiao, buying him a bowl of wine and three black buns and explaining his complaint with many injunctions to secrecy; Jiao nods through it all, understands not a word, finishes the buns and turns to go, and when Pan pulls him to the kang and bends over in mute appeal, looks about, finds a sheet of tea wrapping paper, hands it to him and says: Third Master, wipe it yourself — I only know how to beat the watch, not how to wipe backsides. Next he tries Guizhi, an old acquaintance from the troupes, over thirty now and poor, with wine and the promise of clothes; Guizhi, supposing an old connection is to be revived, takes fright at what he finds and does no better on a second day; afterwards a packet of twenty-nine taels seven mace and a clock off the wall are found missing, and Pan, having invited the man to treat him, must swallow it and curse Zhou Xiaosan and Third Sister in silence. 

Days later he sends for Zhuo Tianxiang, an old customer. The talk turns to the affliction the trade calls the red-hair wind, and Tianxiang says that in his own county two men of forty-odd have it and haunt the theatres looking for anyone who will oblige them, and that it ends only with death. Is there no cure? Tianxiang remembers a man of his own trade who once drew the stuff out with a finger, and explains that the hair only takes root after human seed, and without that the matter simply cannot come away. That man, hearing the errand, laughs: Did Pan San send you? I have known all this a long while. In the first month he played this very trick on Xu Laosan; then Third Sister laid her plan and Xu Laoer served him with his own method, as Xu Laoer told me himself; and Xu Laosan ate buckwheat and purgatives and could pass nothing until I taught him the way, and I hear Third Sister cleaned him out with a silver ear-pick, so that it never became chronic. Pan San is no sort of man, and richly deserves this — let him have it. Only after much pleading does he name his price: three hundred strings, since the man paid three hundred to get the disease and should pay double to be rid of it. He also demands a gold ear-pick seven inches long and as thick as a brush stem, two taels of gold, and insists that silver will not do. Suspecting a swindle, Pan has one made of poor silver gilded over; the treatment works, he pays a hundred strings and, after ten days without itching, the remaining two hundred, while Tianxiang borrows a few tens of strings for his silence. But the practitioner, a barber suddenly rich, decides to have two rings made from the gold, learns from the silversmith and then from the touchstone that it is gilt silver, and, furious at being cheated, sends Tianxiang to say that the ear-pick is now his evidence and that he will tell the world that Third Master Pan hired him to scratch his backside — after which how is he to live? Pan pays another three hundred; and thereafter the two of them squeeze him whenever they are short, raising the subject aloud if he demurs, so that for years he pays out in dribs and drabs like a man who has signed himself into bondage, until the fellow dies.`},48:{zh:`屈道翁选了南昌通判，领凭即行，众名士名旦轮流饯别。殿试揭晓：田春航大魁天下，授修撰；史南湘二甲第四，点了庶常，比起去年春间的潦倒真是天壤。书中特笔点出春航与"苏"字的缘分：去年亏得苏蕙芳成全，本是风月因缘倒成了道义肝胆，逼得他向正路上走，才有今日的功名。今科房官荐了他的卷子，大总裁本已驳落，全仗一位姓苏的总裁——现任兵部大堂、翰林出身而后袭侯爵，正是华光宿的岳父——看了他的文字大加赞赏，说"此人才调不凡，虽过于靡丽，倒是个词臣格调，可以黼黻太平"，又见他《五经》通明、策对平允，才中了三十四名；填榜拆对墨卷时，苏侯见他一笔好楷，便料定殿试必在前列，果然中了状元。春航谒座师时苏侯绝口不提，还是房师告诉他的，因此感激独深。这苏侯年近五旬，位列通侯而艰于嗣子，正夫人只生两位小姐：长的便是华夫人，次女十九岁尚未字人，正要选个才貌双全的女婿；初见春航十分中意，一问已有妻室，暗暗叹息。春航新宅的车马服饰器用，尽是蕙芳一人之力，数年积蓄运用一空；他已辞了班子，天天过来照应，春航留他住下他又不肯，而大小事务无不由他调度，连不曾办过的也先访问明白，务要一无翳障，不教春航费一点心。忽得家信：夫人已于二月内暴病而亡，太夫人伤心万状，家中只一老仆一仆妇；恰值其母舅张桐孙告病回籍，情况不支，将挈眷进京，便偕田太夫人于三月十五起身。春航一悲一喜：喜的是慈母将来，悲的是朱弦已断；想起当年钗荆裙布、唱随之乐，不觉大恸，还是蕙芳劝住，说老太太不日就到，你自己苦坏了教她见了岂不更伤感。

次日便收拾上房，又吩咐下人从此称蕙芳为"苏大爷"。十余日后田太夫人到了，春航接到良乡，母子悲欢各半；老太太路上已知他中了状元，因此更念亡媳。春航在母亲面前极说蕙芳的好处：原是落难才唱戏的，如今已出了班，父亲在云南做过州同，是书香之后，一切都赖他，太夫人遂待他甚好。史南湘馆选后搬进怡园的清凉诗境，脾气与春航两样：把同年同馆一概不放在眼里，也不出去应酬，天天与道翁、萧次贤、徐子云诗酒陶情，闲时又有袁宝珠、陆素兰、王兰保、金漱芳一班人度曲当歌。求文求诗者接踵而来，他嫌烦，常请金粟、梅子玉代笔，不要紧的连琴仙、蕙芳、素兰、宝珠的手笔也搀在里面，偏人人说好，无一人看得出来。他不接夫人来京，一因任上二亲无人侍奉，二因夫人利害常要劝他戒酒，他有些惧内，鳏居倒也自在。五月初四，道翁进城赴华光宿之请。

南湘寻到春风沉醉轩，见琴仙手托香腮颦眉垂泪，便笑他"人生离合是一定之理，何必作楚囚相对"，拉他到吟秋榭看龙舟，又遣人请了金粟、子玉来。四人下船，穿莲萍、过红桥，南湘击碎一个玻璃钵，朗吟东坡《水调歌头》"我欲乘风归去"；金粟笑他端午何以念中秋的词，琴仙随口改作"我欲乘龙归去，只恐珠宫贝阙，深处不胜寒"，南湘赞那个"深"字，说馆中朋友必改作"低"。金粟又论庾子山用字不检、宋之问以土囊谋人佳句、王勃《滕王阁序》最传诵的一联亦不过写景。船上一段对话最是隐痛：琴仙看燕子掠水，问今年去了明年还回来么；子玉引"似曾相识燕归来"作答，琴仙便反问"无可奈何花落去"——落花还吹得上枝么？子玉说落了重开也是一样，不过暂时落劫；琴仙说落花劫太多，有落在水里的，有落在圂里的，落在水里的还干净些，既落了下来，倒也是他的归结之所。又看鸭群不离，叹人不如物类无拘束；又看两来的云，说只怕有横风吹散，子玉说这边未必没有横风吹合。末了看一尾鲤鱼独自摆尾而去，子玉暗祷"你若游转来，玉侬也能转来"，那鱼果然挨着船身转了回来。南湘忆起去年萧次贤的《水浒传》酒令，蕙芳掣着《潘金莲雪天戏叔》的窘态，感叹田春航不负媚香：若非遇着他，只怕真要作个郑元和；可惜他不是女身，不然此刻便是状元夫人了——子玉恐琴仙听着难受，忙用别话岔开。琴仙从袖中取出萧次贤新画的《怡园饯别图》扇子，一面是古香林屋饯别之景，另一面徐子云已填了半阕《金缕曲》；子玉便和其韵，写出"何事云轻散。

问今番、果然真到，海枯石烂"，琴仙念到"海枯石烂"已哽咽，念到"只道今生长厮守……谁又想，境更换"便放声大哭，泪把词稿滴湿了一半。南湘接念"明朝送别长亭畔。忍牵衣、道声珍重，此心更乱"也念不下去，掷了稿子饮酒跷脚而卧，吟"一声《河满子》，双泪落君前"；金粟只得把末段"若虑梦魂飞不到，试宵宵、彼此将名唤。墨和泪，请君玩"念完，叹道这首词一字一珠、一珠一泪、一泪一血，旁人尚不忍读，何况玉侬，便叫子玉索性题在扇上。初六日，袁琪官单请琴仙话别，又托他约了子玉。座上还有个老者，是教他们戏的叶茂林，特来送行。琪官拉着琴仙的手说出一番肺腑：同来的十个人，死的死散的散，只剩他们两个，如今你又要走，就只剩我一个了；船上那一夜你受了气要跳河，我拉住你，你还恨我，我说要跳同跳你才住了，哭了半夜把帕子撕得粉碎，明日才知撕的是我的；天津船坏那日你怕冷，叫我把背拥着你的背才睡着。说得三人同哭。

琪官至今把琴仙路上给的水晶猫儿当镇纸，去年林春喜要他也不肯给；琴仙也还留着他的琥珀扇坠。琴仙说此去好歹随遇而安，或一二年随寄父再进京，或你们出京萍水相逢，再不然只好来生再见，或做了亲弟兄更好；子玉说他父亲现在江西，只要母亲肯放，他便同琪官来访——琴仙瞅着他说：几千里路，你去了又要回来，何苦吃这一路辛苦，倒是我两三年内进京看你们为妙，你们一个都不准来。席上琴仙取出六颗骰子，对二人说"三心和同，有始有终，掷个全红"，一掷果然六红；琪官接着说"三心和同，后是相逢"，也掷了六红，子玉大喜，说两个全红岂是容易碰着的。琴仙还要再掷，琪官忙把骰子收起，只说两掷皆应了口再掷便不灵——其实那副骰子六面皆红，是他做的顽意，琴仙被他赚了，倒解了好些愁闷。临别子玉解下常佩的小玉琴相赠，琴仙也解下五色玉梅花回赠，二人对跪叩头，眼泪像四串珠子。琴仙叮嘱他后日断不要来送。初六日一早行李先发，道翁与琴仙辰初上车。华光宿本要亲送，被道翁再三阻了，只打发家人代叩，预先送了程仪六百金；子云也送六百，文泽二百。子云、次贤一直送出十里外的皇华亭，那里南湘、仲清、文泽、金粟、王恂、子玉、春航领着苏蕙芳、袁宝珠、陆素兰、金漱芳、李玉林、王兰保、王桂保、琪官、林春喜九人早已等候。九人一齐上来，握手的握手、牵衣的牵衣；琴仙又望见子玉躲在人后拭泪，一阵心痛，头晕眼花跌倒在地，众人救醒，子玉直瞪着两眼一句话也说不出，只是泪落如雨。道翁交代刘喜好好服侍，握着子云、次贤的手道："从此别后，只好魂梦相随"，也不觉老泪涔涔。车轮碾动，回首云山已渺；子玉望不见那车，才放声一哭而回。`,en:`Qu Daoweng, appointed assistant prefect of Nanchang, must go as soon as his credentials are issued, and farewell parties follow one another. The palace examination is announced: Tian Chunhang takes first place in the empire and is made a Hanlin compiler, while Shi Nanxiang stands fourth in the second class and is chosen a bachelor — a distance from Chunhang's destitution last spring that is measured in heaven and earth. The novel pauses on his luck with the surname Su. Last year Su Huifang made his wish good, and what began as a romance of wine and song became a bond of principle that drove him onto the upright road, and so to this success. This year his section examiner recommended his paper and the chief examiners had already rejected it, when one of them — a Su, now head of the Board of War, Hanlin-bred and afterwards heir to a marquisate, and father-in-law to Hua Guangsu — read it with admiration: this man's gifts are out of the common, and though his ornament runs too rich, the cadence is a court poet's, fit to adorn an age of peace. The others still doubted; but his command of *The Five Classics* and the evenness of his policy answers carried him in at thirty-fourth. Unsealing the ink papers at the posting of the list, Marquis Su saw his regular script, judged that he would stand high in the palace examination, and was proved right. When Chunhang called on his examiners the Marquis said nothing of it; his section examiner told him afterwards, and his gratitude to this patron is of another order. The Marquis, near fifty, a nobleman of seven generations, is short of an heir; his principal wife bore only two daughters, of whom the elder is Madam Hua and the younger, nineteen and still unbetrothed, waits for a son-in-law of both talent and looks. Seeing Chunhang he wanted the match at once, learned that he had a wife, and sighed in private. 

Every carriage, garment and utensil in Chunhang's new house has come out of Huifang's own purse, exhausting the savings of years; he has left the troupe, comes over daily, refuses to live in, and yet manages everything, informing himself beforehand about matters he has never handled so that nothing shall be left obscure and Chunhang need not think at all. Then a letter comes: his wife died suddenly in the second month, his mother is prostrated with only one old servant and one servant-woman in the house — and his maternal uncle Zhang Tongsun, retired sick from a prefecture and finding his circumstances failing, is coming to the capital and will bring Lady Tian north on the fifteenth of the third month. Chunhang weeps for grief and joy together: his mother is coming, and the zither string is broken; remembering their poor years of thorn hairpins and cloth skirts he breaks down entirely, until Huifang tells him that the old lady arrives within days and will only grieve the more to see him so. Next day the upper rooms are made ready, and the household is ordered henceforth to call Huifang "Master Su." Lady Tian arrives; Chunhang meets her at Liangxiang; she has heard on the road that he is zhuangyuan and mourns her daughter-in-law the more for it. Of Huifang he tells her that he went on the stage in misfortune and has left it, that his father held a departmental post in Yunnan and his family is of scholars, and that everything here is owed to him — after which Lady Tian treats him kindly and he is more at ease. Shi Nanxiang, chosen for the Academy, moves into the Cool Realm of Poetry in the garden and behaves quite otherwise than Chunhang: he thinks nothing of his fellow graduates, goes to no engagements, and spends his days over poetry and wine with Daoweng, Xiao Cixian and Xu Ziyun, with Yuan Baozhu, Lu Sulan, Wang Lanbao and Jin Shufang for music. Petitioners for his writing come one after another until, weary of them, he has Jin Su and Mei Ziyu ghost-write for him — and for the unimportant ones even Qinxian, Huifang, Sulan and Baozhu supply pieces, which everyone praises and nobody detects. He does not send for his wife: his parents at his father's post need attendance, and besides she is formidable and would make him give up wine, and being a little afraid of her he finds bachelor life more comfortable. 

On the fourth of the fifth month Daoweng is in the city at Hua Guangsu's invitation. Nanxiang finds Qinxian at the Pavilion Drunk on Spring Wind with his chin in his hand and his brows drawn, and rallies him — parting and meeting are a fixed principle, why sit like prisoners of Chu? — and carries him off to the Yinqiu Pavilion to see the dragon boats, sending for Jin Su and Ziyu. The four take a boat through duckweed and water-caltrop and under the red bridge; Nanxiang, beating time until he smashes a glass bowl, chants Su Shi's *Prelude to Water Tune* about riding the wind home and fearing the cold of the jade towers, and Jin Su asks why the mid-autumn lyric at the Duanwu — whereupon Qinxian recasts it on the spot for the season, riding a dragon home and fearing the cold of the deep pearl palace, and Nanxiang praises the word "deep," saying his colleagues at the Academy would infallibly have written "low." Jin Su falls to criticism: Yu Xin was careless of his diction and repeated himself within a single piece; Song Zhiwen killed a man for a good line, and what was the line worth; and the couplet in Wang Bo's *Preface to the Pavilion of Prince Teng* that everyone quotes is only description. Then comes the talk on the water that carries the chapter's grief. Watching swallows skim the duckweed, Qinxian asks whether the ones that go this year will come back next; certainly, says Ziyu, these very two, and quotes the line about the swallow that seems an old acquaintance returning — to which Qinxian answers with its companion, that fallen flowers cannot be helped, and asks whether a fallen flower can be blown back onto the branch. Falling and opening again are the same, says Ziyu; it is a temporary calamity only. But there are many ways of falling, says Qinxian: into water, or into a privy; into water is at least clean, and having fallen, that is where it belongs. They watch a line of ducks of which not one leaves the flock, and Ziyu says that human beings are less free than these creatures; and they watch clouds coming from two directions, and Qinxian fears a crosswind to scatter them, and Ziyu answers that where one crosswind can scatter another may drive together. At last a solitary carp turns away downstream, and Ziyu prays silently that if it returns Yunong will return — and it comes back along the side of the boat. 

Nanxiang recalls last year's *Water Margin* drinking game and Huifang drawing *Pan Jinlian Teases Her Uncle-in-Law in the Snow*, and says Xiangfan has not failed Meixiang: with anyone else he would have become another Zheng Yuanhe, and it is a pity only that Huifang was not born a woman, or he would now be the zhuangyuan's wife — at which Ziyu turns the conversation for Qinxian's sake. Qinxian draws from his sleeve a fan newly painted by Cixian, *Farewell at the Garden of Ease*, showing the parting at the Ancient Fragrance Grove House, with half a *Jinlü Qu* by Ziyun on the other face; and Ziyu matches its rhymes. Qinxian reads the new lyric aloud — why should the cloud disperse so lightly; is it truly come at last, though seas dry and stones crumble — and chokes on the second line; and at the words about believing they would be together for life and never dreaming the scene could change, he breaks into open weeping, and his tears soak half the draft. Nanxiang takes it up — tomorrow at the parting pavilion, to bear the clutching of a sleeve and the words "take care of yourself," and the heart more disordered still — and cannot go on either; he throws down the paper, drinks, lies back with his legs up and chants that one strain of *River Full* brings two tears before you. Jin Su finishes it: if you fear the dream-soul cannot fly so far, then night after night let each call the other's name; here is ink mixed with tears for your reading — and says that the lyric does not waste those tears, every character a pearl, every pearl a tear, every tear blood, unbearable to a stranger and how much more to Yunong; and Ziyu writes it out on the fan. On the sixth Yuan Qiguan, who came to the capital with Qinxian and studied under the same master, invites him alone to take leave, and through him invites Ziyu. Their old teacher Ye Maolin is there with parting gifts. Taking Qinxian's hand, Qiguan speaks from the bottom of his heart: of the ten who came north together, some are dead and some scattered, and only the two of them are left — and now that Qinxian goes there will be one. Remember the boat, he says, and the others uncongenial; you said we should live in one place and die in one place; and the night you were insulted and meant to jump into the river, and I held you and you hated me for it until I said we would jump together, and you cried half the night and tore a handkerchief to pieces, and in the morning it turned out to be mine, and you gave me a new one; and at Tianjin when the boat was damaged and you were cold, you had me set my back against yours before you could sleep. 

All three weep. Qiguan still keeps the crystal cat given him on that journey and uses it to weight his writing paper, having refused it to Lin Chunxi last year; Qinxian still keeps his amber fan-pendant. Qinxian says he must take what comes: perhaps in a year or two his adoptive father will come to the capital again and he with him; perhaps they will meet by chance in the provinces; and if not, then this is the meeting heaven allotted, and they must wait for the next life to be acquainted again, or better, to be born brothers. Ziyu says that his own father is in Jiangxi, and that if his mother consents he will go with Qiguan to find him — at which Qinxian looks at him and says that it is a journey of thousands of li and he would only have to come back, and that it is better he himself should come to the capital within two or three years, and none of them must come to him. At table Qinxian produces six dice, says that three hearts being in accord there should be a beginning and an end, and throws six reds; Qiguan follows, wishing that three hearts in accord may meet hereafter, and throws six reds too, so that Ziyu rejoices that two such casts are not lightly met with. Qinxian would throw again, and Qiguan sweeps up the dice, saying that two throws have answered the words and a third would break the omen — the truth being that every face of them is red, a toy of his own making, and that Qinxian is comforted by a kindly cheat. At parting Ziyu unfastens the small jade qin he always wears and gives it to him; Qinxian gives back a five-coloured jade plum blossom; they kneel and kowtow to each other with the tears falling like four strings of beads, and Qinxian charges him on no account to come to the send-off. On the sixth the baggage goes ahead at dawn and the two take carriage at the hour of the dragon. Hua Guangsu had meant to come out of the city himself and was dissuaded, and sent his people to bow in his stead, having already sent six hundred taels for the journey; Ziyun gave six hundred more and Wenze two hundred. Ziyun and Cixian escort them ten li to the Huanghua Pavilion, where Nanxiang, Yan Zhongqing, Wenze, Jin Su, Wang Xun, Ziyu and Chunhang are waiting with nine performers — Su Huifang, Yuan Baozhu, Lu Sulan, Jin Shufang, Li Yulin, Wang Lanbao, Wang Guibao, Qiguan and Lin Chunxi. The nine crowd round him, some taking his hands and some his sleeves; and catching sight of Ziyu hidden behind the others wiping his eyes, Qinxian feels his heart turn over, his sight darkens, and he falls. They bring him round; Ziyu stands staring, unable to speak, the tears running down. Daoweng charges Liu Xi to take care of him, presses Ziyun's and Cixian's hands, and says that from this parting only dreams can follow them and that his gratitude is past speech, his old eyes wet as he says it. The wheels turn and the hills close behind them; and only when the carriage is out of sight does Ziyu let himself cry aloud and go home.`},49:{zh:`梅子玉送别归来，一急一痛，旧病复发，足足病了一月；颜夫人后来知道琴仙已出京、又为屈道翁收作义子，倒也替他欢喜。田春航断弦之后，田太夫人年老无媳总是不惯，命他从权另娶清门。他犹豫未决，先想觅个小星，又嫌北人生硬乏娇柔，只好在老婆子里头找个细致的伺候母亲——谁知京里这些人一万个里也拣不出一个：雇来两个京东妇人，一个麻脸如蜂窝，头插纸花，走起来腰掀屁蹷，专会内外搬弄是非，不是说这个作贼便是说那个偷汉，只为掩自己的丑；一个沙盆大脸、水缸大肚，伺候过一顿饭便一样不肯做，一使唤就装聋做哑，摆开八字脚抽关东烟，去找火夫打杂的坐在厨房土炕上说笑，隔一天还要出去半日找那些赶车挑煤的孤身汉解闷，见了春航还要偷瞧一眼——不到半月都撵掉了。赶车的周小三便向苏蕙芳荐他三姐，说粗细皆能，还会缝衣写算。蕙芳久闻三姐收拾潘其观之名，想是个伶俐人，也想见识见识，就向春航举荐；三姐自那事后也怕潘其观报仇，在家闲了两三个月，正愿意进这个门。

及至进见，只见她不村不俏，薄施香粉、淡扫蛾眉，鬓边一朵榴花，翘然三寸弓鞋，细腰如杵——春航原当赶车的媳妇必是粗笨，一见大为失惊。田太夫人也十分欢喜：她千伶百俐，无一样伺候不到，做出菜来比京里的厨子高十几倍。她先前没听小三说过春航的模样，及见他如金如玉，越发尽心；再见蕙芳的人才，反觉自愧，待他格外殷勤。一日春航不在，老太太命她送点心到书房，她先咳嗽一声才进来，笑叫一声"苏大爷"，蕙芳便回叫"三姐"，又请她只叫自己的名字。蕙芳想问潘其观的事又不敢，三姐看出来，只呆呆望着等他问；他终于陪笑问出"三姐是女中豪杰，把潘三收拾得爽快，可是真的"，三姐脸一红，低低啐了一声，转身便走，只说"谁又认得潘三？是那里听来的话"；走到帘边银挖耳落地，回脸又是一笑，拾起插上急急进去。蕙芳虽碰了钉子，见她并不着恼，也就放心，只悔自己失言。后来又见了两回，她在二门口与小三说话，经过书房门口回过脸来，恰与蕙芳四目相对，低鬟一笑而去，蕙芳才知她并无恼意。另一头，春航要续弦的话传到苏侯耳里，正合他意，便向武选司郎中杨方猷透了口风；杨方猷正是春航的房师，喜得来传话叫他托人求亲。春航踌躇：苏家是世禄之家门庭烜赫，自己虽成了名依旧寒素，又不知那位小姐如何，须先访一访；然而座师愿结姻、房师来讲，也不好推辞，只说禀过家慈再复命。

他到怡园去问徐子云，恰见门口一辆绿围车、八匹马——原来华光宿在园。原来那封恶札写过两日，华公子已自悔；偏子云只当没事，也不来招陪，他心里更觉无趣。及为屈道翁饯行，道翁把子云的好处说了一番，又讲起扶乩判定琴言前世是他女儿，通身把那首诗念给他听，华公子听得骇然，再细想琴言竟没有什么不好，倒是自己冤了他；后来萧次贤又把此事澈底讲明，他才悔孟浪，又想两代世交为这点事绝交必被人议论，自己既是盟弟，只得先下气去招陪。子云大度包容，仍照从前相待，绝不提起；华公子忍不住，自认误信浮言，子云反安慰他几句，留他在春风沉醉轩小饮而散。那日次贤、南湘都不在座——前夜南湘拉次贤下船，一坛酒把次贤灌得大醉，自己却掉进水里灌了几口，今日腹胀腰疼起不来。子云见了春航，一听杨方猷的话便连连称贺，说这喜比中状元还大：苏侯是他乡试座师、又是其兄会试房师、又是其父的盟弟，两重年谊一重世谊；那两位小姐更是绝世无双，人称"苏氏二乔"，长的浣香便是华夫人，次女浣兰同母所生——最奇的是姊妹二人的相貌竟与苏蕙芳一模一样，长的七分像，次女竟有九分，比蕙芳还娇柔艳丽，只因蕙芳到底是个男身。春航听得半信半疑，又要请子云作伐，子云故意推却，经他再三作揖才应允。

过了两日子云回拜华公子，顺路到苏府提亲。苏侯故作沉吟，说田修撰文才品貌极好，也是旧家，只不知品行如何——"我最怕的是轻薄少年"；子云力保他廉隅砥砺、孝友兼全，苏侯大笑道"足见年兄取友必端"。随后一席长谈：问起怡园近况，说等秋凉再来逛；应允了婚事；又叙两家亲眷——子云之父在任平稳、其兄新署运司又添一子、岳父袁浩已由云南升调闽浙兼管洋务；苏侯自言五十一岁尚无子，又说史南湘于前辈礼数似乎不甚合式，有人议论他狂，或是初入翰林不知规矩，况前后辈的规矩本也太严，教他留点神。说到秋间五十年未举的博学宏词，问子云所知的真才实学，子云举屈本立而叹其现任官不能应考，苏侯亦惜南京金粟限于成例不能保举；子云又举梅子玉、颜仲清、刘文泽、王恂并高品、萧次贤诸人，苏侯掀髯大笑说"怎么你举的人多半是我的年侄"，随即历数梅士燮、王文辉、刘侍郎与已故的颜庄、袁浩、史曾望都是同年，感慨"人生不齐之数"。子云回车途中想"湘帆太便宜了"，索性等他再来求。春航连去两日皆不得见，第三日才见着，子云先说"有几分可望"，又编出苏侯听来的一句闲话——"我听说现有个状元夫人在家，也姓苏，还是有恩于他，怎么还要续弦"——臊得春航满脸通红，说"弟固不足惜，兄应为媚香留一地步"。经过一番谑笑，终于讲定，择于七月初七成礼。

春航把子云"那位苏小姐像你竟到九分"的话说与蕙芳，蕙芳笑说这不是糟蹋人么，一个千金小姐像了我，我们算什么人；春航说若果然像你，我就当他菩萨供养，天天拜他，说着果然作了一揖，蕙芳连忙走开，叫他留着拜那位状元夫人。谈到蕙芳自己成家，他说得极透：好人家不肯把女儿嫁我们，小户人家的女儿我又不要；我们这些人被那些无耻的东西闹得不像样，谁肯信我们是清清白白的？与其娶小家之女，倒不如娶大家之婢，那礼貌性德是见惯的，只一件，主人已先受用的十有八九。春航许他将来苏侯赠嫁的丫鬟里若有好的便送他一个，蕙芳笑说那时只好在这里伺候一辈子，算田、苏两姓的家奴。春航又叹世间错配的真有，说周小三倒有这么个好女人，岂不冤枉；蕙芳问"你爱他么"，春航忙说不过说说罢了，蕙芳道爱好之心人人难免，又赞三姐不但生得好，还灵慧异常，倒是个贞节妇人；春航笑说灵慧有之、贞节未确，又反将一军：你说爱好之心人人有之，设使你做了潘三，只怕他就不忍收拾你了。正说着，房师杨方猷有要紧话请他过去，春航便出门了。蕙芳闲着要走，忽听咭咭咯咯之声，回头正是三姐。她先笑说"好几天不见你来"，又看他腰间的表套，说自己也会做，还会做戳纱的荷包；蕙芳讨一个，她说"我的东西不给人"，转而问他年庚——同是十九岁，他三月她正月，于是一个叫"姐姐"，一个叫"兄弟"。她这才说破前事："我前日真怪你有点冒失，怎么问起潘三那事来？那是你姐夫做的事情，与三兄弟报仇，我瞧还没有瞧见潘三是什么样儿呢。这话你若问了别人只怕就不好，幸亏是我，因为是你问我，所以不肯恼你。"又许明日送他一对荷包，只叮嘱不要告诉人，免得这个来要、那个来讨。`,en:`Mei Ziyu comes back from the send-off, and grief and shock together bring his old illness on; he is a full month in bed. Madam Yan, learning afterwards that Qinxian has left the capital and been adopted by Qu Daoweng, is glad for him. Tian Chunhang, widowed, with an aged mother and no daughter-in-law in the house, is told to take a wife from some respectable family. He hesitates, thinks first of a concubine, finds northern women stiff and without softness, and settles for looking among the older servant-women for one careful enough to wait on his mother — of whom, in the capital, one cannot be picked out of ten thousand. Two women of the eastern suburbs are hired: one pockmarked like a wasps' nest, with paper flowers stuck all over her head, who walks with heaving hips and lives by carrying tales in and out, accusing one person of theft and another of adultery to cover her own doings; the other with a face like a sand-basin and a belly like a water jar, who after serving a single meal will do nothing, plays deaf when called, splays her feet and takes her Guandong tobacco off to the kitchen kang to joke with the stokers, and every other day goes out for half a day to amuse herself among the carters and coal-porters — and steals a look at Chunhang besides. Within a fortnight both are gone. 

Then Zhou Xiaosan proposes to Su Huifang that his Third Sister come and wait on the old lady, praising her for coarse work and fine, sewing and accounts alike. Huifang, who has long heard how she dealt with Pan Qiguan and wants to see so clever a woman, recommends her; and she, who has kept indoors two or three months for fear of Pan's revenge, is glad enough of the place. She arrives neither rustic nor showy, lightly powdered, her brows lightly drawn, a pomegranate flower at her temple, three-inch shoes and a waist like a pestle — and Chunhang, who had assumed a coachman's wife must be coarse, is astonished. Lady Tian is delighted with her: she is quick at everything, misses nothing, and cooks ten times better than any hired cook in the city. Never having been told what Chunhang was like, she finds him gold and jade and exerts herself the more; and seeing Huifang's beauty she feels her own inferiority and is warmer to him than to anyone. One day, with Chunhang out, the old lady sends refreshments to the study by her hand; she coughs at the door before entering, smiles, and says "Master Su," and he answers "Third Sister" and asks her to use his name. He wants to ask about Pan Qiguan and dares not; she sees it and waits, looking steadily at him, until he brings it out — is it true that she, a heroine among women, settled Pan San so thoroughly? She reddens, spits softly, turns to go, and says only, who knows any Pan San, and where did he hear it — and at the curtain her long silver ear-pick falls, so that she turns back with another smile, picks it up, sets it in her hair and hurries in. Rebuffed but not repulsed, Huifang is relieved and sorry he spoke. Twice more he comes without seeing her; then he hears her step, watches her speak to Xiaosan at the second gate, and as she passes the study she turns her face and their eyes meet, and she goes with a small bow and a smile — and he knows she is not angry. Meanwhile word of Chunhang's wish to remarry reaches Marquis Su, which suits him exactly, and he drops a hint to Yang Fangyou of the Bureau of Military Appointments — Chunhang's own section examiner — that a proposal would be welcome. Chunhang wavers: the Su house is one of hereditary emolument and blazing prestige, and he, famous or not, is still poor; besides, he knows nothing of the young lady and would like to inquire. But a chief examiner's wish conveyed by a section examiner cannot be refused, and he answers that he must first report to his mother. 

Going to Yiyuan to consult Xu Ziyun, he sees a green-curtained carriage and eight horses at the gate: Hua Guangsu is there. For Hua had repented of his vicious letter within two days, and Ziyun's behaving as though nothing had happened, and making no move toward him, had left him at a loss. At Qu Daoweng's farewell the old man praised Ziyun, told him of the planchette that made Qinyan his own daughter in a former life, and recited the poem through — which shook Hua badly, since on reflection he could find nothing against the boy and much wrong on his own side. Xiao Cixian afterwards set the whole matter straight, and Hua, repenting his rashness and reflecting that to break a friendship of two generations over such a trifle would set people talking, took the first step as the younger sworn brother. Ziyun, being magnanimous, received him exactly as before and never mentioned it, until Hua could not bear it and admitted that he had believed idle talk and been wrong; and Ziyun comforted him and kept him to drink. Cixian and Shi Nanxiang are absent — Nanxiang having taken Cixian out on the boat the night before and emptied a jar with him, leaving Cixian dead drunk and himself fished out of the water with a bellyful, so that he is now aching in bed. Told of Yang Fangyou's message, Ziyun congratulates Chunhang and says the joy is greater than the first place in the empire: Marquis Su was his own provincial examiner, his brother's metropolitan section examiner and his father's sworn younger brother, two examination ties and one hereditary; and the two daughters are matchless, the famous two Qiao of the Su family — Huanxiang, the elder, now Madam Hua, and Huanlan by the same mother. And the strangest thing of all is that both sisters are the image of Su Huifang, the elder seven parts and the younger nine, and softer and more brilliant than he, since Huifang after all is a man. Chunhang laughs, half believing; and when he begs Ziyun to act as go-between, Ziyun refuses for the pleasure of being pressed, and yields only after repeated bows. 

Two days later, returning Hua's call, Ziyun goes on to the Su mansion and makes the proposal. The Marquis affects to consider: Tian's talent and looks are excellent and the family old, but what of his conduct — for what he fears most is a frivolous young man. Ziyun answers for him as scrupulous and dutiful, and the Marquis laughs that this shows how carefully he chooses his friends. A long conversation follows: the garden, which the Marquis has not visited in a year or two and will see when autumn cools; the consent to the match; the news of Ziyun's father at his post, of his brother made salt commissioner and given a son, of his father-in-law Yuan Hao promoted from Yunnan to the two coastal provinces with foreign trade besides; the Marquis's own regret that at fifty-one he has no son; and a word of caution about Shi Nanxiang, said to be careless of the etiquette owed to seniors and called arrogant for it — though the rules between senior and junior, the Marquis allows, have always been too strict, and he himself was criticized in his day. Then the special examination in broad learning, set for the autumn and not held for fifty years: asked whom he knows of real learning, Ziyun names Qu Benli, and regrets that a serving official cannot compete; the Marquis regrets the Nanjing scholar Jin Su, barred by the rules; and Ziyun goes on to Mei Ziyu, Yan Zhongqing, Liu Wenze and Wang Xun, with Gao Pin and Xiao Cixian besides — at which the Marquis strokes his beard and laughs that most of them are his own examination-nephews, and runs over their fathers, Mei Shixie, Wang Wenhui, Vice-Minister Liu, the late Yan Zhuang, Yuan Hao, and Shi Zengwang, all of his year, of whom some are still departmental clerks and some magistrates — the unevenness, he says, of human fortune. Driving home, Ziyun decides that Xiangfan has had it too easy, and refuses to see him two days running; on the third he begins by allowing only a few tenths of hope, and then invents a remark for the Marquis to have made — that he hears there is already a zhuangyuan's lady at home, also surnamed Su, and one to whom he is under obligation, so why should he need a wife? Chunhang, scarlet, protests that Ziyun may make free with him but should leave Meixiang his dignity; and after enough teasing the thing is settled and the wedding fixed for the seventh of the seventh month. When Chunhang repeats Ziyun's remark that the young lady resembles him nine parts in ten, Huifang says that this is an insult to her — a great house's daughter said to look like him, and what does that make them? Chunhang doubts she is his equal, and says that if she truly resembles him he will keep her like a bodhisattva and worship her daily — and bows to Huifang, who moves away laughing and tells him to save it for his bride. Talk turns to Huifang's own marrying, and here he speaks plainly: decent families will not give their daughters to men of his profession, and he will not take a small household's daughter; their calling has been so degraded by shameless men that nobody will believe them clean. Better than a small family's daughter, he says, is a great house's maid, who has at least seen good manners — with the single drawback that in eight or nine cases out of ten the master has had her first. 

Chunhang promises him one of the dowry maids if there is a good one among them, and Huifang laughs that in that case he must stay and serve in this house for life as a bondservant of the Tian and Su families. Then Chunhang remarks that the world is full of ill-matched pairs, and that their own Zhou Xiaosan has got such a woman as is wasted on him. Do you like her, asks Huifang; and Chunhang says it was only a remark. Liking what is good, says Huifang, is natural to anyone — and Third Sister is not only handsome but extraordinarily clever, and a chaste woman besides. Clever certainly, says Chunhang, chaste unproven; and when Huifang cites her treatment of Pan Qiguan, he answers that Pan has that sort of face, and that by Huifang's own rule she might not have had the heart to serve a better-looking man so. Just then his section examiner sends for him and he goes out. Huifang, at a loose end, is leaving when he hears a step and turns to find Third Sister. She has not seen him for days, she says; he answers that he comes daily and never sees her. Looking at the watch-case at his waist she says she can make one, and embroidered purses too; he asks for one, and she says she does not give her things away; then she asks his age. Both are nineteen — he of the third month and she of the first — so she is the elder; and after a exchange of protests about who is unworthy of whom, they settle it, and she calls him younger brother and he calls her elder sister. Only then does she explain herself: she did think him rash the other day, for what has Pan San to do with her? That was her husband's doing, in revenge for her third brother, and she has never so much as seen the man's face. Had he asked anyone else it would have gone badly; but since it was he who asked, she would not be angry. And she promises him a pair of purses tomorrow, on condition that he tells nobody — or one will come begging and then another.`},50:{zh:`田春航已聘定苏侯的小姐，只等七月初七完婚。盛夏日长，刘文泽新补吏部主事，与徐子云同司，借上衙门的日子出来散心，路过林春喜门口便进去看他。春喜正穿着白纻丝衫子刷藕，书室两间甚为幽雅；壁上一幅黄鹤山樵是萧次贤给的，文泽看着不像真迹，春喜笑说"好就是了，何必论真假"。后窗内四五棵芭蕉，两面叶上写满真行大小的字，原来他取"书成蕉叶"之意，写满一面再写一面，擦去再写——"横竖他也闲着，长这些大叶子，不是给我学字的么？我若写在纸上，教人看了笑话。"又在叶上画蜻蜓、螳螂、促织之类。说起袁宝珠，他很有些不平：春间去请教，不是笑他就是薄他，问法子又不肯说，如今他倒常来要看；他索性不给看，要画好了再说，只向次贤要了许多稿子照着画。随即取出一柄团扇，画着杨柳下螳螂捕蝉，蝉翅张开被压住，螳螂两臂扎住蝉项、两眼鼓起、头须一横一竖，宛然欲动；文泽赞叹不信，硬索了去叫他落款。

正说着，颜仲清、王恂同袁琪官、王桂保进来，便一同到唐和尚新开的安吉堂去。那里密室三间，帘幕床帐俱备，只是墙上贺额对联字画俗恶不堪，仲清笑说"怪不得这里热，被这些联额字画看得出汗"。点了出名的生炒翅子、烧鸭子，冰桶里镇着西瓜雪藕，一面小酌一面说起梅子玉：自送行以来他又病了几天，俨然去年夏天光景，仲清说他的元神此时正跟着玉侬在长江里守风，只怕送到南昌才肯回来。琪官想起初六那日的话别与皇华亭的痛哭，至今替他们伤心；王恂说幸亏那天没有生人在旁；文泽感叹子玉与春航苦乐不同，春航既与苏蕙芳朝夕相亲，如今又对了一门才貌双全的阔亲，偏偏也姓苏，这一班朋友里只有他一个得意。说到史南湘因不得鼎甲挫了锐气，如今住在怡园避热就凉，不与新同年往来。

席上桂保又出一个拆字令，分作"旧管、新收、开除、实在"四柱：如旧管酉字、新收三点水成"酒"，开除中间一画，实在是"洒"。众人依样说来：天—笑—竺，射—榭—村，圭—桂—杜，寺—诗—讨，一—吏—丈，串—患—忠，昌—阊—间，贱—溅—浅；文泽把"波—婆—女"说错了自罚一杯，琪官说的"铡"字太冷，被桂保驳了。忽听隔壁唱《南浦》，桂保便问南曲遇入声字该断不该断，春喜就工尺板眼一一剖析，说只要把"妻"字的五字拖长、六字改中眼、工字改作"一"字的头板，音节自然截断，才合南曲唱入声的规矩；又说南曲本无一凡，惟《刺梁》一出南北合套，梁冀唱到"酒困潦倒"处因下接北曲不能不出调，才吹一凡，普天下只此一处。再说《小宴》南北合套音节最好，唱到《惊变》"恁道是失机的哥舒翰"，非用五六五出调高唱不可，既是惊变，慢腾腾低唱是何神理。他更进一层，说班中口白多不妥当：如《阳告》一出，出场时一口说尽，后头曲文与口白文气全不相接，他要把那段口白拆作三截，分别接《滚绣球》《叨叨令》《脱布衫》三支曲子，通身文气才通。众人问何以讲究昆腔的都不晓得这些毛病，春喜说唱清曲的原不用口白，教曲的先生又总是先教曲文、后把口白挤成一篇，没有分开段落，因此沿袭至今。

正谈得高兴，唐和尚从后角门钻了进来，一件黄纻丝短僧衣、青线网凉鞋，摇着鹅毛扇满面堆笑，先抢步作揖，又与三旦拉手，恭惟得众人发笑。他讲了一串闲话：田春航幸遇苏蕙芳管教得好，未遇之前也不过天天在戏园里过日子；高品最刻薄，寺里做法事时不知从哪里买了个角先生塞进他袖兜，客座上一翻袖子落将下来，还在上面写了"归云小像"四字，臊得他要死，他因此天天拜佛保佑高品落第，去年果然应了口。又问苏蕙芳是否已被田太夫人认作义子、宅里称二老爷，文泽说没有的话，只是下人称他苏大爷；唐和尚赞蕙芳斯斯文文、见了和尚也必恭必敬，不像华府里那位琴相公见人板着脸。问起里间吹唱的是谁，才知是魏聘才：他捐了从九品分发湖北，正月被窃去衣服银钱千金，去了一半家私，后来又包着玉天仙每月一百五十吊，四五个月便当卖起来——不料这玉天仙竟拿出历年积蓄替他捐了分发，说定嫁他同走，从此他非但不必花钱，倒可以使她的钱。又说潘其观欠了玉天仙的嫖钱还不出，竟引表侄去逛、留他住下自己偷跑，害得表侄当了两票当才打发得开，回家大闹一场，潘其观没法，自己把手腕上的肉咬下两块，人都说他"为嫖割股"。又说奚十一今年生毒疮几乎送命，还是他医好的，如今七月内有缺就要到班；去年富三爷托魏大爷、魏大爷托他，才选了湖北，贵大爷又得了湖北同知，如今魏大爷也去湖北，三个相好仍旧聚在一处，岂不是缘分。

他还奉承文泽三人将来同做抚、藩、臬台，说得三个相公看着他胁肩谄笑好不难看。琪官说起袁宝珠近日跟金吉甫学琴足不出户，吉甫当年教过梅卿弹琴，自梅卿死后《梅花三弄》再不肯弹，也算深于情了。日已西沉，文泽与王恂争着会账，走堂的却说唐大爷早已会过；唐和尚笑道和尚没有折本的买卖，明日就拿缘簿到宅里去，少爷多写一笔就是。魏聘才这边与玉天仙同居，另租了房子，买了丫头雇了老婆子，玉天仙做起奶奶来——她本是扬州瘦马，年二十七，长聘才两岁，相貌标致而弹唱皆精。聘才想起李元茂去年所借的镯子未还，寻到孙宅，元茂却同两个舅子下通州赴考去了，只好认了晦气。出京前潘其观、奚十一、张仲雨、冯子佩、杨梅窗、唐和尚等轮流饯行。白菊花未从良时与玉天仙同局，结为异姓姊妹，便求奚十一请她过来饯别：玉天仙自道运气不好，去年吃了场官司，见魏大爷为她吃苦而待她更好，才定意嫁他，又拿积蓄替他捐了分发，"虽是磕头虫，到底也算个老爷"；白菊花却说姐姐是正室一马一鞍的安稳，自己虽在大老爷家终是偏房，名分不及。二人又笑说杨八也是个从九，全靠看风水赚人，家里利害，还曾拿着眷晚生的帖子去拜门户人家，到门才被轿夫提醒。姬亮轩在书房里剜破窗纸偷看，不料窗纸经年风日已脆，额角一靠便拍的裂了一块；玉天仙回头瞧见，径自去了，白菊花送客回来抬头望见破处那人还在偷看，勉强忍住笑低头进去。聘才出京之日，唐和尚直送到十里长亭洒泪而别；他回去接了父母同往湖北，从此书中再没有他的事了。`,en:`Tian Chunhang's betrothal to Marquis Su's daughter is settled and only the seventh of the seventh month is waited for. In the long heat, Liu Wenze, newly appointed to the Board of Personnel in the same bureau as Xu Ziyun, uses an office day as an excuse to get out, and passing Lin Chunxi's door goes in. Chunxi, in white ramie, is scraping lotus root; his two study rooms are quietly elegant. A painting on the wall, given him by Xiao Cixian as a genuine Wang Meng, strikes Wenze as no such thing — and Chunxi answers that if it is good, what does authenticity matter. Through the north window stand four or five banana palms whose leaves are covered on both faces with characters, regular and running, large and small: taking his hint from the old line about writing on banana leaves, he fills one side, then the other, wipes them and begins again — the plants are idle anyway, he says, and grow those big leaves for him to practise on; and if he wrote on paper people would laugh. He paints dragonflies, mantises and crickets on them too. Of Yuan Baozhu he speaks with some resentment: when he asked for instruction in the spring he was laughed at or slighted, and when he asked for method he was refused, whereas now Baozhu keeps coming to see his work — so he shows him nothing and waits until he is good, and takes his models from Cixian instead. Then he produces a round fan: under a willow a mantis has seized a cicada, whose spread wing is pinned beneath it, the mantis's forelegs locked on the neck, its eyes bulging and its two antennae set one across the other, the whole thing on the point of moving. 

Wenze refuses to believe he painted it and carries it off, making him sign it. Yan Zhongqing and Wang Xun arrive with Yuan Qiguan and Wang Guibao, and the party moves to the Anji Hall that Tang Heshang has opened. Its three private rooms have curtains and beds ready, but the congratulatory plaques, couplets and paintings on the walls are so vulgar that Zhongqing says no wonder the place is hot — one sweats merely from reading them. They order the famous stir-fried shark's fin and roast duck, with melon and lotus root packed in ice, and talk of Mei Ziyu, who has been ill again since the send-off and looks as he did last summer: his soul, says Zhongqing, is waiting out the wind on the Yangtze with Yunong, and will not come home until it has seen him to Nanchang. Qiguan, remembering the farewell of the sixth and the collapse at the Huanghua Pavilion, grieves for them still; Wang Xun is thankful no stranger was there to see it; and Wenze reflects how differently fortune has served Ziyu and Chunhang, who not only has Su Huifang beside him daily but has now married into wealth and beauty — and into the surname Su besides — the only one of their circle to be envied. 

Shi Nanxiang, they say, has lost his edge since missing the top three, and lives in the garden avoiding his new fellow graduates. Guibao then produces a word-splitting game in four columns, styled old balance, new receipt, expenditure and actual: the character for wine, for instance, is an old balance of one element with the water radical newly received, from which the removal of a stroke leaves the character for sprinkle. The others follow with their own — heaven becoming laughter and then bamboo, archery becoming pavilion and then village, and so on — while Wenze muddles one and fines himself a cup, and Qiguan is overruled for using a character too obscure. Singing starts next door, an aria from *The Southern Shore*, and Guibao asks whether in southern-style singing an entering-tone syllable should be broken off; whereupon Chunxi analyses the notation and the beats note by note, showing that by lengthening one pitch and shifting the others onto different beats the syllable can be cut clean, as the southern rule for entering tones requires. He adds that southern music never uses a certain accidental — except in one place only, *Assassinating Liang*, where the suite mixes southern and northern modes and the singer must modulate because a northern aria follows. He praises the modal mixture in *The Small Banquet*, and says that in *Startled by Change*, at the line about the general who lost his chance, the singer must go up into the higher register — for if the scene is a shock, what sense is there in a slow, low delivery? Then he goes further, to the spoken parts, which the troupes deliver badly: in *The Piteous Appeal* the whole speech is poured out at the entrance, so that the arias which follow have no connection with it, whereas if the speech were broken into three and each part placed before *Rolling Silk Ball*, *Chattering Command* and *Removing the Cloth Jacket* in turn, the sense would run through unbroken. Why, the company asks, do the connoisseurs of Kunqu not know this? Because, he says, those who sing pure concert style use no speeches at all, and the teaching masters always teach the arias first and then cram all the speech into one block without dividing it, so that the fault has been handed down. 

Tang Heshang comes in by the back door in a short yellow monk's coat and net sandals, fanning himself and all smiles, and after bowing and shaking hands regales them: how lucky Chunhang was in Su Huifang, since before that he lived in the theatres; how Gao Pin once bought an obscene toy and slipped it into his sleeve during a temple service, so that when he turned back his cuff before guests it fell out with the words "portrait of Guiyun" written on it in white powder, and how he prayed daily thereafter that Gao Pin might fail — which he duly did. He asks whether Huifang has been adopted by Lady Tian and is called Second Master in the house; Wenze says no, only that the servants call him Master Su; and Tang praises him as gentle and courteous even to a monk, unlike that Master Qin at the Hua mansion, who never smiled at anyone. The singing next door proves to be Wei Pincai's: he has bought a ninth-rank appointment and is posted to Hubei. Robbed in the first month of a thousand taels' worth of clothes and money, half his property, he then kept Yu Tianxian at a hundred and fifty strings a month and within four or five was pawning things — until she astonished everyone by producing her own savings to buy him the appointment, agreeing to marry him and travel with him, so that henceforth he need spend nothing and may live on hers. Tang tells too how Pan Qiguan, unable to pay what he owed her, brought his own nephew to her house and slipped away leaving him there, so that the young man had to pawn two lots of goods to get free and made a great scene at home — after which Pan, at his wits' end, bit two pieces of flesh out of his own wrist, and people said he had cut his thigh for whoring. Xi Shiyi, he adds, nearly died of an ulcer this year and was cured by him, and expects a vacancy in the seventh month; and last year Fu the Third got Hubei through Pincai's word to him, and Gui Fen a sub-prefecture in Hubei after him, and now Pincai goes to Hubei too — the three friends gathered in one province again, which must be fate. He flatters the three gentlemen with a prediction that they will one day govern one province between them as its three chief officers, while the three performers watch his bowing and grinning with distaste. 

Qiguan mentions that Baozhu is studying the qin with Jin Jifu and never goes out, and that Jifu, who once taught a friend now dead, has never played *Three Variations on the Plum Blossom* since — which is feeling of a deep kind. At sunset Wenze and Wang Xun quarrel over the bill and are told that Tang has paid it; monks, he says, do not keep a losing business, and tomorrow he will bring his subscription book to their houses and they may enter one more line. Pincai, meanwhile, has taken a house and set up with Yu Tianxian, bought a maid and hired an old woman, and she is a mistress of a household — a Yangzhou girl of twenty-seven, two years his senior, handsome, and accomplished at singing and the lute. Remembering the bracelet Li Yuanmao borrowed last year, he goes to the Sun house and finds that Yuanmao has gone to Tongzhou with his two brothers-in-law to sit an examination, and writes it off. Before his departure Pan Qiguan, Xi Shiyi, Zhang Zhongyu, Feng Zipei, Yang Meichuang and Tang Heshang give farewell dinners by turns. White Chrysanthemum, who was in the same house with Yu Tianxian before she left the trade and swore sisterhood with her, gets Xi Shiyi's leave to invite her. Yu Tianxian tells her story: her luck was bad and she was prosecuted last year, but Pincai suffered for her sake and treated her better than before, so she resolved to marry him, and spent her savings on his appointment — a kowtowing ninth-rank clerk, but a gentleman for all that. White Chrysanthemum answers that her sister at least will be a wife, one saddle to one horse, whereas she herself, though in a great man's house, is only a secondary wife and has not the standing. They laugh over Yang Eight, also a ninth-rank man with no prospect of a post, living by geomancy, hen-pecked at home, who once went in a sedan chair with a formal card to call at a brothel and had to be told at the door by his own chair-men what sort of house it was. Ji Liangxuan, spying through a hole he has poked in the study window, presses his forehead against paper pasted up a year ago and now brittle with sun and wind, which splits with a crack; Yu Tianxian looks round, sees him, and goes; and White Chrysanthemum, returning from the gate and finding him still at the gap, has to swallow her laughter and go in with her head down. On the day Pincai leaves the capital, Tang Heshang sees him ten li out to the parting pavilion and weeps at the farewell; and Pincai collects his parents and goes with them to Hubei, after which the book has no more to say of him.`}},s={51:{zh:`魏聘才临行讨账时，李元茂正与孙嗣徽、孙嗣元冒了顺天籍到通州院考。元茂寻出旧日先生改过的窗稿直笔抄了；孙嗣徽自己不会作文，托一个廪生朋友雇了枪手，讲定八十两银子写了契约，在场内替他做了两文一诗；孙嗣元既不能作又没雇人，只在卷上一阵乱写。发案时嗣徽、元茂都进了，覆试元茂勉强得来，嗣徽仍是代笔。发落之日忽挂出一道牌：查看宛平县童生孙嗣元文卷，字体草率，一字两格，方言俗语，杂字一篇，无两字可连、无一句可讲，是否染了狂疾抑或本真，殊为可怪，着通州知州会同宛平教谕严为究问。嗣徽却暗暗欢喜，穿了蓝衫皂靴，把红糟脸擦得光亮，大摇大摆去见宗师。宗师问起他兄弟有何毛病，他随口数出三条：截巴的毛病愈急愈说不出、左眼皮高吊时时流泪、一听他说话就要驳；再问那卷子何以不成文章，他竟满口之乎者也，说舍弟"有生之初就有时而昏，有生之后就无时而明"，府县考所以有名，乃"门生中也养不中、才也养不才，此舍弟之乐有贤父兄也"——诸生哄堂大笑，宗师一拍桌案："胡说！你就是个疯子，快下去罢！"嗣元被州官提去。

恰逢家信：孙亮功放了安徽凤阳府。元茂中了秀才又已娶亲，丈人与父亲同在一省，正可携眷同去，只为嗣元之事未了，只得同候。那日饭后他独逛到运河边：粮船如云，官船大旗招展，卖西瓜、桃儿、牛肉、豆腐的挤来挤去，地下还有测字摊子，几个补衣的妇人守着筐子坐在小凳上做活。他戴上眼镜只顾东张西望，不防被一条缆子绊倒，直跌在一个补衣妇人身上，两岸的人齐拍手笑，臊得他满脸紫涨，一袜子泥，衫子又被篙子扎破。那妇人二十四五，容长脸、长眼睛，手也不粗，见他一身绸绢便揽下这桩买卖，问他是哪一帮粮船上的；他说身边没带钱，请她到寓里去取。到了寓中，孙家兄弟都不在，他数出四十大钱，那妇人嫌少来抢他的搭连，一拉一扑便滚在一处，讲定四百钱。偏偏嗣徽回来推门不开，只听元茂推说肚子疼；他脚下踢着一朵妇人戴的纸花，闻有油气，起了疑心，转到窗下——元茂忘了放卷窗——看得清清楚楚，便先到门口候着，把那妇人截住，约她明日饭后再来。

次日嗣徽把家人尽数支使出门，见元茂在炕上闭目似睡，便把梳得光光、插了花、戴着料镯铜戒的妇人一直推进自己房中闩上门。谁知元茂并未睡着，隔着门窗把一场从头听到尾：报数声、躺椅折断的响声、扇扇子声、笑声，还有嗣徽的"其乐只且"。及至讲价，那妇人开口就要两吊，说昨日那位是平等人、连衣也没有脱，今日却是位老爷又费了两三倍工夫；嗣徽一百一百地添，添了五回才凑成一吊。嗣徽还得意问她怎知自己是老爷，只当她看出什么贵相，那妇人却抢白道：泥腿上跷着皂靴还想赖作平人省钱，你若穿了草鞋我只要二百；又把昨日那位数落得一钱不值，气得元茂在门外说不出话。恰在此时嗣元回来了：他在州里押了一日，对着州官期期艾艾还挺撞了几句，州官看孙亮功面上不打他，送到宛平教谕处戒斥，他又得罪了教官，被按在明伦堂板凳上结结实实打了二十竹板，两腿紫烂，一步一攧地扶回来。

他推门不开，双手乱搡几乎把薄板门搡破；嗣徽急中生智，随手撕破一件衣裳交与那妇人缝，才开了门。嗣元一见便骂，踢翻她的篮子；那妇人说"有人请我来的，我又不是挨上门的"，理了碎布到外间去缝。嗣元往躺椅上一躺，那椅子的铁搭早断了，一侧滚下地来，嗣徽笑他"言悖而出者亦悖而入"，气得他一脚踢翻躺椅；后来手在席上摸着湿处，索性把席子扯下地，铺了马褥子大骂，那妇人也扔下衣裳骂着走了。及至嗣元要换汗衫，才发现被撕的正是自己只穿过一回的那一件，且撕的是小衿，分明有意；嗣徽引"缁衣之好兮"作答，嗣元便直戳他的短处："满口之乎者也，倒像是个通朋友，不过花了八十两请人枪来的，当是你的真本事中的了，臊也臊死人！"嗣徽反唇相讥，还拍着手数"一五、一十、十五、二十"，说"父母之体，不敢毁伤，辱莫大焉"。嗣元大怒，忍痛爬起来抄了支窗的棍子劈头打去，正中嗣徽肩胛；两人夺棍互殴，全仗元茂拉开，斗嘴斗到五更，天明才上车回家。孙亮功见长子与女婿都进了学甚喜，却痛骂嗣元出了大丑；嗣元回房又被巴来风羞辱一顿，气苦无处诉，便逢人便说他哥哥是代枪进学、又在炕上闹了补衣的妇人，才害得自己吃苦。

元茂却在媳妇面前夸自己的才学，说嗣徽是代枪、嗣元不通才挨了板子；他媳妇也觉光彩，到底丈夫算个读书人了。他看媳妇虽是假眉假发，五官却好，又高又胖是有福之相，比那妇人干净得多，久别重逢自然贪爱；偏偏他把偷听来的一段细细描摹给媳妇听，媳妇笑说"自然你也是这样的"，他一口咬定没有；不料说得高兴，竟顺口说出亲手摸过那妇人的话来，媳妇气涌心头，拧他咬他："你说我兄弟闹补衣的妇人，你是没有，为什么你又讲出来？"逼得他再三赔礼。孙亮功领凭后在通州写了四个太平船赴任，饯行热闹；独王恂的夫人见父亲哥嫂一齐出京，凄凉悲苦，在母家住了几日，陆氏夫人也只能劝慰。元茂外间的亏空追逼起来，媳妇虽有妆资却不肯与他花消，骂道"不是叫相公，就是嫖婊子，我也不给你钱，你也不许出去"；他苦苦哀求说是进学费用，才得了四十两。谁知他得了银子又起邪念：想着二喜这两年待他不薄，远别不好不给十吊钱，便把去年借聘才的金镯子从当铺取出，打算换钱补贴。谁知在街上恰遇二喜坐车过来，一把拉他上车到寓中：先说"你倒忍心撇得下我么"，窜在他怀里要跟他去，眼圈也红了；元茂只说明年还要来科考乡试。酒过之后开了烟灯，二喜讲起从前一位张少爷临行时不肯给镯子的旧事，说自己不过要个记念，教人心上常记着这个人——说着便把元茂手上的镯子捋下来看，套在自己手上，说"若是你给我，那管是铜的我也当他金的"。元茂近来身子淘虚，一喝就醉，一吹烟就睡，模模糊糊应了一声；二喜被人叫去陪酒，他醒来才知镯子已被赚去，再去找已不在家，一肚子苦说不出，只得丧气而回，回家还支吾说是还账耽搁了。次日他只好厚着脸皮向王恂借了四十金，只说是娶亲时的旧账，到安徽即行寄还，才把那些馆子账、相公开发与嫖钱一总还清，然后随丈人出京，到凤阳住了一月，才同媳妇往父亲任上去了。`,en:`Wei Pincai had come to collect his debt just as Li Yuanmao left for Tongzhou with Sun Sihui and Sun Siyuan, all three having falsely registered as Shuntian residents. Yuanmao dug out an old exercise corrected by his tutor and copied it straight through; Sun Sihui, who cannot write at all, had a stipendiary friend hire him a substitute for eighty taels under contract, who produced two essays and a poem inside the hall; and Sun Siyuan, who could neither write nor hire, simply scrawled. Sihui and Yuanmao passed, and at the re-test Yuanmao managed while Sihui again used his man. Then a notice went up about Siyuan's paper: the hand slovenly, one character sprawling over two squares, dialect and vulgarisms, a page of stray characters with no two joined and not one sentence construable — is the candidate afflicted with madness, or is this his natural state? The Tongzhou magistrate and the Wanping instructor are to investigate strictly. Sihui, secretly delighted, polished his pimpled red face and swaggered up before the examiner in blue gown and black boots. Asked what ailed his brother, he produced three items — a stammer that grows worse with haste, a drooping left eyelid that weeps, and a compulsion to contradict his elder brother; and asked why the paper was no essay at all, answered in mangled Classical tags that his brother has had his dark hours from birth and no bright ones since, and that if he passed the earlier tests it is because one who is fit nourishes the unfit, which is his brother's good fortune in having a worthy elder. The hall roared; the examiner struck the desk and told him that he was the madman and might get out. 

Siyuan was taken away by the magistrate — and at that moment a letter came appointing Sun Lianggong to Fengyang prefecture in Anhui. Yuanmao, now a licentiate and married, with his father and his father-in-law in the same province, may take his wife south, and waits only for his brother-in-law's business to be settled. One evening after dinner he wanders out to the canal: grain boats like clouds, official boats with flags, hawkers of melon, peach, beef and beancurd, fortune-telling stalls, and a row of mending-women sitting on stools beside their baskets. Spectacles on and eyes everywhere, he trips over a mooring rope and falls full upon one of them, to the applause of both banks, and gets up crimson with a stocking full of mud and his gown torn on a pole. The woman — twenty-four or five, long-faced and long-eyed, her hands not coarse — takes in his silk clothes, offers to mend the gown, and asks which grain fleet he belongs to; he says he has no money with him and asks her to come to his lodging for it. There, with the Sun brothers out, he counts out forty cash; she says it is too little and snatches at his purse; the scuffle ends on the kang, and four hundred is agreed. But Sihui comes home, finds the door barred and is told through it that Yuanmao has a stomach ache; kicking a woman's paper flower on the floor and smelling oil on it, he goes round to the window — Yuanmao having forgotten the blind — sees everything, and waits at the gate to book her for the next day. In the morning he sends every servant out on errands, satisfies himself that Yuanmao is napping, and finds her already on the threshold, hair oiled and flowered, in glass bracelets and a brass ring; and takes her into the room he shares with Siyuan. Yuanmao, wide awake, hears the whole performance through door and window — the counting, the collapse of the reclining chair, the fanning, the laughter, and Sihui's classical exclamations of delight. 

Then the haggling: she opens at two strings, arguing that yesterday's man was an ordinary fellow who did not even take off his clothes, whereas this is a gentleman and it has taken three times as long; and Sihui adds a hundred cash five separate times before she will take one string. Pleased with himself, he asks how she knew he was a gentleman, hoping she has seen some auspicious sign — and is told that a man with mud to the knee and black boots on his feet cannot pass himself off as a commoner to save money, and that in straw sandals he would have been charged two hundred; after which she disposes of yesterday's customer in terms that leave Yuanmao speechless outside the door. Just then Siyuan comes home: held a day at the magistrate's, he stammered back at the bench, was spared a beating for Sun Lianggong's sake, was sent to the Wanping instructor and offended him too, and was laid over a bench in the Hall of Illustrious Relations for twenty strokes of the bamboo, so that his legs are purple and he has had to hobble home rather than sit in a cart. He pushes at the barred door until the thin panel nearly gives; Sihui, thinking fast, tears a garment and hands it to the woman to mend before opening. Siyuan sees her, curses her, and kicks over her basket; she retorts that she was invited and did not force her way in, gathers her scraps and moves outside to sew. Siyuan throws himself into the reclining chair, whose broken hinge tips him onto the floor, and Sihui quotes at him that what goes out crookedly comes back crooked; he kicks the chair over, and later, finding a wet patch on the mat, tears it off the kang, has a saddle-cloth laid instead, and curses foully, until the woman flings down the garment and goes off scolding. 

But when Siyuan wants a clean shirt, the garment being mended proves to be his own, worn once, and torn at the lapel — plainly on purpose. Sihui quotes the Songs about a black robe worn out and made again; and Siyuan hits the mark: for all his Classical airs he passed on eighty taels' worth of another man's brush, and does he suppose it was his own ability? Sihui answers with a tag on what separates men from beasts, and claps out the count — five, ten, fifteen, twenty — observing that the body received from one's parents must not be injured, and that there is no greater disgrace. Siyuan drags himself up in spite of his legs, seizes a window prop and strikes at his brother's head, catching him on the shoulder; they wrestle for the stick until Yuanmao parts them, and they wrangle on till the fifth watch. At home Sun Lianggong is pleased that his eldest son and his son-in-law have passed and furious at Siyuan's public disgrace, which he curses him for; Ba Laifeng adds her own humiliation; and Siyuan, with no other outlet, tells everyone he meets that his brother passed by proxy and had a mending-woman on his kang, which is what brought the bad luck on him. Yuanmao meanwhile boasts to his wife of his own scholarship and his brothers-in-law's disgraces, and she is gratified that her husband may at last be called a reading man. Looking at her — false brows and false hair, but good features, tall and plump and lucky-looking, and far cleaner than the woman by the canal — he is affectionate after twenty days apart; and then, in his folly, retails the whole overheard scene to her. She laughs and says he doubtless did the same; he denies it; and then, carried away, lets slip a comparison that could only come from having touched the woman himself. 

She pinches and bites him — you say your brother did it and you did not, then how do you come to know? — and he has to apologize repeatedly. When Sun Lianggong takes up his post he hires four boats at Tongzhou amid the usual farewells; only Wang Xun's wife, seeing father, brother and sister-in-law all leave the capital at once, is desolate and stays some days at her mother's house, where Madam Lu can only comfort her. Yuanmao's outside debts are now pressed hard, and his wife, though she has a dowry, will not let him touch it: it is either actors or whores, she says, and he shall have no money and shall not go out. Only when he pleads that these are examination expenses does she give him forty taels and tell him to pay real debts and not squander it. Having the silver, he at once conceives a further plan: Er Xi has been good to him these two years and cannot be left without ten strings at parting, so he redeems the gold bracelet borrowed from Pincai last year, meaning to sell it at the current rate for the difference. Walking home with it on his wrist he runs straight into Er Xi, who pulls him into his carriage and home. There Er Xi asks how he can bear to leave, climbs into his lap offering to go with him, and reddens about the eyes, while Yuanmao promises to return next year for the examinations. After wine, and the opium lamp, Er Xi tells the tale of a certain young Master Zhang who would give him anything until, at parting, he was asked for a bracelet and found excuses — adding that all he wants is a keepsake to keep a person in mind. Then he slips the bracelet off Yuanmao's wrist to look at it, puts it on his own, and says that a bracelet from anyone else he would forget, but one from Yuanmao he would treasure though it were brass. Yuanmao, whose constitution is now such that one cup makes him drunk and one pipe puts him to sleep, mumbles something; Er Xi is called away to pour wine at a party next door; and Yuanmao wakes to find him gone, goes home, discovers the loss, returns and cannot find him, and comes back with a grievance he cannot even utter, telling his wife only that settling accounts detained him. Next day he brazenly borrows forty taels from Wang Xun, calling it a wedding debt to be repaid from Anhui, clears his restaurant bills, his actors' fees and his brothel debts, takes leave all round, and goes out of the capital with his father-in-law to Fengyang, where after a month he takes his wife on to his own father's post.`},52:{zh:`七月里高品带着本省咨送的文书到京，保荐应考博学宏词，与史南湘同寓怡园；四方名宿纷至，考期定在八月初十。田春航的吉期也到了。苏侯家阔，长女嫁华光宿时妆奁值百万；今知春航寒素，便批了京东田庄二百顷，拨庄头家人丫鬟，珠宝古玩铺垫器皿不计其数，送奁那日用了两千名人夫，苏夫人还嫌薄，铺箱时又添了两万两白银、三千两黄金。徐子云是媒人，见春航房屋窄小铺张不下，把自己宅东一所带花园的空房借他，又请张仲雨来管账房、指点铺设，调度极有章程；春航的母舅张桐孙奉差进京也来帮忙。初六日两处戏酒：聚星堂由张桐孙陪着请座师王文辉、杨方猷并周锡爵、陆宗沅看联珠班，春航则在花园陪萧次贤、高品、史南湘、颜仲清、刘文泽、王恂、梅子玉看联锦班。子玉病才好，勉强出来，见八个名旦独少杜琴言，一人向隅，众人也都会意。忽不见了高品，寻到戏房后头，原来正教王兰保的戏。点戏时子云点《乔醋》，高品点《当巾》——正是郑元和落魄的故事。春航是聪明人，早知是奚落他，抢先辩解说李亚仙真是女中豪杰，若我作了郑元和，宁当身上衣衫也不当此巾；高品便说只怕衣裳有了泥当不得。

及至戏中兰保跌了一交，索性改口："被他一路赶来，跌了一身泥垢。且喜七叔赠我这件衣衫，我且去当了。"众人先骇异后哄堂，又听他唱《玉抱肚》"忍羞惭要乞青眸顾，应怜辱在泥涂"，春航满面通红，骂高品别过一年还是这副歪心肝，高品只说"这不是自己栽了筋斗埋怨地皮么"。正闹着妆奁到了，二千抬夫塞满街道如同出兵；张仲雨跑前跑后，苏蕙芳发赏封上号簿忙不过来，又叫兰保、陆素兰来帮，闹了两三个时辰；里头许三姐同陪房的摆设收拾，腰酸脚疼。送奁亲戚中就有华光宿，绣衣金带、玉貌如仙，春航初见。子云邀他进园，他一眼看见座中最年轻的子玉，如月光珠彩、骨重神清，心里一惊；子玉也暗赞他清华贵重。他坚不肯坐首席，只依齿序坐在子玉之上，心里把春航比子玉，觉得容貌稍逊而神情洒脱过之，可称瑜亮并生。

张仲雨被请入席，只说外面还有一个媚香照应不来，指着子云笑说"总是你好作成"，拱手走开。八旦上来独不见蕙芳，华光宿便问"怎么不见那位状元夫人"，子云笑说如今闹出两位状元夫人，倒与《燕子笺》上的《诰圆》一样，他自觉失言便不再问。他称赏陆素兰，说去年题在《良宵风月图》上的诗已裱成手卷，又说此君风韵不减袁、苏。随即向子玉发问，是全回关节：魏聘才在他处住了半年常提及子玉，又闻子玉与琴言订交最密、矢志不渝，如今琴言随业师出京，"阁下何以忍心割爱，而琴言又何以掉臂游行"？子玉顿口无言、面有愧色。子云代答得极透：庾香长于情而拙于言，正是用情深处——欲成全这个人，才叫他随令业师去，如今认作义子，已如平地而履青云；琴言也明白，"成身以报知己，岂不胜于轻身以事知己"。华光宿点头叹息，子玉方安。

少顷他取过素兰的扇子，一面精画，一面小楷题着《断肠词》，说"肠何可以轻断"，低吟一遍问是谁作的；素兰指着子玉说是梅少爷送玉侬的，他折扇道"看时就有几分猜着是吾兄手笔，非至情人不能道"，又笑问"这梦魂到底唤得来唤不来"，问得子玉无从回答。林珊枝来请，他辞去；素兰追出讨扇，他不忍释手，笑说吉日拿把《断肠词》的扇子岂不犯忌，另取一柄相赠：一面画红白桃花两枝，一面小楷如美女簪花，是两首《梁州序》，后注和《桃花扇》曲。春航说这楷书是闺阁笔迹，子玉说词也是闺阁口气，子云疑是华夫人所题，仲清却说若真是他夫人写的怎肯给人。那边聚星堂一个名旦也不来，王文辉便把蕙芳从账房叫出来歇歇，问他日后打算。蕙芳说出主意：开一个古董书画铺，兼卖绸缎纸张花绣香粉花木，货到苏杭去置办；房子现成——度香有所空房近着住宅，内中还有小花圃；几个相好都愿入股，不想发财，只借此安身，省得四方离散。王文辉要入一分兼作掌柜，蕙芳笑说请不起，只求他把不要的古董借几件摆摆；席间又打趣他的胡子怎么倒黑起来了，他用扇子打了两下，骂他尖酸刻薄，"怪不得田湘帆被你管得服服贴贴——但你也只有今天一天了，明日就有个真状元夫人来"，说得蕙芳脸一红。次日亲迎，笙歌鼎沸；拜过花烛，春航一见新人果如子云所言，真像蕙芳。第三日是内眷的场面：苏夫人请了长女苏浣香与袁绮香来陪新，田太夫人请了王文辉的陆氏夫人带着蓉华与佩秋，刘家来的是吴紫烟。华夫人清早见公子换了扇子，取来读那首词，一评便中："若送朋友，怎么有这『只道今生长厮守』？说是夫妇离别又不像，说是赠妓的也不甚像；然而语至情真，却有可取。"公子赞她好眼力，说破是梅子玉送琴言的，"可不是夫妇不像夫妇，朋友不像朋友，妓又不像妓么"。

夫人随口讨还自己写的那把扇子，叮嘱不要给人瞧——公子这才想起早送了素兰，只得推说怕扇旧了已经收起。她带六个珠字婢，前有顶马、后随八辆大鞍车，流水一般出城。田太夫人迎下阶来，铺红毡行礼；新人拜见婆婆与众夫人，苏家陪房十七八人连同许三姐都来叩头，挤满三间花厅。让席时袁绮香让华夫人坐首席，两人以盟姊妹相推，袁绮香索性两边都不坐，经田太夫人再劝，华夫人才坐了。田太夫人看新人天姿国色，再看华夫人竟分不出次第，又觉眼中很熟，想来原来有些像苏蕙芳。陆氏夫人一段话最有声色：先奉承田太夫人是王母下凡带了一群仙子，转而诉苦——外人看我像个有福气的，殊不知一家就我一个人操心，我们老爷是不管家务的；儿子女婿都还未中，都怨他去年偏作了主考，我早劝他告病躲过，他倒说收几个好门生也与儿子女婿中了一样，"你看如今是一样吗"；八月里又有百年难遇的博学宏词，若再派他作主考，那就是坑死人了。她卷起大袖、金钏叮当，精明爽辣，袁绮香笑说两位夫人正是敌体。底下八桌是各家丫鬟仆妇，全靠许三姐八面张罗。听过几出戏、放过赏，太太奶奶们都到新房中坐，华夫人与妹子说了好一会话才告辞，袁绮香要留她逛园，她只说晚了改日再来。`,en:`In the seventh month Gao Pin reaches the capital with his provincial governor's papers recommending him for the special examination, and lodges with Shi Nanxiang at the Cool Realm of Poetry in Yiyuan; scholars are arriving from every quarter, and the examination is fixed for the tenth of the eighth month. Tian Chunhang's wedding day arrives too. Marquis Su is a great house — his elder daughter's dowry to Hua Guangsu was worth a million — and knowing Chunhang poor he now assigns two hundred qing of land east of the capital, with bailiffs, servants, maids, and jewels, curios, furnishings and vessels beyond counting; two thousand porters carry it on the day, and Madam Su, thinking it thin, adds twenty thousand taels of silver and three thousand of gold laid into the chests. Xu Ziyun, the go-between, finding Chunhang's house too small to lay it all out, lends the empty rooms and small garden east of his own residence, and brings in Zhang Zhongyu — who has a genius for exactly this — to run the counting-house and direct the arrangements, while Chunhang's uncle Zhang Tongsun, in the capital on official business, helps as well. On the sixth there are two entertainments: at Juxing Hall, under Zhang Tongsun's care, his examiners Wang Wenhui and Yang Fangyou with Zhou Xijue and Lu Zongyuan watch the Lianzhu troupe; while in the garden Chunhang keeps Xiao Cixian, Gao Pin, Shi Nanxiang, Yan Zhongqing, Liu Wenze, Wang Xun and Mei Ziyu with the Lianjin troupe. Ziyu, just recovered, has forced himself out, and seeing eight of the famous performers with only Du Qinyan missing, is stricken with a solitary grief that everyone understands. Gao Pin disappears and is found backstage coaching Wang Lanbao; and when plays are chosen, Ziyun takes *Feigning Jealousy* and Gao Pin *Pawned Headscarf* — the scene of Zheng Yuanhe in his beggary. Chunhang, quick enough to see the mockery, defends himself first: Li Yaxian was a heroine among women, and were he Zheng Yuanhe he would pawn the coat off his back before that headscarf. Gao Pin objects that the coat may be too muddy to pawn. Then, when Lanbao takes a fall onstage, he alters his speech on the spot: chased all this way and covered in mud — happily my seventh uncle gave me this coat, and I shall pawn it and hear two more days of the play. 

The room stares, works it out, and roars; and Lanbao goes on into the *Yu Bao Du* aria about swallowing shame to beg one kind glance and pitying himself disgraced in the mire. Chunhang, scarlet, tells Gao Pin that a year away has not straightened his crooked heart, and Gao Pin answers that this is turning a somersault oneself and then blaming the ground. The dowry arrives with music, and among the Su relatives escorting it is Hua Guangsu, in embroidered robe and gold belt, whom Chunhang meets for the first time. Two thousand porters fill the street like an army on the march; Zhang Zhongyu runs about directing; Su Huifang, giving out reward packets and keeping the register, cannot manage alone and calls in Lanbao and Lu Sulan; inside, Third Sister works with the dowry servants until her waist aches and her feet hurt. Ziyun brings Hua Guangsu into the garden; catching sight of the youngest of the company — moonlight and pearl-radiance, heavy of bone and clear of spirit — he starts, thinking he has never seen such a person; and Ziyu privately admires his distinction in turn. He refuses the head seat, saying that having met they are as old friends and will sit by age; being three years older than Ziyu he takes the place above him, and privately measures Chunhang against Ziyu — a shade behind in looks and ahead in freedom of spirit, two rivals born in one age. When Zhang Zhongyu is pressed to sit down he answers that there is only one Meixiang outside and he must not be left to resent him, and goes off pointing at Ziyun: this is all your doing. When the eight performers come up without Huifang, he asks Chunhang where the zhuangyuan's lady is — still in the counting-house? Chunhang cannot answer, and Ziyun turns it off with a joke about there being two such ladies now, as in the *Circular Edict* scene of *The Swallow Letter*; and Hua, aware of his slip, lets it drop. 

He praises Lu Sulan, whose poem on *Fine Night, Wind and Moon* he has had mounted as a scroll, and tells Ziyun that this one's charm is not less than Yuan's or Su's. Then he turns to Ziyu with the question that is the chapter's hinge: Wei Pincai lived with him half a year and often spoke of him; he has heard that Ziyu and Qinyan were bound past changing, and saw something of it while Qinyan was in his house — so how could Ziyu bear to give him up, and how could Qinyan walk away without a backward look? Ziyu is struck dumb, ashamed and inwardly wretched, until Ziyun rescues him with an answer that settles the matter: Gengxiang is strong in feeling and weak in speech, and this is precisely where his feeling runs deepest — wishing to make a man of him, he sent him away with the old teacher, and now, adopted as a son, he has stepped from level ground into the blue clouds; and Qinyan understood it too, for to establish oneself in repayment of a friend who truly knows one is surely better than throwing away one's life in his service. Hua nods and sighs, and Ziyu's mind is eased. Presently Hua takes up Sulan's fan, admires the painting on one face, and on the other finds small regular script headed *Heartbreak Lyrics* — how, he says, can the bowels be broken so lightly? He asks whose it is, and Sulan points at Ziyu and says it is what Master Mei sent Yunong. Folding the fan, Hua answers that reading it he had half guessed the hand, since none but a man of utmost feeling could have written so — and asks, laughing, whether that dream-soul can really be called or not, leaving Ziyu without a reply. Lin Shanzhi comes for him, and when Sulan follows out to reclaim the fan he cannot bear to give it up, tells him with a smile that to bring out a heartbreak fan on such a lucky day is surely unlucky, and hands him one of his own instead. 

Opened, it shows two sprays of peach, red and white, on one side, and on the other, in small regular script like a beauty pinning flowers, two lyrics to *Liangzhou Xu* noted as written on viewing peach blossoms and matching the airs of *The Peach Blossom Fan*. Chunhang says the hand is a woman's; Ziyu says the lyric has a woman's voice as well; Ziyun suspects Madam Hua wrote it; and Zhongqing objects that if his wife had written it he would never have given it away. At Juxing Hall, meanwhile, not one of the famous performers has appeared, and Wang Wenhui has Huifang called out of the counting-house to rest. Asked what he means to do with himself, Huifang lays out his plan: a shop for curios, paintings and calligraphy, selling silks, paper, embroidery, cosmetics and plants besides, the stock to be bought at Suzhou and Hangzhou; the premises are ready, since Duxiang has an empty house near his own with a small flower-garden in it; and several friends will come in, not hoping to grow rich but to be settled together instead of scattered. Wang offers to take a share and be manager, and is told that he cannot be afforded — but he might lend a few unwanted curios for the shelves. Then Huifang asks how Wang Wenhui's beard has turned black again, and Wang taps his shoulder with a fan and calls him a sharp-tongued devil, adding that it is no wonder Tian Xiangfan is kept so obedient — but that he has only today, since tomorrow a real zhuangyuan's lady arrives. On the morrow the bride is fetched with music and a press of carriages, and when the candles are lit Chunhang finds that she is indeed the image of Huifang, exactly as Ziyun promised. The third day belongs to the women. Madam Su sends her elder daughter Su Huanxiang with Yuan Qixiang to keep the bride company; Lady Tian invites Wang Wenhui's Madam Lu with Ronghua and Peiqiu, and from the Liu house Wu Ziyan. Dressing that morning, Madam Hua notices that her husband's fan is not the one written the other day, reads the lyric on it, and judges it exactly: not written to a friend — how else the line about believing they would be together for life? Nor is it a husband's parting from a wife, nor quite a gift to a courtesan; and yet the words are true feeling and have merit. Praising her eye, Hua tells her it was written by Mei Ziyu to Qinyan — not husband and wife, not friend, not courtesan, and yet written to the undoing of the soul. 

She then asks for her own fan back and tells him not to show it to anyone, at which he remembers with a start that he has given it to Sulan, and says he has put it away lest it wear out. She sets off with six of the pearl-named maids, an old steward on the lead horse and eight carriages behind, flowing out of the city like water. Lady Tian comes down the steps to meet her; red felt is spread for the formal bow; the bride salutes her mother-in-law and the guests; and the seventeen or eighteen Su dowry servants, with Third Sister among them, kowtow until the three-bay hall is packed. Over the seating there is a gracious contest: Yuan Qixiang offers Madam Hua the head place, the two sworn sisters press it on each other until Yuan Qixiang declines both head seats outright, and only after Lady Tian urges again is Madam Hua seated. Lady Tian, studying the bride, finds her a beauty out of heaven, and Madam Hua her exact twin, and cannot tell them apart — and puzzles over why both faces seem familiar until she works out that they resemble Su Huifang. Madam Lu supplies the day's best talk: she begins by flattering Lady Tian as the Queen Mother come down with a train of immortals, then pours out her grievance — outsiders think her fortunate, but she alone does the worrying, since her husband takes no interest in the household; her son and sons-in-law are well enough but none has yet passed, and it is all her husband's fault for consenting to be an examiner last year, when she begged him to take sick leave and he insisted that good pupils were as good as a son passing. Is it the same? Had he listened, three men went in, and would not one have come out? And now, with a chance in a hundred years coming in the eighth month, if he is made an examiner again it will be the ruin of them all. With her sleeves rolled back and her gold bracelets ringing she is so brisk and sharp that Yuan Qixiang laughs that the two great ladies are evenly matched deities. Below, eight tables hold the households' maids and servant-women, with Third Sister flying about attending to all eight. After the plays and the rewards the ladies gather in the bridal chamber; Madam Hua talks long with her sister, declines Yuan Qixiang's invitation to see the garden as it is growing late, and takes her leave.`},53:{zh:`华光宿被夫人问起扇子，才想起把她画的桃花扇给了陆素兰，甚是懊悔，便遣人进城叫他带扇来见。素兰正在苏蕙芳处与袁宝珠、金漱芳、王兰保、李玉林商议开铺的事：蕙芳嫌几人凑来的古玩有限、好书名画更少，若只靠花木便成了花局子；宝珠却说不难——徐子云园中晒书时一种有十几部，法帖重的更多，似真似假的画有几十箱，横竖将来饱蠹鱼，分些来他岂有不肯；短的只是香料针黹顾绣一类新鲜东西，房子器皿现成，中秋前后便可开张。正议着华府来叫，素兰便进城。华公子正在画扇——仍是两枝红白桃花；素兰赞是"兼工带写，得意得神"。公子讨回旧扇，又要他就着画上桃花填一阕《梁州序》，笑他"这样灵慧人怕不是绣口锦心"，便命取果品与莲心酒来。席间公子问出一句诛心的话：你们这班人当初何必学戏？既学了戏，又不专于戏，学成这许多本事——从前戏旦中并没有你们这一派。素兰答得极有分寸：我们这点本事算得什么，只因是我们这等人不应会的，写几个字画几笔画，人便另眼相待，先把个"好"字放在心里；若将我们的笔墨换了别人的名氏，只怕尽是笑不好的了。公子说这话也有理，又说这碗饭终非久计。素兰便说出九人合开古董书画兼绸缎香粉铺的打算，货要到苏杭去置办，其实不当买卖作，不过几个人有个坐处的意思。

公子嫌兼卖绸缎香粉未免俗了，素兰说不如此不热闹；公子笑说将来他来买把扇子，只怕也得开个虚价儿，随即许以库房中老太太遗下的几箱刻丝顾绣与中等的古董书画——素兰谢道"府上中等的，就是外头上等的了"。饮酒之间又同去看了屋后竹林中一块八尺多高、浑身是穴的怪石。素兰逛了一转回来，写出所填之词："春光早去，秋光又遍，一片闲情空恋……恨随流水，人想当时，何处重相见？韶华在眼轻消遣，过后思量总可怜。休负了，金樽浅。"公子狂叫称绝，连吟数遍，忽然把素兰看了一会，凄然叹息，追问词中寓意；素兰推说无非题秋日之桃花，公子却一句句点破："这明明是由后思前，翻悔从前轻看春光之意；但凭你怎样惜春，而春不肯留，又将如何？"末了索性说穿："你明明将琴言借题发挥感讽我，但究竟是他负我，非我负他。我如今一想，在我这里也终非了局，如今他倒好了。"素兰只得说：公子待琴言原是没有说的，只是琴言用情专一、不善变通，倘他一进京便遇公子受这番恩典，竟可以杀身相报、至死不怨。公子又问他与梅子玉究竟是怎样交情，素兰说其实不甚亲密，只是两心相照、悲多欢少，一见就哭，人人解不出来，大约前世有些因果。公子索性设问："你若作了琴言，待子玉怎样，在我这里又当怎样？事齐乎，事楚乎？"问得素兰面泛桃花不语；公子说难道一人只许有一个知己？素兰这才说出一番极通透的话：论知己自然越多越好，蕙芳之与田春航、宝珠之与金吉甫，皆是报知己之报，事虽不同情则一也；然而他们待外人也是一样，心里有权衡而外面无轩轾，所以人不能说、也不能妒。

独琴言之心没有一点曲折，这样就是这样，那样就是那样，所谓孤忠苦节，不避艰险、不顾利害，其实也正是他的好处。公子点头自认"我毕竟不是他的知己"，又问徐子云何以算得；素兰说度香第一能包容、第二能体贴，琴言或冷或热、或笑或哭、时时挺撞，他非但不芥蒂，反过意不去、百般安慰。公子叹道："这么看起来，我还不如度香；这也是各人的性情，勉强不来的。"又问漱芳，答以"和而不同"；再问林珊枝，且不许恭惟——素兰暗想实说要闹出乱子，只得赞他忠直正派、知恩报恩，公子大笑说这是违心之论，仍说取他见事明白、说话透彻，只是肚里不通，教他念诗学字总弄不上来。天晚公子留他住下，为的是连夜翻出那几箱刻丝顾绣好由他挑选；素兰便宿在珊枝房中，珊枝听说他在公子面前替自己说好话，欢喜得与他谈了半夜，还要换帖结盟。公子把和词拿给夫人看，夫人赞好，说库房六箱花绣七分皆是顶好材料，如今新的还不及他。次日挑出两箱裙料褂料与荷囊扇袋的花片，估价值数千金；公子又照单点出砚墨图章、铜炉磁瓶、法帖名扇、纸笔香料、玉石烟壶如意并宋元名款的赝笔字画数十轴，由珊枝开单装箱。素兰喜出望外，竟一下有了半个古董铺。回来后蕙芳掌烛开箱细看，说这些东西若全买起来也要好几千银子，再到度香处添几样便可开张；随即定下铺名为"九香楼"，众人都赞素兰能干、赞他的词。

宝珠到子云处一说，子云也点出洋灯洋表、纱缎湖绉、真赝铜器、宝石玉器、香料丸散、扇料对联磁碗等数十项，花木随时搬出不入数内。田春航听说了也要送蕙芳东西，只因苏小姐过门未久恐她疑心吃醋，便托辞要了二百两赤金送去；蕙芳本不肯受，一则怕春航过不去，二则见宝珠、素兰都得了许多，好胜之心也起，只得收了，托子云写信寄到其兄署中，叫管总的徐福亲往苏杭置办。一日子云正与萧次贤、史南湘、高品闲话，书童捧进一包书信——是屈道翁自南京途中寄来的，十几封信都是琴言笔迹，因道翁跌坏了膀子不能写；另有怀念怡园同人的诗篇。琴言又另有三封：一致诸名士，一致众弟兄，一是"庾香才子手启"。众人展开一看，竟是和子玉的《金缕曲》："岂料真如此。只朝朝、泪珠盈把，袖痕凝紫……纵生离、只身还在，自应胜死……自古多情成积恨，恨东流、不接西流水。肠断矣！写此纸。"高品诧异他的学问几时长的，次贤说不过受乃翁陶熔几天便有这些进境，南湘原以为子玉那首不能和，此时也说非玉侬不能。词后另有一纸，记六月初九日阻风燕子矶，见铁索练着孤舟，俗称是陈妙常妆楼下，正是秋江送别之处，回想从前置身优孟曾演此事，不料今日竟履其地，触目伤心、愁多于水；末了只劝子玉勉力加餐、以侍晨昏，夏秋多厉、千万珍重。

子云要请子玉来看，次贤说不可——在这里看了必有一番痛哭，反教他难为情，不如送去让他哭个尽性。这边子玉自在春航处见了诸名旦独少琴言，已感伤数日；一夜梦中云儿来报琴言回来了，他奔出门去只见烟水茫茫、白浪滔天。一叶小艇荡来，荡桨的正是琴言：他埋怨一月之别令人想死，说把那首词烧了灰吞在肚里，变了一肚子眼泪哭也哭不出来。琴言把手搭在他膝上，说"我如今恨你，我作了东流水，你作了西流水，接不到一处来"，随即袅袅婷婷坐到他怀里——子玉要扶他起来，却已不是琴言，变作一个十七八岁的女郎，高鬟滴翠、芬芳竟体，低低说着"良宵风月，千里姻缘；妾家不远，长板桥头，青楼第二门便是"。子玉大骇欲起而两手无力，那女郎的话却步步逼人："你拳拳于杜玉侬，非为色耶？男女相悦，天经地义，君何以胶柱之性作刻舟之想？若以貌论，你看杜玉侬及我么？他如今泪眼将枯、面黄于蜡，劝你不必假惺惺，弃了他罢。"忽有一船拢来，窗窗相对，琴言正在舱里低吟那阕《金缕曲》，凄惋欲泣；子玉喊"玉侬救我"，那女郎大怒推开他而去。跨到那船上一看，琴言果然泪眼将枯、掩面悲啼，反怪他不该老远来："这一派长江，太太心上不惦记你么？"说着把和词递来，子玉接过一看却不是词，正是当日他进华府时寄来的那方罗帕，血泪斑斑可数。子玉问他何以孤身在此、义父何在，琴言长叹泪下，半晌才道："我也为要见你一面；不然，这个地方就是我葬身之地了。"话未了，后舱走出一人，竟是他父亲梅士燮，满面怒容大喝"无耻的东西，在家作得好事，如今又背了你母亲跑出来"；子玉一惊要跳过船去，一脚踏空，扑通落入江中——挣出一身冷汗，原来是梦，唯有虫声唧唧、月照纱窗。次日子云处送来琴言的和词，子玉一恸欲绝，把信与词足足念了百余遍，又喜他学问大进竟成名作，便缝了一个古锦囊盛着，佩在身上。`,en:`Questioned by his wife about the fan, Hua Guangsu remembers with dismay that he gave away the one she painted with peach blossom, and sends into the city for Lu Sulan to bring it back. Sulan is at Su Huifang's, planning the shop with Yuan Baozhu, Jin Shufang, Wang Lanbao and Li Yulin. Huifang doubts that what the few of them can pool will look like anything — few good curios, fewer rare books or famous paintings, and if they fall back on plants it becomes a flower stall. Baozhu answers that it is easy enough: when Xu Ziyun's books were aired there were a dozen copies of some titles, duplicate rubbings past counting and dozens of chests of paintings of doubtful authorship, all destined for the bookworms — he would surely give some away. What is short is incense, needlework and Gu embroidery, which must be new; the house and its furnishings are ready, and they might open at Mid-Autumn. Then the summons comes, and Sulan drives into the city, and finds Hua painting a fan — again two sprays of red and white peach — which he praises as half careful and half free, spirited and inspired. Hua reclaims the old fan and asks him to compose a lyric to *Liangzhou Xu* on the painted blossoms; Sulan protests, and Hua, laughing that so quick a mind must have brocade in its mouth, calls for fruit and lotus-heart wine. Over it he puts a searching question: why did people like them ever learn to act, and having learned, why not stay with acting instead of acquiring so many arts? There were never such performers before. Sulan's answer is finely judged: their skills are nothing in themselves, but because men of their sort are not supposed to be able to do such things, a few characters or brush-strokes earn them a special regard, with the word "good" already in the beholder's mind — whereas if their work bore another man's name it would be laughed at rather than praised. Hua grants some truth in it, and adds that their present livelihood cannot last. 

So Sulan tells him of the shop: nine of them together, curios and paintings with silks, incense and cosmetics besides, the stock to be bought at Suzhou and Hangzhou — less a business, he says, than a place for friends to sit. Hua thinks the silks and cosmetics vulgar; Sulan answers that without them it would be dull; and Hua laughs that when he comes to buy a fan he will doubtless be quoted an inflated price, then makes his gift: several chests of kesi and Gu embroidery in the storehouse, left by his great-grandmother and untouched for seventy or eighty years, with middling curios and paintings besides — at which Sulan bows and says that his mansion's middling is the world's best. Between cups they go out to see a strange stone above eight feet high and riddled with holes in the bamboo behind the house. Coming back from a turn about the garden, Sulan writes out his lyric: spring gone early and autumn everywhere, an idle longing loved in vain; white silk bearing the painted red and white; regret running with the water, thinking of that time, and where they might meet again; the season's brightness lightly spent before the eyes, and pitiable in the thinking over. Hua cries out that it is a masterpiece, chants it four or five times, then looks at him a long while, sighs, and asks what it means. Sulan pleads that it is only a lyric on autumn peaches; Hua reads it back to him line by line — plainly a looking-back and a repentance for having held spring cheap, and however one cherishes spring, spring will not stay — and at last says it outright: you are using Qinyan to reproach me. And yet it was he who failed me, not I him; thinking it over, my house could never have been his ending, and he is better off now. Sulan can only answer that the young lord's treatment of Qinyan was beyond criticism, but that Qinyan's feeling runs in one channel and cannot be turned; had he met the young lord on first coming to the capital and received such kindness, he could have died to repay him and never complained. Asked what there was between Qinyan and Mei Ziyu, Sulan says it was never even very intimate — only two hearts answering each other, with more grief in it than joy; they wept at first sight, so there must be some karma behind it that nobody can explain. Then Hua sets him a test: suppose you were Qinyan — how would you use Ziyu, and how would you behave in this house? Serve Qi or serve Chu? You must have a view. 

Sulan colours and is silent, and Hua presses that they are all above board — may a man have one true friend and not two? — and gets an answer that is the chapter's ethical centre. Of true friends, says Sulan, the more the better: Huifang has Tian Chunhang and Baozhu has Jin Jifu, and what they give is the repayment of being known; the cases differ, the feeling is one. But they treat outsiders the same way, keeping the scales inside and showing no preference outside, so that nobody can accuse or envy them. Qinyan's heart alone has no bend in it — this is this and that is that; a lonely loyalty and a bitter integrity that avoids neither hardship nor harm, and that is in truth his virtue. Hua concedes that he was never Qinyan's true friend, and asks why Duxiang was: because, says Sulan, he can contain and he can enter into another's feeling — Qinyan was cold and warm, laughing and weeping and contradictory by turns, and Duxiang, so far from resenting it, felt himself in the wrong and comforted him. So I am not Duxiang's equal, says Hua; and that is temperament, which cannot be forced. Of Shufang, Sulan says he is harmonious without conforming. Then Hua asks his honest opinion of Lin Shanzhi, forbidding flattery — and Sulan, calculating that honesty here would breed disaster, praises him as loyal, upright and grateful; at which Hua laughs that this is against his heart, and gives his own judgment: a man clear about affairs and penetrating in speech, which is why he likes him — only he is unlettered, and though Hua has tried to teach him poems and calligraphy nothing would take. At dusk Hua keeps him overnight, so that the embroidery chests may be brought out and he may choose in the morning, and Sulan takes a bed in Shanzhi's room — where Shanzhi, delighted to hear that he spoke well of him to the young lord, talks half the night and asks to exchange sworn-brother cards. Hua carries the lyric in to his wife, who praises it, and says of the six chests that seven parts are of material better than anything made now. 

Next day two chests are filled — skirt and jacket lengths, pillow mats, table covers, and pieces for purses and fan cases, worth some thousands of taels — and Hua marks off dozens of unused things besides: inkstones, ink and seal stones, bronze censers and porcelain vases, model calligraphy and famous fans, paper, brushes and incense, jade snuff bottles and ruyi, and dozens of scrolls of pseudo-Song and Yuan painting, which Shanzhi packs and inventories. Sulan goes home with half a curio shop. Opening the chests by candlelight, Huifang reckons that to buy such goods would cost thousands and that the material could hardly be matched; a few more things from Duxiang, he says, and they may open — and he names the shop the Nine Fragrance Tower. Ziyun adds his own list of glass and bead lanterns, foreign woollens, gauzes and satins, clocks and watches, genuine and imitation bronzes, gems and jades, aloeswood and sandalwood and other incenses and medicines, bamboo fan ribs, ironwood couplet boards and gilt porcelain — with plants to be sent as wanted and not counted in. Tian Chunhang wants to contribute too, but with Miss Su so newly in the house he fears her jealousy if Huifang's affairs are raised, and so sends two hundred taels of pure gold on a pretext; Huifang would refuse, and accepts partly to spare Chunhang's feelings and partly because Baozhu and Sulan have received so much and he is competitive — and Ziyun sends an order to his elder brother's yamen so that the chief steward may buy at Suzhou and Hangzhou in person. Then a packet arrives from Qu Daoweng on the road at Nanjing: a dozen letters to friends, all in Qinyan's hand since the old man has injured his arm, with poems remembering the Yiyuan company; and three letters of Qinyan's own, one to the scholars, one to his sworn brothers, and one addressed to Gengxiang. This last is a matching lyric to Ziyu's *Jinlü Qu* — that he had not thought it would truly come to this; that morning after morning his sleeve is stained with tears; that though they live apart he is still alive, which must be better than dying; that he will force himself to eat against a later meeting; and that since ancient times deep feeling has bred accumulated regret, the eastward water never joining the westward. Gao Pin marvels at how his learning has grown in a year; Cixian says a few days under his adoptive father have done it, and asks what two years might do; and Nanxiang, who had thought Ziyu's lyric unanswerable, says that only Yunong could have answered it. A note follows: on the ninth of the sixth month, wind-bound at Yanzi Cliff, he saw an iron chain mooring a solitary boat below what is called Chen Miaochang's dressing-tower, the very place of the autumn-river parting — and remembering that in his stage life he had once acted that story and now stands on its ground, his sorrow is deeper than the water; and he ends by urging Ziyu only to eat well and serve his mother, and to take care in a sickly season. Ziyun would send for Ziyu; Cixian says no — he would have to weep before them and be shamed — and the letters are sent to him instead. 

Ziyu, who has been grieving since he saw every performer at Chunhang's but Qinyan, has meanwhile dreamed. Yun'er announces that Qinyan has come back; he runs out to find only mist and water, and walks the river bank among white waves. A small boat comes, and the rower is Qinyan, who reproaches him — a month's parting has nearly killed him, he burned the lyric and swallowed the ashes and they turned into a bellyful of tears he cannot weep out. Then Qinyan, resting a hand on his knee, says that he has become the eastward water and Ziyu the westward and they cannot meet; and rises and sits in his lap — and is no longer Qinyan but a girl of seventeen or eighteen, hair high and scented, murmuring about a fine night and a marriage destiny and a house at the second door by Changban Bridge. Ziyu, appalled, has no strength to rise, and her argument follows him step by step: your devotion to Du Yunong — is that not for beauty? Man and woman delighting in each other is the constant law of heaven; why so glued-peg a nature, so notched-boat a mind? And if it is looks, look at him and look at me — his eyes are wept dry, his face is yellow as wax; do not play the hypocrite, but give him up. Then a second boat draws alongside, window to window, and Qinyan is in it chanting the very lyric, plaintive to the point of tears; Ziyu cries out to be saved, and the girl in a rage flings him off. On the other boat Qinyan is indeed wept dry and yellow as wax, and hides his face and weeps; and he reproaches Ziyu for coming so far — look at this stretch of the Yangtze, does his mother not worry? He hands over the matching lyric, and it proves to be no lyric at all but the silk handkerchief sent when he entered the Hua mansion, its spots of blood and tears there to be counted. Asked why he is alone and where his adoptive father is, Qinyan sighs, weeps, and says at length only that he wished to see him once — otherwise this place would have been where he was buried. And then out of the after-cabin comes his own father, Mei Shixie, black with anger: shameless creature, and now running out behind your mother's back. Ziyu starts, tries to leap across, misses his footing and falls into the river — and wakes in a cold sweat to crickets and moonlight on the gauze window. Next day Ziyun's man brings the lyric; Ziyu is undone by it, then reads letter and lyric over a hundred times, rejoicing that Qinyan's learning has risen to something that will last, and sews an antique brocade bag to keep it in and wears it about him.`},54:{zh:`梅子玉得了琴言的和词又做了那场怪梦，郁闷数日。恰好梅士燮家书回来：任上取了不少真才，托人带回三千金；说子玉年已十九可以完婚，若等任满则要到明年冬天，况王文辉又系至亲，一切可托颜仲清料理，不丰不俭；另给子玉一个谕帖，说近日诗文颇有进境，今秋宏词之试若自信得过便求人保荐，若不能自信也不必好此虚名。子玉答得诚实："自信呢也拿不稳必定可取，但如我这样的也多，考不上也没有什么不是处。"王文辉却说喜事早已预备停妥，考是必要去的，与颜仲清、王恂一同求人保荐便是；徐子云保了萧次贤，次贤偏抱病不能赴考，子云甚为太息。初九日派阅卷大臣，苏侯又作总裁，华光宿派搜捡官，徐子云派收卷官，刘文泽派弥封官，张仲雨派巡罗官。海内文人自致仕先达以至布衣共七八百人。首试八题，自拟汉诏、拟唐疏、五经条解，以至治河、盐法诸论；二试三赋；三试则依原韵拟杜少陵《北征》、韩昌黎《南山》二诗。八百人贴去五百，二场只剩三百，三场出榜只取六十名：王恂落第，高品四十九，仲清二十七，子玉第二；殿试子玉又占鳌头，取三十二名而仲清、高品皆落，独他最年轻，授了编修。

三试压倒群英，比中状元还难得，他却毫不自得，因见仲清、高品、王恂落第，反谦谨了许多。苏侯见了他，便想起徐子云当日之言。王文辉与陆氏夫人半喜半闷，喜的是子玉考中，闷的是王恂、仲清不中，好在接着要办女儿喜事，也就喜多闷少。一日孙佩秋与蓉华到王琼华房里道喜，说妹夫压倒天下英才，玉堂金马比今科鼎甲还体面；又说状元三年一个，宏词十年一考，岂不强似中状元——说得琼华手拈衣带、低头不语，虽喜而未过门，不好公然领谢。蓉华翻看她妆台上一本闺秀诗草，琼华评说"诗也做得好，就是不脱闺门气，无甚体裁"；题词中却推浣香、浣兰的几首七律为"绣口锦心"。佩秋说她们姊妹像双生一般，比眼前这两位姑娘还像。于是各以花比人：浣香如白牡丹，浣兰如粉芍药，袁绮香如莲花，香能及远而有出尘之致。由此转入一段极精彩的论诗：佩秋自道熟《唐诗三百首》而不会做诗，历数所爱的杜牧之、李义山诸绝句；蓉华另举孟郊、杜工部之沉痛。

琼华的议论最见识力：说《唐诗三百首》是全唐诗的精液，便于初学，然一诗有一诗的好处，不可以优劣论；时人多好七律，只因格局工整可以写景传情，其实最难学，她自己怕做，只好做七古。随即拆解岑参《白雪歌》：写塞外胡天，偏用梨花、珠帘、狐裘、角弓、铁衣相间成文，便成一片清清冷冷的世界，"妙在言语之外"；若论沉痛悲凉，则莫如杜甫《兵车行》诸篇。她再论五律以真挚为贵，推杜甫"国破山河在"一首四十字为五律之冠；七律以浩气流转为上；最真切者则是白香山《望月有感》，"纯是血性语，几于天籁"。蓉华说这是遭遇使然，所谓穷而后工；琼华却翻进一层：后人未尝无此流离之苦，却不写真情而写虚景，把凄风苦雨和在里面，虽也动人，究竟是虚话，何能如此字字真切。佩秋嫌这样近于白话，琼华说诗只要好，白话也一样好看，若极意雕琢而不能稳当，倒反不如白话。蓉华撺掇她议论唐贤的可议处，她先说"我是什么人，敢议唐贤"，随即大胆下笔：杜甫《梦太白》开首绝妙，偏"魂来枫林青，魂去关塞黑"两句梦的成了死太白，不如删去径接下文，倒觉含蓄不尽。接着是一场斗韵：佩秋预先查了来考人，问《北征》多少韵，蓉华认输，琼华说好像七十韵，并指出真正重韵只有一个"日"字，把佩秋所举的两处一一驳回；再问《南山》，佩秋说一百韵，琼华更正为一百零二韵且一个重韵也没有，反考得佩秋哑口，自承是因今科出了这两个题目才新近看熟的。佩秋便打趣说将来过门第一天要教二姑爷背清了诗韵进房，不然叫他跪在门外，好教他知道女人中也有个博学的——琼华红着脸回一句"想是我哥哥跪过的"。

蓉华临去说日子快了，姐妹不能常在一处，又叹自己不比妹妹有福；琼华暗想姐姐一肚子牢骚也难怪，姐夫那样才学终要高发，不过迟早罢了；又想自己的郎君才十九岁已能如此，只是听说从前有个琴言害他病过几场，不知如今怎样了。王文辉定了九月十九日吉期，颜夫人写信回家，一切交与仲清办理；仲清本来旷达，不将得失放在心上，照常张罗。喜期两边请酒，名旦们送戏送席，闹了好几天。洞房花烛之夜，子玉一见便觉心花开放——书中于此点破一段因缘：只因琴言是个男子，虽有情分终不能配偶，皇天可怜子玉这一片苦心，才又生出个琼华小姐来，与琴言上妆时一模一样；此事颜夫人久已知道，当日见了琴言便说像他媳妇。子玉一见之下未免感触，又暗暗解释，遂成就了良缘爱果。次日袁绮香、苏浣香、浣兰、吴紫烟、蓉华、佩秋都来陪新，华夫人因子玉是父亲得意门生、又是年伯母相请，欣然而至。此后闺房之乐真是乐不可言：一个仕女班头，一个才人魁首，或早起看花，或迟眠玩月，或分题拈韵，或论古辨疑，成了闺房良友，想念琴言之心也减了几分。一日子玉被馆中叫去，落下那个小锦囊；琼华拾起解开，见两纸《金缕曲》，一是子玉笔迹的送别之词，一是和作，笺上又写着琴言的名字，心中甚喜："我几次问他那琴言，他总不肯告诉实话，倒取笑我说与他生得一样，如今教我拿着了凭据。

"又想起从前听哥哥与姐夫议论这个琴言，说他这段情来得很奇、令人想不出来，今看这两首词，果然非有情有恨人说不出来。她收起词稿，把空囊仍挂原处。子玉回来一见锦囊挂着便吃一惊，捏了一捏是空的，心里着忙——别样倒还可辩，惟有信上有琴言的名字如何辩得——只时时偷望她一眼。琼华忍不住一笑，子玉借机问她笑什么，她说"我其实也不要笑，偏无故的笑起来"；子玉说"人世难逢开口笑"，她随口接"人生有几断肠时"，一句打到心坎，子玉不敢再问，只想走开。琼华却把话兜住，说今日见了两首好词要念给他听，念完便逐句剖解：第一句"何事云轻散"便如此沉痛，接着一开一合，上半阕已转了三层；"忍牵衣、道声珍重，此心更乱"读到便觉心酸，何况身亲其际；江湖浩漫尚浣不尽愁肠一半；末了想到身虽离开而魂梦可会，隔得老远魂梦未必能来，索性彼此宵宵呼名，两边凑合竟能凑着也未可知——子玉笑说"解得一点不错"。琼华随即逼问："这人与你常相厮守，你却怎样位置他？"子玉说"不过侍书捧研"；她道"侍书捧研，何用魂梦相唤"，子玉急了，说"我说你是我的知己了，自然洞见肺腑，谁道你也不能知我，何况他人"；琼华笑说"我讲得这么透澈，怎说还不能知你"。子玉只得说别人讲些胡涂话由他，你是不应该讲的，"现在相貌还有些……"便住了口；琼华也接"那你就应该……"也不说下去，两人相视，末了子玉只说横竖久后自知，不须分辩，便扯开话头说徐子云的夫人要请她去赏菊，还请王恂、颜仲清诸位夫人，日子未定，要一家一家问明都肯来才定，还须没有大风的日子。琼华说自己过门未满月，须等太太吩咐，且看姐姐嫂嫂去不去；子玉恐她再问琴言的事，只找些闲话与她谈，琼华明知他的心事，也不忍再问，教他难为情。`,en:`After Qinyan's matching lyric and that strange dream, Mei Ziyu is low for days. Then a letter comes from Mei Shixie: he has found many able candidates at his post, and sends three thousand taels home; Ziyu is nineteen and should marry now, since waiting for the end of his term would mean next winter at soonest; Wang Wenhui being close kin, Yan Zhongqing may manage everything, neither lavishly nor meanly. To Ziyu himself he writes that his recent work shows progress, and that if he can trust himself for the autumn examination he should seek a recommendation — and if he cannot, he need not covet an empty name. Ziyu answers honestly that he cannot be sure of success, but that many are in his case and failing would be no disgrace. Wang Wenhui brushes the doubt aside: the wedding has long been prepared, and of course he must sit — he, Zhongqing and Wang Xun can be recommended together. Xu Ziyun recommends Xiao Cixian, who falls ill and cannot compete, to Ziyun's great regret. On the ninth the examiners are named: Marquis Su again as chief, with Hua Guangsu as search officer, Xu Ziyun as collector of papers, Liu Wenze as sealer and Zhang Zhongyu as patrol. Seven or eight hundred candidates come, from retired dignitaries to men in plain cloth. The first session sets eight subjects, from imitation Han edicts and Tang memorials and expositions of the Five Classics to river control and the salt laws; the second sets three grand fu; the third requires imitations, in the original rhymes, of Du Fu's *Northern Expedition* and Han Yu's *South Mountain*. Five hundred of the eight hundred are cut, then three hundred remain, and the final list takes sixty: Wang Xun fails, Gao Pin is forty-ninth, Zhongqing twenty-seventh, Ziyu second — and at the palace round Ziyu heads the thirty-two chosen, while Zhongqing and Gao Pin both fail. Youngest of them all, he is made a Hanlin compiler; to have crushed the whole field through three sessions is rarer than the first place in the empire, and yet he shows no elation, and grows humbler for his friends' sake. 

Marquis Su, seeing him, remembers what Xu Ziyun once said. Wang Wenhui and Madam Lu are glad and vexed together — glad at Ziyu, vexed at Wang Xun and Zhongqing — but with a wedding coming, gladness has the better of it. One day Sun Peiqiu and Ronghua come to congratulate Wang Qionghua, telling her that her bridegroom has crushed the empire's talent, that a place in the Academy outshines this year's first three, and that a zhuangyuan comes once in three years while this examination comes once in ten — until Qionghua, twisting her sash and unable to accept thanks before her marriage, can only sit with lowered head. Ronghua picks up a volume of verse by a gentlewoman from the dressing table, and Qionghua judges it: the poems are good, but they never shake off the boudoir manner and have no structure. Of the colophons she prefers the regulated poems by Huanxiang and Huanlan, whose mouths are brocade and hearts embroidery — sisters so alike, says Peiqiu, that they might be twins, more alike than the two sisters present. They fall to matching women with flowers: Huanxiang a white peony, Huanlan a pink one, Yuan Qixiang a lotus whose scent carries far. And from there the talk becomes a real discussion of Tang poetry. Peiqiu, who knows the *Tang Poems Three Hundred* by heart and cannot write a line, lists her favourite quatrains; Ronghua adds the poems of grief. But Qionghua's criticism carries the chapter. The anthology, she says, is the essence of all Tang verse and good for beginners, but every poem has its own merit and none can be simply graded. Her contemporaries prefer regulated verse in seven characters because its frame is neat and will hold both scene and feeling — and it is the hardest form of all, which is why she avoids it and writes in the older seven-character measure. 

She takes the poems apart: Cen Shen's *White Snow Song*, which paints the frontier by way of pear blossom, pearl curtains, fox furs, horn bows and iron armour, until a whole world of clear cold stands outside the words; and, for sheer desolation, Du Fu's *Ballad of the Army Carts*. Five-character regulated verse, she goes on, prizes sincerity, and Du Fu's forty characters on the broken state and the surviving hills are the crown of the form; seven-character regulated verse is best when the breath runs through it; and the truest of all is Bai Juyi's *On the Moon, with Feeling*, pure blood-and-nature speech, close to the music of heaven. Circumstance made it, says Ronghua — poverty perfects the craft. Qionghua goes one better: later men have known the same scattering and cannot write so, because they write not true feeling but empty scenery, stirring in bleak wind and bitter rain, which moves a reader and is still empty beside words every one of which is true. Peiqiu objects that such poems verge on plain speech; and Qionghua answers that a poem need only be good, that plain speech is as good as anything, and that carving which will not sit is worse. Pressed to name faults in the Tang masters, she first protests that she is nobody to judge them, and then does it: in Du Fu's *Dreaming of Li Bai* the opening is superb, but the couplet about the soul coming through green maples and going out into dark passes makes the dream a dead Li Bai instead of a living one, and would be better struck out, leaving the poem more suggestive. There follows a contest of rhymes: Peiqiu, who has looked it up in order to catch them, asks how many rhymes are in the *Northern Expedition*; Ronghua gives up, and Qionghua says about seventy, names the single genuine repetition, and refutes both of Peiqiu's supposed ones with exact philology. Of *South Mountain* Peiqiu says a hundred rhymes, and is corrected to a hundred and two with no repetition at all; her supposed repetition is shown to be a different character with the cart radical; and she is caught out in turn. Qionghua admits that she has only lately got these poems by heart because they were this year's examination subjects. Peiqiu jokes that on the wedding night the bridegroom should be made to recite the whole rhyme book before entering the chamber, and kneel outside the door if he cannot, so that he may learn there is learning among women; and Qionghua, colouring, retorts that her brother has presumably knelt. 

As they part Ronghua reflects that the sisters will soon be separated and that Qionghua is the fortunate one; and Qionghua thinks privately that her sister has grievance enough, that Zhongqing's learning must rise in time, that her own bridegroom is only nineteen and already this — and that she has heard of a certain Qinyan who made him ill more than once, and wonders what has become of him. Wang Wenhui fixes the nineteenth of the ninth month; Madam Yan writes home and leaves everything to Zhongqing, who, being naturally large-minded, sets his own disappointment aside. Both houses feast for days, with the famous performers sending in plays and dinners. On the wedding night Ziyu's heart opens at first sight — and here the book explains the design: because Qinyan was a man and could never, whatever the feeling, be a match, heaven took pity on Ziyu's bitter heart and produced Miss Qionghua with exactly Qinyan's face as it appeared in stage makeup. Madam Yan had long known it, having said on first seeing Qinyan that he resembled her son's betrothed. Ziyu is shaken at the sight, quietly reconciles himself, and the marriage becomes a true match — nothing like the pedant of his dream, who would have been the death of a bride's patience. Next day Yuan Qixiang, Su Huanxiang, Huanlan, Wu Ziyan, Ronghua and Peiqiu come to keep the bride company, Madam Hua coming gladly since the bridegroom is her father's favourite pupil. The married life that follows is a joy past telling: a leader of ladies and a chief of talents rising early to see flowers, sitting late over the moon, dividing topics and drawing rhymes, arguing antiquity and resolving doubts — companions of the inner chamber; and his longing for Qinyan grows a little less. Then one day, called away to the Academy, Ziyu drops the small brocade bag. Qionghua opens it and finds two sheets: his own farewell lyric to *Jinlü Qu*, which moves her deeply, and a matching one, with a letter bearing Qinyan's name. She is delighted — several times she has asked about this Qinyan and been put off with teasing about how alike they are said to be, and here at last is proof; and remembering how her brother and brother-in-law used to call that feeling so strange that nobody could account for it, she finds that only a person of feeling and regret could have written such words. 

She puts the sheets away and hangs the empty bag back where it was. Returning, Ziyu starts at the sight of it, takes it down, squeezes it flat and is thoroughly alarmed — everything else could be argued away, but the letter bears Qinyan's name — and can only steal glances at her. When she cannot help laughing he seizes the opening and asks why; she answers that she does not want to laugh and is laughing all the same; he quotes that in this world an open laugh is hard to meet; and she caps it with the line about how many times in a life the heart breaks — which goes straight home, so that he dares ask nothing more and decides to leave the room. She holds him with a manoeuvre: she has seen two fine lyrics today and will recite them. Having recited, she takes the first apart line by line — the opening so grievous that nothing could follow it, the three turns of the first half, the unbearable clutching of a sleeve and the words "take care of yourself," the vast rivers and lakes that cannot wash away half the sorrow, and at last the device of calling each other's names night after night so that two dream-souls, setting out from both sides, might yet contrive to meet. Not one word wrong, says Ziyu. Then she strikes: if such a person were always at your side, what place would you give him? Only to attend my books and hold my inkstone. And why, she asks, should attendants on books need to call each other's dream-souls? Alarmed, Ziyu says he had thought her the one who saw into his heart, and if even she cannot know him, how should anyone; and she answers that having explained it so thoroughly she can hardly be said not to know him. He says that others may talk nonsense but she should not, and that as for the face, there is still something — and stops; and she begins "then you ought to —" and stops too; and they look at each other until he says only that time will make it plain and no defence is needed, and turns the subject to Xu Ziyun's wife, who means to invite her to see the chrysanthemums with the wives of Wang Xun and Yan Zhongqing and the rest, asking each household in advance because all must come and none may be missing, and the day must be windless. Qionghua answers that she is not a month married and must wait for her mother-in-law's word, and will go if her sister and sister-in-law go and not otherwise. Ziyu, afraid of more questions about Qinyan, keeps to small talk; and Qionghua, knowing his mind exactly, has not the heart to press him further.`},55:{zh:`琴仙出京一路涕零，旱路到王家营渡了黄河，在清江浦换了江船。随行的长随刘喜四十多岁，老实忠厚，跟过江宁的侯石翁，善于烹调，如今派来伺候琴仙，饥则问食、寒则问衣，竟像个老婆子。三日到扬州，道翁怕盐商缠扰求诗求画，不肯上岸，一早却带琴仙雇小船去逛平山堂。沿河先见一个围墙半倒、楼屋全欹、古木鸦啼的花园——道翁说这是小虹园，当年他与诸名士在此虹桥修禊，眼见琳宫梵宇、瑶草琪花，如今成了这个模样。过虹桥、莲花桥，两岸绿柳中露出红墙梵刹，宛然图画。忽有两只游船迎面而来，船上两个浓妆妇人眼澄澄地看着琴仙，擦身而过时把一方白绢包着的枇杷杨梅桃梨掷到船板上；道翁笑说"这倒成了安仁掷果了"，琴仙脸便红起来，终不肯吃。到平山堂，松风谡谡，过御书楼便见欧文忠公的亲笔。一个知客僧殷勤献上第二泉水泡的龙井，又说起去年盐台寿旦要做架屏，扬州城内竟选不出一个作家，只得到江宁去请侯石翁，送了十二色礼、六百银子，写又是三百两；及至逛园，那僧人借着指点景致挨来挤去，遇石径难走处便搀一把扶一扶，琴仙的手竟被他握了好几回，满脸怒容只推恐要下雨，道翁听得隐隐起雷，也就辞了。

过瓜州，一日半到江宁，泊在龙江关。道翁记着侯石翁，一早带琴仙与刘喜进城到凤凰山拜访。这侯石翁今年七十四岁，二十岁点翰林，三十余岁便致仕遨游天下三十年，在凤凰山造了个极精雅的园子，生平无书不读，当时推为天下第一才子。只是年逾七十而性尚风流，姬妾满堂，执经问字者非但青年俊士，兼多红粉佳人；论诗专主性灵，虽妇人孺子偶有一二佳句便极力揄扬，时人称为"诗佛"，而好谈格调者亦以此轻之。园中不设围墙，只编槿竹为篱，长松夹道、飞瀑如帘、鹤鹿在栏。石翁不衫不履迎出来，一把拉了道翁的手，又拉琴仙的手；听说这位郎君是小儿，呵呵大笑："你是个秃尾猢狲，怎么忽然有个小儿？难道这位玉郎是你口里吐出来的？"及知是螟蛉，又拉住琴仙两目注定，说"好个玉郎！何物老妪，得此宁馨儿"。二人随即高谈：石翁声如洪钟，把经史子集上的疑难一一考问，道翁一一答出，末了各自抚掌大笑。饭间石翁劝他不必做官，何必守这六品前程；道翁却道：我并非老马恋栈，只是数十年来依然故我，反成孑身，立锥无地。

石翁说仗文章尽可自豪，何必浮沉宦海；道翁引汤临川答梅国桢的话——"少与诸公比肩事主，老而为客，所不能也"——又说自己彩笔已还、虚名有限而大敌恒多，此后只想焚弃笔砚、善刀而藏，浮沉于半刺之间以终老。谈到京中后辈，道翁首推梅士燮之子梅子玉为"人中鸾凤"，说今年若考宏词必中；石翁却笑宏词科没有什么稀奇，熟读几部类书就取得中，道翁便回敬"这是你老先生没有考上，所以题起牢骚来"。及听怡园分题的那篇序，石翁赞是一篇唐文、宋人四六无此谨严，却指出两句可议：只要点去四个字改作"常置端溪""时安湘管"，便是六朝句法——道翁拜服，称他"一字之师"。道翁也回敬一箴：老兄诗力益肆，只是游戏之言不该尽入全稿，何不分内外集？石翁援《三百篇》不废风诗为辩，道翁却说那是存本国土风、且寓惩创，若以现身说法，便如群仙齐集瑶池而曲巷妖婢连袂而来，识者既灌而往已不欲观；有妨名教之作尤宜割爱——石翁也只得说"敬佩良箴"。偏偏他看着琴仙说一句"琼枝太艳"，又诵"无逾我园，无折我树檀"，琴仙听了不悦；道翁便望着园子念"树深时见鹿，溪午不闻钟"，石翁初不为异，忽然会意，直叫"可恶！可恶！"石翁索一副对子，道翁出"天下词人皆后辈"，下联先以"卢家少妇是乡亲"相戏，石翁大笑说只可作印章；末了另出一副"腹不负我，我不负腹；文如其人，人如其文"，石翁想了一想，说虽非好心却于我颇合，便用了。石翁又见琴仙玉笋尖尖，要看他的扇子，顺便拉着手翻来覆去看了两回，赞他"有文在手，是有夙慧的"，随手把自己的扇子递与他，却把琴仙那柄道翁所画的梅妻鹤子图拿去扇着。琴仙到路上才知换了扇；次早石翁遣人来请，他执意不去，来人送还扇子说昨日拿错了。取过自己的扇子一看，上面竟题着一首诗："谁咏枝高出手寒，云郎捧研想应难。

羡他野外孤飞鹤，日傍瑶林偷眼看。"他恍记得"云郎捧研"的故事，细想之下恼将起来，几乎要撕，转念留着等义父回来看看，便撂过一边倒在床上；又想起京中光景，除了怡园一班名士，每见一个生人必遭戏侮，越想越气，不觉掉下泪来。刘喜送早饭进来他也不肯吃，便撺掇他进城散闷，历数莫愁湖、秦淮河诸处。琴仙想起怡园扶乩"后日莫愁湖上去，莲花香绕女郎坟"之句，便说有个亲戚的坟在莫愁湖，要去祭奠；刘喜问是哪一家、姓什么，他竟答不出，只得实说是请仙时乩上判出他前世的坟墓在此，却没有判出姓氏——刘喜说这话渺茫得很，他却道"真得很，他各样事都判出来"。到了莫愁湖，先进观音庵，满壁题咏中便有侯石翁的诗文与江西学使梅士燮的一副对子；游人来来去去只管看他，几个村妇嚼着甜瓜黄瞪瞪地瞧，当他是戏台上跑下来的张生，看得他不耐烦，便还了茶钱出来。湖上红荷如杨玉环初酣御酒、白荷如赵昭仪新浴兰汤，他对着一湖默祷：上仙既指示了我的前身，又没有判出姓来，叫我身亲其地无从寻觅，怎样灵验出来指点迷途？祷未毕，莲花丛中荡出一只小艇，一个红衣垂髫女郎年可十四五，长眉秀颊、皓齿明眸，船头集着一群翠雀，啾啾唧唧毫无畏人之态；琴仙谛视，以为天仙游戏，自觉形神俱俗，肃然而立。船拢岸时那群翠雀刷的一声都飞向北去。刘喜问湖那边有什么可逛，女郎说只有城墙与一座杜仙女墓，看兰苕花、翡翠雀最好，"方才那一群翠雀就是杜仙女墓上的，他懒得飞，搭我的船过来"。

琴仙一听便触动心事，问那杜仙女是几时人；女郎说不知，只听说有七八十年，也是个官家的女儿，葬在这里；何以称仙女？只因常见她荡个小船在莲花丛里或隐或现，人若去赶就不见了；那小坟周围许多斑竹，坟后一盘凌霄花盖得有一间屋子大，无数翠雀在里面作窠，又有许多奇怪的兰花一年开到头，人若采了回去就要生病，所以地方上人不敢作践，初一月半还有人过湖烧香。琴仙求她渡过去，便打发船家先回，带了香酒同渡。女郎拿了一张荷叶、一朵荷花领他穿过树林、走入斑竹丛中：马缨花开满两树，还有几棵紫薇木槿，果然一个小小坟墓幽香扑鼻，开满无数蕙兰；山脚下一盘凌霄缠石结成圆顶，绿荫如伞盖，里头翠鸟乱鸣，清风一吹香入心骨。琴仙先自伤心，到了这地方翻觉尘心涤尽、栩栩欲仙，只想结庐在此便胜过什么所在；扪苔剔藓看了许久，才叫刘喜取火点香浇酒，摆上果子。女郎剥下一瓣荷花斟满作杯，问他为什么不拜两拜；琴仙道"我即是他，他即是我"，女郎笑道："好呆话。既有了你，就没有他；既还有他，就没有你。"琴仙觉这话里有灵机，说"你不知道我，只知道他"，她说"我倒没见着他，倒见着你，无缘无故的祭他作甚"。琴仙坐在地下留恋不肯就走，倒是女郎催他；他要把果子送她，她笑说自己不吃这些，索性替他种在此处，"等你将来再来看罢"，便从头上拔下簪子掘了几个小坑，把桃、李、苹、梨四样种下。渡过湖来，刘喜要给船钱，她笑说"不要，我不是撑渡船的"，嫣然一笑仍荡入莲花丛里；琴仙留心望去，只见花光湖水一片迷离，望不清她的去处，只得惆怅回船。刘喜又荡进水西关去逛秦淮河：两岸画楼绣幕、香气氤氲，楼上妓女一见琴仙这般美貌便唤姐姐呼妹妹，俯首争看，把他羞得只埋怨刘喜；急要转船，前后游船又挤将拢来，个个挤眉擦眼，他真成了看杀的卫玠，幸得刘喜替他挡开，才一径荡了出关。天黑上了大船，只见两个家人慌慌张张迎上来："老爷在山上跌了一交，晕了过去，救转来现在还哼声不止呢！"琴仙听了，唬得一身冷汗，连忙进舱。`,en:`Qinxian weeps the whole way out of the capital: by land to Wangjiaying, the Yellow River crossed, a river boat taken at Qingjiangpu. Their retainer Liu Xi, a plain honest man of forty and more who once served Hou Shiweng of Jiangning and cooks well, is set to wait on Qinxian, and proves as fussy as an old nurse, asking after food when he is hungry and clothes when he is cold. Three days bring them to Yangzhou, where Daoweng will not land for fear of the salt merchants who besiege him for poems and paintings, but waits at the customs barrier — and one morning takes Qinxian by small boat to Pingshan Hall. Along the water they pass a garden with half its walls down, its towers all askew, crows in the old trees: this, says Daoweng, is the Little Rainbow Garden, where he once held the Rainbow Bridge purification with the scholars of the day among jewelled halls and immortal flowers. Beyond lie Rainbow Bridge and Lotus Bridge, with red temple walls showing through green willows like a painting. Two pleasure boats come up, and from one two heavily painted women stare at Qinxian; as the hulls scrape past, a white silk bundle of loquats, bayberries, peaches and pears is thrown onto the deck. So Pan An is pelted with fruit, laughs Daoweng — at which Qinxian reddens and will not touch it. At Pingshan Hall the pines sigh, the halls and galleries rise tier upon tier, and beyond the Imperial Library hangs Ouyang Xiu's own calligraphy. A guest-prefect hurries out with Longjing brewed from the Second Spring, and lectures at length on drawing the First Spring at Jinshan, where the rope must be measured to the exact depth of the tide or river water floods the bucket. He gossips too: at the salt commissioner's birthday last spring the administration wanted a screen of panels, and in all Yangzhou no writer could be found, so they went to Jiangning for Hou Shiweng with twelve kinds of gifts and six hundred taels and paid three hundred more to have it written out — whereas had Daoweng been there, one table of wine would have got it composed and copied in two days. 

In the garden the same monk crowds and presses about Qinxian on pretence of pointing out the views, taking his arm at every awkward step and getting hold of his hand several times, so that Qinxian, furious, pleads coming rain; and Daoweng, hearing thunder, takes his leave. Past Guazhou, a day and a half more brings them to Jiangning. Remembering Hou Shiweng, Daoweng takes Qinxian and Liu Xi into the city and up Phoenix Hill. Hou is seventy-four, a Hanlin at twenty, retired past thirty and thirty years a traveller, owner of a garden of great elegance, a man who has read everything and writes for posterity, and is called the first talent of the empire. But past seventy he is still amorous, his house full of concubines, and among those who come to him for instruction are not only young gentlemen but many women; in poetry he holds only for native sensibility, and will extol a chance good line by a woman or a child, so that people call him the Buddha of Poetry — while those who talk of form despise him for it. His garden has no walls, only hedges of hibiscus and bamboo, with pines along the drive, a waterfall like a curtain, cranes in a railing and deer in a pen. He comes out in undress, takes Daoweng's hand and then Qinxian's; and told that the young man is a son, roars with laughter — you, a tailless monkey, have suddenly a son? Did this jade youth come out of your mouth? Told it is an adoption, he holds Qinxian's hands and stares: a fine jade youth — what sort of old woman got such a child? Then the two old men fall to it, Hou's voice like a bell, setting knotty passages out of the Classics and the histories, which Daoweng answers one by one, until both clap their hands and laugh. Over the meal Hou urges him to give up office — why keep a sixth-rank prospect for the sake of rice? Daoweng answers that he is no old horse pining for its stall, but that after decades he is still himself, and alone, and without room to plant an awl. Literature alone is enough to be proud of, says Hou; why float on the official sea? And Daoweng quotes Tang Xianzu's refusal — in youth he stood with them serving the sovereign, and to be a dependant in age he cannot — adding that his coloured brush has been given back, that empty fame is limited and formidable rivals many. Henceforth he will burn his brushes, sheathe his knife, and end his days drifting in a minor post. 

Of the younger men in the capital he names first Mei Shixie's son Mei Ziyu, a phoenix among men, certain to pass the special examination this year — at which Hou scoffs that the examination is nothing and a few encyclopaedias thoroughly read would carry a man through, and gets the retort that this is the grievance of a man who never passed it. Hearing the Yiyuan garden preface, Hou pronounces it a Tang piece, more disciplined than Song parallel prose, but faults two lines whose specifying of the inkstone's markings and the brush's rat-whisker tip drops them into the fashionable — strike out four characters and the Six Dynasties syntax is restored. Daoweng bows to him as a teacher of a single word, and then returns the favour: Hou's poetic force grows ever more torrential, but his jeux d'esprit should not go into the collected works — why not an inner and an outer collection? Hou defends himself with the *Three Hundred Poems*, which keep the licentious airs; and Daoweng answers that those preserve local custom and carry a warning, whereas Hou, teaching by his own example, makes licence the orthodox note — as if, among the immortals at the Queen Mother's pool, the painted girls of the back lanes came in arm in arm and sat down beside them, so that the discerning look away after the first libation; and what injures the moral order should above all be cut. Hou accepts the admonition. Yet he looks at Qinxian and remarks that the jade branch is too lovely, and quotes the Songs about not climbing into his garden or breaking his sandalwood tree — which Qinxian does not like; and Daoweng, gazing at the grounds, quotes Li Bai on seeing deer in the deep woods and hearing no bell at noon, a stroke Hou feels only after a moment, when he cries out that it is abominable. Asked for a couplet, Daoweng offers that all the poets of the empire are of a later generation, and teases him with a second line about the young wife of the Lu house being a fellow-townswoman, which Hou says will do for a seal and not for a couplet; and at last gives him one that Hou, after thinking, accepts as fitting though not kindly meant — that his belly does not fail him nor he his belly, and that the writing is like the man and the man like his writing. Then, noticing Qinxian's slender fingers, Hou asks to see his fan, turns his hand over twice, praises the lines in the palm as a mark of innate cleverness — and hands him his own fan while keeping Qinxian's, which Daoweng had painted with a plum-wife and crane-son. Only on the road does Qinxian discover the exchange. Next morning Hou sends to invite them both; Qinxian will not go, and the messenger returns the fan as taken by mistake. Looking at it, Qinxian finds a poem written on it — who sang of the branch so high that the hand comes away cold; the boy who held the inkstone must have found it hard; and how he envies the crane that flies alone in the wild and steals a look each day beside the jasper grove. Half remembering the story behind the boy and the inkstone, he thinks it over and grows angry enough to tear the fan, then decides to keep it for his adoptive father to see, flings it aside and lies down; and thinking of the capital, where apart from the Yiyuan circle every stranger he met insulted him, he weeps. 

Liu Xi brings breakfast, which he will not eat, and urges an outing, listing Mochou Lake and the Qinhuai. Remembering the planchette's line about going upon Mochou Lake where lotus fragrance surrounds a maiden's grave, Qinxian says a relative is buried there and he wishes to make an offering — and, pressed for the surname, must admit that the spirit named the grave of his own former life and gave no name at all. Liu Xi finds it very vague; Qinxian insists that the spirit was right about everything else. At the lake they visit the Guanyin nunnery, whose walls are covered with famous inscriptions, among them Hou Shiweng's writings and a couplet by the Jiangxi examiner Mei Shixie. Passers-by stare at him, and some village women, chewing melon, gape as though a stage lover had walked down among them, until he pays for the tea and leaves. At the water's edge, where the red lotus is like Yang Guifei flushed with wine and the white like Zhao Feiyan fresh from her bath, he prays silently: the immortal showed him his former body but gave no surname, so that standing on the very ground he cannot find it — let some sign be given. Before he has finished, a small skiff comes out of the lotus with a red-clad girl of fourteen or fifteen, long-browed and bright-eyed and so beautiful that he takes her for an immortal at play and feels his own coarseness; on her bow sits a flock of kingfishers, chirping and preening without fear, which scatter northward as the boat touches. Asked what there is beyond the lake, she says only the wall and the grave of Du Xiannv, where the orchids and the kingfishers are worth seeing — the flock just now came from that grave and, being lazy, took a ride on her boat. Who was this Du Xiannv? She does not know; only that it was seventy or eighty years ago and that she was an official's daughter buried there; and she is called an immortal maiden because she was often seen poling a little boat among the lotus, half seen and half hidden, and vanished if anyone gave chase. The small grave is ringed with spotted bamboo, with a trumpet-creeper behind it spread as wide as a room where countless kingfishers nest, and strange orchids that flower the year round and make anyone who picks them ill — so that the country people dare not damage it, and cross the lake to burn incense on the first and fifteenth. 

Qinxian asks to be ferried over, sends his own boat back, and takes wine and incense. She leads him with a lotus leaf and a lotus flower through the trees and the spotted bamboo, past silk-trees and crape myrtle, to a small mound breathing hidden fragrance and covered with orchids, with the creeper twined over a rock into a green dome full of chirping kingfishers, and a wind that carries the scent into the bone. He came in sorrow, and here feels the dust of the world washed out of him, as though he might become an immortal — and thinks that to build a hut here would be better than anywhere on earth. He studies the mound a long while, then has Liu Xi strike a light, burns incense, pours the wine and sets out the fruit. The girl helps, tearing off a lotus petal for a cup, and asks why he does not bow twice. I am he and he is I, says Qinxian. Foolish talk, she laughs: if there is you there is no him, and if there is still him there is no you. Struck by something quick in it, he says that she does not know him, only the other; but I have not seen him, she answers, I have seen you — why sacrifice to him for nothing? He sits on the ground unwilling to leave, and it is she who urges him up. Offered the fruit, she laughs that she does not eat such things and will plant it for him here against his coming again — and with a pin from her hair digs four small holes and sets in peach, plum, apple and pear. Ferried back, she refuses the fare — I am not a ferrywoman — smiles, and poles away into the lotus; and watching, he can make out nothing but the blaze of flower and water and does not know where she has gone, and turns back to the boat disconsolate. Liu Xi then takes him through the water gate into the Qinhuai, between painted towers and embroidered curtains thick with perfume, where the courtesans lean at the windows and, seeing him, call to one another and crowd down to look, until he is scarlet and blames Liu Xi for bringing him. Trying to turn the boat, they are hemmed in by pleasure craft all staring and winking, and he becomes Wei Jie stared to death, until Liu Xi fends them off and they pole out through the barrier. It is dark when they reach the big boat, and two servants come out in a fluster: how is it the young master comes only now — the master fell on the hill and fainted, and though he has been brought round he is groaning still. Qinxian breaks into a cold sweat and hurries into the cabin.`},56:{zh:`琴仙进舱时，屈道翁半个身子已动不得，却还问他今日到何处去；听说莫愁湖果有一座杜仙女墓，与乩上所判相对，也觉诧异，猜那红衣女郎是莲花神指点。及见侯石翁题在扇上的那首诗，道翁登时动气："此老游戏散漫，习与性成，老来还是这样。我素鄙其人，不过爱其才耳。"叫他把扇子撕了，琴仙便撕得粉碎。次早将要过关，忽起大顶风走了锚，白浪滔天把船倒打上去，直打到燕子矶才收住。道翁膀子疼，便念着叫琴仙代笔，写了十几封谢京中诸友的信与好些诗；傍晚风小，刘喜领他上岸逛了燕子矶诸胜。他那阕和子玉的词，正是那一夜卧在床上想了半夜才妥。道翁一生耗于笔墨又伤于酒，这一跌已中了心，时清时昏，痰涌上来便连话也说不出；下人不肯服事，只有刘喜一人兼管饮食，琴仙便彻夜在中舱伺候。道翁自忖此病未必能好，就好也是半身不遂，一个瘫痪人到省怎见上司，索性租了旱西门内护国寺的客房养病。谁知非但不好，反添出旧年呕血的老病，一日数吐，二十余日更加沉重。琴仙心如油沸，日夜在神前焚香祷告愿以身代；道翁自知不免，见他这般孝心更增伤感——"设或中道弃捐，教他如何归着，依靠谁人"，想到此泪流不已。

一日琴仙捧药进来，道翁执着他的手叫一声"琴儿"便噎住了，半晌才说"我害了你了，你好端端……"；琴仙忍哭劝他保重，他却道病已至此还服什么药，只嘱咐："我死后，你仍旧到京去。我看你心气已定，我可放心。但我生无以为家，死无以为墓，照伍大夫以鸱夷裹尸，沉我于燕子矶下罢，切勿殡葬。"琴仙肝肠寸断，双膝跪在床前捧着药碗。那几日寺中景象极凄厉：柏树上老鸦呀呀不住，破楼上枭鸟鼓吻弄舌；琴仙夜间去穿衣，只见窗外树边人影一闪，回房又被一条偷食的大狗撞倒，后院响得像棺材暴起；他勉强在院中跪祷三刻，起来时肩上竟爬着一条三寸长的蝎虎，吓得哭叫，亏刘喜回来一扇子打落。刘喜是去请医的，王大夫往仪征未归，只得改请二十里外的李大夫。谁知他到后院一看，箱笼已搬运一空，只剩几个书画箱——张贵、汪升、钱德三人连行李都不见了：盘费银子在内，连道翁的文凭也在那衣箱里。琴仙不是心疼东西，只怕道翁一旦不测连殡殓之费都无，哭了半日，还叮嘱断不可教老爷知道。道翁却叫人取出他一生的诗文集来，一部一部过了目，只留下一本近作诗稿、一本书画册，其余尽叫烧了。琴仙哭道这是一生心血正可传世，道翁却说："我没有这些东西，也不至今日这个模样，总是他误了我；若留下他，将来是要害人的——教人学了我，也与我一样偃蹇一生，为造物所忌。"琴仙无奈，烧了几种，又私藏了几种。

道翁把留下的付与他作纪念，又扶正了身子颤巍巍写下一纸遗言：记六月八日偕侯石翁游凉山，登绝巘为罡风吹落堕地致伤腰足，归卧不起呕血数斗，现寓白下萧寺，旦夕间事；"一抔之土何方，六尺之孤谁托？琴儿素蒙青眼，令其来依。呜呼！度香知我，自能慰我于九原也"，落款"七月七日屈本立绝笔"。琴仙看罢恸倒在地。道翁又嘱他殡殓之后即埋于江岸，不必等过百日，速速进京；文凭送到石翁处，托他在制台前缴了，并求作一篇传——"人虽不足传，但我一生之困苦艰难也就少有的"。少顷满屋忽觉香风拂拂，道翁命刘喜擦身换衣，焚了一炉香，跏趺而坐，提笔写下"一世牢骚到白头，文章误我不封侯。江山故国空文藻，重过南朝感旧游"，题罢掷笔而逝。琴仙昏而醒、醒而昏，哭到泪枯声尽；再看时道翁容颜带笑、玉柱双垂，室中余香未散，他便对刘喜说"你看老爷是成了仙了"。后事全仗刘喜张罗：扣下的船价并他自己可当的破烂箱笼，勉强凑得五十吊；棺材买不到，只得向和尚买了后楼停着的那一口，便去了二十二千，其余孝衣、鼓手、僧经，忙得不了。琴仙诸事不能，只在床前守尸痛哭，两日水浆不入口；入殓停灵后，他穿了麻衣在灵帏伴宿。正值中元鬼节，南京处处施食烧纸、放河灯照九泉，古寺中落叶满阶，夜来月映纸窗、秋虫乱叫，他茕茕顾影、前路茫茫，真是死无死法、活无活法——若死了，这灵柩怎样，岂不做了负恩人；若活了，又怎熬这伤心日子。一个来月，如花如玉的容颜便憔悴了下去。

一夜酸风微雨，他辗转不寐，忽见一只耗子在刘喜铺上闻他鼻子；喝了一声，刘喜竟一抢跑出，撞开长窗抱住大树不放——推醒了才知是梦见张贵来扯被窝，琴仙又害怕又好笑。回床恍惚间，他梦见自己走出寺来，忽然锣声作乐，旌旗幡盖、金盔执刀、玉女金童过了半个时辰，末后一座七香宝辇坐着一位珠璎蔽面的女神，仪从尽入寺中——却已不是那寺，而是宝殿巍峨的极大所在。他躲在树后偷看：一个十二三岁、清眉秀目而头角峥嵘的童子上阶长揖不拜，那尊神连连拍案骂他，童子分辩了好一会颜色才和；随后侍女捧出一个古锦囊，把百十来枝新新旧旧的笔一齐倒进童子衣兜里。继而一位戴金幞头穿红袍的神人进来，竟是他的义父。琴仙上前牵衣痛哭，道翁安慰他："琴儿，你受苦了，也是你命里注定的。不过百日困苦，耐烦等候，自有个好人来带你回去。"问那童子是谁，道翁说这童子前身不小，从六朝转劫到此，方才骂的是他从前的罪孽，"后来是个大作家，名传不朽的；三十年后见他一部小小的著作，四十年后还有大著作出来"；问那尊神名号，道翁只教他低声，用指头在他掌中写了两字。转眼又到燕子矶边，一只船靠拢来，梅子玉坐在舱里长吁短叹——却又走出一个艳妆美人与他并坐，细看竟与自己扮戏时的面貌装束毫无二致。二人拿一面镜子同照，笑说"一镜分照两人，心事不分明"；琴仙连叫两声"庾香"，子玉全不听见，只说"今日好耳热，不知有谁骂我"，那美人却望见他，喝一声"什么人在这里偷看人"，把镜子掷来。琴仙拾起一照，见自己变了莫愁湖上采莲船里的红衣女子；镜中又映过一班名士名旦，反过来隐隐又是魏聘才、奚十一一流；正看时镜子旋转如月成了一颗大珠，忽有一只满臂鳞甲的蓝手伸进舱来，五爪一抓把镜子夺去——他惊醒时已是日高三丈。他只把中间一段说与刘喜，说老爷像是成了神，刘喜说"老爷一生正直，岂有不成仙之理"。

刘喜随即去凤凰山报丧。那侯石翁自见道翁跌了一交便不放心，正在胡思乱想琴仙见了那首诗是喜是恼——"可惜如此美男，若能收他作个门生，足以娱此暮年"——不料刘喜进来叩头，细说了归天、遗言、张贵等偷去衣箱银钱并文凭、少爷在寺守灵而衣食将绝的光景。石翁大惊落泪，一口应承：写信、刻讣闻、发分子，官事明日去见制台，着两县缉拿逃奴，并行文江西防他们持文凭撞骗。次日他果然见了制台，又替刘喜补了呈子；过了一日备了祭筵联额，亲到寺中上香奠酒，痛哭一场，老泪盈盈，琴仙在孝帏里听着倒也觉得不像假的。谁知他收了泪进帏，便蹲下身子一把挽住，盘腿坐下挨近了他，握着他的手迷离老眼——那一阵幽香攒入鼻孔，此心不醉而自醉。琴仙移远些，他偏凑近些，先说了些吊唁的话，问他日后打算；琴仙说守过百日、觅地安葬再作主意。石翁便一层层地劝：你令先尊是湖北人，数代单传并无亲族，你去那里没有一个人认得；如今孑然一身，东西又偷光了，回湖北这个念头可不必起。京里人情势利，从来说人在人情在；你太生得娇柔又在妙龄，如何受得那奔走求食的苦？我与你令尊是三十年文章道义之交，我不提拔你教谁提拔你。又说灵柩且寄停在此，一两月后找着地再葬，"歇天我来接你回去……有我在此，决不教你无依无靠"。琴仙见他一双生花老眼看定了自己，口中虽说得正大光明而神色间总不像好人，又气又怕，脸已涨红，低头不答。石翁把他的手握在掌中轻轻搓了几搓，笑迷迷问"前日扇上那首诗，看了可懂得么"——琴仙更气，缩了手要走。石翁又拉住问他在京认得些什么人，他忍气答"都是一班正正经经的，倒也没有那种假好人"，说出徐子云、梅子玉并几位名士；石翁一听徐度香便说"了不得了，快不要去"，说这些纨袴公子外面虽好、心上不把你当朋友，京城风气极坏、嘴贫舌薄，"你去了也要懊悔的"，不如拜他作义爷或作老师——"我又是七十多岁的人，人家还有什么议论？且我家里姬妾也有好几个，疼你的人也多，娘儿们一样，自然有个照应"；又说若放你独自进京，人还要议论我把至交的遗孤撇在脑后。琴仙只当没有听见，洒脱了手站得远远的；石翁没趣，睁大三角眼瞅了他一会，说"我是一片好心，你倒不要错了主意"，起身要走，琴仙只叩了两个头说不认得外边、就算谢过孝了，人已入帏。次日石翁着人送来一担米、一担炭、四两银子，试他受不受——琴仙执不肯受，刘喜也不敢作主，只得原物璧还。石翁大怒，骂他不受抬举，此后也无颜再来；心里却一分恨、一分爱、一分怜，终日方寸交战，作了许多诗，幸得苏州巡抚请去，勾留两月才归。`,en:`When Qinxian reaches the cabin, half of Qu Daoweng's body will not move, and yet the old man asks where he has been. Told that Mochou Lake really does hold a grave of Du Xiannv, exactly as the planchette foretold, he marvels, and guesses that the girl in red was the lotus spirit pointing the way. But shown the poem Hou Shiweng wrote on the fan, he flares up: the old man's dissipation has become his nature and he is the same in age as in youth; he himself has always despised the man and loved only the talent. Tear it up — and Qinxian tears it to shreds. Next morning, as they wait to pass the barrier, a head wind drags the anchor and drives the boat back on white water as far as Yanzi Cliff. With his arm too painful to write, Daoweng dictates and Qinxian copies more than a dozen letters of thanks to their friends in the capital, and many poems besides; and at dusk Liu Xi takes the boy ashore to see the cliff. That night, lying in his bunk, Qinxian works out the lyric answering Ziyu's. Daoweng's life has been spent on the brush and injured by wine, and the fall has struck at the heart: he is by turns clear and clouded, and when the phlegm rises he cannot speak; the servants will not serve him, so that Liu Xi alone does everything and Qinxian sits up night after night in the middle cabin. Judging that even recovery would leave him paralysed — and how should a paralysed man present himself to his superiors? — Daoweng rents rooms in the Huguo Temple inside the West Dry Gate to be cured before going on. Instead the old spitting of blood returns, several times a day, and after twenty days he is far worse. Qinxian, his heart frying in oil, burns incense day and night offering his own life instead; and Daoweng, seeing such devotion, grieves the more — if he is taken midway, where is the boy to belong, and on whom is he to depend? One day, as Qinxian brings the medicine, the old man takes his hand and calls him by name and cannot go on, and at last says only that he has ruined him, who was doing so well. 

What use is medicine now, he says, and gives his instructions: after my death go back to the capital; your mind is settled and I can be easy. But I have had no home in life and shall have no grave in death: wrap me like Wu Zixu in a leather sack and sink me below Yanzi Cliff, and do not bury me. Those days in the temple are ghastly: crows crying all day in the cypress, an owl clacking on the ruined upper storey; a shadow slipping past the trees at night; a great black shape knocking him down in his own room, which proves to be a dog that has stolen his supper; noises in the back court like a coffin bursting; and, when he forces himself to kneel and pray in the courtyard, a gecko three inches long crawling onto his shoulder, so that he cries out until Liu Xi comes back and knocks it down. Liu Xi has been for a physician: Doctor Wang being away, Doctor Li has been fetched from ten li off. And then Liu Xi looks into the back rooms and finds the boxes gone — all but a few of paintings — and Zhang Gui, Wang Sheng and Qian De gone with them, and the travelling silver, and Daoweng's official credentials in the same chest. It is not the goods that frighten Qinxian but the thought that if the worst comes there will be nothing even for a coffin; he weeps half a day and charges Liu Xi that the master must on no account be told. Daoweng meanwhile calls for his collected writings, looks them over volume by volume, keeps one book of recent poems and one album of painting and calligraphy, and orders the rest burnt. This is a lifetime's blood and should be handed down, Qinxian protests; and the old man answers that but for these things he would not have come to this — they have been his ruin, and if they were left they would ruin others, who would learn from him and be thwarted all their lives as he has been, for the Maker envies such men. Qinxian burns a few and secretly keeps a few. Then, propped up, with a trembling hand, Daoweng writes his last testament: that on the eighth of the sixth month he climbed Cool Hill with Hou Shiweng, was blown from the summit by a gale and hurt his back and legs, took to his bed and has vomited blood by the quart, and now lies in a bare temple at Nanjing with his hours numbered; that he does not know where his handful of earth is to be, nor to whom his orphan is to be entrusted; that Qinxian has long had Duxiang's favour and should go to him — and, signed with his name, that Duxiang knows him and will console him in the world below. Qinxian falls down in a fit of grief. 

He is told to bury the coffin on the river bank without waiting out the hundred days and to hurry back to the capital; to send the credentials to Hou Shiweng to be surrendered to the governor-general; and to ask him for a biography — not that the man is worth recording, but the hardships of his life have been rare. Presently a fragrance moves through the room; Daoweng has himself wiped and changed, incense lit, and sits up cross-legged; and taking the brush writes four lines — a lifetime of grievance to a white head; literature betrayed him and no title came; the rivers and hills of the old country are empty of anything but writing; and passing again through the southern dynasties' land he grieves for old journeys — and, having written, throws down the brush and dies. Qinxian faints and revives and faints again until his tears are dry and his voice gone; and looking at last he sees the face smiling and two lines like jade pillars, and the room still fragrant, and says to Liu Xi that the master has become an immortal — to which Liu Xi answers that a man upright all his life could hardly fail to. Everything falls on Liu Xi: what is left of the boat money and his own shabby boxes in pawn make up perhaps fifty strings; no coffin can be bought, so they take the one stored in the temple's upper storey for twenty-two, and then mourning clothes, musicians, monks, a cook. Qinxian can do nothing but keep watch by the body and weep, and touches neither food nor water for two days; and after the encoffining, in hemp, he sleeps beside the bier. It is the Ghost Festival, and all Nanjing is feeding the dead, burning paper and floating oiled-paper lanterns down the river to light the Nine Springs; leaves lie thick in the old temple, the moon prints the paper windows and the autumn insects cry — and for him, alone with his shadow and no road ahead, there is no way to die and no way to live: if he dies, what becomes of the coffin, and would he not have failed the man who took him in; and if he lives, how is such grief to be got through? Within a month his face is wasted. One night of sour wind and thin rain, unable to sleep, he sees a rat sniffing at Liu Xi's nose; he shouts, and Liu Xi bolts up, bursts through the window and clasps a great tree and will not let go — waking, he explains that he dreamed Zhang Gui was pulling off his quilt and gave chase and caught the tree instead, which leaves Qinxian both frightened and laughing. Back in bed he dreams. He walks out of the temple; then gongs and music, and rank on rank of banners, gold helmets and drawn blades, jade girls and golden boys, pass for half an hour, and last a seven-scented carriage bearing a goddess with her face veiled in pearls — and the whole train enters the temple, which is now no longer that temple but an immense hall. Hiding behind a tree, he watches a boy of twelve or thirteen, clear-browed and startlingly proud, go up the steps and bow without kneeling; the goddess strikes the table and rebukes him and he answers back, until her colour softens; and an attendant brings out an antique brocade bag and pours into his lifted skirt a hundred and more brushes, new and old and of every colour. 

Then a spirit in a gold headdress and red robe comes in, and it is his adoptive father. Qinxian catches his sleeve and weeps, and is comforted: you have suffered, and it was fated; a hundred days of hardship, and then a good man will come and take you home. Who was the boy? A soul of no small history, says Daoweng, come down by transmigration since the Six Dynasties, and rebuked just now for his old sins; hereafter he will be a great writer whose name will not die — in thirty years a small book of his will be seen, and in forty a great one. And the goddess's name? Lower your voice — and he writes two characters on the boy's palm. Then the scene is Yanzi Cliff; another boat draws alongside with Mei Ziyu in it sighing — and out of the cabin comes a painted beauty who sits down beside him, and who is, feature for feature, Qinxian himself in stage costume. They hold up a mirror between them, and she laughs that one mirror shows two people and the heart's business is not clear; and though Qinxian calls twice, Ziyu hears nothing and says only that his ears are burning and somebody must be cursing him — while the woman sees him, cries out that someone is spying, and flings the mirror at him. Picking it up, he sees himself become the red-clad girl of the lotus boat at Mochou Lake; a procession of the scholars and performers crosses the glass, and reversed it shows dimly Wei Pincai and Xi Shiyi and their kind; and as he looks the mirror spins bright as the moon into a great pearl — until a blue hand scaled to the elbow reaches into the cabin and five claws carry it off. He wakes crying out, with the sun high. He tells Liu Xi only the middle part, that the master seems to have become a god; and Liu Xi says that a man upright all his life must. Liu Xi then goes to Phoenix Hill with the news. Hou Shiweng has been uneasy since the fall, and has been turning over whether the boy was pleased or angry at his poem, and reflecting what a pity it is that so beautiful a youth, if he could be taken as a pupil, would enliven his old age — when Liu Xi comes in and kowtows and tells everything: the death, the testament, the theft of the boxes and money and credentials, and the young master keeping watch in the temple with food and clothing running out. 

Hou is genuinely startled and sheds tears, and undertakes it all — letters, printed announcements, subscriptions; he will see the governor-general tomorrow, have the county magistrates pursue the runaways, and send word to Jiangxi lest they use the credentials to swindle. Next day he does see the governor-general and helps Liu Xi file the plaint; a day later he comes to the temple with a table of offerings and a scroll, burns incense, pours the wine and weeps until his old eyes brim — and Qinxian, listening from behind the mourning curtain, thinks that the grief at least is not feigned. But then Hou dries his eyes, comes inside, crouches down and takes hold of him, sits close with his legs crossed, holds his hand and lets his old eyes swim — and, sitting as if in a bed of fragrant herbs with the scent stealing into his nostrils, is drunk without wine. Qinxian edges away and he edges nearer, asking what the boy means to do. To keep the hundred days, find ground, and bury his father, and then decide. That is no plan at all, says Hou, and lays out his argument step by step: your father was a Hubei man of a line without collateral kin, and nobody there would know you; you are alone now and stripped of everything, so put Hubei out of your mind. The capital is a place where feeling follows advantage; you are far too delicate and far too young to bear the running about after a living. He and your father were thirty years friends in letters and principle — if he does not raise you, who will? The coffin may stay a month or two until ground is found, and one of these days he will come and fetch him home; with him there, the boy shall never be without support. Seeing those old eyes fixed on him, and the words upright while the face is not, Qinxian reddens with anger and fear and will not answer; and Hou takes his hand into both of his, rubs it gently, and asks with a smile whether he understood the poem on that fan — at which the boy pulls his hand away and rises. Held back and asked whom he knows in the capital, he says shortly that they are all thoroughly respectable people, with none of the false sort among them, and names Xu Ziyun and Mei Ziyu. At Duxiang's name Hou breaks in: that will never do, these young men of great houses may be friendly to your face but do not count you a friend; the capital's manners are utterly corrupt and its tongues cheap and cruel, and you would repent of going. Better to take him as adoptive father, or as teacher — he is over seventy, so what could anyone say? — and his house is full of concubines who would pet the boy and look after him like his own women; and if he let him travel so far alone, people would say he had thrown his old friend's orphan aside. Qinxian behaves as if he has not heard, frees his hand and stands well away; and Hou, thwarted, opens his triangular eyes wide, stares, says that his intentions are entirely good and the boy had better not mistake them, and rises to go — receiving two kowtows and the excuse that as Qinxian knows nobody outside he will consider the mourning-thanks paid, after which the boy is behind the curtain again. Next day Hou sends a load of rice, a load of charcoal and four taels of silver to test him; and Qinxian refuses them absolutely, and Liu Xi, not daring to decide, returns them untouched. Hou is furious and calls him ungrateful, and has no face to come again — though hatred, love and pity war in him all day and produce a great many poems, until an invitation from the Suzhou governor takes him away for two months.`},57:{zh:`笔锋自江宁转回京中。十月初旬小春天气，晴光和蔼，怡园菊花开满。徐子云的夫人袁绮香于初十日请了华光宿的夫人苏浣香、田春航的夫人浣兰、刘文泽的夫人吴紫烟、颜仲清的夫人蓉华、梅子玉的夫人王琼华、王恂的夫人佩秋；萧次贤、高品、史南湘都回避了。绮香一早带十二个"红"字丫鬟先到园中候客，宝香堂另是一番铺设：一色锦裀绣褥、翠幕银屏，中间堆着七层菊花。众人陆续到齐，苏氏姊妹一到，只见"一群蝴蝶拥着两朵花王出轿来，莲步未移，香风已到"。绮香提议：今日众姊妹都是通家世好，苏家二浣、王氏双华本是同胞，索性仿竹林七贤作个桃园结义，序齿团拜，以后相见不许谦让。浣香说自己前日就有这心；紫烟更喜从此改了排行。序齿是绮香二十五、紫烟二十三、佩秋与蓉华二十二、浣香二十一、浣兰十九、琼华十八居末。

焚了一炉百和香，铺下大锦毯，七美顺着年次团团拜过，此后便以大姐至七妹相称。琼华说今日原为游园，绮香却笑说逛园不过是个名目——五六里大地方山石荦确，"况你那金莲三寸还不满，如何走得来"；议论一番，终于弃山就水，主仆四十余人分乘十几只小船荡到吟秋榭，在第二层设席，红雪等调丝品竹。绮香说自己新年无事与丈夫编了一个酒令，不论多少人都放得进去；浣香笑道："这么说来，竟不是个酒令，是个阵图了。"那令名唤"秦灭六国"，又叫"六国伐秦"：七根大筹分七国，数十根小筹是七国人物，掣着谁便扮谁；国君不出战，遣将出战，三胜秦则秦王领群臣跪献三樽，败则反是。掣国的结果：绮香得秦，紫烟楚，佩秋燕，蓉华赵，浣香魏，浣兰齐，琼华韩。众人先斗嘴：浣香说六国并力定要杀她个片甲不留，蓉华盼掣着廉颇、蔺相如，琼华盼张子房的博浪一椎，佩秋盼荆轲，浣兰要驱田单的火车，紫烟只恨没有项重瞳。绮香却先"点将"，把浣香的爱珠、花珠调到自己"大国"来，共二十四婢分派停当。

及至掣人物，笑话便层出不穷：秦国得白起、商君、韩非子、吕不韦、李斯、赵高，绮香笑说"你看我们文武皆全"；楚国得令尹子兰、高唐神女、宋玉，紫烟叹"一个佞人，一个梦神，一个风流鬼，这如何打得仗来"；燕国得荆轲、田光与骏马，蓉华笑说"尚未出兵，倒已先砍了两个脑袋"；赵国得廉颇、蔺相如、平原君，算是好签；魏国得信陵君、侯生与"醇酒妇人"，掌珠掷得此签羞得要换，被人拦住；齐国最惨——小翠掣着"鸡鸣"，许三姐掣着"狗盗"，笑得众人如花枝乱颤，幸而荷珠掣了孟尝君稍可解嘲；韩国得张子房、博浪椎与圯上老人。于是击鼓传花，一国一国出马。赵国先出：秋莲扮廉颇，三次豁拳皆败，依谱戴上假白须，众婢用金钩挂在耳上，佩秋脱口说"倒像人蝇拂子"——那正是她丈夫说过的笑话，被蓉华一问"这蝇拂子是谁家的"，臊得两颊微红。蔺相如又三次被猜中，罚酒由赵王代饮；平原君作交线之戏，红雯酒量最小又胆小，十指发颤，越笑越颤。到猜物时花珠藏了一颗莲子，同伴踢脚示意，红雯却错看成鸭掌，被人笑说"难道你裙下的不是金莲，定要算鸭掌么"。赵国九阵只胜一阵，蓉华只得领队纳降。楚国的高唐神女遇上白起，绮香笑称"这叫做无情遇"，行的是按戏中脚色定生旦净丑的藏阄令，她一连点《刘唐》《闹庄》两出净脚戏，把生旦罚了个遍，红薇不服，紫烟改点生旦戏想罚爱珠，偏爱珠空手，反罚了侍香。魏国信陵君行"运筹点将令"，掣出的诗句一句罚戴金镯的，满堂皆饮而独浣兰不饮——她腕上是一对碧霞玺；一句罚插金丝双凤钗的，红雪偏去缠爱珠；一句"轻敛翠蛾呈皓齿"，被指着敬了侍香，侍香一笑喷了人一身；一句"暗中惟觉睡鞋香"最难，红雪竟寻着红薇——因她常用松子粉浆缠足带。侯生行顶针续麻令，输了要喝酱油，两人为"哉"字能否起句争执，明珠赌气真喝了一口，红雪才引《书经》"哉生魄"说破，众人称是。

掌珠的"醇酒妇人"输了要跪请两国之君出令，于是有了全回最风雅的一段：浣香集词牌成句"宴清都，清平乐，八声甘州金缕曲"，绮香应以"高阳台，尉迟杯，貂裘换酒醉蓬莱"，一来一往四五联，众人满堂贺酒，尊二人为盟主。燕国荆轲投壶、田光作五行哑令、骏马行马吊谱，一局凑成"玉鲫鱼背"竟是一百贺，通场分饮，一回算来喝了一百七十二杯。齐国孟尝君掷骰问答，红霙以四句唐诗答"有的、没有的、要的、不要的"，浣香赞绮香"强将手下无弱兵"；荷珠不服，反以四句刻薄唐诗嘲他扮的赵高是个老公，气得红霙要打，赔了半日礼才罢。小翠的"鸡鸣"接牌接死了三次，谱上是学鸡啼三声、从板凳下钻过三次——她倒投其所好，钻得又快又灵，惹得满堂大笑。三姐的"狗盗"输了五毒令，要装狗叫、伏地而爬，她啐了一声撒腿就跑，被六个丫鬟围住乱捏，笑作一团只得汪汪叫了三声；心中不忿，便假意伏下去，乘其不备一把抱住爱珠两脚，捋起裤腿学狗叫着乱抓，唬得爱珠神号鬼叫瘫倒在地，又照样咬了红雪一口，四个人没命地跑开，满堂笑得堕钗翻酒。爱珠骂她"将来总教疯狗咬一口"，她只笑说"谁教你们太作恶了，我还容情"。绮香笑着要拿丫鬟同浣兰换她，紫烟笑说"姐姐你招集这些亡命作甚，你真作秦始皇么"。

末了韩国出马：蕊珠扮张子房行金门射策令，以三颗骰子摆成诗句，"九重春色醉仙桃""十三筝柱雁行斜"皆被红香摆中，独"词源倒流三峡水"红香摆不出，揭开却是三个四——正取"倒流"之意，红香拍手叫妙而自领罚；韩非子的谱是"作法自弊"，红香求免涂脸不得，被人在脸上画了一副眼镜。圯上老人对红香下棋谱，反被这爱打谱的一着挂角将死，依谱要脱鞋置酒遍敬席上，一场抢夺笑闹才算了结。最后青琴扮博浪椎，谱上是闷雷、劈雷可打秦国通国人马；诸人纷纷叮嘱她与主人争脸，绮香却笑"谅此孤军深入重地，焉有生还之理"，命六人一齐上前——不料被青琴一顿闷雷劈雷打得个个心惊胆怯，琼华得意点头道"一将功成万骨枯"，绮香也只得笑说"众志成城，坚不可破，我让了你罢"。日落浣香要赶城先回，余人移到春风沉醉轩煮茗清谈；紫烟拉了蓉华、佩秋打马吊，琼华与绮香、浣兰打诗牌，直到二更后独琼华斗成一首七律："饯别春光已半年，小春天气最堪怜。酒分捭阖纵横策，人比瑶池阆苑仙。任说朝朝依玉树，终应步步让金莲。彩云明月如相妒，照彻楼台分外鲜。"众人赞是字字稳当、且切今日之事，绮香最爱那一联"任说朝朝依玉树，终应步步让金莲"，说是"为我辈闺阁吐气——不然，这个园几成了那几个名旦的梨园了"。蓉华问她可曾见过那几个名旦，她说年节下也来贺过节，见过五六个，生得都好，"不是我说，我们今日这一班人，倒有几个像他们"——浣兰、琼华恐她说破，不约而同拿闲话拦住。将近三更方散，一对对手灯相照，穿过石门竹径送到园门，各自上车而回。`,en:`The narrative returns from Nanjing to the capital. In the small spring of the tenth month, with mild sunlight and the chrysanthemums out all over Yiyuan, Xu Ziyun's wife Yuan Qixiang invites, for the tenth, Hua Guangsu's wife Su Huanxiang, Tian Chunhang's wife Huanlan, Liu Wenze's wife Wu Ziyan, Yan Zhongqing's wife Ronghua, Mei Ziyu's wife Wang Qionghua, and Wang Xun's wife Peiqiu; Xiao Cixian, Gao Pin and Shi Nanxiang all withdraw. Qixiang comes early with twelve maids whose names all carry the character for red, and Baoxiang Hall is newly dressed in matching brocade cushions, kingfisher curtains and silver screens, with seven tiers of chrysanthemums heaped in the middle. As the sedan chairs arrive, a flock of butterflies seems to carry two queens of flowers out of their chairs, the fragrance reaching the hall before their steps do. Qixiang proposes that, since all present are families long connected and two pairs among them are sisters by blood, they should imitate the Seven Sages of the Bamboo Grove and swear sisterhood as in the Peach Garden, ranking by age and never deferring to one another again. Huanxiang says she had the same thought; Wu Ziyan is delighted to be given a new place in the order. By age it is Qixiang at twenty-five, Ziyan twenty-three, Peiqiu and Ronghua twenty-two, Huanxiang twenty-one, Huanlan nineteen, Qionghua eighteen and last. Incense is burnt, a great brocade rug spread, and the seven bow to each other in a circle, thereafter addressing one another as eldest sister down to seventh. 

Qionghua reminds them that they came to see the garden; Qixiang laughs that the garden was only the pretext, since five or six li of rough rock are not to be walked on feet that scarcely fill three inches. After some debate they abandon the hills for the water, and mistresses and forty-odd maids embark in a dozen small boats for the Yinqiu Pavilion, where the feast is laid on the second storey and the maids make music. Qixiang has, she says, devised a drinking game with her husband over the new year that will accommodate any number of players — at which Huanxiang says this sounds less like a drinking game than an order of battle. It is called Qin Destroys the Six States, or the Six States Attack Qin: seven great slips for the seven kingdoms, and dozens of small ones for their famous personages; the ruler does not fight but sends generals; three victories over Qin oblige the Qin ruler and her ministers to kneel and offer three cups, and a defeat obliges the same in reverse. The kingdoms fall out as Qin to Qixiang, Chu to Ziyan, Yan to Peiqiu, Zhao to Ronghua, Wei to Huanxiang, Qi to Huanlan and Han to Qionghua — and the boasting begins: Huanxiang promises to leave Qin not a scrap of armour, Ronghua hopes for Lian Po and Lin Xiangru, Qionghua for Zhang Liang's hammer at Bolang, Peiqiu for Jing Ke, Huanlan for Tian Dan's fire-oxen, and Ziyan regrets only that she cannot have Xiang Yu. Qixiang first conscripts two of Huanxiang's maids into her "great state," and the twenty-four are divided. Then the personages are drawn, and the comedy begins. Qin takes Bai Qi, Lord Shang, Han Feizi, Lü Buwei, Li Si and Zhao Gao — civil and military complete, says Qixiang. 

Chu takes the flattering minister, the Goddess of Gaotang and Song Yu, at which Ziyan groans that a sycophant, a dream-goddess and a romantic ghost can hardly fight a war. Yan takes Jing Ke, Tian Guang and a fine horse, and Ronghua observes that two heads have been cut off before the army has marched. Zhao takes Lian Po, Lin Xiangru and Lord Pingyuan, a good hand. Wei takes Lord Xinling, Hou Sheng and "strong wine and women," which Zhangzhu, who draws it, tries in vain to exchange. Qi is the disaster — Xiaocui draws "cock-crow" and Third Sister draws "dog-thief," to laughter that shakes the room, redeemed only by Hezhu's Lord Mengchang. Han takes Zhang Liang, the Bolang hammer and the Old Man of the Bridge. The flower is passed to the drum, and the kingdoms take the field in turn. Zhao goes first, and Qiulian as Lian Po loses three bouts and must wear a false white beard hooked over her ears with gold, at which Peiqiu blurts out that it looks like a fly-whisk — her own husband's joke — and colours when Ronghua asks whose fly-whisk it was. Lin Xiangru is guessed out three times and the King of Zhao must drink for her; Lord Pingyuan must play cat's cradle, and Hongwen, the poorest drinker and the most timid, shakes so that her fingers will not obey her and laughs herself into worse shaking. 

When Huazhu hides a lotus seed and a friend kicks her foot to hint, Hongwen guesses a duck's foot instead, and is asked whether what is under her own skirt is a golden lotus or a duck's. Having won only one bout of nine, Ronghua must lead her forces to surrender. In Chu, the Goddess of Gaotang meets Bai Qi — a meeting without feeling, says Qixiang — in a game of hidden counters matched to the role-types of named plays; Qixiang names *Liu Tang* and *Naozhuang*, both painted-face pieces, and fines the leading man and lady all round; Hongwei protests, and Ziyan seizes the right to name a play herself, only to find her intended victim empty-handed and Shixiang fined in her place. In Wei, Lord Xinling draws lines of verse that fine everyone wearing gold bracelets — the whole hall, save Huanlan, whose bracelets are carved jade — and then all who wear phoenix pins, and then Shixiang, the lady with knitted brows and white teeth, who laughs and sprays her wine over the challenger; and the hardest line, about knowing only the fragrance of a sleeping-slipper in the dark, is triumphantly resolved by pointing out Hongwei, who powders her foot-binding cloth with pine-seed flour. Hou Sheng plays a chain-word game whose forfeit is soy sauce, and the argument whether a certain particle may begin a line is settled — after one player has swallowed her mouthful in temper — by a quotation from the *Book of Documents*. When Zhangzhu’s "strong wine and women" loses and must kneel and beg both rulers to set a game, the chapter reaches its most elegant passage: Huanxiang builds a rhymed couplet entirely out of the titles of ci tunes, Qixiang answers with another, and the two cap each other four or five times while the whole company drinks to them as league-leaders. In Yan there is pitch-pot, a silent game of the five elements, and a game scored by majiang tallies, one throw of which comes to a hundred toasts and brings the evening's count to a hundred and seventy-two cups. In Qi, Lord Mengchang questions the loser on what she has, what she lacks, what she wants and what she does not, each to be answered with a line of Tang verse; the four given are so admired that Huanxiang says no weak soldier serves under a strong general — whereupon Hezhu, piqued, answers with four lines of her own so cruelly apt to the eunuch Zhao Gao that the loser tries to strike her. 

Xiaocui, as "cock-crow," loses at dominoes and must crow three times and crawl under a bench three times, which suits her so exactly that she does it faster than anyone could wish. And Third Sister, as "dog-thief," loses and must bark on all fours, spits, and bolts — is surrounded by six maids and tickled until she yields and barks three times — and then, feigning submission, catches Aizhu by both ankles, pushes up her trouser leg, and bites and tickles her calf until she collapses screaming, and serves Hongxue the same, scattering the rest of them in laughter that drops hairpins and spills wine. Cursed for it, she answers only that they were too cruel and that she let four of them off; Qixiang offers to trade a maid for her, and Ziyan asks whether her sister means to be the First Emperor in earnest. Han comes last: Ruizhu as Zhang Liang sets lines of verse to be pictured with three dice, and Hong Xiang matches two of them before the third — the source of words pouring backward down the Three Gorges — defeats her; she claps her hands at the answer of three fours and takes her own forfeit. As Han Feizi, hoist by her own law, Hong Xiang is denied mercy and has a pair of spectacles painted on her face; and the Old Man of the Bridge, playing her at a chess-manual game, is checkmated in a corner and must offer wine from her own shoe, which produces a scramble of snatching and throwing. Then Qingqin, as the Bolang hammer, is licensed to strike the whole state of Qin with muffled and splitting thunder; and though Qixiang declares that a lone force so deep in enemy country can hardly come back alive and sends all six against her, she beats every one, so that Qionghua nods and says that one general's success is built on ten thousand bleached bones, and Qixiang concedes with a laugh that a united will is a wall not to be broken. At sunset Huanxiang must go back into the city, and the rest move to the Pavilion Drunk on Spring Wind for tea; Ziyan takes Ronghua and Peiqiu to majiang while Qionghua, Qixiang and Huanlan play at poetry cards until, past the second watch, Qionghua alone completes a regulated verse — half a year since spring was seen off, the small spring most to be loved, wine dividing the strategies of alliance, the company like immortals of the jasper pool; let them say he leans day after day on jade trees, in the end step by step they must yield to golden lotuses; and the coloured clouds and bright moon, as if jealous, light the towers the more brilliantly. The others declare it too finished to have been dealt from cards, and Qixiang loves best the couplet about the jade trees and the golden lotuses, which vents the grievance of the inner chambers — for otherwise, she says, this garden would have become the theatre of those few famous performers. Asked whether she has ever seen them, she says she has met five or six when they came to pay their new-year respects, that they are all handsome, and that — though she says it herself — several of the company here today rather resemble them; at which Huanlan and Qionghua, afraid of what may be said next, both break in with other talk. Near the third watch they part, walking two by two with hand-lanterns through the stone gates and bamboo paths to the garden gate, and drive away.`},58:{zh:`怡园群芳之后，作者笔锋一转，专写几个下作人做几件下作事。奚十一选了广西一个极苦的知州，本不愿去，只因白菊花是广西人，力劝成行。此时家信频来：洋行倒了，盐场为海水冲了，老太翁又得腿疾，家事一败涂地；而他盘费全无，尚欠潘其观三四千两。潘其观是京中大帐局，奚家败落的消息一日之内传遍，别家更不必说，奚十一竟告贷无门。日里在外张罗，夜间惟以吃烟为事，一连数夜把个白菊花熬得清水直流——自三月修肾之后，那物虽壮观而不中用，头重脚轻，停顿多而纵送少。一日主人出门，白菊花闲步至跟班房外，从门缝里窥见春兰与巴英官私相狎昵，听得两人互夸长短，还说"与老爷的差不多"，气得咬牙，回房脸红，心想原来兔子也会起阳。及至奚十一回来，方知借贷已成：唐和尚、张仲雨作保，潘其观将三千旧账加息算作四千，另借二千盘缠，却要他立一万两的欠票，江南任上先还五千，广东再还五千，还派两个伙计一路跟去讨账。白菊花只骂潘其观不是个东西，转口便要九香楼的刻丝花袖。当夜奚十一取出纸包，是几条二两四钱一条的药绫带，又吞了三丸春药，灯灭帐合，一场狂纵，先则美满，继则如炭火烧心，带子被水浸紧解不开，两人皆伤，白菊花自此病倒。

次日奚十一同姬亮轩、巴英官往逛九香楼。门内一座屏风，两旁金字招牌：一收买秦汉唐宋古玩书画，一发卖苏杭花绣衣料兼各色洋货，一内看金珠宝玉、四时花卉。九个名旦均已出班，未满师的也由袁宝珠、苏蕙芳公同赎出，都搬到园中居住，正楼额曰"九香楼"，是田春航的手笔。东厢看绸缎，西厢看洋货，正屋看书画——掌事的竟是乌大傻，从前打茶围诓过姬亮轩的旧账，含糊揭过。奚十一点了花袖等物共费十金，又要看花木，大傻领他们穿游廊、过假山，几千盆盆景，池边杨柳芙蓉，对岸笑语喧然，说是王兰保住处，今日田春航与史南湘在座，奚十一便不便过桥。忽见一个二十余岁的人新衣新靴、手提马鞭昂然过桥，正是潘其观从前的赶车的，如今靠着他女人做了状元府的家人。奚十一烟瘾发作，大傻只借得一个积垢半寸的瓦灯盏来，被他嫌弃，方换了干净的。正吃烟间，园中大闹：巴英官把两盆金橘、佛手摘得干干净净，与那车夫互骂厮打。奚十一出来，遥见芙蓉花外立着两个玉人，认得是苏蕙芳、王兰保，涎水先流，出言无状；苏蕙芳一见是他，拉了王兰保便走。

奚十一大怒，闯过桥去，掀翻残席，砸了碗盏，骂道虽出了班子总是小旦，"兔子变得成狗么"。两人出来还以礼数，他更口出污言，王兰保按住他手一扠，他踉跄扑向桌角，胯间那颗镶嵌狗肾正压在花梨桌角上，痛如裂缝，两臂发软，趴在桌上说不出话，只得由园丁扶出，忿忿上车而回。数日后唐和尚设席送行，潘其观托言跌坏鼻子不来。奚十一醉归，药性又发，想起巴英官久疏此事，吞了四丸、扎上绫带，唤他到书房。正狂纵间，年久糟朽的顶篷豁喇塌下，两担灰土倾泻，二人惊极猛拔，一齐滚倒昏死。众家人执灯来看，奚十一阳物血淋淋只剩半截，巴英官脏头拖出三四寸。白菊花带病趿鞋奔出，姬亮轩慌乱中竟忘了穿裤，蹲在地下替巴英官热敷，与炕上的白菊花隔灯相对，闹出一场无人敢说破的窘态。阳善修到，诊后直言只能救命，不能再接；又误以为是白菊花不肯行方便，被她骂了个满脸通红，方陪笑改口，取活鸡皮和药捣敷。十余日伤平，医者劝他吃十剂寒凉药以绝其性，奚十一无奈依从，从此春蚕如死，谢了五十两。

白菊花守起活寡，外面却做出从良极正派的样子以博虚名；恨极巴英官，等他好了痛打一顿撵出门去，姬亮轩也只得忍心割爱。巴英官转到卓天香铺里做伙计，生意兴隆，不到一年众毒齐发，活活烂死。奚十一带了白菊花赴任，潘其观派伙计同去讨账，唐和尚与得月送出城外，倒算全始全终。至于潘其观的鼻子，另有缘故：他四十岁续娶山西石氏，今年二十八，情性风骚，初时恩爱，后见他外面偷鸡盗狗，由醋生恨、由恨生厌；他却由爱生顺、由顺生怕，一个成了篾，一个成了铁，连房事也须叩头哀告。六月唐和尚生日送面，是得月送去，石氏见他标致，认作干儿子，来往渐密。一日暑天席地抹牌，两人眉眼勾动，遂成私情。潘其观驮了石碑尚不自知，一心只想顽那得月，后来看出情景，便半路截住得月，以不许进门相挟，得月无奈依了。石氏久等不来，寻到书房，舔破窗纸窥见，怒极搬凳守在门口；得月出来满面通红，潘其观一跨出门便被揪住胡须，骂他自己屁股才请人挖净，如今又敢动她干儿子，一掌打去——手上银指甲折断，又薄又尖，竟将他鼻尖刮落，血流满面，胡须也被扯去半边。仍是阳善修与他配了个假鼻子，他怕再挨打，索性住在城里靴铺，服药两月方愈，从此得了"抓三爷""大眼三儿"的美名。奚十一断肾正在潘其观抓鼻这几日，故未能送行。那个烂鸡巴，这个便害臀风；那个接狗肾，这个便掏粪门；那个断龟头，这个又断鼻子——两人竟是同病相怜，只便宜了得月，害得两人成了残疾，他倒好端端又多拜了一个干娘。`,en:`After the ladies of Yiyuan, the brush turns deliberately downward, to a few base people doing a few base things. Xi Shiyi has been assigned a wretched prefecture in Guangxi and would rather plead illness, but White Chrysanthemum is a Guangxi native and urges him to go. Letters from home bring ruin: the foreign-trade house has failed, the sea has swept away the salt fields, his father is crippled in the leg. He has no travelling funds and owes Pan Qiguan three or four thousand taels. Pan runs one of the capital's great credit houses, and the news of the Xi family's collapse crosses the city in a day; no other house will lend either, and Xi finds every door shut. By day he scrambles for money, by night he does nothing but smoke, until White Chrysanthemum is left thoroughly frustrated — since the spring's surgery his repaired member is impressive but useless, heavy at the head and weak at the root. One day, wandering while he is out, she peeps through a door crack and catches Chunlan and Ba Yingguan at their own business, comparing their endowments and remarking that one of them nearly matches the master's; she bites her teeth and retreats, flushed, reflecting that catamites are not so incapable after all. Xi returns with the loan settled: Tang Heshang and Zhang Zhongyu standing surety, Pan has compounded the old three thousand into four, advanced two thousand more for the road, and required a note for ten thousand — five to be repaid at the Jiangnan post, five in Guangdong — with two clerks sent along to collect. White Chrysanthemum curses Pan for a scoundrel and in the same breath demands a pair of kesi embroidered sleeves from the Nine Fragrance Pavilion. That night Xi produces a paper packet of medicated white silk bands at two taels four a piece, swallows three aphrodisiac pills instead of the prescribed one, and their revel turns from pleasure to burning: the soaked band cannot be untied, and both are injured, White Chrysanthemum taking to her bed. Next day Xi drives with Ji Liangxuan and Ba Yingguan to the Nine Fragrance Pavilion. 

Inside the gate stands a screen flanked by gilt signboards — antiquities and paintings of the Qin, Han, Tang and Song bought; Suzhou and Hangzhou embroidery, cloth and every foreign ware sold; pearls, jade and flowers of the four seasons shown within. All nine of the famous performers have left their troupes, those still bound having been redeemed jointly by Yuan Baozhu and Su Huifang, and all now live in the garden behind; the main building's tablet is in Tian Chunhang's hand. Silks are shown in the east wing, foreign goods in the west, paintings in the main hall — where the man in charge proves to be Wu Dasha, whose old tea-house swindle of Ji Liangxuan is passed over with a smooth excuse. Xi spends ten taels, then asks to see the flowers, and Dasha leads them through covered walks and rockeries past thousands of potted plants to a pool edged with willow and hibiscus; laughter comes from the far bank, which is Wang Lanbao's lodging, where Tian Chunhang and Shi Nanxiang are guests, so Xi does not cross. A man of twenty-odd in new clothes and boots strides over the little bridge with a riding-whip: Pan Qiguan's former carriage-driver, now, on the strength of his wife, a retainer in the top graduate's household. Xi's craving comes on, and Dasha can produce only a tile lamp on a tray with half an inch of grease on it, until he is shamed into borrowing a clean one. Then uproar in the garden: Ba Yingguan has stripped two pots of kumquat and buddha's-hand bare, and is trading blows and insults with the driver. Coming out, Xi sees two jade figures beyond the hibiscus — Su Huifang and Wang Lanbao — begins to drool, and speaks insolently; Su Huifang, recognising him, takes Wang Lanbao inside. Enraged, Xi charges across the bridge, overturns the table, smashes the dishes, and shouts that leaving a troupe does not unmake a boy-actor, that a rabbit cannot turn into a dog. The two come out and address him with shop-keeping courtesy, which only inflames him further; when he strikes, Wang Lanbao catches his hand and jabs him under the ribs, and he stumbles into the corner of a rosewood table, where the grafted dog-kidney takes the whole weight. 

The pain splits his skull, his arms go slack, he sprawls across the table speechless, and must be helped out by the gardeners to drive home in silent fury. Days later Tang Heshang gives a farewell dinner; Pan sends word that he has injured his nose and cannot face the wind. Xi comes home drunk with the drug working, remembers that Ba Yingguan has long been spared, swallows four pills, binds the band, and calls him to the study. At the height of it the rotten ceiling matting gives way with a crack, two loads of dust and plaster come down, and the two, wrenching apart in terror, roll to the floor senseless. The servants bring lamps: half of Xi's member is gone in blood, and Ba Yingguan's bowel protrudes three or four inches. White Chrysanthemum, still ill, runs out with her shoes half on; Ji Liangxuan, rushing from bed, forgets his trousers entirely and crouches applying hot cloths to the boy across a candle from her, producing an embarrassment nobody dares name. Yang Shanxiu comes, says plainly that he can save the life but cannot reattach what is lost, and blunders into blaming White Chrysanthemum's appetite until she abuses him scarlet-faced and he retreats into apology, then pounds fresh chicken-skin with his drugs for a poultice. In ten days the wounds close, and he advises ten doses of cooling medicine to extinguish desire altogether; Xi submits, the spring silkworm dies, and the doctor is thanked with fifty taels. White Chrysanthemum keeps a living widowhood — how truly, no one can check — while cultivating outside the perfectly correct air of a woman reformed. Hating Ba Yingguan, she waits until he heals, beats him, and drives him out, and Ji Liangxuan, needing his post, must let him go. The boy takes work in Zhuo Tianxiang's shop, where trade is brisk; within the year every poison in him breaks out at once and he rots to death alive. 

Xi takes White Chrysanthemum to his post with Pan's clerks in tow, and Tang Heshang and Deyue see him beyond the city walls — a friendship at least complete from beginning to end. As for Pan Qiguan's nose, that has its own history. At forty he took a second wife, a Shanxi woman née Shi, now twenty-eight and of a passionate temper. Affectionate at first, she found him thieving abroad, and jealousy turned to hatred and hatred to disgust, while his love turned to compliance and compliance to fear: she became iron, he became split bamboo, until even the marriage bed required his kowtowing petition. When Tang Heshang's birthday noodles were delivered in the sixth month, Deyue brought hers; charmed, she made him her adopted son, and his visits grew frequent. One sweltering day, playing dominoes on a mat spread on the floor, glances passed into contact and contact into an affair. Pan, wearing the horns unawares, wanted only Deyue for himself; suspecting at last, he waylaid the boy, threatened to bar him from the house, and got his way. Shi Shi, waiting in vain, traced them to the study, licked a hole in the window paper, saw, and set a stool by the door in cold rage. Deyue came out crimson; Pan had barely stepped over the threshold when she had him by the beard, denouncing a man who had lately paid to have his own corruption scraped clean and now dared touch her godson, and struck him — her silver nail-guard, thin and sharp, snapped and sheared off the tip of his nose, so that blood covered his face and half his beard came away in her hand. Yang Shanxiu again fitted him with a false nose; too frightened to go home, Pan lodged in his boot shop in the city and dosed himself for two months, earning the fine new names of Third Master Claw and Big-Eyed Third. Xi's mutilation fell in the very days of Pan's, which is why he could not see his friend off. One rots, the other suffers boils; one takes a grafted kidney, the other has his fundament cleaned out; one loses his head, the other his nose — fellow sufferers to the last. Only Deyue, who crippled them both, walks away whole, with one more excellent godmother than he began with.`},59:{zh:`琴仙在南京护国寺守灵已满百日。主仆二人日日供饭烧纸，连房租一月须十金，三个月来把随身衣物典卖殆尽；初冬已至，他尚无棉衣，刘喜更不必说。刘喜劝他投奔侯石翁——那老名士门生满天下，交游比怡园的徐子云还阔——琴仙只答一句"我就饿死也不去的"，转而托便差写了几封哀切的信，寄与梅子玉、徐子云、苏蕙芳诸人求接，其中给子云的一封还封着屈道翁的遗言。寺中做法事，男女杂沓，常有人来他屋外张望；刘喜便卖了两件衣裳，在庙门口摆个水果干果的小摊，一天趁得百十钱，琴仙则在寓中一粥一饭，闲时写字画画。侯石翁自苏州回来，听说他窘到这般地步，爱根未断，老着面皮带二十金来。到门口先见刘喜摊边壁上挂着生纸花卉，一张牡丹卖了二百钱、一张梅花一百五十钱，还有人肯出两千钱订八幅屏，便先赞画，继而婉转劝他搬到自己家去："梅花可耐冰雪，究竟玉骨难受风霜"，又说南京拐子专用迷药诱骗年少美貌之人，或装作女人哄客，或弄成残疾在十字街敲锣骗钱，字字关切；末了却露出本相，自夸门墙之下裙屐风流、钗钿娟秀，说什么萤火须借烛龙之光、蝇飞须附骥尾，又道海棠与梨花并植亦可无猜、歌童小婢尽多乐趣。琴仙听得满脸发烧，看那老面皮里透出一团邪气，因他是长辈只得忍住；石翁反当他面嫩心回，亲手把银子送来。琴仙谢而不受，说前次所赏尚不敢领，冻馁是命中注定，"就使沿门乞食，古之英雄尚且不免"，冻死饿死也死得光明，决不做那贪生忘耻之事，说罢不顾而走。

石翁捏着银包，如钢刀削面，须眉欲竖，只得在刘喜面前把他痛斥一顿，扬言此后一概不管，上轿而去；琴仙独在房中呜咽，那歹意却对刘喜也说不出口。与此同时，梅士燮在江西学政任上取士有方、表烈阐幽，连奏要事，一月三迁，由詹事而左副都御史而吏部左侍郎，奉旨来京供职，学政改放陆宗沅。他得家信知子玉中了宏词、授职编修，又已娶妇，十一月初一起程，十日后到南京，回乡祭扫。拜见制台时，制台问起江西通判屈本立——原来吉安府差役拿获窃犯张贵、钱德，起出凭文、皮箱、白银三百十七两并金镯衣物，从犯汪升已死，行文要交还其嗣子。梅士燮心中恻然，次日备了奠仪祭桌，亲到护国寺来。琴仙孝衣俯伏于帏间，既喜是子玉之父，又怕问及根底。梅士燮上香奠酒，请他出来一看，点头叹道："道翁可为有子"，问明年十七、病故经过与目下光景，慨叹世态炎凉，许他明日去领回失物，又把葬事一概揽下。琴仙请葬于莫愁湖杜仙女墓侧，委婉说了那段前生底里，梅士燮半信半疑，只说明日去看。

他生得身瘦而长、一脸秋霜，与子玉半点不像；又不许琴仙称"大人"，命他叔侄相称。当夜梦见屈道翁纱帽红袍执手而来，拜托将骸骨付于莫愁湖畔的小女，又求为流落无依的小儿借一枝之栖；临去复入，手执莲花一枝道："此花出于淤泥而临清波，岂得以淤泥为辱？既往不咎。"梅士燮醒后细详"出淤泥"与"既往不咎"，已明白这义子必是小旦出身，转念只要人好总是一样。次日同游莫愁湖：严冬大雪之后，一入斑竹林反觉春风和煦，凌霄开了数百朵，兰蕙齐芳，盛夏才开的马缨花也含苞吐萼，杜仙女手植的苹、梨、桃、李四棵小树各开一花，翠雀成群绕人鸣叫而后飞向湖边。梅士燮素不信神仙，至此肃然，当即决意为屈道翁建祠，并供杜仙女牌位。琴仙跪谢泣下。他到江宁县议定，丈量官地、鸠工庀材，择二十四日下葬，亲撰墓志、神道碑勒石；江宁绅士闻此义举，送葬者数百人，诗歌文联纷纷。梅士燮不能久待，留二千金与老总管梅成督造，又画杜仙女像命塑彩身。侯石翁被绅士拉来走了一走，琴仙不与他讲话，他既恨且妒，连拜也未拜便走。

后来绅士倡捐集腋，屈公祠扩为亭台楼阁，莫愁湖中造湖心亭、九曲红桥，墓前立石坊华表翁仲，竟成南京一处名胜。二十八日开船北上，梅士燮把琴仙当作子玉一般朝夕相依，爱其稳重灵警，打算到京后教他读书作文；因道翁梦中之语，绝不查问他的出身，还命家人一律称"屈大爷"。京中子玉久盼父信不至，腊月十五忽接一封江西转来的信，喜以为琴仙已到任所，拆开却是"哀启者"三字，读到衣物尽被逃奴窃去、守棺萧寺衣食全无、屡遭侯石翁戏侮、本拟一死只因旅榇无归的话，泪如泉涌，套车去怡园遍寻不着萧次贤、徐子云，又奔九香楼，见史南湘、高品、金吉甫与诸名旦都在，将信传观，无不洒泪。徐子云寻到后读了道翁遗言，更是涕泪滂沱，当即许下明日遣人南下接人办丧，发一千银子星夜起程；萧次贤一夜之间摹成道翁神像，众名士在九香楼设位而哭，奠了三日，华光宿也来哭奠，一个九香园竟成了屈道翁的丧居。子玉日间不敢入内，怕颜夫人问、怕王琼华看出，偏王琼华心细，盘诘再三，只得直说。腊月二十二，子玉同颜仲清出城三十里迎候，二更时车马到门，父子叙话之后，梅士燮却问"屈大爷的车子怎么还不来"；灯笼引进一个美少年，二人大奇，琴仙早已看得分明，忍住心酸上前问安。梅士燮为他们引见，说是屈道生的令郎，同他进京，"将来要在一处的"，子玉始而大骇，继而乐得笑出声来。

三人到那边屋里，一个不敢问、一个不敢说，竟成三个哑子，只以眉目相语，一时颦眉泪眼，一时眉欢眼笑，如黄梅时节阴晴不定。夜深人静，子玉与琴仙同枕和衣而卧，细说出京后的思念、南京的游历、莫愁湖亲见前生坟墓、道翁临终与死后成神、群奴窃逃后的苦楚、刘喜的尽心、侯石翁的戏侮，以及梅士燮仗义安葬建祠始末；子玉也说自己送行后如何得病、得信后如何悲伤。颜仲清在旁看他们并头私语，暗想这两人两年之内伤了无数的心、流了无数的泪，才换得今日一叙。天明时琴仙尚枕着子玉的膀子熟睡，被颜仲清唤醒，两下都赧然而笑。至于身世如何交代，颜仲清主张不可隐瞒，宜先请颜夫人向梅士燮婉婉说明，免得外人先说破，反教他难堪。进城之后，梅进、云儿见了琴仙个个骇异，猜是又猜不是。颜夫人听了原委，只说"这个孩子本来原好，如今既做了屈家的儿子，从前的出身倒也不必提起，算他转了个劫罢"；及至梅士燮面圣回家，她委婉说明，梅士燮道"我已猜着了几分"，也说了梦中之语，赞他"没有一点优伶习气，不说破真令人看不出来"，遂唤梅进吩咐阖府一律称"屈大爷"，不许有一分怠慢，又命琴仙日间随子玉在书房念书，还叮嘱子玉尽心教他、不许轻看——这句话在子玉听来比中宏词科还得意几倍。数日后子玉带他遍访众名士，又到九香楼与九位名旦相见，各诉离情，牵衣执手谈了一整天；琴仙最喜的是众人都已跳出孽海，保全了清白身子。腊尽春回，又是一年。`,en:`A hundred days have passed with Qinxian keeping vigil at Huguo Temple in Nanjing. Daily offerings, paper money, and rent come to ten taels a month, and in three months everything he owns has gone to the pawnshop; early winter has come and he has no padded coat, still less Liu Xi. Liu Xi urges him to go to Hou Shiweng, whose disciples fill the empire and whose connections outshine even Xu Ziyun's — and gets the single answer that he would sooner starve. Instead Qinxian writes several piteous letters to Mei Ziyu, Xu Ziyun, Su Huifang and others, begging that someone be sent to fetch him, enclosing in Ziyun's the dying words of Qu Daoweng. When the temple holds services the crowds press in and strangers loiter outside his door; Liu Xi sells two garments and sets up a small stall of fruit and nuts at the gate, taking a hundred cash a day, while Qinxian lives on gruel and paints in his idle hours. Hou Shiweng, back from Suzhou and hearing of his straits, cannot let the matter rest, and comes with twenty taels of gold and a thick face. At the gate he sees flower paintings on raw paper hung by the stall — a peony sold for two hundred cash, a plum for a hundred and fifty, and a commission for an eight-panel screen at two thousand — and begins with praise of the brush before working round to his purpose: plum blossom may endure ice, but jade bones cannot bear the frost; Nanjing swarms with kidnappers who drug handsome youths, pass them off as women for hire, or cripple them into freaks to be shown at crossroads for money. Every word is solicitude, and then the true face appears — his own gate, he boasts, is not lonely of pretty things; a firefly must borrow the torch-dragon's light and a fly must ride the horse's tail to travel far; crab-apple and pear may be planted together without scandal, and among his singing boys and little maids there is no lack of pleasure. Qinxian's face burns; behind the old man's leathery mask he sees a knot of foulness, and only his seniority keeps the reproach unspoken. Mistaking silence for a girlish yielding, Hou has the silver brought and puts it in his hands. Qinxian thanks him and refuses: he dared not take the earlier gifts and dares not take this; cold and hunger are decreed by fate, and even the heroes of old have begged from door to door; to freeze or starve is at least to die in the open light, and he will not do the shameless thing that clings to life. 

With that he walks away without a backward look. Hou is left holding the packet as though a blade had shaved his old hide, and can only abuse him to Liu Xi, declare that he washes his hands of the whole business, and depart; Qinxian weeps alone, unable to tell his servant what was really said. Meanwhile Mei Shixie, as education commissioner in Jiangxi, has selected scholars so well and memorialised so effectively that he is promoted three times in a month, ending as vice-minister of personnel and ordered to the capital, his old post going to Lu Zongyuan. Word from home tells him Ziyu has won the erudite-composition examination, been made a compiler, and married. He sets out on the first of the eleventh month, reaches Nanjing ten days later to sweep the family graves, and calls on the governor-general, who asks after one Qu Benli of Jiangxi: constables at Ji'an have taken the thieves Zhang Gui and Qian De with the commission papers, two trunks, three hundred and seventeen taels, a gold bracelet and clothing, their accomplice Wang Sheng being dead, and the goods are to be returned to the dead man's heir. Moved, Mei Shixie goes next day with offerings to Huguo Temple. Qinxian, prostrate in mourning white, is glad to see Ziyu's father and terrified of being questioned about his origins. Mei Shixie offers incense and wine, calls him out, looks at him, nods, and sighs that Daoweng may be said to have had a son; he asks his age — seventeen — how Daoweng died, and how he now lives, and rails at a world that abandoned so famous a man the moment he was dead. He promises the recovery of the stolen goods and takes the whole burden of the burial on himself. Qinxian asks that the grave be made beside the Lady Du's at Mochou Lake, and tells, haltingly, the story of that former life; Mei Shixie half believes and says only that he will go and look. He is thin and tall with a face like autumn frost, not in the least like his son, and he forbids Qinxian to address him as Excellency, insisting on the terms of uncle and nephew. 

That night he dreams that Qu Daoweng comes in gauze cap and red robe, takes his hand, and begs that his bones be given to his daughter by Mochou Lake and that his homeless boy be lent one branch to perch on; then, turning back at the door with a lotus in his hand, says that this flower rises out of mud to face clear water and is not disgraced by the mud, and that what is past should not be held against it. Waking, Mei Shixie reads the dream: the adopted son must have been a boy-actor. He decides it makes no difference if the person is good. Next day the two visit Mochou Lake. Deep winter, after heavy snow — yet inside the mottled bamboo the air is as mild as the second month; hundreds of trumpet-creeper flowers are open, orchids are in bloom together, the silk-tree that flowers only at midsummer is budding, and the four saplings the Lady Du planted — apple, pear, peach and plum — each bear a single blossom, while kingfishers rise in flocks, circle the two men crying, and fly off across the water. Mei Shixie, who has never believed in immortals, stands in awe and resolves to build a shrine to Qu Daoweng with a tablet for the Lady Du beside it. Qinxian kneels weeping. The magistrate of Jiangning agrees at once; ground is measured, materials gathered, the twenty-fourth chosen for the burial, and Mei Shixie writes the epitaph and the spirit-way stele himself. Hundreds of Nanjing gentry come to the funeral with poems, songs, essays and couplets for the shrine. Unable to stay, he leaves two thousand taels with his old steward Mei Cheng to finish the work, and paints a portrait of the Lady Du to be modelled in clay. Hou Shiweng, dragged along by the gentry, finds Qinxian will not speak to him, and leaves in hatred and envy without even making his bow. Later the gentry raise subscriptions, ten and twenty taels at a time, until the shrine spreads into pavilions and towers, with a mid-lake kiosk, a nine-turn red bridge, stone archways and guardian figures before the graves — a famous sight of Nanjing. 

On the twenty-eighth they sail north, and Mei Shixie, keeping Qinxian by him as he would Ziyu, delights in his steadiness and quickness and plans to teach him letters in the capital; because of the dream he never once asks about his past, and orders the household to call him Master Qu. In the capital Ziyu has waited in vain for word from his father when, on the fifteenth of the twelfth month, a letter forwarded from Jiangxi arrives. Delighted, thinking Qinxian has reached the post, he opens it to find the words of a mourning announcement — and reading how the runaway servants stripped him bare, how he keeps a coffin in a cold temple without food or clothing, how Hou Shiweng has repeatedly insulted him, and how he would have died but for the unburied coffin, weeps helplessly. He drives to Yiyuan and finds neither Xiao Cixian nor Xu Ziyun, then to the Nine Fragrance Pavilion, where Shi Nanxiang, Gao Pin, Jin Jifu and the performers pass the letter round in tears. Xu Ziyun, fetched at last, reads Daoweng's dying words and breaks down; he undertakes at once to send men south to bring Qinxian home and bury Daoweng, and despatches a thousand taels that night. Xiao Cixian copies a portrait of Daoweng in a single night; the company sets up a tablet at the pavilion and mourns three days, Hua Guangsu coming to weep with them, until the garden has become a house of mourning with no one in it entitled to wear white. Ziyu dares not go to the inner rooms, fearing Lady Yan's questions and Wang Qionghua's eyes — and it is Qionghua who presses him until he tells her everything. On the twenty-second he and Yan Zhongqing ride thirty li out to meet the party. Carriages arrive at the second watch; after the first words with his son, Mei Shixie asks why Master Qu's carriage has not come, and a lantern leads in a beautiful youth. The two young men stare in astonishment; Qinxian, who has known him at once, masters himself and pays his respects. 

Introduced as the son of Qu Daosheng, travelling to the capital with him and destined to live with them, Qinxian leaves Ziyu first stunned and then laughing outright with joy. In the next room the three sit like mutes, one afraid to ask and one afraid to tell, speaking only with their eyes — now knitted brows and wet lashes, now open delight, like plum-rain weather that cannot decide. Late at night Ziyu and Qinxian lie on one pillow in their clothes and talk it all out: the longing after the parting, the sights of Nanjing, the grave of his former life at Mochou Lake, the Lady Du's marvels, Daoweng's death and his apotheosis, the misery after the servants fled, Liu Xi's devotion, Hou Shiweng's insults, and Mei Shixie's righteous burial and shrine; and Ziyu tells of his illness after the farewell and his grief at the news. Yan Zhongqing, watching them whisper head to head, reflects that these two have broken their hearts and wept their eyes out for two years to earn this one night. At dawn Qinxian is still asleep on Ziyu's arm, and both blush when Zhongqing wakes them. As for what to say about his origins, Zhongqing argues that concealment will not serve — better that Lady Yan explain it gently to Mei Shixie than that an outsider let it out. In the city, Mei Jin and Yun'er stare at Qinxian and cannot decide whether it is he or not. Lady Yan, told the truth, says only that the boy was always good, that now he is a son of the Qu family his old calling need never be mentioned, and that he may be counted as having passed through a rebirth. When Mei Shixie returns from his audience, she tells him carefully, and he answers that he had already half guessed, recounts the dream, and remarks that the boy has not a trace of the theatre about him and could never be found out unspoken. He summons Mei Jin, orders the whole household to call him Master Qu and to show him not the least discourtesy, sets him to study with Ziyu, and charges Ziyu to teach him faithfully and never to look down on him — a charge that pleases Ziyu more than his examination honours. A few days later Ziyu takes him round the scholars and to the Nine Fragrance Pavilion, where the nine performers cling to his sleeves and talk a whole day away; and what gladdens Qinxian most is that all of them have climbed out of that sea of bitterness with their persons unspoiled. The old year ends and spring comes round again.`},60:{zh:`新年过后又值元宵，六街三市火树银花。正月十三日徐子云大会群贤，华光宿、田春航、梅子玉、史南湘、高品、颜仲清、刘文泽、王恂、萧次贤、金吉甫、屈琴仙与九香园诸人齐集。琴仙见了华光宿仍有些不安，华光宿绝口不问前事，只以礼相待；他此时已出旦党而入士党，从前作旦时傲睨一切，如今反谦谦自守，上下益发敬重，无一人敢笑他，而他待众名旦仍如往日毫不生疏。是日觥筹交错，入夜灯火交辉，如见万花齐放，炮竹之声闻于数里，二更后煮茗清谈。琴仙历尽艰辛，此时方觉魔难尽释，回想萧寺孤灯残月，恍如梦觉。萧次贤把他从前的梦境细说与金吉甫听，金吉甫便笑说：九香楼是绝好一个花园，百花全有，却只在僻处供一个花神牌位，与土地祠一般，岂不亵渎花神？不如借这九人作个九香花史。萧次贤久有此意，正要画他们九人小像，请金吉甫润色，刻石供养楼下。只是九人凑不成十二位，金吉甫献策：以屈道翁为总持花主，再借琴仙前生所遇的杜仙女，连琴仙凑成十二。

田春航主张画要传神而不必用真姓名，各缀别号，每人一篇赞语，说得似真似幻、人花两合；颜仲清主张单画人物不补景，后系小传；刘文泽主张分刻两石，嵌在东西两楹；王恂说杜仙女便画采莲船上的样子；金吉甫说琴仙梦中那面镜子必非无因，当画一龙执镜作为头幅。于是拈阄分题——文字有灵，前缘暗合：徐子云拈得道翁，梅子玉拈得杜仙女与琴仙，金吉甫拈得袁宝珠，田春航拈得苏蕙芳，颜仲清拈得秦琪官，刘文泽拈得林春喜，史南湘拈得王兰保，王恂拈得王桂保，高品拈得李玉林，萧次贤拈得金漱芳，独陆素兰无人拈着，只好送与华光宿去作。萧次贤妙腕灵思画了十日，经金吉甫一一改定，画一幅刻一幅，二十七日连传赞刻齐，各人书丹，二十八日搬往九香楼镶嵌，一日完工。三十日琴仙先去看碑，十人先自祭花神——"这个花神就是我们的像，若叫他们来祭，我们也当不起"。东楹第一方是云水中隐一龙，露爪托镜，题曰《品花宝鉴》，萧次贤赞其"烛微照幽，隐奸显贤"，能媸其媸而妍其妍；第二方是纶巾道服的花主与左右花史，梅子玉赞以"公气为云，公神为水"。袁宝珠看罢说：这些赞语实在做得好，若把我们的实事叙进去，虽不至辱身，究竟也是贱行；苏蕙芳接口道，那些花谱花评纵把人赞得色艺俱佳，终不免梨园习气，"我们这一关倒可以算跳出了"。其下九方：锦文花史苏仙有田春航跋，称其"抟雪作肤，镂月为骨"；弄珠花史袁仙有金吉甫赞；素心花史陆仙有华光宿小传，说他常衣紫绡衣行吟风露之间，殆莲花化身；纤纤花史金仙有萧次贤七律，写尽金漱芳的瘦骨清姿；娟娟花史李仙是高品的诗，众人笑他这一回总算没有刻薄人，又说起他连表叔都要顽笑的旧话；侠隐花史王仙是史南湘的七古，写王兰保舞剑如"去如飞鸟来如霆"，琴仙以为比《公孙大娘舞剑器行》刻画更细，苏蕙芳则说去年奚十一闹来全亏了他；报春花史林仙是刘文泽的小赋；蟾宫花史王仙是王恂的五古；及第花史秦仙是颜仲清的骈序。琴仙叹道：虽是些小文章，然吉光片羽、彩散人间，"有此一赞，也不孤负我们数年辛苦"。

饭后袁宝珠忽然说：我们十人同在京师沉沦菊部，如今个个跳了出来，算不得世间少有；难得的是那一班名士把我们抬举到这个地位——感激便当思报答。他的法子是：他们既刻我们的像作花神，我们便刻他们的像供在楼上，他们称我们花史，我们便称他们文星，仿司空图《诗品》各作四言赞语。众人称妙，公推陆素兰与林春喜分画，且说不必画服饰，不衫不履最妙。是夜各自构思，次日到楼上互评推敲，由袁宝珠端楷誊清。诸名士到齐，仍要祭花神，被众人拦住，只摆香案奠酒焚香而不下拜，然后序齿设四席。酒至半酣，袁宝珠避席致辞：十人同入迷津，今登觉岸，将来勉盖前愆、勤修后果，得齿于人皆诸君提拔之力；感恩有心而报德无力，唯有供设文星禄位、朝夕顶礼。又说徐子云提唱风雅，当另立一品在诸文星之上，曰"群仙领袖"。于是十一篇赞语次第展读：群仙领袖徐文星、仙中逸品萧文星、趣品高文星、狂品史文星、高品颜文星、和品刘文星、乐品王文星、华品田文星、豪品华文星、上品金文星、正品梅文星，众名士谦让称谬赞，读毕却喜动颜色，说"可谓木桃之投，而得琼瑶之报矣"。

陆素兰、林春喜画了几日，摹石书丹，二十余日完竣，择三月初三供设楼上为长生禄位。琴仙提议须作一篇祝文方表诚意，十人公同斟酌，改削数遍，竟不见联缀痕迹。袁宝珠又说：既跳出梨园，从前一切之物都用不着了，孽根须净、色界尽除，不如当着众名士将钗钿首饰一齐熔化，舞袖歌裙一火焚之，岂不爽快；众人都说正合心意，独琴仙已无此物，便各自检出聚在一处。初三日楼上红烛双辉、香烟云绕，花排姐妹之班、雁次弟兄之序，十花史齐齐下拜，诸名士连忙扶起。高品高声朗读祝文：文星者川岳之灵，在天为列宿，在世为传人，其光明如火，其和煦如春，根于性者为纲常伦纪，见于词者为变化奇神……末以神娥授笔作结，祝"笔之色兮有五，笔之花兮半含吐"。读毕众花史齐拜，将那些舞衫歌扇、翠羽金钿在园中太湖石畔烧化。火光五色，吐金闪绿；将烬时忽起一阵香风，把灰烬吹上半空，飘飘点点映着红日，如无数花朵蝴蝶飞舞，香气扑鼻，越旋越高，到了半天化作万点金光，一闪不见。园中万花如笑，颤巍巍的像要说话一般。全书至此作结：琴言由名旦而为琴仙，由被人品第者而入图传名；子玉之情、子云之义、道翁之教、群士之风流，都在这一部书里得了归宿——"我自有情君莫问，此中得失寸心知"。`,en:`The new year passes and the Lantern Festival comes, the streets ablaze with fire-trees and silver flowers. On the thirteenth Xu Ziyun assembles the whole company: Hua Guangsu, Tian Chunhang, Mei Ziyu, Shi Nanxiang, Gao Pin, Yan Zhongqing, Liu Wenze, Wang Xun, Xiao Cixian, Jin Jifu, Qu Qinxian, and everyone from the Nine Fragrance garden. Qinxian is still uneasy before Hua Guangsu, who never once alludes to the past and treats him with plain courtesy. He has crossed from the party of the performers to the party of the scholars, and where once, as a famous boy-actor, he looked past everyone, he is now modest and self-contained, so that the whole household respects him the more and no one presumes to laugh; toward the performers he is exactly as before. Cups pass all day, and at night the lanterns blaze until it seems every flower in the world has opened at once, the firecrackers heard for li around, the company settling to tea and talk after the second watch. Qinxian, who has come through everything, feels the ordeal finally lifted, and the cold temple with its lonely lamp and setting moon seems a dream he has woken from. Xiao Cixian recounts his dreams in detail to Jin Jifu, and Jin Jifu says, laughing, that the Nine Fragrance garden holds every flower there is and yet keeps only a single spirit-tablet in a corner like a village earth-shrine — an insult to the flower gods. Why not make the nine of them the flower-historians of the garden? Xiao Cixian has long meant to paint their portraits; let Jin Jifu polish the inscriptions, and let the whole be cut in stone beneath the pavilion. Only nine will not make the requisite twelve — so Jin Jifu proposes taking Qu Daoweng as the presiding lord of flowers and borrowing the Lady Du of Qinxian's former life, which with Qinxian himself completes the number. 

Tian Chunhang says the likenesses must be true but the names invented, each with a sobriquet and an encomium poised between the real and the visionary, so that person and flower are read as one; Yan Zhongqing wants figures alone without scenery, each followed by a short life; Liu Wenze wants two stones set into the east and west bays; Wang Xun wants the Lady Du painted in her lotus-gathering boat; and Jin Jifu says the mirror of Qinxian's dream cannot have been meaningless, and should head the series as a dragon holding a mirror. Lots are drawn, and the writing seems to know its own destiny: Xu Ziyun draws Daoweng, Mei Ziyu draws both the Lady Du and Qinxian, Jin Jifu draws Yuan Baozhu, Tian Chunhang draws Su Huifang, Yan Zhongqing draws Qin Qiguan, Liu Wenze draws Lin Chunxi, Shi Nanxiang draws Wang Lanbao, Wang Xun draws Wang Guibao, Gao Pin draws Li Yulin, Xiao Cixian draws Jin Shufang; only Lu Sulan falls to no one, and his portrait is sent to Hua Guangsu to write. Xiao Cixian paints for ten days, Jin Jifu corrects each sheet, the carvers keep pace with the brush, and by the twenty-seventh the lives and encomia are cut as well, each in its author's own hand; on the twenty-eighth the stones go into the pavilion and are set in a single day. On the thirtieth Qinxian comes early to see them, and the ten make their own offering first — these flower gods being their own portraits, they could not sit and be worshipped by their friends. The first stone in the east bay shows a dragon half hidden in cloud and water, one claw holding a mirror, titled *Pinhua Baojian*, with Xiao Cixian's encomium on a light that searches out the hidden, exposing the corrupt and revealing the worthy, making the ugly ugly and the fair fair. The second shows the lord of flowers in scholar's cap and robe with an attendant on either hand, and Mei Ziyu's praise of a spirit that is cloud and water at once. Yuan Baozhu, reading, remarks that these are finely made, and that to have set down their actual histories, while no disgrace to the body, would still have been a record of a base calling; Su Huifang answers that the old registers and rankings of the flowers, however they praised a performer's looks and art, never escaped the smell of the theatre — and that this is a barrier the ten of them may be said to have got over. Then come the nine: Su Huifang as the flower-historian of brocade patterns, with Tian Chunhang's colophon on a skin moulded from snow and bones carved from moonlight; Yuan Baozhu with a pearl in hand and Jin Jifu's praise; Lu Sulan holding an orchid, with Hua Guangsu's life declaring him a lotus made flesh, wandering in purple gauze and chanting in the wind and dew; Jin Shufang light as a dancer on a palm, with Xiao Cixian's regulated verse; Li Yulin beneath jade trees, with Gao Pin's poem, which the company agrees is the one occasion he has not been cruel — and which leads to the old story of the joke he played on his own uncle; Wang Lanbao with a sword at his waist like Lady Gongsun, with Shi Nanxiang's long ballad of a blade that goes like a bird and returns like thunder, which Qinxian thinks finer in detail than the *Ballad of Lady Gongsun's Sword Dance*, and which prompts Su Huifang to say that it was Lanbao who saved them when Xi Shiyi came raging into the garden; Lin Chunxi under plum blossom with a magpie, with Liu Wenze's little rhapsody; Wang Guibao beneath the cassia, with Wang Xun's five-word poem of the moon-palace; and Qin Qiguan under apricot flowers, with Yan Zhongqing's parallel-prose preface. 

Qinxian says that these are small pieces of writing, but a stray feather of the sun-bird scattering colour through the world — that with such praise, their years of hardship have not been wasted. After the meal Yuan Baozhu speaks: the ten of them sank together into the theatre of the capital and have all climbed out of it, which is not so rare a thing; what is rare is the company of scholars who raised them to this position — and gratitude ought to look for a way to repay. His proposal is that, since their friends have carved them as flower gods, they should carve their friends and enshrine them above; since they are called flower-historians, their friends shall be called stars of letters, each with a four-word encomium in the manner of Sikong Tu's *Poetic Categories*. Everyone approves; Lu Sulan and Lin Chunxi are chosen to divide the painting, and it is agreed the figures should wear no formal dress at all — undressed and unshod is best. That night each composes; next morning at the pavilion they criticise, revise and polish together, and Yuan Baozhu copies the whole out in careful regular script. The scholars arrive and want to sacrifice to the flower gods, but are prevented; incense and wine are offered without prostration, and four tables are set by age. Half drunk, Yuan Baozhu leaves his seat to speak: the ten entered the ford of delusion together and have reached the far shore, and if they can now atone for what is past and cultivate what is to come and stand as people among people, it is by their patrons' lifting hands; feeling gratitude but powerless to repay it, they ask leave to set up tablets of long life and do reverence morning and evening. And since Xu Ziyun led the whole enterprise of elegance, he must have a rank of his own above the rest: Leader of the Immortals. The eleven encomia are then read out in order — the leader; the untrammelled Xiao; the whimsical Gao; the wild Shi; the lofty Yan; the harmonious Liu; the joyous Wang Xun; the splendid Tian; the magnificent Hua; the supreme Jin; the upright Mei — and the scholars protest that the titles are far too flattering, yet their faces shine, and they say that a gift of peaches has been repaid with jade. 

Lu Sulan and Lin Chunxi paint for some days, the images are transferred to stone and the encomia written out, and in twenty days all is finished; the third day of the third month is chosen for the enshrining. Qinxian says a prayer-text is needed to make the offering sincere, and the ten draft it jointly, revising until the seams do not show. Yuan Baozhu then proposes that, having left the theatre, they have no use for anything belonging to it: the root of sin should be cleansed and the realm of desire wholly cleared, so let the hairpins and ornaments be melted down before their friends' eyes and the dancing sleeves and singing skirts burnt in one fire. All agree; only Qinxian has nothing left to give, and the rest gather their things into one pile. On the third the pavilion is bright with paired candles and cloudy with incense, the ten ranged like sisters and ordered like brothers; they bow together and the scholars hurry to raise them. Gao Pin reads the prayer aloud — that the stars of letters are the spirit of rivers and mountains, constellations in heaven and transmitters on earth, bright as fire and warming as spring, rooted in nature as the bonds of human order and appearing in words as marvellous transformation — closing with the goddess who bestows the brush, and the wish that its colours be five and its flower half-opened. The flower-historians bow again, and the dancing gowns, singing fans, kingfisher feathers and gold ornaments are burnt beside the lake-rock in the garden. The flames run through five colours, spitting gold and flashing green; and as the fire dies a scented wind lifts the ashes into the air, where, drifting against the red sun, they look like numberless flowers and butterflies in flight, sweet on the air, spiralling higher until at mid-heaven they become ten thousand points of gold and vanish in a flash. The flowers of the garden all seem to be laughing, trembling as though about to speak. So the book ends: Qinyan, once Qinyan the famous performer, is now Qinxian, no longer one whom others rank and appraise but one who has entered the picture and been given a name that lasts; Ziyu's feeling, Ziyun's generosity, Daoweng's teaching, and the brilliance of the whole company find their resting place here — I have my own feelings, and you need not ask; what has been gained and lost in all this, the heart alone knows.`}},o={...e,...a,...n,...t,...i,...s};export{o as c};
